{"_type":"item","id":"019d2231359971bf9ea6108fa527afd3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Port CodeTools policy.py to GDScript","description":"Port ~/github/CodeTools/src/codetools/policy.py to GDScript. Create at src/Scripts/Services/CodeTools/Policy.gd. Include test. Must handle: JSON loading, regex deny patterns, baseline protection, immutable at runtime.","created_at":"2026-03-24T23:32:23","updated_at":"2026-03-24T23:37:51","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","port","sonnet"],"parent":"minerva:019d222b53d17ccb93746c71f733ce38"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d22313c5f7456b6d50a42343fc9ac","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Port CodeTools glob.py to GDScript","description":"Port ~/github/CodeTools/src/codetools/tools/glob.py to GDScript. Create at src/Scripts/Services/CodeTools/GlobTool.gd. Include test. Must handle: recursive directory walk, glob patterns (*, **, ?), DirAccess API.","created_at":"2026-03-24T23:32:25","updated_at":"2026-03-24T23:36:51","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","port","sonnet"],"parent":"minerva:019d222b53d17ccb93746c71f733ce38"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2269e58b7e06b3060987ba8ce7bd","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Terminal virtual blocks: interleaved MCP tool call visualization","description":"Interleave MCP tool call/response blocks into the terminal timeline alongside real PTY command blocks. Provides 100% traceability — every action (shell commands AND file operations) is visible, injectable, and auditable in the terminal UI.\n\n**Motivation:** Secure codebase workflows (e.g., SIEM tools) require full traceability. Agent actions via MCP (file reads/writes/edits, grep, glob) currently bypass the terminal and are invisible to the user. They should appear inline in the terminal timeline.\n\n**Two block types in the terminal:**\n1. **Real blocks** (existing) — command typed in PTY, output from PTY\n2. **Virtual blocks** (new) — MCP tool calls rendered as styled cards, interleaved chronologically\n\n**Visual design:**\n- Virtual blocks rendered as bordered cards (distinct from PTY output)\n- Show: tool name, key arguments, result summary\n- Different background/border color from real blocks\n- Collapsible for long results (e.g., file_read with 100 lines)\n- Gutter checkbox for injection (same as real blocks)\n\n**Data model:**\n- Extend TerminalBlock with a `block_type` enum: COMMAND (existing) vs VIRTUAL\n- Virtual blocks store: tool_name, arguments dict, result dict, timestamp\n- `format_for_injection()` formats virtual blocks as tool-call markdown\n- Both types live in the same `_blocks` array, ordered by time\n\n**Which tools create virtual blocks:**\n- All CodeTools: file_read, file_write, file_edit, file_glob, file_grep, bash, cwd\n- Future: any MCP tool call that modifies state or returns data\n\n**`minerva_bash` specifically:**\n- Should execute through the real PTY (visible, creates real block)\n- NOT through OS.execute() headless\n- This means bash becomes a real block, not virtual\n- Requires terminal MCP tools (write to PTY, wait for prompt)\n\n**Integration points:**\n- MinervaMCPServer: after each tool execution, emit signal with tool call data\n- TerminalNew: listen to signal, create virtual block, render inline\n- Virtual blocks injectable into chat via same proxy mechanism\n- Virtual blocks saveable as notes via same pipeline\n\n**Not in scope (future):**\n- Dedicated agent terminal tabs\n- Terminal MCP tools (list/create/write/read) — separate DCR\n- Replay/playback of tool call history","created_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:18","updated_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:18","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","mcp","terminal","traceability","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d226a106271d4a786454ef5b2c435","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Extend TerminalBlock data model with virtual block type","description":"Extend terminal_block.gd to support virtual blocks.\n\n**Changes to TerminalBlock:**\n- Add `enum BlockType { COMMAND, VIRTUAL }`\n- Add `var block_type: BlockType = BlockType.COMMAND`\n- Add `var tool_name: String` (for virtual blocks)\n- Add `var tool_arguments: Dictionary` (for virtual blocks)\n- Add `var tool_result: Dictionary` (for virtual blocks)\n- Add `var timestamp: int` (unix time, for chronological ordering)\n- Update `format_for_injection()` to handle virtual blocks:\n  ```\n  ```tool-call\n  minerva_file_write\n  path: /tmp/test.txt\n  content: \"hello world\"\n  → success, 11 bytes\n  ```\n  ```\n\n**Backward compatible:** Existing COMMAND blocks unchanged. New fields default to empty.","created_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:29","updated_at":"2026-03-25T00:38:34","priority":1,"tags":["data-model","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d2269e58b7e06b3060987ba8ce7bd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d226a23a877048dd0b708e6688e94","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add MCP tool call signal to MinervaMCPServer","description":"Emit a signal from MinervaMCPServer after each CodeTools tool execution so the terminal can capture it.\n\n**Signal on SingletonObject (or MinervaMCPServer):**\n```gdscript\nsignal mcp_tool_executed(tool_name: String, arguments: Dictionary, result: Dictionary)\n```\n\n**Emit in `_execute_tool_impl()`** after each CodeTools match case returns. Only emit for codetools tool set (not all 163 tools).\n\n**Payload:** tool_name, arguments (sanitized — truncate large content values), result dict.","created_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:34","updated_at":"2026-03-25T00:38:55","priority":1,"tags":["phase-1","signal"],"parent":"minerva:019d2269e58b7e06b3060987ba8ce7bd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d226a34d87e2d99f073bfd7ee5835","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"TerminalNew: listen to signal, create virtual blocks","description":"Terminal listens to `mcp_tool_executed` signal and creates virtual blocks.\n\n**On signal received:**\n- Create a TerminalBlock with `block_type = VIRTUAL`\n- Set tool_name, tool_arguments, tool_result, timestamp\n- Append to `_blocks` array\n- Create gutter checkbox (same as real blocks)\n- Trigger re-render of the virtual block area\n\n**Which terminal receives it:**\n- The active terminal in the active group\n- If no terminal visible, queue and display when terminal opens\n\n**Blocked by:** items 1 and 2","created_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:38","updated_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:38","priority":2,"tags":["integration","phase-2"],"parent":"minerva:019d2269e58b7e06b3060987ba8ce7bd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d226a4a1179e58e8750549dea6b26","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Render virtual blocks as styled cards in terminal","description":"Render virtual blocks visually distinct from PTY output.\n\n**Visual design:**\n- Bordered card/panel with subtle background (e.g., dark blue-grey, 1px border)\n- Header: tool name + icon (e.g., wrench icon)\n- Body: key arguments formatted as key: value pairs\n- Footer: result summary (success/fail, key metrics like bytes_written)\n- Collapsible: click to expand full arguments/result for large payloads\n- Gutter checkbox aligned with real block checkboxes\n\n**Rendering approach:**\n- Virtual blocks are Control nodes (not cell-grid rendered)\n- Inserted into the terminal's output container at the correct position\n- Must scroll with terminal content\n- Must not interfere with PTY rendering above/below\n\n**Blocked by:** item 3","created_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:44","updated_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:44","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","rendering"],"parent":"minerva:019d2269e58b7e06b3060987ba8ce7bd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d226a5b627744b35865717ada350a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Virtual block injection and note proxy support","description":"Virtual blocks should be injectable into chat via the same checkbox + proxy mechanism as real blocks.\n\n**format_for_injection() for virtual blocks:**\n```markdown\n```tool-call\nminerva_file_edit\npath: /tmp/test.txt\nold_string: \"hello\"\nnew_string: \"goodbye\"\n→ success, 1 replacement\n```\n```\n\n**Proxy creation:** Same Note.Proxy pattern as real blocks — check gutter → proxy created → consumed on chat send.\n\n**Blocked by:** item 4 (rendering for checkbox placement)","created_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:48","updated_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:48","priority":2,"tags":["injection","phase-2"],"parent":"minerva:019d2269e58b7e06b3060987ba8ce7bd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d226a726f76e3905e08b8cbf634c8","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Route minerva_bash through terminal PTY instead of OS.execute","description":"Change minerva_bash to execute through a real terminal PTY tab instead of headless OS.execute(). This makes bash commands real blocks (not virtual), fully visible and traceable.\n\n**Implementation:**\n- Find or create an agent terminal tab\n- Write command to PTY via terminal.write_input(command + \"\\\n\")\n- Wait for block finalization (next prompt arrives)\n- Return the block's captured output_text\n- The command and output appear in the terminal UI in real-time\n\n**Requires:** Terminal MCP tools (minerva_terminal_write, minerva_terminal_read) or direct access to TerminalNew instance.\n\n**Challenge:** Async — need to await block finalization signal. OS.execute is synchronous; PTY execution is async.\n\n**Fallback:** If no terminal is visible, fall back to OS.execute + create virtual block for traceability.","created_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:54","updated_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:54","priority":2,"tags":["bash","phase-3"],"parent":"minerva:019d2269e58b7e06b3060987ba8ce7bd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d226a80a0742fb3d8be14a103242a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Write tests for virtual block lifecycle","description":"Headless tests for virtual block data model and injection formatting.\n\n**Tests:**\n- Create virtual block, verify fields set correctly\n- format_for_injection() produces correct tool-call markdown\n- Virtual blocks interleave correctly with real blocks in _blocks array\n- Virtual block proxy creation and consumption\n- Large result truncation in format_for_injection\n\nSame SceneTree test pattern as other tests.","created_at":"2026-03-25T00:34:58","updated_at":"2026-03-25T00:42:16","priority":3,"tags":["phase-3","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d2269e58b7e06b3060987ba8ce7bd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d22c8bbd0765f8b0b1d4e148748d0","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"1-agent-1-terminal: per-agent terminal tabs for MCP tool traceability","description":"Each MCP agent gets its own terminal tab for tool call traceability. Virtual blocks route to the agent's tab, not the user's terminal.\n\n**Rules:**\n- Agent with ID → gets its own named tab (\"Agent: Claude Code\")\n- No agent ID → goes to default \"MCP Activity\" tab\n- User typing → normal terminal, no virtual blocks\n- Tab auto-created on first tool call from that agent\n- Tab labeled with agent identity, visually tagged\n- Tab persists after agent finishes (audit trail)\n- User can close agent tabs manually\n\n**Data flow:**\n1. MCP tool call arrives with optional agent_id\n2. MinervaMCPServer emits mcp_tool_executed with agent_id\n3. TerminalTabContainer routes to agent's tab (creates if needed)\n4. Virtual block written to that tab via write_to_screen()\n\n**Changes needed:**\n- Signal: add agent_id parameter to mcp_tool_executed\n- MCP HTTP server: extract agent identity from request headers or session\n- TerminalTabContainer: maintain agent_id → terminal tab mapping\n- TerminalNew: remove is_visible_in_tree() gate, receive blocks via direct call\n- Agent tab styling: different tab icon/color\n\n**Multi-agent scenario:**\n- 5 sub-agents running → 5 agent tabs in terminal\n- User can split-view to watch 2 agents side-by-side\n- Each tab is a clean sequential audit trail\n- No interleaving between agents","created_at":"2026-03-25T02:17:53","updated_at":"2026-03-25T02:17:53","priority":2,"tags":["agent","mcp","terminal","traceability"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d22c8e5387f719e3fac9caa599ed3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add agent_id to mcp_tool_executed signal and MCP call path","description":"Add agent_id to the signal and plumb it through the MCP call path.\n\n- Signal: `mcp_tool_executed(tool_name, arguments, result, agent_id)`\n- MinervaMCPServer: pass agent_id in all 7 emit calls\n- MCP HTTP server: extract agent identity from request (X-Agent-Id header, session, or default \"\")\n- Default agent_id: empty string = \"MCP Activity\" catch-all\n\nFiles: singleton_object.gd, MinervaMCPServer.gd, MinervaMCPHttpServer.gd","created_at":"2026-03-25T02:18:04","updated_at":"2026-03-25T02:20:24","priority":1,"tags":["phase-1","signal"],"parent":"minerva:019d22c8bbd0765f8b0b1d4e148748d0"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d22c8fa2f7e2d8d8abeffdee747f3","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"TerminalTabContainer: agent tab registry and auto-creation","description":"TerminalTabContainer maintains agent_id → TerminalNew mapping. Auto-creates agent tabs.\n\n- `var _agent_terminals: Dictionary = {}` — agent_id → TerminalNew\n- `func get_or_create_agent_terminal(agent_id: String) -> TerminalNew`\n- Empty agent_id → \"MCP Activity\" default tab\n- Non-empty → \"Agent: {agent_id}\" tab\n- Agent tabs created in left group (or whichever group has space)\n- Tab label includes agent name\n- Returns the TerminalNew instance for direct write_to_screen calls\n\nFiles: TerminalTabContainer.gd, TerminalTabGroup.gd","created_at":"2026-03-25T02:18:09","updated_at":"2026-03-25T02:18:09","priority":1,"tags":["phase-1","routing"],"parent":"minerva:019d22c8bbd0765f8b0b1d4e148748d0"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d22c90e7f7b59bc9eeae9c737f9b2","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Route virtual blocks to agent terminal instead of visible terminal","description":"Change virtual block creation to route through TerminalTabContainer to the agent's tab.\n\nCurrent: each TerminalNew listens to mcp_tool_executed, only visible one acts.\nNew: TerminalTabContainer listens to signal, routes to agent's tab via get_or_create_agent_terminal().\n\n- Remove mcp_tool_executed listener from TerminalNew._ready()\n- Add listener to TerminalTabContainer\n- TerminalTabContainer calls agent terminal's write methods directly\n- Move _on_mcp_tool_executed, _write_virtual_summary, _expand_virtual_block to work on any TerminalNew (pass as parameter)\n\nBlocked by: items 1 and 2","created_at":"2026-03-25T02:18:15","updated_at":"2026-03-25T02:18:15","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","routing"],"parent":"minerva:019d22c8bbd0765f8b0b1d4e148748d0"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d22c919f4776e99a51e37dd998163","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Agent tab visual styling: icon, color, label","description":"Visually distinguish agent terminal tabs from user terminal tabs.\n\n- Tab label: \"Agent: Claude Code\" or \"MCP Activity\"\n- Tab icon: robot or gear icon (use existing Minerva icon assets)\n- Optional: different tab background color tint\n- Tooltip: \"MCP agent terminal — auto-created for tool call traceability\"\n\nBlocked by: item 2","created_at":"2026-03-25T02:18:17","updated_at":"2026-03-25T02:18:17","priority":3,"tags":["phase-2","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019d22c8bbd0765f8b0b1d4e148748d0"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d237cc59c731faa978904f0a08353","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Activity Log editor: per-agent MCP tool call traceability in editor pane","description":"Replace terminal-based virtual blocks with a dedicated Activity Log editor type in the editor pane. Each agent gets its own tab. Terminal goes back to being a pure terminal.\n\n**Design:**\n- New editor type: `Editor.Type.ACTIVITY_LOG`\n- Each agent gets its own Activity Log tab, auto-created on first tool call\n- Tab label: \"Activity: Claude Code\" or \"Activity: MCP\" (default)\n- Read-only scrollable log of MCP tool calls with collapsible entries\n- Entries show: timestamp, tool name, key args, result summary\n- Click to expand: full arguments + full result\n- Injectable into chat (select entries → reference block via proxy)\n- Auto-scrolls to latest entry\n\n**What to REVERT (terminal virtual block experiment):**\nThese commits added virtual blocks to the terminal and should be undone:\n- `0efdd9fc` Virtual terminal blocks: MCP tool calls rendered inline\n- `331fbd74` Fix type inference for create_virtual\n- `0bebb273` Virtual blocks: write ANSI text via write_to_screen\n- `54663914` Virtual blocks: terminal-native expand via gutter button\n- `48e2adad` Virtual blocks: remove ▸ prefix, enlarge button\n- `fb06bbfd` Virtual blocks: save/restore cursor\n- `00945dd5` Fix duplicate esc variable\n- `78ae5952` 1-agent-1-terminal: route to per-agent terminal tabs\n- `b34d2969` Fix agent tab: defer write_to_screen\n\n**What to KEEP:**\n- `mcp_tool_executed` signal on SingletonObject (with agent_id) — the Activity Log listens to this\n- `TerminalBlock.BlockType.VIRTUAL` + `create_virtual()` + `_format_virtual_for_injection()` — reused by Activity Log entries\n- `write_to_screen()` C++ method — useful for future terminal features, costs nothing to keep\n- CodeTools MCP tools (file_read/write/edit/glob/grep/bash/cwd) — working, tested\n- Agent tab concept — moves from terminal to editor pane\n\n**What to REMOVE from terminal:**\n- `_on_mcp_tool_executed` handler in TerminalNew\n- `_write_virtual_summary`, `_expand_virtual_block` in TerminalNew\n- Agent terminal registry in TerminalTabContainer\n- `get_or_create_agent_terminal`, `_write_virtual_to_terminal`","created_at":"2026-03-25T05:34:32","updated_at":"2026-03-25T05:34:32","priority":2,"tags":["activity-log","agent","editor","mcp","traceability"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d237cf64f761a8b6c843d34d3c9c8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Revert terminal virtual block code, keep signal + data model","description":"Clean up terminal code — remove virtual block rendering, agent tab routing. Keep reusable pieces.\n\n**Remove from TerminalNew.gd:**\n- _write_virtual_summary(), _expand_virtual_block()\n- _on_mcp_tool_executed handler (already a comment)\n- Any remaining virtual block UI code\n\n**Remove from TerminalTabContainer.gd:**\n- _agent_terminals registry\n- get_or_create_agent_terminal()\n- _on_mcp_tool_executed(), _write_virtual_to_terminal()\n- mcp_tool_executed signal connection\n\n**KEEP (reused by Activity Log):**\n- TerminalBlock.BlockType enum, virtual fields, create_virtual(), _format_virtual_for_injection()\n- SingletonObject.mcp_tool_executed signal (with agent_id)\n- MinervaMCPServer emit calls\n- write_to_screen() C++ method\n- test_virtual_blocks.gd tests","created_at":"2026-03-25T05:34:45","updated_at":"2026-03-25T05:38:19","priority":1,"tags":["phase-1","revert"],"parent":"minerva:019d237cc59c731faa978904f0a08353"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d237d0d3a77a7beb342bf92b06d5c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Create ActivityLogEditor type in Editor system","description":"Add ACTIVITY_LOG to Editor.Type enum and create the editor panel.\n\n**Follow existing editor patterns (see PCBEditor as reference):**\n- Add `Type.ACTIVITY_LOG` to Editor.Type enum\n- Add match case in Editor.create()\n- Add case in Editor._ready() for button hiding\n- Add case in EditorPane.add() for tab naming\n- Add case in Editor.get_saved_state()\n\n**ActivityLogPanel.gd (new file):**\n- Extends Control (or VBoxContainer)\n- ScrollContainer with VBoxContainer for log entries\n- Auto-scrolls to bottom on new entries\n- Each entry: timestamp label + tool summary + expand button\n- Read-only — no editing\n\n**Scene:** Build UI in _build_ui() like PCBEditor (no .tscn needed)","created_at":"2026-03-25T05:34:51","updated_at":"2026-03-25T05:40:00","priority":1,"tags":["editor","phase-2"],"parent":"minerva:019d237cc59c731faa978904f0a08353"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d237d267c79a497574adabc7c7804","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"ActivityLogPanel: render tool call entries with collapse/expand","description":"Render each tool call as a compact entry with expand/collapse.\n\n**Collapsed entry (default):**\n```\n12:04:01  file_write /tmp/test.txt → 33 bytes written\n```\nOne line: timestamp + tool name + key arg + result. Color-coded.\n\n**Expanded entry (click to toggle):**\n```\n12:04:01  file_write /tmp/test.txt → 33 bytes written\n  path: /tmp/test.txt\n  content: \"Testing compact virtual blocks\"\n  success: true\n  bytes_written: 33\n```\n\n**Implementation:**\n- Each entry is an HBoxContainer or custom Control\n- Collapse button toggles visibility of details VBoxContainer\n- Details show all arguments + all result fields\n- Long values truncated with \"...\" \n- Color: tool name in blue, args in grey, success green / fail red\n\nBlocked by: ActivityLogEditor type","created_at":"2026-03-25T05:34:57","updated_at":"2026-03-25T05:34:57","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","rendering"],"parent":"minerva:019d237cc59c731faa978904f0a08353"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d237d38fa790fabdefe7d3f706f30","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Route mcp_tool_executed signal to Activity Log editors","description":"Listen to mcp_tool_executed signal, route to correct Activity Log tab.\n\n**Logic:**\n- EditorPane or a manager listens to SingletonObject.mcp_tool_executed\n- On signal: find or create Activity Log editor tab for this agent_id\n- Empty agent_id → \"Activity: MCP\" tab\n- Named agent → \"Activity: {agent_id}\" tab\n- Add entry to that Activity Log's panel\n\n**Auto-create on first call:**\n- No Activity Log exists yet → create one via Editor.create(Type.ACTIVITY_LOG)\n- Store agent_id → editor mapping (similar to old _agent_terminals)\n\nBlocked by: ActivityLogEditor type","created_at":"2026-03-25T05:35:02","updated_at":"2026-03-25T05:35:02","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","routing"],"parent":"minerva:019d237cc59c731faa978904f0a08353"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d237d4df07407ab4a57de6d1fef50","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Activity Log entries: injectable into chat as reference blocks","description":"Allow Activity Log entries to be selected and injected into chat.\n\n**Approach:**\n- Checkbox next to each entry (like terminal block gutter)\n- Checked entries create Note.Proxy with target_chat_id\n- format_for_injection() on virtual blocks already exists — reuse it\n- Entries consumed on chat send via injection_consumed signal\n\n**Or simpler:** Use the existing Editor \"Send to LLM\" toggle, which sends the entire Activity Log content as a note. Individual entry selection is a future enhancement.\n\nBlocked by: Activity Log rendering","created_at":"2026-03-25T05:35:07","updated_at":"2026-03-25T05:35:07","priority":3,"tags":["injection","phase-3"],"parent":"minerva:019d237cc59c731faa978904f0a08353"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d237d5c00782995e5a8c9f25b3790","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Write tests for Activity Log editor lifecycle","description":"Headless tests for Activity Log entry creation, formatting, routing.\n\n- Test: create virtual block, format for injection\n- Test: multiple entries maintain chronological order\n- Test: agent_id routing creates separate logs\n- Test: entry expand/collapse data integrity\n\nReuse existing test_virtual_blocks.gd data model tests.","created_at":"2026-03-25T05:35:11","updated_at":"2026-03-25T05:35:11","priority":3,"tags":["phase-3","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d237cc59c731faa978904f0a08353"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d27433533752685776558b8046c78","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Interactive terminal control via MCP: read, write, wait","description":"Enable MCP agents to interactively control terminal sessions — read screen content, write keystrokes without blocking, and detect new output. This enables launching and conversing with interactive CLI tools (like Claude Code) from within Minerva.\n\n**The three missing capabilities:**\n\n1. **minerva_terminal_read** — Read current terminal screen content (or row range from scrollback). Returns plain text of what's visible. Uses existing get_cell_screen() for scrollback-safe reads.\n\n2. **minerva_terminal_write** — Send keystrokes/text to a terminal's PTY input without waiting for a prompt. Non-blocking. Uses existing write_input() on the C++ terminal object.\n\n3. **minerva_terminal_wait** — Block until new output appears on the terminal, with a timeout. Uses the existing vt_state_changed signal. Returns the new screen content when output arrives or times out.\n\n**Additional terminal MCP tools (from earlier DCR, now needed):**\n- minerva_terminal_list — list open terminal tabs with metadata\n- minerva_terminal_create — open a new terminal tab\n- minerva_terminal_close — close a terminal tab\n\n**Interactive flow example (agent drives claude CLI):**\n1. terminal_write \"claude\n\" → launches claude\n2. terminal_wait timeout=10000 → waits for claude to show its prompt\n3. terminal_read → sees claude's welcome/prompt\n4. terminal_write \"build hello world in rust\n\" → sends prompt\n5. terminal_wait timeout=60000 → waits for response\n6. terminal_read → reads claude's response\n7. Repeat or terminal_write \"/exit\n\" to quit\n\n**Implementation approach:**\n- terminal_read: new MCP tool calling get_cell_screen() across row range, returns text\n- terminal_write: new MCP tool calling terminal.write_input(), returns immediately\n- terminal_wait: new MCP tool that awaits vt_state_changed signal with timeout, returns screen content after change\n- All tools take a terminal_id parameter to target specific tabs\n- terminal_list/create/close for tab management\n\n**Key design decisions:**\n- terminal_read returns plain text (not cell data) — sufficient for agent use\n- terminal_wait needs a settle delay (output may arrive in chunks; wait for N ms of silence)\n- Terminal targeting: by tab index or auto-created agent terminal","created_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:09","updated_at":"2026-03-25T23:13:06","priority":1,"tags":["agent","interactive","mcp","terminal"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d27435bdf7bd4ad45d89f683e8516","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add minerva_terminal_list MCP tool","description":"Register MCP tool to list all open terminal tabs.\n\nReturns array of: {id (instance_id), name, group (left/right), cols, rows, visible}\n\nNeeds to traverse TerminalTabContainer → TerminalTabGroup(s) → TerminalNew instances. Use get_tree().get_nodes_in_group(\"terminal_pane\") or traverse the container.\n\nRegister in MinervaMCPServer._register_codetools(). Add handler function.","created_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:19","updated_at":"2026-03-25T23:14:21","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d27433533752685776558b8046c78"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d274367da7ce2bfc5d30af7cae626","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add minerva_terminal_create and minerva_terminal_close MCP tools","description":"Register MCP tools to create and close terminal tabs.\n\nterminal_create: optional name param, creates via TerminalTabGroup.add_terminal(), returns {id, name}.\nterminal_close: takes terminal_id, finds and closes the tab.\n\nNeed to make terminal visible if hidden (toggle terminal container visibility).","created_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:22","updated_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:22","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d27433533752685776558b8046c78"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2743790a74d0badac0756322ac00","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add minerva_terminal_read MCP tool","description":"Register MCP tool to read terminal screen content as plain text.\n\nParameters: terminal_id (optional, defaults to active), start_row (optional), end_row (optional)\n\nImplementation: iterate rows calling _extract_row_text_screen() or get_cell_screen(). Return {content: String, rows: int, cols: int}.\n\nIf no row range specified, return the full visible viewport. If start/end given, read that range from scrollback.\n\nThis is the critical \"look at screen\" capability. Uses existing get_cell_screen() which we already built and tested.","created_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:26","updated_at":"2026-03-25T23:15:46","priority":1,"tags":["core","mcp","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d27433533752685776558b8046c78"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2743890e79afa72aeb40655c220b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add minerva_terminal_write MCP tool (non-blocking)","description":"Register MCP tool to send text/keystrokes to a terminal's PTY input.\n\nParameters: terminal_id (optional, defaults to active), text (the input to send)\n\nImplementation: find the TerminalNew instance, call terminal.write_input(text). Returns immediately (non-blocking). The text appears in the terminal as if typed.\n\nCommon patterns: send \"command\n\" to execute, send \"\\x03\" for Ctrl+C, send \"\t\" for tab completion.\n\nUses existing write_input() C++ method — just needs MCP registration and routing.","created_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:30","updated_at":"2026-03-25T23:14:21","priority":1,"tags":["core","mcp","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d27433533752685776558b8046c78"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d27439fd770b7b6c347687e40aea3","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add minerva_terminal_wait MCP tool (await output with timeout)","description":"Register MCP tool that waits for new terminal output, then returns screen content.\n\nParameters: terminal_id (optional), timeout_ms (default 30000), settle_ms (default 500 — wait for output to stop before returning)\n\nImplementation:\n- Connect to vt_state_changed signal on the terminal\n- Start a timer for timeout\n- On each vt_state_changed, reset a settle timer\n- When settle timer expires (no new output for settle_ms), return screen content\n- If timeout expires first, return what's on screen + timed_out: true\n\nThis is the \"wait for response\" capability. The settle delay handles chunked output (programs write in bursts — we wait until it stops).\n\nReturns: {content: String, timed_out: bool, rows: int}","created_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:36","updated_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:36","priority":1,"tags":["core","mcp","phase-2"],"parent":"minerva:019d27433533752685776558b8046c78"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2743ad83795ab1eeada1d7da3bab","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Helper: find terminal by ID across groups","description":"All terminal MCP tools need to find a TerminalNew by ID (or default to active). Create a shared helper.\n\nfunc _find_terminal(terminal_id) -> TerminalNew:\n- If terminal_id provided: search all terminals in group \"terminal_pane\" by instance_id\n- If empty/null: return active terminal from TerminalTabContainer\n- Return null if not found\n\nUsed by: terminal_list, terminal_read, terminal_write, terminal_wait, terminal_close","created_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:40","updated_at":"2026-03-25T23:14:20","priority":1,"tags":["helper","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d27433533752685776558b8046c78"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2743bdeb7246a836e64b0aa549c6","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Write tests for terminal MCP tools","description":"Integration tests for terminal MCP tools. These need a running terminal (can't fully test headless).\n\nHeadless-testable:\n- Terminal helper finds terminals by group\n- Screen content extraction (reuse existing text extraction tests)\n\nManual test script:\n- Create terminal, write command, read screen, verify content\n- Wait for output with timeout\n- List terminals shows correct count","created_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:44","updated_at":"2026-03-25T23:10:44","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d27433533752685776558b8046c78"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d278a9d097bd382b4044b970c4501","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Automatic tool token management: search-based discovery + LRU budget","description":"Replace sending all ~170 tool schemas upfront (~25K tokens) with search-based discovery + LRU-managed tools array. Automatic, zero user management.\n\n**Architecture:**\n\n1. **System prompt (~400 tokens):** Short starter list of common tool names + category hints. No schemas.\n```\nYou have MCP tools available. Common tools:\nFiles: minerva_file_read, file_write, file_edit, file_glob, file_grep, bash, cwd\nChat: minerva_send_message, list_chats, create_chat\nTerminal: minerva_terminal_write, terminal_read, terminal_wait\nNotes: minerva_create_note, get_note, enable_notes\n\nMore tools available (spreadsheet, PCB, graphics, video, agents, etc.)\nCall minerva_tool_search(query) to discover and activate any tool.\n```\n\n2. **Tools array:** Starts with ONLY `minerva_tool_search` schema (~150 tokens).\n\n3. **Search activates tools:** Calling `tool_search(\"file_edit\")` returns the schema AND adds it to the tools array for subsequent turns. The LLM can then call the tool directly.\n\n4. **LRU with token budget:** Max ~3,000 tokens for the tools array. Each tool tracks `last_used_turn`. When adding a tool would exceed budget, prune least-recently-used. `tool_search` is never pruned.\n\n5. **Compaction resets:** On chat compaction, tools array resets to just `tool_search`.\n\n6. **Pruned tool recovery:** If LLM tries to call a pruned tool, return \"Tool not loaded. Call minerva_tool_search to reload.\" — graceful, 1 extra turn.\n\n7. **Workflow hints in descriptions:** Tool descriptions include prerequisites (\"call X first to get Y\"). Costs ~20 extra tokens per tool but prevents Haiku from calling tools with wrong parameters.\n\n**Token budget comparison:**\n\n| Scenario | Current | New |\n|----------|---------|-----|\n| First tool use | ~25,000 | ~1,150 |\n| 5 tools in use | ~25,000 | ~2,500 |\n| Just chatting (no tools) | ~25,000 | ~550 |\n| After compaction | ~25,000 | ~150 |\n\n**Test scenarios (must pass):**\n1. Simple: \"edit a file\" → search finds file_edit, 1 discovery call\n2. Esoteric: \"annotate a PCB component\" → search finds pcb_add_annotation, workflow hints guide multi-step plan\n3. Multi-tool: \"create spreadsheet, add data, format cells, make chart\" → progressive discovery, LRU keeps all spreadsheet tools active\n4. Budget pressure: use 10+ different tools → LRU prunes idle ones, active ones stay\n5. Pruned tool recovery: tool gets pruned, LLM re-discovers it gracefully\n6. Compaction: tools reset, LLM re-discovers what it needs\n7. Haiku-specific: Haiku agent discovers and correctly uses pcb_add_annotation with coordinate resolution","created_at":"2026-03-26T00:28:08","updated_at":"2026-04-11T17:51:03","priority":1,"tags":["agent","haiku","mcp","token-efficiency","tools"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d278ad56b7dd78b5208c315ab8a26","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Build ToolBudgetManager: LRU tracking + token budget enforcement","description":"New class: ToolBudgetManager.gd (~100 lines)\n\n**State:**\n- `_active_tools: Dictionary` — tool_name → {schema: Dictionary, last_used_turn: int, token_cost: int}\n- `_current_turn: int`\n- `_token_budget: int` (default 3000, configurable)\n- `minerva_tool_search` always present, never pruned\n\n**Methods:**\n- `activate_tool(name, schema)` — add to active set, prune LRU if over budget\n- `mark_used(name)` — update last_used_turn\n- `get_active_schemas() -> Array[Dictionary]` — for the tools array in API requests\n- `get_token_usage() -> int` — current total\n- `advance_turn()` — increment turn counter\n- `reset()` — compaction, back to just tool_search\n- `try_call(name) -> Dictionary` — returns schema if active, error message if pruned\n\n**Token estimation:** ~4 chars per token on the JSON schema string.\n\n**Prune logic:** When activate_tool would exceed budget, sort active tools by last_used_turn ascending, remove oldest until under budget. Never remove tool_search.","created_at":"2026-03-26T00:28:23","updated_at":"2026-03-26T00:33:57","priority":1,"tags":["core","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d278a9d097bd382b4044b970c4501","resolution":"Created ToolBudgetManager.gd and test_tool_budget_manager.gd with 13 tests covering LRU eviction, budget enforcement, protected tool, and reset."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d278aefd97a279de932676a61a196","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implement minerva_tool_search MCP tool","description":"Register minerva_tool_search in MinervaMCPServer.\n\n**Parameters:**\n- query: String — keyword search or exact tool name\n- category: String (optional) — filter by tool_set\n- limit: int (default 5)\n\n**Search logic (keyword index):**\n- Build inverted index at registration time: tokenize each tool's name + description into keywords → Dictionary of keyword → [tool_names]\n- On search: tokenize query, find tools matching most keywords, rank by match count\n- Exact name match always returns that tool\n\n**Returns:**\n- Array of {name, description, input_schema} for matching tools\n- Each returned tool is AUTO-ACTIVATED via ToolBudgetManager.activate_tool()\n\n**Registration:** Always in tools array (never pruned). Schema: ~150 tokens.\n\nBlocked by: ToolBudgetManager","created_at":"2026-03-26T00:28:30","updated_at":"2026-03-26T00:28:30","priority":1,"tags":["core","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d278a9d097bd382b4044b970c4501"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d278b076b7581aaf6018398146719","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add workflow hints to tool descriptions","description":"Review all ~170 tool descriptions and add workflow hints where tools have prerequisites.\n\n**Pattern:** \"Description. Call X first to get Y.\"\n\n**Known tools needing hints:**\n- PCB tools: need editor_name from list_editors, component positions from describe_component\n- Spreadsheet tools: need editor_name, column/row data from get_spreadsheet_data\n- Chart tools: need editor_name + data range\n- send_message: needs chat_id from list_chats\n- terminal_write: use \\r for Enter, prefer minerva_bash for normal commands\n- file_edit: read file first to find exact string\n\n**Estimated effort:** ~30 tools need hints. Most are fine as-is.\n\nCan be done by Sonnet agent — mechanical review with clear pattern.","created_at":"2026-03-26T00:28:36","updated_at":"2026-03-26T00:35:35","priority":1,"tags":["descriptions","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d278a9d097bd382b4044b970c4501"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d278b223d71bebe28468a5589a0a8","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Integrate ToolBudgetManager into MCP request pipeline","description":"Wire ToolBudgetManager into the existing tool-sending pipeline.\n\n**Changes to MCPManager.get_tools_for_chat():**\n- When automatic tool management is active, use ToolBudgetManager.get_active_schemas() instead of iterating all tools\n- When a tool is called, call ToolBudgetManager.mark_used(name)\n- On each LLM turn, call advance_turn()\n\n**Changes to MinervaMCPHttpServer:**\n- tools/list response uses ToolBudgetManager active schemas\n- Before tool execution: check try_call(name), return \"not loaded\" error if pruned\n\n**Changes to chat compaction handler:**\n- Call ToolBudgetManager.reset()\n\n**Backward compatible:** When automatic management is OFF (preference toggle), use existing full-schema pipeline. Default: ON.\n\nBlocked by: ToolBudgetManager + tool_search","created_at":"2026-03-26T00:28:42","updated_at":"2026-03-26T00:28:42","priority":2,"tags":["integration","phase-2"],"parent":"minerva:019d278a9d097bd382b4044b970c4501"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d278b35c57289a8412f6b8387b611","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Generate starter tool list for system prompt","description":"Create the short system prompt insert listing common tool names + category hints.\n\n**Content (~400 tokens):**\n- ~20 most common tool names grouped by category (no schemas)\n- Category hints for less common tools: \"More tools: spreadsheet, PCB, graphics, video, agents...\"\n- Instruction: \"Call minerva_tool_search(query) to discover and activate any tool.\"\n\n**Where it goes:** Injected into the system prompt by ChatPane when automatic tool management is active. Similar to how notes and skills inject prompt fragments.\n\n**Must be dynamic:** If the user has a PCB editor open, include PCB tool names in the starter list. Context-aware starter.","created_at":"2026-03-26T00:28:47","updated_at":"2026-03-26T00:28:47","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","prompt"],"parent":"minerva:019d278a9d097bd382b4044b970c4501"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d278b58467553b619120a372b6c6c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Write tests: tool discovery + LRU + budget scenarios","description":"Comprehensive test suite — all automated via `godot --headless`, zero API calls.\n\n**1. ToolBudgetManager unit tests (headless):**\n- activate_tool adds to active set\n- activate_tool over budget triggers LRU prune\n- tool_search schema never pruned\n- mark_used updates last_used_turn\n- Oldest unused tool pruned first (not most recently used)\n- reset() clears all except tool_search\n- try_call on active tool returns schema\n- try_call on pruned tool returns error message\n- Token budget respected after N activations\n- Token estimation is accurate (verify against known schema sizes)\n\n**2. Keyword search tests (headless):**\n- Exact name \"minerva_file_edit\" → returns that tool\n- Keywords \"edit file\" → file_edit ranked first\n- Keywords \"pcb annotation\" → pcb_add_annotation found\n- Category filter \"terminal\" → only terminal tools\n- No match → empty result\n- Limit=1 → only top result\n\n**3. Simulated multi-turn session (headless, no LLM):**\n\nSimulate a 20-turn coding session programmatically:\n```\nTurn 1:  search(\"file_read\") → activate\nTurn 2:  use file_read, search(\"file_edit\") → activate  \nTurn 3:  use file_edit\nTurn 5:  search(\"bash\") → activate\nTurn 8:  search(\"pcb_add_annotation\") → activate\nTurn 10: search(\"pcb_describe_component\") → activate\nTurn 12: search(\"spreadsheet\") → activate 5 tools\nTurn 15: budget exceeded → assert LRU prunes file_read (unused since turn 2)\nTurn 18: try_call(\"file_read\") → \"not loaded\" → search to reload\nTurn 20: compaction → assert only tool_search remains\n```\n\n**Assertions at each turn:**\n- `manager.get_token_usage() <= budget` (budget never exceeded)\n- `manager.get_active_schemas().size()` (correct tool count)\n\n**4. Token savings measurement (headless):**\n\nCalculate and report:\n```\nvar old_total: int = sum of ALL tool schema token costs (~25,000)\nvar turn_costs: Array[int] = [] # token cost per turn\n\nfor each simulated turn:\n    turn_costs.append(manager.get_token_usage())\n\nvar avg_new_cost: float = average(turn_costs)\nvar savings_pct: float = (old_total - avg_new_cost) / old_total * 100\nvar total_savings: int = old_total - int(avg_new_cost)\n\n# Assert\nassert(savings_pct > 90.0, \"Expected >90%% savings, got %f%%\" % savings_pct)\nassert(avg_new_cost < 3000, \"Expected avg <3000 tokens, got %d\" % avg_new_cost)\n```\n\nOutput: print a report showing per-turn cost and cumulative savings.\n\n**5. Turn penalty tracking (headless):**\n\nCount discovery overhead in simulated session:\n```\nvar discovery_calls: int = 0   # tool_search invocations\nvar wasted_calls: int = 0      # pruned tool recovery attempts\nvar total_turns: int = 20\n\n# Assert\nassert(discovery_calls <= 8, \"Too many searches: %d\" % discovery_calls)\nassert(wasted_calls <= 2, \"Too many pruned recoveries: %d\" % wasted_calls)\nvar overhead_pct: float = float(discovery_calls) / total_turns * 100\n# Report: \"Discovery overhead: X% of turns required a search\"\n```\n\n**6. Scenario-specific simulations (headless):**\n\na) **\"Edit a file\" scenario:**\n   - search(\"file_edit\") → 1 discovery call\n   - Assert: file_edit in active schemas, budget < 600\n\nb) **\"Annotate PCB from U1 to C3\" scenario:**\n   - search(\"pcb annotation\") → finds pcb_add_annotation with workflow hint\n   - Assert: description contains \"describe_component\"\n   - search(\"pcb describe\") → activate\n   - search(\"list editors\") → activate\n   - Assert: 3 PCB tools active, budget < 1500, discovery_calls == 3\n\nc) **\"Create spreadsheet + format + chart\" scenario:**\n   - search(\"spreadsheet\") → activate create + update + add_row\n   - search(\"format\") → activate format_cells\n   - search(\"chart\") → activate create_chart\n   - Assert: 5 tools active, budget < 2500\n\nd) **\"Budget pressure\" scenario:**\n   - Activate 15 different tools sequentially\n   - Assert: budget never exceeds cap\n   - Assert: most recently used tools survive\n   - Assert: oldest idle tools pruned\n\n**7. NOT automated (requires real LLM — manual test only):**\n- Haiku end-to-end: does Haiku actually find and correctly use pcb_add_annotation?\n- Sonnet multi-tool: does Sonnet do a spreadsheet + chart workflow?\n- Token measurement against real API response usage fields\n\n**Test output format:**\n```\n=== Tool Token Management Tests ===\n\n--- Unit Tests ---\n  PASS: activate adds tool\n  PASS: budget enforced\n  ...\n\n--- Simulated Session ---\n  Turn 1:  550 tokens (file_read activated)\n  Turn 5:  1200 tokens (bash activated)\n  Turn 12: 2800 tokens (spreadsheet activated, 8 tools)\n  Turn 15: 1500 tokens (LRU pruned 3 idle tools)\n  Turn 20: 150 tokens (compaction reset)\n\n--- Token Savings ---\n  Old approach: 25,000 tokens/request\n  New average:  1,450 tokens/request\n  Savings:      94.2%\n\n--- Turn Penalties ---\n  Discovery calls: 7 / 20 turns (35%)\n  Pruned recoveries: 1 / 20 turns (5%)\n\n=== Results: XX passed, 0 failed ===\n```","created_at":"2026-03-26T00:28:56","updated_at":"2026-03-26T00:31:23","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d278a9d097bd382b4044b970c4501"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d278b6934709aa1b8c180d9b2677d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Preference toggle: automatic vs manual tool management","description":"Add preference in settings to toggle between automatic (new) and manual (existing) tool management.\n\n**Default: automatic (new system).**\n\nWhen OFF: existing behavior — all tool schemas sent, tool_sets/profiles filter as before.\nWhen ON: ToolBudgetManager controls the tools array, minerva_tool_search available.\n\nSingle checkbox in preferences. Persisted to config. No other UI changes needed — the existing tool set/profile UI still works for manual mode.","created_at":"2026-03-26T00:29:01","updated_at":"2026-03-26T00:29:01","priority":3,"tags":["phase-3","preferences"],"parent":"minerva:019d278a9d097bd382b4044b970c4501"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d292bf3977bdc92522cf5857eecda","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Tool calling reliability: idempotency, loop detection, workflow planning","description":"Improve tool call reliability for LLM agents. Observed failure: agent called create_note_tab 10 times with identical args instead of advancing to next step. The LLM's self-diagnosis: orchestration failure, not tool failure. But Minerva can help prevent this.\n\n**Root causes (from LLM postmortem):**\n- Missing state transition after successful tool call\n- No duplicate-action guard\n- No idempotency check (created 10 tabs instead of reusing 1)\n- Over-eager intermediate narration without advancing\n\n**Four improvements:**\n\n1. **Idempotency in tool responses.** Tools that create resources (create_note_tab, create_chat, create_spreadsheet_editor, etc.) should detect duplicates and return `already_existed: true` instead of silently creating duplicates. The LLM sees \"already existed\" and knows to move on.\n\n2. **Duplicate call detection.** Track last N tool calls per session. If same tool called with identical arguments 2+ times in a row, return a warning: `\"warning\": \"This exact call was already made. Consider advancing to the next step.\"` Doesn't block — just warns.\n\n3. **Workflow hints for multi-step operations.** Add workflow hints to tool descriptions for common multi-step flows:\n   - \"Create spreadsheet → populate → create chart → link to note\"\n   - \"Create note tab → create note → update note\"\n   - Similar to the PCB workflow hints we already added\n\n4. **minerva_plan_workflow tool.** Given a goal description, returns a step-by-step plan with specific tool names and order. Pre-built recipes for common workflows + LLM-generated plans for novel goals. This is the most ambitious item — could be a separate DCR.\n\n**Impact:** Prevents agent loops, reduces wasted tool calls, improves Haiku reliability significantly. Items 1-2 are quick defensive wins. Item 3 is a content pass. Item 4 is a larger feature.","created_at":"2026-03-26T08:03:59","updated_at":"2026-03-26T15:00:51","priority":2,"tags":["agent","haiku","mcp","reliability","tool-calling"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d292c265f7d55b5832aef517ab727","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add idempotency to resource-creating tools","description":"Tools that create resources should check for existing duplicates before creating.\n\n**Tools to update:**\n- minerva_create_note_tab: check if tab with same name exists → return it\n- minerva_create_chat: check if chat with same name exists → return it\n- minerva_create_text_editor: check if editor with same name/file exists → return it\n- minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor: same\n- minerva_create_graphics_editor: same\n- minerva_create_pcb_editor: same\n- minerva_create_video_editor: same\n- minerva_terminal_create: check if terminal with same name exists → return it\n\n**Return format when duplicate found:**\n```json\n{\"success\": true, \"already_existed\": true, \"id\": \"existing_id\", \"name\": \"Corvette Test\"}\n```\n\n**Behavior:** Don't block creation — just detect and return the existing one. The LLM sees `already_existed: true` and knows to move on instead of creating another.\n\n**Test:** Call create_note_tab(\"Test\") twice → second call returns already_existed: true.","created_at":"2026-03-26T08:04:12","updated_at":"2026-03-26T15:03:16","priority":1,"tags":["phase-1","quick-win"],"parent":"minerva:019d292bf3977bdc92522cf5857eecda"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d292c427f7fb193798b7accb4a7cc","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add duplicate call detection with warning","description":"Track recent tool calls and warn on exact duplicates.\n\n**Implementation in _execute_tool_impl:**\n- Maintain a ring buffer of last 5 calls: Array of {tool_name, arguments_hash}\n- Before executing, check if this exact call (name + args hash) matches the previous call\n- If duplicate: still execute, but add `\"warning\": \"Identical call repeated. Consider advancing to the next step in your plan.\"` to the result\n- If 3+ consecutive identical calls: add stronger warning `\"warning\": \"This tool has been called 3+ times with identical arguments. You are likely stuck in a loop. Stop and reassess your plan.\"`\n\n**Hash method:** JSON.stringify(arguments).sha256_text() — fast, deterministic\n\n**Does NOT block execution** — just adds warning field. The LLM sees it and self-corrects.\n\n**Test:** Call create_note_tab(\"X\") 3 times → third call has loop warning.","created_at":"2026-03-26T08:04:19","updated_at":"2026-03-26T15:01:54","priority":1,"tags":["phase-1","quick-win"],"parent":"minerva:019d292bf3977bdc92522cf5857eecda"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d292c5f457b159c0a74d9295451e7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add workflow hints for common multi-step operations","description":"Add multi-step workflow hints to tool descriptions for common flows.\n\n**Workflows to document in descriptions:**\n\nSpreadsheet workflow:\n- create_spreadsheet_editor: \"After creating, use minerva_update_spreadsheet_data to add data, minerva_format_cells for styling, minerva_create_chart for visualization, and minerva_link_spreadsheet_to_note to save as a note.\"\n\nNote workflow:\n- create_note_tab: \"After creating a tab, use minerva_create_note to add notes to it.\"\n- create_note: \"After creating, the note is immediately available. Use minerva_update_note to modify content.\"\n\nChat workflow:\n- create_chat: \"After creating, use minerva_set_system_prompt to configure, then minerva_send_message to start conversation.\"\n\nEditor workflow:\n- create_text_editor: \"After creating, use minerva_update_editor to set content, minerva_save_editor to write to disk.\"\n\n**Pattern:** Each \"create\" tool's description should end with \"Next steps: ...\" listing the typical follow-up tools.\n\nCan be done by Sonnet agent — mechanical description updates.","created_at":"2026-03-26T08:04:27","updated_at":"2026-03-26T15:03:24","priority":2,"tags":["descriptions","phase-2"],"parent":"minerva:019d292bf3977bdc92522cf5857eecda"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d292c803778078fe1f0fb24bee780","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Design minerva_plan_workflow tool","description":"Design a tool that returns a step-by-step execution plan for a given goal.\n\n**Concept:**\n```\nminerva_plan_workflow(goal=\"create a spreadsheet about corvettes and save as a note\")\n→ {\n  \"steps\": [\n    {\"step\": 1, \"tool\": \"minerva_create_note_tab\", \"args\": {\"name\": \"Corvettes\"}, \"purpose\": \"Create a tab for the note\"},\n    {\"step\": 2, \"tool\": \"minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor\", \"args\": {\"name\": \"Corvette Data\"}, \"purpose\": \"Create spreadsheet\"},\n    {\"step\": 3, \"tool\": \"minerva_update_spreadsheet_data\", \"args\": {}, \"purpose\": \"Populate with corvette data\"},\n    {\"step\": 4, \"tool\": \"minerva_format_cells\", \"args\": {}, \"purpose\": \"Style the spreadsheet\"},\n    {\"step\": 5, \"tool\": \"minerva_link_spreadsheet_to_note\", \"args\": {}, \"purpose\": \"Link to the note tab\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Implementation options:**\nA. Pre-built recipes for common workflows (dictionary of goal keywords → plans)\nB. LLM-generated plans (call a small model to generate the plan — recursive tool use)\nC. Graph-based: tools have declared inputs/outputs, planner finds a path from goal to result\n\n**This is a research/design task.** Output: a design document, not code. Implementation is a follow-up DCR.\n\n**Questions to answer:**\n- How many workflows can we pre-build vs need dynamic planning?\n- Does the plan need to be editable by the user before execution?\n- Should the agent be required to follow the plan, or is it advisory?","created_at":"2026-03-26T08:04:35","updated_at":"2026-03-26T08:04:35","priority":3,"tags":["design","phase-3"],"parent":"minerva:019d292bf3977bdc92522cf5857eecda"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d292c937c7f2fa231bcb175bcef7b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Write tests for idempotency and loop detection","description":"Tests for items 1 and 2.\n\n**Idempotency tests:**\n- create_note_tab(\"X\") twice → second returns already_existed: true\n- create_chat(\"Y\") twice → second returns already_existed: true\n- Different names → both create successfully (no false positive)\n\n**Loop detection tests:**\n- Same call 1x → no warning\n- Same call 2x → warning on second\n- Same call 3x → stronger warning on third\n- Different calls → no warning (not a loop)\n- Same tool, different args → no warning (different operation)\n\nHeadless testable with mock tool calls against _execute_tool_impl.","created_at":"2026-03-26T08:04:40","updated_at":"2026-03-26T08:04:40","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d292bf3977bdc92522cf5857eecda"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2bc2aef478daa40618524db0cdd1","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"format_cells crashes: bold/italic passed as String instead of bool to SpreadsheetCell","description":"SpreadsheetEditor.format_cell_with_history (line 1684) crashes when bold is passed as String \"true\" instead of bool true.\n\nStack: MinervaMCPServer._format_cells → SpreadsheetEditor.format_cell_with_history\nError: Invalid assignment of property or key 'bold' with value of type 'String' on a base object of type 'RefCounted (SpreadsheetCell)'\n\nRoot cause: MCP JSON deserializer may return booleans as strings. The _format_cells handler needs to coerce bool parameters before passing to SpreadsheetCell.\n\nFix: In _format_cells, add type coercion:\n```gdscript\nif arguments.has(\"bold\"):\n    cell.bold = bool(arguments[\"bold\"]) # or: arguments[\"bold\"] == true or arguments[\"bold\"] == \"true\"\n```","created_at":"2026-03-26T20:07:52","updated_at":"2026-03-26T20:07:52","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["mcp","spreadsheet","type-coercion"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2c56cdfd7b10853d20ee7864e3cc","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"ToolSearchIndex: keyword and wildcard+category queries return 0 results after reconnect","description":"Two search patterns that should work return empty results:\n\n1. **Keyword search**: `query: \"spreadsheet\"` → 0 results. Should match tools like `minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor`, `minerva_format_cells`, `minerva_update_spreadsheet_data`, etc.\n\n2. **Wildcard + category**: `query: \"*\", category: \"spreadsheet\"` → 0 results. Should return all tools in the spreadsheet category.\n\nObserved after MCP reconnect. Possible causes:\n- Search index not rebuilt on reconnect\n- Keywords not extracted from tool names/descriptions at registration time (e.g. \"spreadsheet\" not tokenized from `minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor`)\n- Category filter not matching registered categories","created_at":"2026-03-26T22:49:39","updated_at":"2026-03-26T22:49:39","priority":2,"tags":["bug-adjacent","mcp","tool-search"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2c60c721721f95d4ff40c54922a1","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Spreadsheet bold/italic formatting has no visible effect — UI and MCP","description":"Bold and italic properties are set on SpreadsheetCell but have no visible effect in the rendered spreadsheet. Affects both human UI (toolbar buttons) and MCP (minerva_format_cells). The cell.bold/cell.italic values are stored but never applied to the rendered text.","created_at":"2026-03-26T23:00:33","updated_at":"2026-03-26T23:00:33","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["formatting","spreadsheet","ui"],"repro_steps":"1. Create a spreadsheet with data\n2. Select cells, click Bold toolbar button (or call minerva_format_cells with bold:true)\n3. Observe: no visual change in the cell text"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2c8ed6557d62972757c29e9f8476","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"WebViewEditor: LLM-generated HTML panels in Minerva editor pane via Godot WRY","description":"## Problem\n\nMinerva's vision extends beyond IDE — it should support any informational workflow: presentations, data analysis, movie-making, sales outreach, stock analysis, etc. Non-developer users need polished, custom UIs that both they and the LLM can drive. Current editor types (spreadsheet, text, graphics) aren't visually rich enough for presentation-quality output.\n\n## Architecture Decision\n\n**Webview (Godot WRY) for all LLM-generated panels.** Dynamic GDScript was rejected after crash testing — infinite loops nearly crashed the entire PC, invalid API calls caused severe impact, no sandboxing possible. Webview provides full fault isolation (WebKitGTK runs in separate process), and LLMs excel at HTML/CSS/JS generation.\n\nExperiment validated at ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/rich-panel (Godot 4.6.1).\n\n## Solution: WebViewEditor Type\n\nA new `Editor.Type.WEBVIEW` that hosts a Godot WRY WebView control in the editor pane. The LLM generates HTML/CSS/JS content, Minerva renders it in the tab. Standard editor controls work: save to note, save to file (.html), save all.\n\n### Key Components\n\n**1. Godot WRY GDExtension integration**\n- Build godot_wry from source (v1.0.2 has GTK panic on Linux, need PR #79 fix)\n- Linux: libgtk-3-dev + libwebkit2gtk-4.1-dev + cargo build\n- Register in .godot/extension_list.cfg\n- Gotchas: set url=\"\" before html, full_window_size=false\n\n**2. WebViewEditor.gd**\n- New script at src/Scripts/UI/Controls/WebViewEditor/WebViewEditor.gd\n- Extends Control, builds UI in _ready() (PCBEditor pattern)\n- Hosts WRY WebView node sized to fill the panel\n- Stores HTML source string for save/reload/note export\n- JS↔GDScript bridge for data access (read spreadsheets, notes, docket)\n- content_changed signal for unsaved indicator\n\n**3. Editor.gd integration**\n- Add WEBVIEW to Editor.Type enum\n- Add webview_editor var\n- Add match branch in Editor.create()\n- Hide irrelevant buttons (autowrap, syntax, find) in _ready()\n- get_saved_state() support\n- Save to file as .html\n- Save to note as rendered HTML or screenshot\n\n**4. MCP tools**\n- minerva_create_webview_panel(name, html) — create tab with HTML content\n- minerva_update_webview_panel(editor_name, html) — replace HTML content\n- minerva_get_webview_source(editor_name) — read current HTML source\n\n**5. JS↔Minerva bridge**\n- JS library injected into every panel: window.minerva.call(tool, args)\n- Routes through MCP HTTP server on localhost\n- Panels can read spreadsheet data, trigger workflows, update docket items\n- Buttons in the HTML can invoke MCP tools\n\n### Guiding Scenario: Web Dev Sales Guy\n\n1. Research law firms → co-browser + spreadsheet (existing)\n2. Competitive analysis → spreadsheet + charts (existing)\n3. Custom dashboard → LLM generates HTML with Chart.js/D3, opens in WebViewEditor tab. Polished, branded, animated.\n4. Video pitch → video recorder captures the dashboard walkthrough\n5. Outreach → email tool sends video + personal note\n6. Pipeline → docket tracks prospects\n\nThe sales guy never sees code. The LLM generates, iterates, and fixes the HTML invisibly.","created_at":"2026-03-26T23:50:51","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:11:05","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","editor-type","extensibility","sales-scenario","webview","wry"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"rich-panel: Experimental test bed for LLM-generated runtime panels","description":"Experimental Godot app to empirically test approaches for LLM-generated UI panels in Minerva. Tests GDScript hot-loading vs webview rendering, measures crash behavior, and prototypes progressive disclosure UI for LLM work visibility.\n\nMotivated by research on DCR 019d2c8e (RuntimePanel). Located at ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/rich-panel.\n\nFour phases: (1) GDScript hot-load test bed, (2) Webview test bed, (3) MCP generation loop, (4) Progressive disclosure prototype.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:25:59","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:28:27","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","experiment","rich-panel","runtime-panel"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf16187a33b58e73bbec2e4f7f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1: Scaffold Godot project, main scene, CLAUDE.md","description":"Create project.godot (4.6.1, gl_compatibility), main.tscn with split layout (CodeEdit left, PanelContainer right, build log bottom), CLAUDE.md with architecture and run commands. Follow ccsandbox experiment conventions.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:05","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:32:11","priority":1,"tags":["phase-1","rich-panel","scaffold"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf2406773bb54ca60fd411b971","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1: Implement GDScript hot-loader (GDScript.new → reload → instantiate)","description":"Core loader: takes GDScript source string, creates GDScript resource, sets source_code, calls reload(), checks for errors, instantiates into panel host container. Must handle reload() error return gracefully. Log all steps as structured events for build log consumption.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:08","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:32:13","priority":1,"tags":["gdscript","hot-load","phase-1","rich-panel"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf30a87cc4a0fa8f069de37b32","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1: Build control sidebar UI (CodeEdit, Load button, status)","description":"Left sidebar: CodeEdit with GDScript syntax highlighting, \"Load Panel\" button, status label showing last load result (success/error). Dropdown to select from sample panels. Later: approach picker (GDScript vs webview).","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:12","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:34:24","priority":2,"tags":["phase-1","rich-panel","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf3cc4753c9688714319f526ee","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1: Create sample GDScript panels (dashboard, cards, table)","description":"3-4 sample GDScript panels that extend Control and demonstrate different UI patterns: (1) metric dashboard with StyleBoxFlat cards, (2) bar chart using _draw(), (3) sortable data table, (4) animated counter. These serve as baseline quality reference and LLM generation targets.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:15","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:32:13","priority":2,"tags":["phase-1","rich-panel","samples"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf4ca67394b2e33ed130da6334","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1: Create crash scenario test scripts and document results","description":"Create intentional crash scripts: (1) null reference access, (2) infinite loop in _process(), (3) stack overflow via recursion, (4) invalid API call, (5) resource exhaustion (spawn 10k nodes). Load each via the hot-loader, document: does Godot crash? Freeze? Recover? What error info survives? This is the key data point for the architecture decision.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:19","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:34:24","priority":1,"tags":["crash-test","critical","phase-1","rich-panel"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf5e5c71198182bd4f238a2ca7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P2: Evaluate Godot WRY plugin for 4.6.1 compatibility","description":"Download and test Godot WRY (doceazedo/godot_wry) with Godot 4.6.1. Does it compile/load? Can it render a basic HTML page? Test on Linux first. If WRY fails, evaluate GDCef and godotwebview.com as fallbacks. Document: binary size impact, API surface, stability, input forwarding quality.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:23","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:11:09","priority":1,"tags":["evaluation","phase-2","rich-panel","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf6a997d80a9f90e29a752679c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P2: Integrate webview loader + create HTML sample panels","description":"Create webview_loader.gd that loads HTML/CSS/JS into a webview panel. Create HTML versions of the same sample panels from Phase 1 for direct comparison. Implement JS↔GDScript bridge for data passing (postMessage or equivalent).","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:26","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:11:10","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","rich-panel","samples","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf78a976dfb1ede39eb776ff44","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P2: Run crash isolation tests with webview and compare to GDScript","description":"Same crash scenarios as Phase 1 but in webview: infinite JS loop, memory exhaustion, thrown exceptions, invalid DOM ops. Key question: does the webview crash kill Minerva or just the panel? Produce a comparison table: GDScript vs webview crash behavior for each scenario.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:30","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:11:10","priority":1,"tags":["crash-test","critical","phase-2","rich-panel","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf83567733a0c514e2445f9248","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3: Python MCP server with create_panel / update_panel tools","description":"FastMCP server (stdio transport). Tools: create_panel(title, source, language), update_panel(id, source), get_panel_status(id). Each tool call includes step_description field for build log. Server pushes panel source to Godot via HTTP endpoint.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:33","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:32:13","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","phase-3","rich-panel"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf8f7278f5814ac4815569a982","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3: Godot-side MCP HTTP handler for panel operations","description":"HTTP server in Godot (port 3030) accepting panel create/update requests from the Python MCP server. Routes to the appropriate loader (GDScript or webview). Returns structured status (success, error with details, panel screenshot if possible).","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:36","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:34:26","priority":2,"tags":["godot","mcp","phase-3","rich-panel"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2caf9da37e1fb48d49ed2ee83127","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"P3: End-to-end LLM generation test — describe → generate → render → iterate","description":"Full loop test: user describes a dashboard in natural language → Claude generates panel code via MCP → Godot renders it → user gives feedback → Claude iterates. Test with both GDScript and HTML targets. Measure: iteration time, code quality, error recovery, how many rounds to a usable panel.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:39","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:56","priority":1,"tags":["critical","e2e","phase-3","rich-panel"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2cafacdf7efd9facb50b5787c90f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P4: Build log UI — activity indicator + structured event log","description":"Level 1: status bar with progress (building... N/M components). Level 2: collapsible build log showing human-readable narrative from structured step_description events. Timestamp, description, warnings, completion. Both GDScript (RichTextLabel) and webview (HTML) implementations for comparison.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:43","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:34:26","priority":2,"tags":["phase-4","progressive-disclosure","rich-panel","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2cafb7f17a94b89bc9766a65b891","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"P4: Source + diff view — code inspection with iteration history","description":"Level 3: syntax-highlighted source view with diffs between iterations. Build log entries link to code ranges. For webview approach: embed CodeMirror/Monaco. For GDScript approach: CodeEdit with custom highlighting. Show what changed and why on each iteration.","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:46","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:26:46","priority":3,"tags":["phase-4","progressive-disclosure","rich-panel","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2cb8513479abb13c189bcd376381","type":"question","status":"asked","title":"Do LLM-generated panels need direct access to Minerva core services, or is LLM-as-broker sufficient?","description":"## Question\n\nWhen an LLM-generated panel needs data from Minerva core services (media-gen, model-chat, spreadsheets, etc.), what's the right integration model?\n\nThree candidates:\n1. **Direct service access** — panel code calls Minerva services directly (GDScript: trivial via scene tree; webview: needs JS↔GDScript bridge or local HTTP API)\n2. **LLM-as-broker** — the LLM fetches data from services via MCP tools and \"bakes\" it into the panel source before loading. Panel is static/self-contained. LLM re-generates when data changes.\n3. **Hybrid** — LLM bakes initial data, panel has thin bridge for live updates (refresh button calls back through Minerva)\n\n## Why this matters\n\nOption 2 (LLM-as-broker) is simpler and works identically for both GDScript and webview panels — no bridge needed. But it means panels are snapshots, not live dashboards. Option 1 enables reactive UIs but requires the bridge infrastructure that differs per approach.\n\n## Initial thinking (from discussion with user)\n\nUser notes the LLM already has MCP access to core services and can bake data in or act as a broker. Direct panel→service access may not be needed for v1. Worth validating experimentally: mock a core service, test both patterns, see which the LLM generates more naturally and which produces better UX.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- Mock a simple service (e.g., fake media_gen returning placeholder images after delay)\n- Have LLM generate a panel using baked data (option 2)\n- Have LLM generate a panel with direct service call (option 1, GDScript path)\n- Compare: code complexity, user experience, iteration speed","created_at":"2026-03-27T00:36:10","updated_at":"2026-03-27T00:36:10","priority":3,"tags":["architecture","phase-3.5","research","rich-panel","services"],"parent":"minerva:019d2caefe867cfc9e38055ae7494d54"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2d3502b37893aeb3cf57daf8a585","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Sightline delivery architecture for Minerva: built-in Explore suite core plus modular capabilities","description":"Define how the long-term Explore tooling suite (currently being prototyped as Sightline) should be delivered as part of Minerva.\n\nProblem statement:\n- Minerva is a Godot app and the Explore tooling needs to ship as part of the product without requiring a separate install.\n- The suite should remain extensible over time, but the default delivery path should be reliable and cross-platform.\n- The tooling should support a plugin/capability model without making runtime compilation or dynamic native loading the only story.\n\nProposed architectural direction:\n- Ship a built-in Sightline core as a native component for Minerva, likely a Rust core with a GDExtension wrapper.\n- Treat the initial capability set as built-in modular capabilities registered with the core, even if statically linked.\n- Store runtime state, caches, indexes, artifacts, and optional downloaded capability assets under `user://sightline/`.\n- Treat on-device compilation (for advanced plugin development or optional capability builds) as an advanced or developer-mode path, not as the primary delivery mechanism.\n\nWhy this direction:\n- No extra install for end users.\n- Preserves a plugin/capability model logically without taking on the full complexity of dynamic native plugin loading on day one.\n- Gives Minerva a stable internal API for Explore/Inspect/Validate-style capability growth.\n- Keeps future optional capability bundles possible.\n\nDecision areas this DCR should cover:\n- core Rust/GDExtension boundary\n- internal capability registry model\n- built-in vs optional capability packaging\n- `user://` layout for caches, manifests, artifacts, and optional builds\n- update/install story for optional capabilities\n- long-term relationship between agent-facing capability discovery and the internal plugin registry\n\nDesired outcome:\nA delivery model that makes Sightline a first-class Minerva subsystem rather than an external tool, while still allowing incremental capability growth over time.","created_at":"2026-03-27T02:52:22","updated_at":"2026-03-27T02:52:22","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","explore","gdextension","godot","minerva","rust","tooling"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2d35039c77c79e7308de2be61a33","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Define built-in Sightline delivery model for Minerva (GDExtension core, modular capabilities, user:// state)","description":"Capture the recommended delivery architecture for shipping Sightline as part of Minerva.\n\nRecommendation:\n- Build Sightline as a Rust core library with a GDExtension wrapper for Godot.\n- Ship the baseline Explore capability set inside Minerva as built-in modular capabilities rather than as a separately installed tool.\n- Use a runtime capability registry inside the core so built-in modules and future optional modules look the same to the rest of Minerva.\n- Store caches, indexes, session state, artifact handles, manifests, and related runtime data under `user://sightline/`.\n- If Minerva later supports user-extensible or downloadable capability modules, treat those as optional bundles. Keep on-device Rust compilation as a developer-mode or advanced path, not the baseline product path.\n\nWhy:\n- avoids extra install requirements\n- preserves a clean extensibility model\n- avoids making dynamic native plugin loading or runtime compilation mandatory for every user\n- keeps Minerva in control of cross-platform delivery and version compatibility\n\nSuggested subtopics:\n- internal capability registry API\n- built-in capability packaging strategy\n- optional bundle manifest format\n- `user://sightline/` directory layout\n- how agents discover available capabilities through the registry\n- when and whether downloaded bundles are compiled, loaded dynamically, or shipped as prebuilt artifacts","created_at":"2026-03-27T02:52:22","updated_at":"2026-03-27T02:57:20","priority":1,"tags":["delivery","explore","gdextension","minerva","rust","tooling"],"parent":"minerva:019d2d3502b37893aeb3cf57daf8a585"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2d79d06b7621bb8e19a8abd8c84d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Integrate Godot WRY GDExtension into Minerva build","description":"Add Godot WRY as a GDExtension dependency to Minerva.\n\n1. Clone godot_wry repo, build from source (NOT v1.0.2 release — GTK panic on Linux)\n2. Linux deps: libgtk-3-dev, libwebkit2gtk-4.1-dev, cargo\n3. Copy libgodot_wry.so to src/bin/ or addons/godot_wry/bin/\n4. Add WRY.gdextension file\n5. Create/update .godot/extension_list.cfg with gdextension path\n6. Update scripts/build-extensions.sh to include WRY build step\n7. Verify WebView node is available in Godot after build\n\nReference: ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/rich-panel for working setup.\nGotchas documented in DCR comments #81 and #82.","created_at":"2026-03-27T04:07:31","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:12:53","priority":1,"tags":["build","webview","wry"],"parent":"minerva:019d2c8ed6557d62972757c29e9f8476"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2d79efcd7a3399c14cccad1f09f2","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Create WebViewEditor.gd control","description":"New script at src/Scripts/UI/Controls/WebViewEditor/WebViewEditor.gd following the PCBEditor pattern.\n\n1. Extends Control, builds UI in _build_ui() called from _ready()\n2. Creates WRY WebView node, sizes to fill panel\n3. Critical setup: webview.url = \\\"\\\" before setting html, webview.full_window_size = false\n4. Stores html_source: String for persistence and note export\n5. set_html(source: String) method — updates content and emits content_changed\n6. get_html() method — returns current source\n7. content_changed signal for dirty/unsaved tracking\n8. Simple .tscn scene wrapper (just the root Control node)\n\nDepends on: WRY GDExtension integrated (task 1)","created_at":"2026-03-27T04:07:39","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:23:23","priority":1,"tags":["editor","ui","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2c8ed6557d62972757c29e9f8476"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2d7a15d27c1ca3f7d9bd2e0bf876","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add WEBVIEW to Editor.Type enum and Editor.create()","description":"Wire WebViewEditor into the existing editor system. Follow the established pattern (see PCBEditor, SpreadsheetEditor).\n\n1. Add WEBVIEW to Editor.Type enum\n2. Add webview_editor var on Editor\n3. Add match branch in Editor.create() — instantiate WebViewEditor, add to vbox, connect content_changed\n4. In Editor._ready() — hide irrelevant buttons for WEBVIEW type (autowrap, syntax highlight, find, reload)\n5. Enable note check button for WEBVIEW (save to note)\n6. Add WEBVIEW to EditorPane.add() tab naming\n7. Add EditorPane helper: add_webview_editor(name) -> Editor\n\nDepends on: WebViewEditor.gd created (task 2)","created_at":"2026-03-27T04:07:48","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:23:24","priority":1,"tags":["editor","integration","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2c8ed6557d62972757c29e9f8476"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2d7a45bc7e138271aba23c73aa6e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Save/load support for WebViewEditor","description":"Persistence for webview panels.\n\n1. get_saved_state() — check if html_source matches last saved version\n2. Save to file — write html_source as .html file via FileDialog (add *.html filter)\n3. Load from file — read .html and call set_html()\n4. Save to note — export as HTML text note (markdown code block) or as screenshot (viewport capture of webview)\n5. Project save/load — include webview panels in .minproj with html_source and tab name\n6. Handle the \\\"save all\\\" flow for webview tabs\n\nDepends on: Editor.Type.WEBVIEW integration (task 3)","created_at":"2026-03-27T04:08:01","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:08:01","priority":2,"tags":["editor","persistence","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2c8ed6557d62972757c29e9f8476"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2d7aae2a7341823122709eeef4f3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP tools: create, update, and read webview panels","description":"Register webview MCP tools in MinervaMCPServer.gd.\n\n1. minerva_create_webview_panel(name, html) — creates a WebViewEditor tab, sets HTML content, returns editor_name. Workflow hint: \\\"After creating, use minerva_update_webview_panel to modify content.\\\"\n2. minerva_update_webview_panel(editor_name, html) — replaces HTML content in existing tab. Supports iterative LLM development (generate → view → refine).\n3. minerva_get_webview_source(editor_name) — returns current HTML source string. Lets LLM read what's currently rendered before making changes.\n\nRegistration: _register_webview_tools() in _init() chain, tool_set = \\\"editor\\\"\nHandler: _find_webview_editor(name) pattern (same as _find_spreadsheet_editor)\nAdd to tool search index with category \\\"editor\\\" or new \\\"webview\\\" category.\n\nDepends on: Editor.Type.WEBVIEW integration (task 3)","created_at":"2026-03-27T04:08:27","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:23:24","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","tools","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2c8ed6557d62972757c29e9f8476"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2d7ad6237632a0e8251f4ec17d4b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"JS↔Minerva bridge for webview panels","description":"A small JS library auto-injected into every webview panel that lets the HTML call back into Minerva.\n\n1. window.minerva.call(toolName, args) → Promise — routes to MCP HTTP server on localhost:9315\n2. Convenience helpers: minerva.getSpreadsheet(name), minerva.updateSpreadsheet(name, updates), minerva.createNote(title, content)\n3. Event listener: minerva.on('data_changed', callback) — notified when linked data changes\n4. The bridge JS is injected by WebViewEditor.gd before the panel's HTML (prepended to source or injected via WRY's JS evaluation)\n5. Authentication: local-only, no tokens needed (same-machine localhost)\n\nThis enables interactive panels — buttons, forms, live data binding. The sales guy clicks \\\"Send Pitch\\\" in the HTML dashboard and it triggers an MCP workflow.\n\nDepends on: MCP tools (task 5), WebViewEditor (task 2)","created_at":"2026-03-27T04:08:38","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:08:38","priority":2,"tags":["bridge","javascript","mcp","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2c8ed6557d62972757c29e9f8476"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2d7b1d7d7eda81c775d85d595413","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Cross-platform WRY build support (macOS + Windows)","description":"Extend WRY integration to macOS and Windows.\n\n- macOS: WRY uses WebKit natively, should build with just cargo. Verify rendering matches Linux.\n- Windows: WRY uses WebView2 (Edge). May need WebView2 runtime bundled or detected.\n- Update scripts/build-extensions.sh and scripts/build-extensions.ps1\n- Test full_window_size, url/html precedence, JS bridge on all platforms\n- Document platform-specific gotchas\n\nLower priority — Linux-first is fine for validation. But needed before any release.\n\nDepends on: Linux WRY integration working (task 1)","created_at":"2026-03-27T04:08:56","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:08:56","priority":3,"tags":["build","cross-platform","webview","wry"],"parent":"minerva:019d2c8ed6557d62972757c29e9f8476"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2da826d2767abee88e0200714096","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"WebViewEditor has extra vertical gap between toolbar and webview content","description":"The WebViewEditor tab has approximately one row of extra vertical space between the ButtonsHBoxContainer toolbar and the start of the webview content. Other editor types that hide the toolbar entirely (LOGS, KANBAN, ACTIVITY_LOG) don't have this issue.\n\n## Attempted fixes (all failed)\n\n1. **Hide entire ButtonsHBoxContainer** — removed the gap but also removed all editor controls (save, note toggle, create note). Reverted.\n\n2. **Hide individual text-editing buttons** (mic, autowrap, syntax, find, diff, reload, export) while keeping container visible — gap persists. The hidden buttons aren't causing it.\n\n3. **Hide spacer nodes** ($VBoxContainer/Control, $VBoxContainer/FillerControl3) — gap persists even with .hide().\n\n4. **queue_free() spacer nodes + TextureRect** — freed Control, FillerControl3, and TextureRect (which has size_flags_vertical=EXPAND_FILL). Gap still present.\n\n## Likely root cause\n\nThe VBoxContainer has theme_override_constants/separation = 10 (Editor.tscn line 65). Even after removing spacer nodes, there are still hidden children (FindStringContainer) that may occupy layout space, or the separation between ButtonsHBoxContainer and the webview child itself creates the gap.\n\n## Potential fixes to try\n\n- Set VBoxContainer separation to 0 for WEBVIEW type\n- Check if FindStringContainer.hide() still occupies space (try queue_free)\n- Inspect at runtime with Godot remote debugger to see which node is taking the space\n- The webview Control itself may have margin/offset that creates the gap","created_at":"2026-03-27T04:58:07","updated_at":"2026-03-27T04:58:07","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["layout","ui","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2c8ed6557d62972757c29e9f8476","repro_steps":"1. Create a webview panel via MCP: minerva_create_webview_panel(name, html)\n2. Observe the gap between the top toolbar row and the webview content\n3. Compare with a text editor tab — the text editor content starts immediately below the toolbar"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2db67d1678a6bbf5fa21934311a5","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"WebView note type: render HTML/charts visually in notes pane","description":"## Problem\n\nWhen saving a webview panel to a note, the raw HTML source is stored and displayed. Users expect to see the rendered chart/dashboard in the note itself. The current text-note approach forces users to look at HTML code instead of the visual output they created.\n\n## Solution: HTML Note Type\n\nAdd a new note type that renders HTML content using a WRY WebView control in the notes pane. This enables:\n- Charts and dashboards visible directly in notes\n- Markdown preview notes\n- Mixed-media formatted content\n- Edit button opens the HTML in a WebViewEditor tab\n\n## Architecture\n\n### WebView in Notes\n- Add HTML to Note types (alongside text, image, audio, video)\n- HTML notes render using a WRY WebView control instead of RichTextLabel\n- The WebView is sized to the note's area, displays the rendered HTML\n- Edit button opens the HTML source in a WebViewEditor tab in the editor pane\n- Changes in the WebViewEditor sync back to the note\n\n### Performance: Lazy Loading\n- WRY WebViews are OS-native (WebKitGTK) — lightweight but not free\n- Only create WebView instances for notes currently visible on screen\n- Destroy/recycle WebViews when notes scroll off-screen\n- Show a static placeholder (thumbnail or \\\"HTML Note\\\" label) when not visible\n- This keeps memory bounded regardless of how many HTML notes exist\n\n### MCP Integration\n- minerva_create_note with type=html creates an HTML note\n- Save from WebViewEditor creates/updates the linked HTML note\n- get_note returns the HTML source for HTML notes\n\n### Future Possibilities\n- Markdown notes rendered via HTML (markdown→HTML conversion)\n- Mixed-media notes: text + embedded charts + images all in one HTML document\n- Interactive notes: JS bridge enables buttons/forms within notes\n- Note templates: pre-built HTML layouts for common patterns (dashboard, report, presentation slide)","created_at":"2026-03-27T05:13:47","updated_at":"2026-03-27T05:23:09","priority":1,"tags":["html","notes","ux","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2c8ed6557d62972757c29e9f8476"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2db6b5777cc7a430d0f4f8d7a8a0","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add HTML note type to Note system","description":"Add HTML as a note type in the Note data model.\n\n1. Add HTML to Note type enum/constants (alongside text, image, audio, video)\n2. Note.create_html_note(title, html_source) factory method\n3. HTML notes store the source string (same as text notes store text)\n4. Serialization: save/load HTML notes in project files and note threads\n5. Note.Proxy support for HTML notes (for injection into chat if needed)\n\nThis is the data model change only — no rendering yet.","created_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:01","updated_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:01","priority":1,"tags":["core","notes","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2db67d1678a6bbf5fa21934311a5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2db6c9e8786290c6a22a31d4092f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Render HTML notes with WebView in notes pane","description":"When an HTML note is displayed in the notes pane, render it using a WRY WebView control instead of RichTextLabel.\n\n1. In Note.gd (or wherever note UI is built), detect HTML note type\n2. Create a WebView control (via ClassDB.instantiate) sized to the note area\n3. Set url=\\\"\\\", full_window_size=false, html=source (same pattern as WebViewEditor)\n4. Inject the Minerva JS bridge for interactivity\n5. Fallback: if WebView addon not installed, show HTML as code block in RichTextLabel\n6. Note height: either fixed height (e.g., 300px) or auto-size based on content\n\nDepends on: HTML note type in data model (task 1)","created_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:07","updated_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:07","priority":1,"tags":["notes","rendering","ui","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2db67d1678a6bbf5fa21934311a5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2db6dcca7afa989f377c8500f867","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Hard cap: max 3 concurrent WebViews in notes pane (LRU eviction)","description":"Resource control for HTML notes. Moved from Phase 3 optimization to Phase 1 requirement.\n\n1. Track active note WebViews in an Array on the notes pane (or a shared manager)\n2. When creating a WebView for an HTML note, check if count >= 3\n3. If at cap, destroy the oldest (LRU) WebView and replace with placeholder\n4. Placeholder shows note title + clickable \"HTML Note — click to preview\"\n5. Clicking placeholder creates a WebView (and evicts oldest if needed)\n6. Edit button always works regardless of WebView state — opens WebViewEditor tab\n7. Configurable cap via constant (default 3), can expose in preferences later\n\nThis runs inside the rendering task (task 2) — not a separate implementation. Integrate directly into the HTML note rendering code.\n\nDepends on: HTML note type (task 1), rendering (task 2)","created_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:12","updated_at":"2026-03-27T05:18:09","priority":1,"tags":["notes","performance","resource-control","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2db67d1678a6bbf5fa21934311a5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2db6ec6a79c6beca782503fce862","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Edit button: open HTML note in WebViewEditor","description":"When the user clicks the edit button on an HTML note, open the HTML source in a WebViewEditor tab in the editor pane.\n\n1. Detect HTML note type in the edit button handler\n2. Create a WebViewEditor tab (via EditorPane.add_webview_editor) with the note's HTML\n3. Set the note as the associated_object on the Editor so changes sync back\n4. When the WebViewEditor content changes, update the linked note\n5. When saved, the note's HTML source is updated and the note re-renders\n\nDepends on: HTML note rendering (task 2), WebViewEditor already exists (Phase 1)","created_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:16","updated_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:16","priority":1,"tags":["editor","notes","ux","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2db67d1678a6bbf5fa21934311a5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2db6fddc7d4ca68341000f773077","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Wire WebViewEditor save-to-note to create HTML notes","description":"Update the WebViewEditor's create-note-button handler to create HTML notes instead of text notes.\n\n1. In Editor.gd _on_create_note_button_pressed, change the WEBVIEW case from Note.create_text_note to Note.create_html_note\n2. The note should display the rendered webview, not raw HTML code\n3. If the editor has an existing linked note, update it instead of creating a new one\n\nDepends on: HTML note type (task 1)","created_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:20","updated_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:20","priority":1,"tags":["editor","notes","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2db67d1678a6bbf5fa21934311a5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d2db70c50760086525702aacd6c51","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"MCP support for HTML notes","description":"Enable MCP tools to create and read HTML notes.\n\n1. minerva_create_note: accept type=html parameter, create HTML note with provided content\n2. minerva_get_note: return HTML source for HTML notes\n3. minerva_update_note: update HTML content for HTML notes\n4. minerva_link_webview_to_note: convenience tool that takes a webview editor_name and creates/updates a linked HTML note (similar to minerva_link_spreadsheet_to_note)\n\nDepends on: HTML note type (task 1)","created_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:24","updated_at":"2026-03-27T05:14:24","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","notes","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d2db67d1678a6bbf5fa21934311a5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d301392b77fd6a137ca7f6dfd28cf","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Investigate vertical misalignment of WRY WebView in editor pane and HTML notes","description":"Bug: the native WRY-backed WebView renders vertically shifted downward when embedded both in the center editor pane ('webview editor') and inside HTML notes. The visible effect is that the webview content overflows down relative to its intended container slot.","created_at":"2026-03-27T16:14:42","updated_at":"2026-03-27T16:25:50","priority":2,"tags":["editor","godot","layout","needs-review","notes","webview","wry"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d324122887ba881e3cd05cc19deee","type":"discussion","status":"resolved","title":"Live Plugin System: Compiled Extensions with MCP + WebView Integration","description":"## Vision\n\nMinerva ships with an embedded compiler. When a user wants a new capability, the LLM writes backend code, compiles it, tests it, and deploys it as a live service — all within Minerva. No external toolchain required.\n\n## Runtime Model\n\nA standing HTTP server hosts compiled plugins under namespaced paths (`/extensions/movie-editor/...`). Two consumers talk to these plugins:\n- **Webview panels** — user-facing UI, via REST calls\n- **MCP tools** — LLM access, auto-registered into Minerva's tool system\n\n## MCP Integration\n\nWhen a plugin is deployed, it declares MCP tool definitions (name, description, input schema). Minerva registers these as live MCP tools, discoverable through `minerva_tool_search`. A plugin is both a UI backend and an MCP tool provider simultaneously.\n\n## Goals\n\n- **Self-contained**: Download Minerva, get everything — compiler, runtime, tool registration\n- **LLM-driven lifecycle**: LLM writes, compiles, tests, deploys, and registers tools — user describes intent\n- **Isolated execution**: Plugins run as separate processes, can't crash Minerva\n- **View + Service + Tools triad**: Webview for UI, compiled process for logic, MCP for LLM access — REST is the common glue\n- **Dynamic tool discovery**: New plugins surface in tool search automatically\n\n## Open Questions\n\n1. Language choice (compile speed vs. power vs. binary size)\n2. Server architecture (one router vs. one process per plugin)\n3. Plugin permission/sandboxing model\n4. How tool definitions are declared (manifest? introspection endpoint? convention?)\n5. Persistence — do plugins survive restarts? Versioning/updates?","created_at":"2026-03-28T02:23:42","updated_at":"2026-03-28T03:28:35","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","extensibility","mcp","plugins","webview"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d327c148c7ef7bef081eacd650295","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Plugin System: Supervised Out-of-Process Extensions with Policy Enforcement","description":"## Problem\n\nMinerva users and LLMs cannot extend Minerva with new backend capabilities at runtime. WebView panels solve the UI extension problem, but there's no mechanism for server-side logic — interacting with other apps, processing data, running complex workflows, etc. Users wanting a movie editor, firmware builder, or email integration have no path to add these without recompiling Minerva.\n\n## Vision\n\nLLMs can write, test, and deploy plugin backends that run as supervised out-of-process services. Minerva acts as the middleware broker — enforcing policy, registering tools, and mediating all communication between plugin UIs, plugin backends, and host capabilities.\n\n## Architectural Decisions (settled in discussion 019d324122887ba881e3cd05cc19deee)\n\n### 1. Minerva is the broker\n- Plugin UI (webview) → Minerva IPC → plugin backend\n- Agent/MCP → Minerva → plugin tool → plugin backend\n- Plugin → Minerva capability request → host internal API\n- Plugins NEVER directly access Core, arbitrary localhost MCP endpoints, or host internals\n\n### 2. Manifest-driven, deny-by-default policy\n- Plugins declare requested capabilities in a manifest (the \"request\")\n- Minerva enforces capabilities deterministically in host code (the \"grant\")\n- User is the authority that approves/narrows permissions\n- Policy enforcement at every boundary crossing: UI→host, agent→tool, plugin→capability\n- Structured machine-readable policy errors for LLM iteration\n\n### 3. Language-agnostic protocol\n- The manifest contract is the interface, not the language\n- Plugins can be Python, Go, Node, shell scripts, compiled binaries — anything that honors the transport protocol and manifest contract\n- Minerva spawns the process and doesn't need to understand the runtime\n- Managed toolchain support (venv creation, compiler install) is a convenience feature, not a prerequisite\n\n### 4. Tool namespacing: `minerva_<plugin>_<tool>`\n- Plugin tools appear in the same `minerva_tool_search` as built-in tools\n- No separate discovery path — flat namespace with predictable prefix\n- Tool metadata includes `\"source\": \"plugin:<name>\"` for provenance\n- Good descriptions matter more than naming conventions\n\n### 5. Audit logging is first-class\n- All policy denials, capability grants/revokes, brokered operations, and plugin crashes logged\n- Feeds the Plugin Manager UI and LLM iteration feedback loop\n\n### 6. Host API versioning\n- Plugins declare the host capability API version they target\n- Minerva can reject or degrade gracefully when versions diverge\n\n## Manifest Spec (initial)\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"plugin_id\",\n  \"name\": \"Human-Readable Name\",\n  \"version\": \"0.1.0\",\n  \"host_api_version\": \"1\",\n  \"backend\": {\n    \"transport\": \"stdio\",\n    \"entrypoint\": \"python plugin.py\",\n    \"args\": []\n  },\n  \"ui\": {\n    \"panels\": [\"panel_name\"],\n    \"ipc_messages\": [\"plugin.action_name\"]\n  },\n  \"tools\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"minerva_pluginid_tool_name\",\n      \"description\": \"What the tool does\",\n      \"input_schema\": { ... }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"permissions\": {\n    \"host_capabilities\": [\"notes.create\", \"artifacts.read\"],\n    \"network\": { \"mode\": \"none\" },\n    \"filesystem\": {\n      \"mode\": \"scoped_paths\",\n      \"paths\": [\"user://plugins/data/plugin_id/\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Initial Capability Taxonomy (Phase 1)\n\n- `notes.create`, `notes.read`, `notes.update`\n- `artifacts.read`, `artifacts.write`\n- `webview.create`, `webview.update`\n- `filesystem.scoped` (paths declared in manifest)\n- `network.none` (deny marker, expandable later)\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 1a — Plugin lifecycle\n- PluginDB: manifest parsing, CRUD, persistence, MCP tool access\n- Plugin process supervision: spawn/stop/restart, health monitoring\n- Tool registration into minerva_tool_search\n- Stdio transport validation (exists in code but untested — must validate)\n- **Vertical slice**: one toy plugin (`notes_helper`) proving the full path\n\n### Phase 1b — Policy enforcement\n- Capability declarations parsed from manifest\n- Deny-by-default checks at every boundary crossing\n- Structured policy errors (`capability_not_granted`, `schema_validation_failed`, `rate_limit_exceeded`, etc.)\n- Audit log for all policy decisions\n- The Phase 1a plugin immediately runs under policy\n\n### Phase 1c — Plugin Manager UI\n- Built-in Minerva UI (NOT a plugin-hosted webview — the control plane should not depend on the system it controls)\n- View installed plugins, status, declared vs granted capabilities\n- Toggle permissions, view denial log, grant from denial\n- User is the authority — manifest requests, user approves\n\n### Phase 2 — Iterate and expand\n- Richer capability taxonomy based on Phase 1 learnings\n- Rate limits, filesystem scoping, network allowlists\n- Hot reload (kill → recompile → restart → reconnect)\n- Plugin data directory management (`user://plugins/data/<id>/`)\n- Event/notification channel for live UI updates (streaming, progress)\n- MCP export path generalization (verify plugin tools flow through existing HTTP export)\n\n### Phase 3 — Ecosystem\n- On-demand managed toolchain install (Python venv first, Go later)\n- Plugin packaging/distribution\n- Trust tiers (local dev → unsigned → signed)\n- Plugin-to-plugin communication rules\n- OS-level sandboxing for defense-in-depth\n\n## Vertical Slice Target (Phase 1 proof-of-architecture)\n\nPlugin: `notes_helper`\n- Backend: one stdio process exposing one tool (`minerva_notes_helper_create_summary`)\n- Capability: requests `notes.create`\n- Policy: Minerva validates manifest → schema → capability → allow/deny\n- Denial: structured error returned and logged\n- Success: Minerva creates note through existing host code, returns result\n\nThis proves: manifest loading, process supervision, transport wiring, tool registration, policy enforcement, structured deny path, brokered host capability dispatch.\n\nFollow-on slice adds one UI path: plugin panel sends IPC message, Minerva enforces capability, returns result. Proves the UI broker model.\n\n## What is explicitly deferred\n- Broad multi-runtime toolchain management\n- Plugin ecosystem / package manager\n- Core/service-bus integration for plugins\n- Advanced sandboxing (OS-level, WASM)\n- Plugin-to-plugin communication\n- Manifest signing\n\n## Research: Language Ecosystem (informing language-agnostic decision)\n\n| Dimension | Python | Go | Rust |\n|-----------|--------|-----|------|\n| LLM generation success | Best | Good | Poor |\n| Iteration speed | Instant | Fast (1-2s) | Slow (30-300s) |\n| Library ecosystem | Largest (550K+) | Good (200K+) | Good (160K+) |\n| Dependency risk | High | Low | Moderate |\n| Deployment | Needs runtime | Single binary | Single binary |\n| Existing Minerva infra | Strong | None | Minimal |\n\nRecommendation: LLMs should default to Python for rapid prototyping (existing venv infrastructure), Go for production/deployment optimization. Rust unsuitable for iterative LLM generation. Protocol-level language agnosticism means this is a guidance choice, not an architectural constraint.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:28:05","updated_at":"2026-03-28T03:45:32","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","extensibility","mcp","plugins","policy","webview"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implementation plan: Plugin system vertical slice and policy-first host integration","description":"Implementation plan for DCR 019d327c148c7ef7bef081eacd650295.\n\n## Goal\n\nImplement the plugin-system architecture in a policy-first, vertical-slice order inside Minerva itself, proving lifecycle, registration, brokering, and deterministic enforcement before expanding to richer runtimes, packaging, or ecosystem features.\n\n## Success criteria\n\nA minimal plugin (`notes_helper`) can be installed/registered, started as an out-of-process backend, surface one plugin tool through Minerva, request one host capability (`notes.create`), and receive either:\n- a successful brokered result, or\n- a structured deterministic policy denial that is logged and inspectable.\n\n## Phase 1a: Plugin lifecycle + registry vertical slice\n\n### 1. Add PluginDB / manifest model\nCreate the host-side data model for installed plugins.\n\nDeliverables:\n- `PluginDefinition` / manifest parser model\n- persistent storage for plugin records\n- minimum fields:\n  - id\n  - name\n  - version\n  - host_api_version\n  - backend transport\n  - command / args / working_dir\n  - declared tools\n  - declared permissions\n  - plugin data directory\n  - autostart flag\n- plugin runtime state model:\n  - installed\n  - starting\n  - running\n  - stopped\n  - error\n  - crash_loop\n\n### 2. Add PluginManager service\nCreate a Minerva-owned manager responsible for plugin lifecycle.\n\nResponsibilities:\n- load/save PluginDB\n- install/register plugin from manifest directory\n- start / stop / restart plugin process\n- track runtime status and failures\n- expose status/log accessors for later UI/MCP use\n- own plugin data/cache path conventions\n\n### 3. Validate one backend transport\nDo not build on assumptions here.\n\nPreferred order:\n- first validate stdio with a tiny toy backend\n- if stdio is not reliable enough yet, fall back to localhost HTTP for the first slice\n- keep the manifest + policy architecture transport-agnostic\n\nDeliverables:\n- one transport path proven end-to-end\n- explicit notes on any stdio gaps discovered\n\n### 4. Register plugin tools into Minerva runtime registry\nSurface plugin-declared tools in Minerva’s tool system.\n\nRequirements:\n- namespaced tool names: `minerva_<plugin>_<tool>`\n- provenance metadata retained (`source = plugin:<id>` or equivalent)\n- visible in `minerva_tool_search`\n- collision handling and reserved-prefix enforcement\n\n### 5. Build the `notes_helper` toy plugin\nMinimal vertical-slice test backend.\n\nBehavior:\n- one plugin tool: `minerva_notes_helper_create_summary`\n- one requested host capability: `notes.create`\n- no direct filesystem/network/Core access beyond manifest defaults\n\n## Phase 1b: Policy enforcement\n\n### 6. Add deterministic manifest-backed policy model\nImplement the host-side policy engine that treats the manifest as request and Minerva as enforcement authority.\n\nRequirements:\n- deny-by-default posture\n- plugin capabilities are checked in host code only\n- no LLM judgment in allow/deny path\n- capability evaluation is explicit and auditable\n\nInitial capability scope:\n- `notes.create`\n- `notes.read`\n- `notes.update`\n- `artifacts.read`\n- `artifacts.write`\n- `webview.create`\n- `webview.update`\n- `filesystem.scoped`\n- `network.none`\n\n### 7. Add boundary enforcement points\nPolicy checks must happen at every boundary crossing relevant to the slice.\n\nInitial enforcement points:\n- agent/MCP -> plugin tool\n- plugin backend -> host capability dispatch\n- later: webview IPC -> host\n\nChecks:\n- tool/message exists and belongs to plugin\n- input schema validation\n- capability granted?\n- target/scope allowed?\n- payload size and simple limits\n\n### 8. Add structured policy errors\nReturn machine-readable denial/failure payloads.\n\nInitial error vocabulary:\n- `permission_denied`\n- `capability_not_granted`\n- `schema_validation_failed`\n- `payload_too_large`\n- `target_not_allowlisted`\n- `rate_limit_exceeded`\n- `confirmation_required`\n\n### 9. Add audit/event logging\nRecord key decisions and failures.\n\nMust log:\n- plugin install/register/start/stop/crash\n- policy denials\n- grants/revokes once those exist\n- brokered sensitive operations\n- restart loops / crash_loop transitions\n\n## Phase 1c: Host capability dispatch\n\n### 10. Add explicit host capability broker layer\nPlugins should not call arbitrary host internals.\n\nCreate a narrow dispatch layer mapping capability names to explicit host code paths.\n\nInitial dispatch target:\n- `notes.create`\n\nBehavior:\n- plugin requests brokered operation\n- Minerva validates capability + schema\n- Minerva calls existing note creation path\n- Minerva returns structured success/error result\n\n### 11. Prove success + denial paths end-to-end\nAcceptance tests for the vertical slice:\n- allowed plugin note creation succeeds\n- denied plugin note creation returns structured error\n- tool appears in tool search\n- plugin state transitions are recorded\n- audit log contains decision trail\n\n## Phase 2: UI + broader plumbing\n\n### 12. Add plugin webview IPC broker path\nOnly after backend slice is solid.\n\nDeliverables:\n- one plugin panel message routed through Minerva IPC\n- deterministic policy enforcement on IPC path\n- no direct raw localhost MCP/plugin calls from plugin UI\n\n### 13. Build initial built-in Plugin Manager UI\nControl plane should be Minerva-owned first.\n\nInitial features:\n- list plugins\n- show status and restart/crash information\n- show declared vs granted capabilities\n- show denial/audit log\n- basic grant/revoke/narrow controls\n\n### 14. Generalize MCP export path as needed\nVerify and close any gaps in external/plugin tool export.\n\nQuestions to resolve in implementation:\n- do dynamically registered plugin tools fully flow through Minerva’s exported MCP surface already?\n- if not, what explicit export/broker changes are required?\n\n## Phase 3: Hardening and expansion\n\n### 15. Expand capability taxonomy and scoping\nAdd scope-aware permissions such as:\n- filesystem path scopes\n- network allowlists\n- container allowlists\n- MCP proxy scopes\n\n### 16. Add managed runtime/toolchain support\nOnly after the architecture is proven.\n\nInitial likely support:\n- Python venv convenience path first\n- Go toolchain support later\n- keep manifest/transport contract language-agnostic\n\n### 17. Add trust tiers and packaging\nLater ecosystem work:\n- local/dev plugins\n- unsigned user-installed plugins\n- signed/published plugins\n- packaging / import / export story\n\n### 18. Consider deeper sandboxing\nDefense-in-depth only after core architecture is working:\n- OS-level sandboxing\n- process isolation hardening\n- plugin-to-plugin communication rules\n\n## Suggested task breakdown\n\nA reasonable execution order inside the repo:\n1. PluginDefinition + PluginDB persistence\n2. PluginManager lifecycle/state model\n3. toy backend transport validation\n4. plugin tool registration + search visibility\n5. policy engine skeleton + capability checks\n6. host capability dispatch for `notes.create`\n7. `notes_helper` plugin vertical slice\n8. audit logging\n9. built-in Plugin Manager UI\n10. webview IPC broker path\n\n## Risks to watch during implementation\n\n- stdio transport may not be production-ready despite existing code\n- dynamic plugin tool export may reveal MCP plumbing assumptions\n- broad manifest/runtime flexibility may complicate lifecycle code too early\n- policy scope can balloon if capability taxonomy is not kept intentionally small at first\n- UI bootstrapping should not depend on plugin authority model too early\n\n## Definition of done for this work item\n\nThis work item is complete when:\n- the policy-first plugin vertical slice exists in Minerva\n- one toy plugin runs end-to-end through Minerva\n- one brokered host capability is enforced deterministically\n- denial logging and structured error handling are in place\n- the implementation exposes the next real bottlenecks for Phase 2 rather than leaving the architecture untested","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:31:52","updated_at":"2026-03-28T03:40:34","priority":1,"parent":"minerva:019d327c148c7ef7bef081eacd650295"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d3280c8107e989f594d6f1766210a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Validate stdio MCP transport end-to-end","description":"Test the existing stdio MCP transport (SubProcess GDExtension + MCPServerConnection.gd) against a real stdio MCP server. Prove: process spawn, MCP handshake, tool listing, tool call, response parsing, error handling, process cleanup. If stdio has blocking issues, document them and validate HTTP as fallback. This unblocks all downstream plugin work.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:33:13","updated_at":"2026-03-28T03:47:54","priority":1,"tags":["plugins","stdio","transport"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d3280d54d7033a4efb99026d3a6bb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"PluginDefinition model + PluginDB persistence","description":"Create the host-side data model for installed plugins. PluginDefinition: manifest parser for id, name, version, host_api_version, backend (transport, entrypoint, args), declared tools, declared permissions, UI panels/IPC messages, data directory, autostart flag. PluginDB: persistent storage (JSON file at user://plugins/plugins.json or similar), CRUD operations, query by id/name/status. Runtime state model: installed, starting, running, stopped, error, crash_loop.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:33:17","updated_at":"2026-03-28T03:47:53","priority":1,"tags":["data-model","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d3280e1c97106a24ab5559e9cb739","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"PluginManager service: lifecycle and process supervision","description":"Minerva-owned manager for plugin lifecycle. Responsibilities: load/save PluginDB, install/register plugin from manifest directory, start/stop/restart plugin processes (via SubProcess or HTTP), track runtime status and failures, detect crash loops, handle plugin data/cache path conventions (user://plugins/data/<id>/). Exposes status/log accessors for later UI/MCP use. Owns the plugin process references and cleanup on Minerva shutdown.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:33:20","updated_at":"2026-03-28T03:50:10","priority":1,"tags":["lifecycle","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d328101687232a12fcf336909739f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plugin tool registration + unregistration in Minerva tool system","description":"Surface plugin-declared tools in Minerva's runtime tool registry. Namespaced as minerva_<plugin>_<tool>. Provenance metadata: source=plugin:<id>. Visible in minerva_tool_search. Collision handling: reject if name conflicts with built-in or other plugin tool. Reserved prefix enforcement: plugins cannot register minerva_* tools that don't include their plugin name. CRITICAL: unregister tools when plugin stops, crashes, or is removed — stale tools must not remain discoverable.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:33:28","updated_at":"2026-03-28T03:55:39","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","plugins","tools"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d328114e17987aa27978c4ca0b0a7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Policy engine + host capability broker","description":"Implement together (untestable separately). Policy engine: deny-by-default, manifest capabilities as requests, deterministic host-code-only allow/deny (no LLM judgment), capability evaluation is explicit and auditable. Enforcement points: agent/MCP→plugin tool, plugin→host capability dispatch. Checks: tool exists and belongs to plugin, input schema validation, capability granted, target/scope allowed. Host capability broker: narrow dispatch layer mapping capability names to host code paths. Initial dispatch: notes.create → existing note creation path. Plugin requests brokered operation, Minerva validates capability+schema, calls host code, returns structured result. Initial capability scope: notes.create, notes.read, notes.update, artifacts.read, artifacts.write, webview.create, webview.update, filesystem.scoped, network.none.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:33:33","updated_at":"2026-03-28T03:52:41","priority":1,"tags":["plugins","policy","security"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d328121557669a4f12044bbe75a8f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Structured policy errors + audit logging","description":"Structured errors: machine-readable denial payloads for LLM iteration. Error vocabulary: permission_denied, capability_not_granted, schema_validation_failed, payload_too_large, target_not_allowlisted, rate_limit_exceeded, confirmation_required. Audit logging: record plugin install/register/start/stop/crash, policy denials, grants/revokes, brokered sensitive operations, restart loops/crash_loop transitions. Log must be queryable for Plugin Manager UI (denial history, per-plugin filtering).","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:33:36","updated_at":"2026-03-28T03:49:39","priority":1,"tags":["logging","plugins","policy"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d328140a4707696dfaa388a77c468","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"notes_helper vertical slice: end-to-end acceptance tests","description":"Build the notes_helper toy plugin and prove the full architecture end-to-end. Plugin: one stdio backend, one tool (minerva_notes_helper_create_summary), one capability (notes.create). Acceptance tests: (1) plugin installs from manifest, (2) plugin starts and tool appears in tool search, (3) allowed note creation succeeds via brokered host capability, (4) denied note creation returns structured policy error, (5) plugin state transitions are recorded, (6) audit log contains decision trail, (7) plugin stop removes tool from registry, (8) crash detection and state transition to error/crash_loop. This is the proof-of-architecture milestone.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:33:44","updated_at":"2026-03-28T04:02:14","priority":1,"tags":["plugins","testing","vertical-slice"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d32814e117574a1c37346fc41e974","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP tools for plugin CRUD and management","description":"Expose PluginDB and PluginManager operations as MCP tools so LLMs can manage plugins programmatically. Tools: minerva_plugin_list (list installed plugins + status), minerva_plugin_install (register from manifest path), minerva_plugin_remove (uninstall), minerva_plugin_start/stop/restart, minerva_plugin_inspect (show manifest, granted capabilities, recent audit log). These should flow through existing minerva_tool_search.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:33:48","updated_at":"2026-03-28T03:54:47","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d328162f076e4af1ce887c085a6bd","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Built-in Plugin Manager UI","description":"Built-in Minerva UI (NOT plugin-hosted webview — control plane must not depend on the system it controls). Features: list installed plugins with status indicators, show declared vs granted capabilities with toggle controls, denial/audit log viewer with per-plugin filtering, grant-from-denial action (user sees a denial, clicks to grant that capability), start/stop/restart controls, crash loop indicators. User is the authority — manifest requests, user approves via this UI.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:33:53","updated_at":"2026-03-28T04:07:28","priority":2,"tags":["plugins","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d328174df7e45a6dbb0fddae9032a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Webview IPC broker path for plugin UI panels","description":"Route plugin webview UI through Minerva's IPC broker. Webview does NOT talk directly to plugin backends or localhost MCP. Flow: webview sends IPC message → Minerva validates (plugin owns panel, message declared in manifest, payload schema valid, capability granted) → Minerva dispatches to plugin backend or host capability → returns result. Deny with structured policy error if any check fails. Prove with one plugin panel sending one IPC message through the full brokered path.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:33:58","updated_at":"2026-03-28T04:05:22","priority":2,"tags":["plugins","security","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d328fbb637b88bdf1d190acc20f6c","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"SubProcess GDExtension merges stderr into stdout pipe, corrupting stdio MCP transport","description":"In `src/gdextension/terminal/unix/subprocess.cpp` line 77, `dup2(stdout_pipe[1], STDERR_FILENO)` routes the child process's stderr into the same fd as stdout. For stdio MCP transport, stdout is the JSON-RPC channel. Any stderr output from a plugin (Python tracebacks, warnings, library debug output) will corrupt the JSON-RPC stream and cause parse failures.\n\nThis is a critical blocker for reliable plugin subprocess communication.","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:49:33","updated_at":"2026-03-28T06:43:05","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["plugins","stdio","subprocess","transport"],"parent":"minerva:019d3280c8107e989f594d6f1766210a","resolution":"Separate stderr pipe added to subprocess.cpp. New API: has_stderr(), read_stderr_line(), read_all_stderr(), stderr_ready signal. Plugin servers restored to use stderr for logging.","repro_steps":"1. Launch a stdio MCP server via SubProcess that writes to stderr (e.g. Python with a warning or traceback)\n2. Observe that stderr output appears interleaved with JSON-RPC responses on stdout\n3. JSON parsing fails on the Minerva side"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d328fd67b78fda9ea54b48bf9e274","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"MCPManager references wrong property name `is_connected` instead of `server_connected`","description":"In `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/MCPManager.gd` at lines 455, 543, and 550, the code references `connection.is_connected` but `MCPServerConnection.gd` declares the property as `server_connected`. GDScript returns null for missing properties silently, so `is_server_connected()` always returns false for live connections.\n\nThis causes:\n- Tool availability checks to fail (tools appear unavailable even when the server is running)\n- Spurious dead-connection cleanup (live connections get treated as dead)","created_at":"2026-03-28T03:49:40","updated_at":"2026-03-28T06:43:06","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["mcp","plugins","stdio"],"parent":"minerva:019d3280c8107e989f594d6f1766210a","resolution":"Fixed 3 references in MCPManager.gd: connection.is_connected → connection.server_connected at lines 455, 543, 550.","repro_steps":"1. Connect to any MCP server via MCPManager\n2. Call `is_server_connected()` or check connection liveness\n3. Always returns false because `connection.is_connected` resolves to null"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d329a301978178650553d6d50547a","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"MCP Result Filters: deterministic host-side post-processing for model-facing tool output","description":"Add a deterministic Minerva-owned result-filter layer between MCP tool execution and model-visible tool results. The goal is to reduce context waste and improve tool-result readability without changing the underlying tool contract or relying on prompt behavior.\n\nMotivation:\n- Some MCP tools return noisy but low-value output (for example repeated divider lines like '-----').\n- Raw JSON is often not the most efficient representation for LLM consumption.\n- Tool-result cleanup should be explicit, reversible, and enforced in code.\n- Filters should be enableable per tool and per individual tool call.\n\nProposal:\n- Introduce an MCP result middleware layer that runs after Minerva normalizes an MCP tool result and before the result is stringified into chat history for model continuation.\n- Preserve both raw and filtered output. The raw result remains canonical for debugging/audit/UI inspection. The filtered result is what is inserted into model context when enabled.\n- Support a small deterministic set of built-in filter types first:\n  - line regex removal (for example remove lines matching ^-{5,}$)\n  - repeated-line collapse\n  - whitespace normalization\n  - structured JSON reformatting\n  - JSON -> TOON conversion (or another Minerva-chosen compact stable structured format)\n- Filters are declared and executed by Minerva code, not by the LLM directly mutating raw text in prompts.\n- Allow filter application at two scopes:\n  - per-tool defaults\n  - one-shot per-call overrides for the next invocation\n- Keep UI rendering concerns separate from model-context filtering. Tool call UI may show filtered output by default, but must allow viewing raw output.\n\nLikely insertion point in current Minerva:\n- MCP transport/result normalization remains unchanged.\n- After MCPManager.execute_tool() returns a normalized Dictionary result, but before ChatPane stringifies it for tool-result history entries and continuation.\n- ToolCallBlock should be extended to understand raw vs filtered result display.\n\nSuggested architecture:\n- New service: MCPResultFilterEngine\n- New concepts:\n  - filter definition: id, type, config, enabled\n  - filter chain: ordered list of filter definitions\n  - filter application record: which filters ran, what changed, any errors/skips\n- Execution pipeline:\n  1. raw result dictionary\n  2. normalized result dictionary\n  3. optional structural formatter(s)\n  4. optional text cleanup filters\n  5. final model-facing string\n  6. truncation\n- Storage:\n  - preserve raw result\n  - preserve filtered/model-facing result\n  - preserve applied filter metadata\n\nControl surface:\n- Prefer Minerva-owned MCP tools or host APIs for setting/clearing filters instead of modifying every existing tool schema.\n- Candidate management tools:\n  - minerva_filter_list\n  - minerva_filter_set_default\n  - minerva_filter_clear_default\n  - minerva_filter_apply_next\n- Keep filter application visible and reversible.\n\nConstraints / non-goals for v1:\n- Not a general arbitrary script/expression engine.\n- Not prompt-based filtering.\n- Not destructive mutation of the canonical stored tool result.\n- Not UI-only pretty-printing; the primary target is model-facing context hygiene.\n\nRisks and concerns:\n- Over-aggressive filters can hide important errors or edge-case data.\n- Arbitrary regex from the model can become brittle or surprising.\n- Display formatting and model-context formatting can diverge if not tracked clearly.\n- Result filtering must remain auditable so debugging stays possible.\n\nMitigations:\n- Preserve raw output alongside filtered output.\n- Restrict v1 to built-in filter types and explicit configuration.\n- Add structured denial/error reporting for invalid filter configs.\n- Record applied filters in tool execution metadata.\n\nSuggested vertical slice:\n- One built-in regex line-removal filter for divider spam.\n- One structured JSON formatter.\n- Per-tool default filter chain for a single noisy MCP tool.\n- One-shot override for the next call.\n- Tool call UI toggle between raw and filtered result.\n\nOpen questions:\n- Whether TOON is the right structured target format, or whether Minerva should define a smaller canonical compact JSON-like representation.\n- Whether filter defaults belong to chat state, global prefs, or MCP tool metadata.\n- Whether external agents consuming Minerva MCP should receive filtered output, raw output, or an explicit choice.","created_at":"2026-03-28T04:00:58","updated_at":"2026-03-28T04:00:58","priority":2,"tags":["context-window","llm","mcp","tooling","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d3384feac7573a4157531fb94149a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Hot reload: kill → recompile → restart → reconnect cycle","description":"When a plugin's source files change on disk, Minerva should detect the change and offer (or auto-perform) a restart cycle. This is critical for the LLM-driven plugin development workflow: LLM writes code, plugin restarts, LLM tests — no manual intervention.\n\n## Subtasks\n1. File watcher on plugin data_directory — detect changes to .py/.js/.sh files\n2. Debounced restart trigger — don't restart on every keystroke, wait for settle (500ms)\n3. Stop → start cycle preserving grants and autostart (already works via restart_plugin)\n4. Tool re-registration on reload (already works via plugin_started signal → on_plugin_started)\n5. MCP tool: `minerva_plugin_reload` — explicit reload trigger for LLMs\n6. Plugin Manager UI: show \"changed on disk\" indicator, manual reload button\n7. Optional auto-reload flag in manifest (like autostart but for file changes)\n\n## Key constraint\nThe plugin process must be fully stopped before restarting. No attempt at in-process hot patching — kill and restart is the model.","created_at":"2026-03-28T08:17:27","updated_at":"2026-03-28T08:29:04","priority":1,"tags":["hot-reload","phase-2","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d33851fe776ce863d33bc4fa33239","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"mcp.proxy capability: policy-gated access to Minerva's MCP tool surface","description":"Instead of expanding the capability taxonomy to mirror every Minerva MCP tool, add one meta-capability: `mcp.proxy`. Plugins request permission to call specific Minerva MCP tools through the broker.\n\n## Design\n- Manifest declares: `\"host_capabilities\": [\"mcp.proxy:minerva_create_note\", \"mcp.proxy:minerva_list_notes\"]`\n- Wildcard support: `\"mcp.proxy:minerva_note_*\"` grants all note tools\n- Broker validates the specific tool name against the granted mcp.proxy list\n- Dispatches to MinervaMCPServer's existing tool execution path\n- Returns the tool result to the plugin\n\n## Policy model\n- `mcp.proxy:minerva_bash` — explicitly dangerous, user must grant deliberately\n- `mcp.proxy:minerva_file_*` — scoped by filesystem permissions in manifest\n- `mcp.proxy:minerva_create_note` — safe, easy grant\n- User sees the full list of requested proxied tools in Plugin Manager UI\n\n## Implementation\n1. Add mcp.proxy pattern matching to PluginPolicy.check_capability()\n2. Add mcp.proxy dispatch to CapabilityBroker — calls MinervaMCPServer._execute_tool_impl()\n3. Update Plugin Manager UI to show mcp.proxy grants with tool names\n4. Update manifest spec documentation\n\nThis replaces the need for individual capabilities like notes.update, artifacts.read, etc. — they're all just mcp.proxy to existing tools.","created_at":"2026-03-28T08:17:35","updated_at":"2026-03-28T08:26:43","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","phase-2","plugins","policy","taxonomy"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d33854b6a7f3fa856cdf1787cbabb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Bidirectional stdio channel: plugin-initiated capability requests","description":"Replace the current capability-request-in-response pattern with a proper bidirectional channel. Currently, the plugin embeds `capability_requests` in its tool response JSON, and Minerva processes them post-hoc. This works but is limited — the plugin can't request a capability mid-execution and use the result before responding.\n\n## Design\nThe stdio transport becomes bidirectional:\n- Minerva → Plugin: JSON-RPC requests (tool calls) — exists today\n- Plugin → Minerva: JSON-RPC requests (capability requests) — new\n\nPlugin sends on stdout (interleaved with responses):\n```json\n{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":\"cap-1\",\"method\":\"minerva/capability\",\"params\":{\"capability\":\"mcp.proxy:minerva_create_note\",\"args\":{\"title\":\"...\",\"content\":\"...\"}}}\n```\n\nMinerva's stdio reader detects this is a request (has `method` field, no matching pending request ID), dispatches through CapabilityBroker, writes result back to plugin's stdin:\n```json\n{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":\"cap-1\",\"result\":{\"success\":true,\"uuid\":\"...\"}}\n```\n\nPlugin awaits the response and uses the result in its tool execution.\n\n## Implementation\n1. Modify MCPServerConnection._stdio_request read loop to detect plugin-initiated requests\n2. Route detected requests through CapabilityBroker.dispatch()\n3. Write broker result back to subprocess stdin\n4. Update notes_helper to use the bidirectional pattern\n5. Remove the post-hoc capability_requests parsing from PluginToolRegistry\n\n## Constraint\nMust handle interleaving correctly — a plugin might send a capability request while Minerva is waiting for a tool response. The request IDs distinguish them.","created_at":"2026-03-28T08:17:46","updated_at":"2026-03-28T08:33:04","priority":2,"tags":["phase-2","plugins","stdio","transport"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d3385604f727787a971a5f9d298d9","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plugin data directory management and filesystem scoping","description":"Plugins need a managed data directory for persistent state, caches, and output files. The manifest declares `filesystem.scoped` with allowed paths. Minerva should create/manage these directories and enforce the scoping.\n\n## Implementation\n1. Auto-create `user://plugins/data/<plugin_id>/` on plugin install\n2. Pass data directory path to plugin via environment variable or init params\n3. Enforce filesystem.scoped in CapabilityBroker — validate paths are within declared scope\n4. Cleanup option on plugin removal (keep data vs delete)\n5. Show data directory size in Plugin Manager UI","created_at":"2026-03-28T08:17:52","updated_at":"2026-03-28T08:24:57","priority":2,"tags":["filesystem","phase-2","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d338571a97ecba292a4d22b0c848d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Rate limits and resource controls for plugins","description":"Add per-plugin rate limiting and resource controls to prevent runaway plugins from overwhelming host capabilities.\n\n## Controls\n- Capability call rate limit (e.g., max 10 notes.create per minute)\n- Payload size limits (already partially implemented)\n- Plugin stderr output rate limit (prevent log spam flooding toasts)\n- Configurable per-plugin in Plugin Manager UI\n- Default limits in manifest, user can adjust\n\n## Implementation\n1. Add rate limit tracking to PluginPolicy (sliding window counter per capability)\n2. Return `rate_limit_exceeded` structured error when exceeded\n3. Plugin Manager UI: rate limit configuration section\n4. Manifest: optional `rate_limits` section with defaults","created_at":"2026-03-28T08:17:56","updated_at":"2026-03-28T08:32:07","priority":3,"tags":["phase-2","plugins","policy"],"parent":"minerva:019d327f894f7c7daf8264dbbbc9e65f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d357548767d5a9fa01db0d2c7b753","type":"discussion","status":"resolved","title":"OBS Controller Plugin: webcam overlay positioning + scene management","description":"## Goal\n\nReplace Minerva's built-in video recording (limited by Godot's input/filtering issues) with a plugin that controls OBS Studio. The plugin provides:\n\n1. **Webview UI** — draggable webcam overlay on a schematic screen canvas, scene picker, recording controls, playback library\n2. **LLM tools** — programmatic scene switching, camera positioning, recording control\n3. **OBS integration** — via obs-websocket (built into OBS 28+, port 4455)\n\n## Use case\n\n\"Record me using Minerva with a talking head\" — user wants to reposition the webcam overlay during recording, switch OBS scenes, and have LLMs assist with camera direction.\n\n## Plugin manifest\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"obs_controller\",\n  \"name\": \"OBS Controller\",\n  \"version\": \"0.1.0\",\n  \"host_api_version\": \"1\",\n  \"backend\": {\n    \"transport\": \"stdio\",\n    \"entrypoint\": \"python3\",\n    \"args\": [\"server.py\"]\n  },\n  \"ui\": {\n    \"panels\": [\"obs_controller\"],\n    \"ipc_messages\": [\n      \"obs.move_camera\",\n      \"obs.resize_camera\",\n      \"obs.switch_scene\",\n      \"obs.get_status\",\n      \"obs.start_recording\",\n      \"obs.stop_recording\",\n      \"obs.list_recordings\",\n      \"obs.play_recording\",\n      \"obs.connect\",\n      \"obs.disconnect\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"tools\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"minerva_obs_controller_list_scenes\",\n      \"description\": \"List available OBS scenes\",\n      \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {},\n        \"required\": []\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"minerva_obs_controller_switch_scene\",\n      \"description\": \"Switch to a named OBS scene\",\n      \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"scene\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"Scene name to switch to\"}\n        },\n        \"required\": [\"scene\"]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"minerva_obs_controller_move_camera\",\n      \"description\": \"Move the webcam overlay to a position on screen. Coordinates are 0.0-1.0 normalized (0,0 = top-left, 1,1 = bottom-right).\",\n      \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"x\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"description\": \"Horizontal position (0.0-1.0)\"},\n          \"y\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"description\": \"Vertical position (0.0-1.0)\"},\n          \"width\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"description\": \"Width as fraction of screen (default: current)\"},\n          \"height\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"description\": \"Height as fraction of screen (default: current)\"}\n        },\n        \"required\": [\"x\", \"y\"]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"minerva_obs_controller_get_status\",\n      \"description\": \"Get current OBS status: connection state, active scene, recording state, camera position\",\n      \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {},\n        \"required\": []\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"minerva_obs_controller_start_recording\",\n      \"description\": \"Start recording in OBS\",\n      \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {},\n        \"required\": []\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"minerva_obs_controller_stop_recording\",\n      \"description\": \"Stop recording in OBS. Returns the output file path.\",\n      \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {},\n        \"required\": []\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"minerva_obs_controller_list_recordings\",\n      \"description\": \"List video files in the OBS output directory\",\n      \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"limit\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"description\": \"Max files to return (default: 10)\"}\n        },\n        \"required\": []\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"permissions\": {\n    \"host_capabilities\": [\n      \"mcp.proxy:minerva_create_note\",\n      \"mcp.proxy:minerva_create_webview_panel\",\n      \"mcp.proxy:minerva_update_webview_panel\"\n    ],\n    \"network\": {\n      \"mode\": \"localhost\",\n      \"ports\": [4455]\n    },\n    \"filesystem\": {\n      \"mode\": \"scoped_paths\",\n      \"paths\": [\"user://plugins/data/obs_controller/\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Plugin file structure\n\n```\nsrc/plugins/obs_controller/\n├── manifest.json\n├── server.py              # MCP stdio server + OBS websocket client\n├── requirements.txt       # obsws-python\n└── ui/\n    └── panel.html         # Webview UI (self-contained HTML/CSS/JS)\n```\n\n## server.py architecture\n\n```python\n# Single Python process handling:\n# 1. MCP stdio (JSON-RPC on stdin/stdout) — tool calls from LLM\n# 2. OBS WebSocket (ws://localhost:4455) — controls OBS\n# 3. IPC messages (via minerva/capability reverse channel) — webview requests\n\nclass OBSController:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.obs = None          # obsws-python client\n        self.connected = False\n        self.camera_source = \"\"  # Name of the webcam source in OBS\n        self.output_dir = \"\"     # OBS recording output path\n    \n    def connect(self, host=\"localhost\", port=4455, password=\"\"):\n        # Connect to OBS via websocket\n    \n    def move_camera(self, x, y, width=None, height=None):\n        # SetSceneItemTransform on the camera source\n    \n    def switch_scene(self, scene_name):\n        # SetCurrentProgramScene\n    \n    def start_recording(self):\n        # StartRecord\n    \n    def stop_recording(self):\n        # StopRecord, return output path\n    \n    def list_scenes(self):\n        # GetSceneList\n    \n    def get_status(self):\n        # GetRecordStatus + GetCurrentProgramScene + source transform\n    \n    def list_recordings(self, limit=10):\n        # List files in output_dir sorted by mtime\n```\n\n## Webview UI (panel.html)\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ OBS Controller          [🔴 Recording]  │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Scene: [Dropdown ▼]    [⏺ Start] [⏹ Stop] │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ ┌─────────────────────────────────┐     │\n│ │                                 │     │\n│ │          Screen canvas          │     │\n│ │                                 │     │\n│ │                        (●)      │     │\n│ │                     ← drag →    │     │\n│ │                                 │     │\n│ └─────────────────────────────────┘     │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Recordings:                              │\n│  📹 2026-03-28_session1.mp4  [▶ Play]   │\n│  📹 2026-03-28_session2.mp4  [▶ Play]   │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n- Canvas is a `<canvas>` or positioned `<div>` elements\n- Circle is draggable via pointer events\n- On drag end, sends IPC: `obs.move_camera {x, y}`\n- Scene dropdown populated via IPC: `obs.get_status`\n- Recording list populated via IPC: `obs.list_recordings`\n- Playback via `<video>` element pointing to file served through plugin\n\n## IPC message flow (webview → plugin)\n\n1. Webview JS calls `minerva_bridge.send_ipc(\"obs.move_camera\", {x: 0.75, y: 0.85})`\n2. Minerva's PluginWebviewBroker validates message is declared in manifest\n3. Broker dispatches to plugin backend via stdio\n4. Plugin calls OBS WebSocket API\n5. Result returns through broker to webview JS callback\n\n## Open questions\n\n1. **OBS source name**: How does the plugin know which source is the webcam? Auto-detect by type (video capture), or require user to configure it?\n2. **OBS password**: obs-websocket can have authentication. Store in plugin data dir or prompt user?\n3. **Network permission**: The manifest declares `network.localhost` port 4455. This capability isn't enforced yet — it's a declaration for future enforcement. Acceptable for now?\n4. **Dependency**: `obsws-python` is a pip package. Plugin needs a venv. We don't have managed toolchain yet (Phase 3). Options: (a) require user to pip install, (b) bundle a vendored copy, (c) use raw websocket from stdlib.\n5. **File serving for playback**: The webview needs to play video files from OBS's output directory. How does the file get from disk into the webview? Options: (a) plugin serves it via a local HTTP endpoint (violates no-direct-localhost), (b) Minerva provides a file-serving capability, (c) webview reads via IPC message returning base64.\n6. **First real test of PluginWebviewBroker**: This plugin exercises the webview IPC path end-to-end for the first time. We should expect integration issues.","created_at":"2026-03-28T17:19:31","updated_at":"2026-03-28T18:36:48","priority":1,"tags":["obs","plugins","video","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d327c148c7ef7bef081eacd650295"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35bb95e67f7c9c8f24d2ee06d4e4","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"OBS Controller Plugin + Plugin Event/State Broker","description":"## Problem\n\nTwo related gaps in Minerva:\n\n1. **No OBS integration**: Users recording themselves using Minerva (talking head + screen capture) have no way to control OBS from within Minerva. Camera positioning, scene switching, and recording control require leaving Minerva to interact with OBS directly. LLMs cannot assist with camera direction or recording workflow.\n\n2. **No plugin event/state model**: The plugin system has tools (commands) but no mechanism for plugins to push notifications or state changes to Minerva. Without this, plugin webview UIs must poll their backends, and agents cannot react to external events (recording started, scene changed, build completed). This blocks any plugin with a stateful UI.\n\n## Vision\n\nA Go-based OBS controller plugin that serves as both a useful feature and the proving ground for the plugin event/state architecture. The plugin controls OBS via its WebSocket API, exposes tools for LLMs, and — once the event broker exists — pushes live state to a webview UI.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. Prove the plugin system works with Go (language agnosticism)\n2. Give users and LLMs real-time OBS control from within Minerva\n3. Build the generic plugin event/state broker as a platform feature\n4. Complete the webview IPC broker path end-to-end (first real consumer)\n5. Establish the tools/events/state trichotomy as the plugin communication model\n\n## Non-Goals\n\n- Replacing Minerva's native video recording pipeline (OBS is a parallel capture path, not a backend swap)\n- Importing OBS recordings into Minerva's frame-based editor format (Phase D, separate decision)\n- Managed Go toolchain installation (user builds the binary for now)\n- OS-level sandboxing or network enforcement for plugins\n- Plugin packaging or distribution (Phase 3 of the plugin DCR)\n- Advanced OBS features (filters, audio mixing, streaming) — just scene/camera/recording control\n\n## Architectural Decisions\n\n### 1. Go for the plugin language\nResearched both Go and Python ecosystems:\n\n| Dimension | Go | Python |\n|-----------|-----|--------|\n| OBS library | `goobs` — code-generated, 100% v5.7.2 coverage, active (Mar 2026) | `obsws-python` — partial v5, manual, active |\n| MCP SDK | Official `go-sdk` with StdioTransport built in (4.2k stars) | None — hand-roll JSON-RPC |\n| Deployment | Single binary, zero dependencies | Needs Python + pip + venv |\n| Concurrency | Goroutines — natural for stdin MCP + OBS websocket | Threading/asyncio — workable but more complex |\n| LLM iteration | Slower to generate | Faster |\n\nGo wins on every practical dimension except LLM generation speed. The single-binary deployment eliminates the managed toolchain problem entirely.\n\n### 2. Semantic 3x3 grid for camera positioning\nInstead of raw normalized coordinates (`{x: 0.75, y: 0.85}`), use a semantic grid:\n- Rows: `top`, `middle`, `bottom`\n- Columns: `left`, `center`, `right`\n- Sizes: `small`, `medium`, `large`\n\nThe plugin maps these to OBS transform math internally (alignment bitfield, boundsWidth/Height, positionX/Y). The LLM says `{row: \"bottom\", column: \"right\", size: \"medium\"}` — no coordinate math.\n\n### 3. Tools / Events / State trichotomy for plugins\nThe generic plugin communication model (not OBS-specific):\n\n- **Tools**: Commands callable by agents and Minerva (we have this)\n- **Events**: Edge-triggered notifications from plugin to Minerva (`obs.recording_started`, `obs.scene_changed`). For transitions.\n- **State**: Latest canonical snapshot of the plugin (`{connected, scene, recording, camera}`). For late consumers who missed events.\n\nPlugins emit events/state to Minerva. Minerva validates, stores, and fans out to consumers (webviews, agents, triggers).\n\n### 4. PluginEventBroker as a dedicated service\nNOT TriggerManager. Triggers are a later consumer of plugin events, not the transport. The broker:\n- Validates event names and payloads against manifest declarations\n- Stores latest state per plugin\n- Emits Godot signals for internal consumers\n- Pushes updates to plugin webviews\n- Optionally bridges to TriggerManager later\n\n### 5. Manifest extensions for events and state\nEvents and state are top-level manifest sections (NOT under `ui`). A headless plugin can emit events without having a webview.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"events\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"obs.state_changed\",\n      \"payload_schema\": { ... }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"state\": {\n    \"schema\": { ... }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 6. Webview is never the source of truth\n- UI actions → PluginWebviewBroker → plugin backend\n- Plugin emits event/state → Minerva → pushes to webview\n- Agent actions and UI actions converge on the same state path\n- No polling, no stale UI\n\n## OBS Protocol Details (from research)\n\n### Source positioning gotchas\n- `SetSceneItemTransform` requires `sceneItemId` (integer, fetched via `GetSceneItemId`)\n- `width`/`height` fields are READ-ONLY computed values — silently ignored on write\n- Two sizing models: `scaleX/Y` (relative to source native resolution) vs `boundsWidth/Height` (absolute pixel bounding box). Must pick one consistently.\n- `boundsType` must be set to non-NONE for bounds to take effect (e.g., `OBS_BOUNDS_STRETCH`)\n- Alignment bitfield: 5=top-left (default), 0=center, 10=bottom-right\n- The semantic grid abstracts all of this from the user/LLM\n\n### Key Go libraries\n- `github.com/andreykaipov/goobs` v1.8.2 — full OBS v5.7.2 API, code-generated\n- `github.com/modelcontextprotocol/go-sdk` v1.4.1 — official MCP SDK with StdioTransport\n- No other dependencies needed — websocket is handled internally by goobs\n\n### OBS WebSocket connection\n- Default: `ws://localhost:4455`\n- Optional password authentication (configured per-OBS installation)\n- Built into OBS 28+ (no plugin install needed on OBS side)\n\n## Implementation Phases\n\n### Phase A: Go backend MCP tools (OBS Controller)\n\nThe plugin as a Go binary with MCP tools only. No webview, no events.\n\n**Plugin structure:**\n```\nsrc/plugins/obs_controller/\n├── go.mod\n├── go.sum\n├── main.go          # MCP stdio server entry point\n├── obs.go           # OBS WebSocket client wrapper\n├── tools.go         # Tool handlers\n├── config.go        # Config file management\n├── grid.go          # Semantic grid → OBS transform mapping\n└── manifest.json    # Plugin manifest (tools only, no UI)\n```\n\n**MCP tools:**\n- `minerva_obs_controller_connect` — connect to OBS (host/port/password)\n- `minerva_obs_controller_disconnect` — disconnect from OBS\n- `minerva_obs_controller_get_status` — connection state, current scene, recording state, camera position\n- `minerva_obs_controller_list_scenes` — available OBS scenes\n- `minerva_obs_controller_switch_scene` — change active scene\n- `minerva_obs_controller_list_sources` — all sources in current scene (for config discovery)\n- `minerva_obs_controller_set_camera_position` — semantic grid: row, column, size\n- `minerva_obs_controller_start_recording` — start OBS recording\n- `minerva_obs_controller_stop_recording` — stop recording, return output path\n- `minerva_obs_controller_list_recordings` — files in OBS output directory\n- `minerva_obs_controller_configure` — set OBS connection details, webcam source name, output directory\n\n**Config file** (`user://plugins/data/obs_controller/config.json`):\n```json\n{\n  \"obs_host\": \"localhost\",\n  \"obs_port\": 4455,\n  \"obs_password\": \"\",\n  \"camera_source\": \"Webcam\",\n  \"output_directory\": \"\",\n  \"auto_connect\": true\n}\n```\n\n**What this proves:**\n- Plugin system works with Go (language agnosticism)\n- Single binary deployment (no runtime dependencies)\n- Tool discovery by internal LLM\n- Real external state control (OBS)\n- Practical daily-use feature\n\n**Manifest (Phase A):**\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"obs_controller\",\n  \"name\": \"OBS Controller\",\n  \"version\": \"0.1.0\",\n  \"host_api_version\": \"1\",\n  \"backend\": {\n    \"transport\": \"stdio\",\n    \"entrypoint\": \"./obs_controller\",\n    \"args\": []\n  },\n  \"tools\": [ ... ],\n  \"permissions\": {\n    \"host_capabilities\": [],\n    \"network\": { \"mode\": \"localhost\", \"ports\": [4455] },\n    \"filesystem\": {\n      \"mode\": \"scoped_paths\",\n      \"paths\": [\"user://plugins/data/obs_controller/\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Phase B: PluginEventBroker (Platform Feature)\n\nGeneric plugin event/state infrastructure. Not OBS-specific.\n\n**New file:** `src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginEventBroker.gd`\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Handle `minerva/plugin_event` and `minerva/plugin_state` messages from stdio\n- Validate event names against manifest `events` declarations\n- Validate payloads against declared schemas (basic type checks)\n- Store latest state per plugin (`_plugin_states: Dictionary`)\n- Emit Godot signals: `plugin_event(plugin_id, event_name, payload)`, `plugin_state_changed(plugin_id, state)`\n- Push state to subscribed webview panels\n\n**Persistent stdio reader:**\nThe current stdio reader only processes plugin-initiated messages during active tool calls. For async events, we need continuous draining.\n\nTechnical approach: connect to SubProcess's `output_ready` signal (already emitted from the background read thread via `call_deferred`). When output arrives outside of a tool call, drain the queue, parse JSON-RPC messages, and route:\n- Messages with matching `id` → pending tool response (shouldn't happen outside _stdio_request, but handle gracefully)\n- `method: \"minerva/capability\"` → CapabilityBroker dispatch\n- `method: \"minerva/plugin_event\"` → PluginEventBroker.handle_plugin_event()\n- `method: \"minerva/plugin_state\"` → PluginEventBroker.handle_plugin_state()\n- Anything else → log as unrecognized\n\n**Manifest extensions to PluginDefinition:**\n- `events: Array[Dictionary]` — each with `name` and optional `payload_schema`\n- `state: Dictionary` — with `schema` for the plugin's state shape\n\n**What this proves:**\n- Plugins can push notifications without tool calls\n- Minerva can fan out plugin state to multiple consumers\n- The tools/events/state model works generically\n\n### Phase C: OBS Webview UI\n\nDepends on: PluginEventBroker + PluginWebviewBroker fully wired.\n\n**Wire PluginWebviewBroker end-to-end:**\n- `WebViewEditor._on_ipc_message()` → route to PluginWebviewBroker\n- `minerva_bridge` gets `_dispatchEvent()` for push from Minerva\n- Remove direct localhost MCP fetches from minerva_bridge\n\n**Minimal webview panel (first iteration):**\n- Connection status indicator\n- Scene picker dropdown\n- Start/stop recording buttons\n- Current state display (from PluginEventBroker push)\n\n**Second iteration (after basic panel works):**\n- Draggable camera overlay on schematic canvas\n- Grid snap positions (visual representation of the 3x3 grid)\n- Resize handles\n\n**IPC messages** (declared in manifest, all brokered):\n- `obs.set_camera_position` — drag/grid click\n- `obs.switch_scene` — dropdown selection\n- `obs.start_recording` / `obs.stop_recording` — button clicks\n\n**OBS plugin emits events:**\n- `obs.state_changed` — after any mutation\n- `obs.recording_started` / `obs.recording_stopped`\n- `obs.scene_changed`\n\nMinerva pushes these to the webview. Webview redraws from pushed state. No polling.\n\n### Phase D: Recording Library + Pipeline Decisions (deferred, separate discussion)\n\nExplicit design decision needed:\n1. OBS recordings stay in plugin-local library UI\n2. Import path into Minerva's native recording format\n3. Generalized external-video editor flow\n\nThis should be a separate discussion item, not assumed.\n\n## Risks and Mitigations\n\n| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Async stdio delivery is unreliable | Events lost or delayed | Use SubProcess `output_ready` signal for immediate delivery. Test with rapid event bursts. |\n| Go MCP SDK protocol version mismatch with Minerva | Plugin can't connect | Verify `MCP_PROTOCOL_VERSION` constant in MCPServerConnection.gd matches go-sdk supported versions. Fix if needed. |\n| OBS not installed or not running | Plugin fails silently | `get_status` tool returns connection state. Plugin handles reconnection gracefully. |\n| SetSceneItemTransform complexity | Camera positioning bugs | Encapsulate in `grid.go` with the semantic grid abstraction. Test all 9 grid positions. |\n| PluginWebviewBroker not fully wired | Phase C blocked | Phase A and B don't need it. Phase C explicitly depends on completing the broker wiring. |\n| Manifest grows too complex for LLM generation | Barrier to plugin creation | Keep Phase A manifest minimal (tools only). Consider `minerva_plugin_scaffold` tool in Phase 3. |\n| Go binary not cross-platform | Plugin only works on build platform | Accept for now. Cross-compilation is a Phase 3 packaging concern. |\n\n## Suggested Task Breakdown\n\n### Phase A tasks (independent, can parallelize)\n1. **Go plugin scaffold** — go.mod, main.go with MCP SDK StdioTransport, manifest.json\n2. **OBS connection + basic tools** — connect/disconnect/get_status/list_scenes/switch_scene using goobs\n3. **Camera positioning** — grid.go semantic mapping + set_camera_position tool + list_sources\n4. **Recording control** — start/stop recording + list_recordings + output directory\n5. **Configuration** — config.go + configure tool + config.json persistence\n6. **Integration test** — install in Minerva, verify tool discovery, control OBS end-to-end\n\n### Phase B tasks (sequential)\n1. **PluginEventBroker service** — new GDScript file, signals, state storage, manifest extensions\n2. **Persistent stdio reader** — wire SubProcess output_ready to continuous message draining\n3. **Message routing** — distinguish tool responses / capability requests / events / state in the reader\n4. **OBS plugin events** — add event emission to Go plugin (obs.state_changed, obs.recording_*)\n5. **Integration test** — verify events flow from Go plugin through broker to Godot signals\n\n### Phase C tasks (sequential, depends on Phase B)\n1. **Wire PluginWebviewBroker** — WebViewEditor._on_ipc_message → broker → plugin backend\n2. **Wire webview push** — minerva_bridge._dispatchEvent from PluginEventBroker\n3. **Basic OBS panel** — connection status, scene picker, recording controls\n4. **Camera canvas** — draggable overlay with grid snap\n5. **Integration test** — full loop: drag camera → IPC → plugin → OBS → event → push → webview redraws\n\n## Definition of Done\n\nPhase A: A Go binary plugin installed in Minerva that an internal or external LLM can discover and use to control OBS — switch scenes, position the webcam overlay via semantic grid, start/stop recording.\n\nPhase B: Any running plugin can emit events and state updates at any time (not just during tool calls), and Minerva validates, stores, and fans out those notifications via Godot signals.\n\nPhase C: A webview panel in Minerva shows live OBS state (scene, recording, camera position) updated via push, and the user can drag the camera overlay or click controls to send brokered IPC commands to the plugin.","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:36:19","updated_at":"2026-03-28T18:50:06","priority":1,"tags":["events","go","obs","plugins","video","webview"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implementation plan: OBS controller plugin and plugin event/state broker","description":"Execution plan for DCR 019d35bb95e67f7c9c8f24d2ee06d4e4.\n\n## Objective\nDeliver the OBS controller plugin in staged phases while keeping the generic plugin event/state broker as a separate platform concern with explicit dependencies.\n\n## Guiding constraints\n- Keep OBS Phase A useful before solving the whole plugin platform.\n- Treat async stdio event delivery as the main technical risk center.\n- Keep emitted plugin events separate from UI-originating IPC in the manifest model.\n- Preserve the separation between tools, events, and state.\n\n## Phase 1: OBS tools-only vertical slice\nGoal: ship a useful Go-based OBS plugin with no webview dependency.\n\nDeliverables:\n- Go plugin scaffold under `src/plugins/obs_controller/`\n- tools-only manifest\n- config persistence in plugin data dir\n- semantic 3x3 camera positioning API\n- internal/external LLM tool discovery and successful OBS control\n\nScope:\n- `connect`\n- `disconnect`\n- `get_status`\n- `list_scenes`\n- `switch_scene`\n- `list_sources`\n- `set_camera_position`\n- `start_recording`\n- `stop_recording`\n- `list_recordings`\n- `configure`\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- Internal Minerva agent can discover and call the plugin tools.\n- External MCP client can call the same tools through Minerva.\n- Camera positioning uses semantic row/column/size, not raw coordinates.\n- Tool results return current state snapshot after mutations.\n\n## Phase 2: PluginEventBroker platform work\nGoal: add generic plugin-originated events and state updates to Minerva.\n\nDeliverables:\n- `PluginEventBroker.gd`\n- manifest extensions for top-level `events` and `state`\n- persistent plugin stdout draining path for async notifications\n- routing for:\n  - tool responses\n  - `minerva/capability`\n  - `minerva/plugin_event`\n  - `minerva/plugin_state`\n- latest-state storage per plugin\n- Godot signals for plugin event/state fan-out\n\nKey risk to resolve first:\n- how async plugin stdout is continuously drained when no tool call is active\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- A plugin can emit event/state notifications while idle.\n- Minerva validates and stores them deterministically.\n- Internal consumers can subscribe via Godot signals.\n- OBS plugin can emit `obs.state_changed` and recording/scene events successfully.\n\n## Phase 3: Webview broker completion + OBS panel\nDepends on: Phase 2.\n\nGoal: complete the first push-synchronized stateful plugin UI.\n\nDeliverables:\n- fully wired `PluginWebviewBroker`\n- `WebViewEditor._on_ipc_message()` routed through broker\n- `minerva_bridge` updated for host-pushed plugin events\n- minimal OBS panel:\n  - connection state\n  - scene selector\n  - recording controls\n- later enhancement: camera canvas / grid placement UI\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- UI sends brokered IPC requests only.\n- Agent-issued OBS actions update the panel without polling.\n- Panel-issued actions update OBS and redraw from pushed state.\n\n## Deferred / out of scope\n- replacing Minerva’s native video recording pipeline\n- importing OBS recordings into Minerva’s frame-based editor\n- managed Go toolchain installation\n- plugin packaging/distribution\n- OS/network sandbox enforcement\n- trigger integration as a primary bus\n\n## Suggested execution order\n1. Build Phase 1 first and prove daily usefulness.\n2. File or create child tasks for the generic PluginEventBroker as a platform feature.\n3. Do not begin the OBS webview panel until async event/state push is working.\n\n## Recommended child-task breakdown\n- OBS Go scaffold and manifest\n- OBS connection and scene control tools\n- semantic grid camera positioning\n- recording/config tools\n- plugin discovery/integration verification\n- PluginEventBroker service\n- persistent async stdio drain path\n- plugin event/state manifest support\n- OBS event emission integration\n- WebViewEditor / PluginWebviewBroker wiring\n- OBS panel UI\n\n## Definition of done\n- Phase 1: Minerva can use the OBS plugin as a practical tools-only feature.\n- Phase 2: Minerva has a generic deterministic plugin event/state broker.\n- Phase 3: OBS panel stays live-synced via push with no polling.\n","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:38:13","updated_at":"2026-03-28T18:41:05","priority":1,"tags":["events","go","implementation-plan","obs","plugins","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bb95e67f7c9c8f24d2ee06d4e4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c0414d78af9f6863d9a9f7b867","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Validate Go binary + Minerva SubProcess MCP handshake","description":"GATES ALL OTHER TASKS. Before writing any OBS code, prove that Minerva can spawn a Go binary via SubProcess and complete an MCP handshake.\n\n## Steps\n1. Read `MCP_PROTOCOL_VERSION` from `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/MCPServerConnection.gd` — confirm the go-sdk (v1.4.1, supports 2024-11-05 through 2025-11-25) accepts this version. If not, fix Minerva's constant or negotiate.\n2. Create a minimal Go MCP server at `src/plugins/obs_controller/`:\n   - `go.mod` with `github.com/modelcontextprotocol/go-sdk` dependency\n   - `main.go` using `mcp.StdioTransport` — handles initialize, tools/list (one echo tool), tools/call\n   - Build: `go build -o obs_controller .`\n3. Create `manifest.json` with `\"entrypoint\": \"./obs_controller\"` and one tool definition\n4. Verify PluginManager resolves the binary path correctly (currently resolves `.py/.js/.sh` files — check if bare executables or `./` paths work, extend if needed)\n5. Install in Minerva, start the plugin, verify:\n   - SubProcess spawns the Go binary\n   - MCP handshake completes (initialize + notifications/initialized)\n   - Tool appears in minerva_tool_search\n   - Tool call returns a result\n   - Plugin stop kills the process cleanly\n\n## Success criteria\n- Go binary runs as a Minerva plugin\n- Internal LLM can discover and call the echo tool\n- External MCP can call the echo tool\n- No stderr corruption (separate pipe working)\n\n## Notes\n- The go-sdk handles all JSON-RPC and MCP protocol details\n- stdout must be line-buffered (Go's fmt.Println is fine)\n- stderr goes to separate pipe (for logging)\n- If PluginManager's path resolution doesn't handle executables, fix `start_plugin()` to also resolve files without known extensions","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:41:25","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:01:27","priority":1,"tags":["go","obs","plugins","transport"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c06aaf7f0e9403f2eb52fe5fd1","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"OBS connection + scene control tools","description":"Add OBS WebSocket connection and scene management to the Go plugin.\n\n## Dependencies\n- Task: Validate Go binary + SubProcess handshake (must pass first)\n\n## Deliverables\nAdd to the Go plugin:\n\n**`obs.go`** — OBS WebSocket client wrapper using `github.com/andreykaipov/goobs`:\n- `Connect(host, port, password)` — connect to OBS\n- `Disconnect()` — close connection\n- `IsConnected() bool` — connection state\n- `ListScenes()` — returns scene names + current scene\n- `SwitchScene(name)` — SetCurrentProgramScene\n- `GetStatus()` — connection state, current scene, recording state\n\n**Tools:**\n- `minerva_obs_controller_connect` — args: `{host?, port?, password?}` (defaults from config). Returns connection status.\n- `minerva_obs_controller_disconnect` — no args. Returns ok.\n- `minerva_obs_controller_get_status` — no args. Always works. Returns `{connected, scene, recording, sources_count}`.\n- `minerva_obs_controller_list_scenes` — returns array of scene names + which is current.\n- `minerva_obs_controller_switch_scene` — args: `{scene: string}`. Returns new status.\n\n**Error handling:**\n- All tools check connection state first\n- Return structured error if not connected: `{\"error\": \"Not connected to OBS. Call minerva_obs_controller_connect.\"}`\n- OBS disconnection sets internal state to disconnected (check goobs disconnect callback)\n- No auto-reconnect in Phase 1\n\n## goobs API reference\n```go\nclient, err := goobs.New(\"localhost:4455\", goobs.WithPassword(\"...\"))\nscenes, _ := client.Scenes.GetSceneList()\nclient.Scenes.SetCurrentProgramScene(&scenes.SetCurrentProgramSceneParams{SceneName: \"Main\"})\n```","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:41:35","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:01:19","priority":1,"tags":["go","obs","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c0a43f727d824b2d60fabd16cf","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Semantic grid camera positioning tool","description":"Add camera overlay positioning using the 3x3 semantic grid.\n\n## Dependencies\n- Task: OBS connection + scene control tools\n\n## Deliverables\n\n**`grid.go`** — maps semantic positions to OBS transforms:\n- Grid: rows (top/middle/bottom) × columns (left/center/right)\n- Sizes: small (15% of canvas), medium (25%), large (35%)\n- Computes: positionX, positionY, boundsWidth, boundsHeight, boundsType, alignment\n- Uses `OBS_BOUNDS_STRETCH` for sizing\n- Alignment = 5 (top-left anchor) for all positions\n- Canvas size from `GetVideoSettings()` (don't assume 1920x1080)\n\n**Position mapping** (for 1920x1080 canvas, medium size = 480x270):\n| | left | center | right |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| top | (20, 20) | (720, 20) | (1420, 20) |\n| middle | (20, 405) | (720, 405) | (1420, 405) |\n| bottom | (20, 790) | (720, 790) | (1420, 790) |\n\nPositions should have margin from edges (e.g., 20px) and be computed relative to actual canvas dimensions.\n\n**Tools:**\n- `minerva_obs_controller_set_camera_position` — args: `{row: \"top\"|\"middle\"|\"bottom\", column: \"left\"|\"center\"|\"right\", size?: \"small\"|\"medium\"|\"large\"}`. Default size: medium.\n- `minerva_obs_controller_list_sources` — returns all sources in current scene with type info. For user/LLM to identify the webcam source name.\n\n**OBS API for positioning:**\n```go\n// 1. Get scene item ID\nparams := &sceneitems.GetSceneItemIdParams{SceneName: scene, SourceName: sourceName}\nresp, _ := client.SceneItems.GetSceneItemId(params)\nitemId := resp.SceneItemId\n\n// 2. Set transform\ntransform := &sceneitems.SetSceneItemTransformParams{\n    SceneName: scene,\n    SceneItemId: itemId,\n    SceneItemTransform: typedefs.SceneItemTransform{\n        PositionX: x,\n        PositionY: y,\n        BoundsType: \"OBS_BOUNDS_STRETCH\",\n        BoundsWidth: width,\n        BoundsHeight: height,\n        Alignment: 5,\n    },\n}\nclient.SceneItems.SetSceneItemTransform(transform)\n```\n\n**IMPORTANT gotcha:** `Width` and `Height` fields in GetSceneItemTransform responses are READ-ONLY computed values. Use `BoundsWidth`/`BoundsHeight` for sizing, never `Width`/`Height`.\n\n## Config dependency\nCamera source name comes from `config.json` field `camera_source`. If not configured, `set_camera_position` returns error: `\"camera_source not configured. Call minerva_obs_controller_configure or use list_sources to find it.\"`","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:41:50","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:01:20","priority":1,"tags":["go","obs","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c0d3e971209a643a87ce2956eb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Recording control + config + list_recordings tools","description":"Add recording management and plugin configuration to the Go plugin.\n\n## Dependencies\n- Task: OBS connection + scene control tools\n\n## Deliverables\n\n**`config.go`** — config file management:\n- Load/save `config.json` from plugin data directory\n- Fields: obs_host (default \"localhost\"), obs_port (default 4455), obs_password (default \"\"), camera_source (default \"\"), output_directory (default \"\", auto-detect from OBS), auto_connect (default true)\n- Config path passed via environment or resolved from binary location\n- On plugin start, if auto_connect=true and config exists, connect to OBS automatically\n\n**Recording tools:**\n- `minerva_obs_controller_start_recording` — calls `client.Record.StartRecord()`. Returns status.\n- `minerva_obs_controller_stop_recording` — calls `client.Record.StopRecord()`. Returns `{output_path: string}` from OBS response.\n- `minerva_obs_controller_list_recordings` — lists video files in output_directory sorted by mtime descending. Args: `{limit?: int}` default 10. Returns `[{filename, path, size_mb, modified}]`. If output_directory not configured, try to read it from OBS via `GetRecordDirectory()`.\n\n**Configuration tool:**\n- `minerva_obs_controller_configure` — args: `{obs_host?, obs_port?, obs_password?, camera_source?, output_directory?, auto_connect?}`. Merges provided fields into existing config, saves to disk. Returns full config (password redacted). LLM workflow: call `list_sources` to find webcam name, then `configure` with `camera_source`.\n\n**Auto-connect on start:**\nIn `main.go` init: load config, if `auto_connect` is true and host is set, attempt OBS connection. Log result to stderr. Don't fail plugin start if OBS isn't available — just set state to disconnected.\n\n## OBS API\n```go\nclient.Record.StartRecord()\nresp, _ := client.Record.StopRecord()  // resp has OutputPath\nclient.Record.GetRecordDirectory()\n```","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:42:02","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:01:21","priority":1,"tags":["go","obs","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c1096f7cceb1c2e420090e483f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"OBS plugin Phase A integration test: end-to-end verification","description":"Verify the complete OBS plugin works end-to-end with Minerva and OBS Studio.\n\n## Dependencies\n- All Phase A tasks must be complete\n\n## Test plan\n\n### 1. Plugin lifecycle\n- Build Go binary: `cd src/plugins/obs_controller && go build -o obs_controller .`\n- Install in Minerva via Plugin Manager UI or `minerva_plugin_install`\n- Verify plugin starts (auto-start if configured)\n- Verify plugin stops cleanly\n- Verify hot-reload: edit a Go file, rebuild binary, verify file watcher detects change and restarts\n\n### 2. Tool discovery\n- Internal LLM: `minerva_tool_search(\"obs\")` finds all OBS tools with correct schemas\n- Internal LLM can call tools and get results\n- External MCP client (curl) can call tools via HTTP\n\n### 3. OBS connection\n- `minerva_obs_controller_configure` — set OBS host/port, camera source name\n- `minerva_obs_controller_connect` — connects to running OBS\n- `minerva_obs_controller_get_status` — shows connected, current scene\n- `minerva_obs_controller_disconnect` — disconnects\n- All tools return structured error when disconnected\n\n### 4. Scene control\n- `minerva_obs_controller_list_scenes` — shows all OBS scenes\n- `minerva_obs_controller_switch_scene` — switches scene, verify in OBS\n\n### 5. Camera positioning\n- `minerva_obs_controller_list_sources` — shows all sources in scene\n- `minerva_obs_controller_set_camera_position` with all 9 grid positions\n- Verify webcam overlay moves correctly in OBS for each position\n- Test all 3 sizes (small, medium, large)\n- Verify error when camera_source not configured\n\n### 6. Recording\n- `minerva_obs_controller_start_recording` — OBS starts recording\n- `minerva_obs_controller_stop_recording` — OBS stops, returns file path\n- `minerva_obs_controller_list_recordings` — shows the recording\n\n### 7. LLM-driven workflow\n- Tell internal Minerva LLM: \"move my camera to the bottom right and start recording\"\n- Verify it discovers tools, calls set_camera_position + start_recording\n- Tell it: \"switch to my fullscreen scene\"\n- Verify it calls switch_scene\n\n### 8. Error cases\n- Plugin starts but OBS not running — get_status shows disconnected, tools return errors\n- Invalid scene name — structured error\n- Invalid grid position — structured error\n- OBS crashes during recording — plugin detects disconnect\n\n## Success criteria\nAll tests pass. The OBS plugin is practically useful for daily recording workflow.","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:42:16","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:15:24","priority":1,"tags":["go","obs","plugins","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c1540b7cb4ba8b4a415a6c8b50","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"PluginEventBroker service: event/state validation, storage, fan-out","description":"Create the generic plugin event/state broker. This is a platform feature — not OBS-specific.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n**New file: `src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginEventBroker.gd`**\n\n```gdscript\nclass_name PluginEventBroker\nextends RefCounted\n\nsignal plugin_event(plugin_id: String, event_name: String, payload: Dictionary)\nsignal plugin_state_changed(plugin_id: String, state: Dictionary)\n\n# Latest state per plugin\nvar _plugin_states: Dictionary = {}  # plugin_id → Dictionary\n\nfunc handle_plugin_event(plugin_id: String, event_name: String, payload: Dictionary) -> Dictionary\nfunc handle_plugin_state(plugin_id: String, state: Dictionary) -> Dictionary\nfunc get_plugin_state(plugin_id: String) -> Dictionary\nfunc subscribe_panel(plugin_id: String, panel_name: String) -> void\nfunc unsubscribe_panel(panel_name: String) -> void\n```\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Validate event name exists in plugin's manifest `events` declarations\n- Validate payload against declared `payload_schema` (basic type checks, not full JSON Schema)\n- Store latest state per plugin (overwrite on each `minerva/plugin_state`)\n- Emit Godot signals for internal consumers\n- Log events to PluginAuditLog\n- Push state to subscribed webview panels (Phase C integration point)\n\n**Manifest extensions to PluginDefinition.gd:**\n- Add `events: Array[Dictionary]` — each entry: `{name: String, payload_schema: Dictionary}`\n- Add `state_schema: Dictionary` — JSON Schema for the plugin's state shape\n- These are TOP-LEVEL manifest fields, NOT under `ui`\n- Update `to_dict()` / `from_dict()` / `_from_dict_internal()` accordingly\n\n**MCP tool for state query:**\n- Add `minerva_plugin_state` tool to PluginMCPTools.gd — args: `{id: String}`, returns latest state for a plugin. Allows LLMs to query plugin state without calling a plugin tool.\n\n**Initialize in singleton_object.gd:**\n- Create PluginEventBroker in `initialize_plugins()`\n- Wire to PluginManager (accessible via `get_event_broker()` or similar)\n- Follow existing pattern: `load()` to avoid parse-order issues","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:42:35","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:29:53","priority":1,"tags":["events","platform","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c18cdd74b891ccef0d798c579e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Persistent async stdio reader for plugin events outside tool calls","description":"The current stdio reader only processes plugin-initiated messages during active tool calls (inside `_stdio_request`'s polling loop). For async events (OBS scene changed while no tool call is in flight), we need continuous draining.\n\n## The problem\nSubProcess has a background read thread filling an output queue. `_stdio_request()` drains this queue while waiting for a tool response. But between tool calls, nobody reads the queue. Plugin events emitted during idle time sit unprocessed until the next tool call.\n\n## Solution: wire SubProcess `output_ready` signal\n\nSubProcess already emits `output_ready` via `call_deferred` from its read thread (see `subprocess.cpp` line 146). Wire this to a handler on PluginManager or MCPServerConnection.\n\n**Implementation in MCPServerConnection.gd:**\n- Add `_in_tool_call: bool = false` flag\n- Set to `true` at start of `_stdio_request`, `false` on exit\n- Connect `_subprocess.output_ready` to `_on_subprocess_output_ready()`\n- `_on_subprocess_output_ready()`:\n  - If `_in_tool_call` → return (polling loop handles it)\n  - Otherwise drain queue, for each line:\n    - Parse JSON\n    - If has `method` field → route to appropriate handler\n    - `minerva/capability` → dispatch through CapabilityBroker\n    - `minerva/plugin_event` → dispatch to PluginEventBroker\n    - `minerva/plugin_state` → dispatch to PluginEventBroker\n    - Else → log unrecognized message\n\n**Wire in PluginManager.start_plugin():**\nAfter creating the connection and connecting the `disconnected` signal, also connect `output_ready`:\n```gdscript\nif conn._subprocess:\n    conn._subprocess.output_ready.connect(conn._on_subprocess_output_ready)\n```\n\n**Add event_broker reference to MCPServerConnection:**\n- Add `var event_broker = null` property\n- Set from PluginManager during start_plugin()\n\n## Testing\n- Start a plugin that emits a `minerva/plugin_event` after a 2-second delay (not during a tool call)\n- Verify Minerva receives and processes it\n- Verify no interference with normal tool call flow","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:42:50","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:29:54","priority":1,"tags":["events","platform","plugins","stdio"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c1c5937309b0dee7b97f4303c4","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"OBS plugin event emission: state_changed, recording, scene events","description":"Add event/state emission to the Go OBS plugin. First real consumer of the PluginEventBroker.\n\n## Dependencies\n- PluginEventBroker service must be built\n- Persistent async stdio reader must be working\n\n## Deliverables\n\n**Update Go plugin to emit events after mutations:**\n\nAfter every tool that changes OBS state, emit a `minerva/plugin_state` notification:\n```json\n{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"minerva/plugin_state\",\"params\":{\"state\":{\"connected\":true,\"scene\":\"Main\",\"recording\":true,\"camera\":{\"row\":\"bottom\",\"column\":\"right\",\"size\":\"medium\"}}}}\n```\n\n**OBS event listener:**\ngoobs supports event subscriptions. Listen for:\n- `CurrentProgramSceneChanged` → emit `obs.scene_changed` event + state update\n- `RecordStateChanged` → emit `obs.recording_started` or `obs.recording_stopped` + state update\n- `ExitStarted` → emit `obs.disconnected` event + state update\n\nThese are OBS-originated events (user changed scene in OBS directly, not via plugin tool). The plugin forwards them to Minerva.\n\n**Event emission over stdio:**\nWrite `minerva/plugin_event` JSON-RPC notifications to stdout. These are fire-and-forget (no `id`, no response expected). The persistent stdio reader on Minerva's side picks them up.\n\n```go\nfunc emitEvent(name string, payload map[string]any) {\n    msg := map[string]any{\n        \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n        \"method\":  \"minerva/plugin_event\",\n        \"params\": map[string]any{\n            \"event\":   name,\n            \"payload\": payload,\n        },\n    }\n    json.NewEncoder(os.Stdout).Encode(msg)\n}\n```\n\n**IMPORTANT:** stdout is the MCP transport. Event notifications must be valid JSON-RPC and newline-terminated. They must NOT interfere with pending tool responses. Since Go's goroutine model handles the OBS event listener on a separate goroutine, serialize stdout writes with a mutex.\n\n**Update manifest** to declare events and state:\n```json\n{\n  \"events\": [\n    {\"name\": \"obs.state_changed\", \"payload_schema\": {...}},\n    {\"name\": \"obs.scene_changed\", \"payload_schema\": {\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"scene\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}}},\n    {\"name\": \"obs.recording_started\", \"payload_schema\": {}},\n    {\"name\": \"obs.recording_stopped\", \"payload_schema\": {\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"output_path\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}}},\n    {\"name\": \"obs.disconnected\", \"payload_schema\": {}}\n  ],\n  \"state\": {\n    \"schema\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"connected\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"},\n        \"scene\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n        \"recording\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"},\n        \"camera\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"row\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"column\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"size\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}}\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:43:04","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:30:00","priority":2,"tags":["events","go","obs","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c2225371e983189f2d09907df2","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Wire PluginWebviewBroker end-to-end: WebViewEditor IPC → broker → plugin","description":"Complete the webview IPC broker path. PluginWebviewBroker.gd exists but WebViewEditor and minerva_bridge aren't connected to it.\n\n## Dependencies\n- PluginEventBroker must be working (for push back to webview)\n\n## Current state\n- `PluginWebviewBroker.gd` — has the right shape: register_plugin_panel, handle_ipc_message, validation chain\n- `WebViewEditor._on_ipc_message()` — currently just logs raw IPC\n- `minerva_bridge.gd` — currently does direct localhost MCP fetches (bypasses broker)\n\n## Deliverables\n\n### 1. WebViewEditor IPC routing\nIn WebViewEditor, when an IPC message arrives from the webview JavaScript:\n- Check if this panel is registered as a plugin panel (via PluginWebviewBroker.is_plugin_panel)\n- If yes: route to PluginWebviewBroker.handle_ipc_message()\n- If no: handle as a regular Minerva webview IPC (existing behavior)\n\n### 2. minerva_bridge update\nAdd `window.minerva.pluginIPC(messageType, payload)` function that:\n- Sends an IPC message to Minerva (already exists in some form)\n- Returns a Promise that resolves with the result from the broker\n- This replaces any direct localhost fetch pattern for plugin panels\n\n### 3. Webview push path\nAdd `window.minerva._dispatchPluginEvent(eventName, payload)` and `window.minerva._dispatchPluginState(state)`:\n- Called by Minerva (via `evaluate_javascript`) when PluginEventBroker receives events/state for this plugin\n- Plugin webview JS registers handlers: `minerva.onPluginEvent((name, payload) => { ... })` and `minerva.onPluginState((state) => { ... })`\n\n### 4. Wire PluginEventBroker → webview push\nIn PluginEventBroker, when state changes or events arrive for a plugin that has subscribed panels:\n- Get the WebViewEditor instance for the panel\n- Call `evaluate_javascript(\"window.minerva._dispatchPluginState(\" + JSON.stringify(state) + \")\")` or similar\n\n## Testing\n- Create a test plugin with a webview panel that sends an IPC message\n- Verify message flows: webview JS → Minerva IPC → PluginWebviewBroker → plugin backend via stdio → response back to webview JS\n- Verify push: plugin emits state → PluginEventBroker → webview JS callback fires","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:43:28","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:46:44","priority":2,"tags":["platform","plugins","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c24dba73128426d3a6b012790b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"OBS webview panel: connection, scenes, recording controls","description":"Basic OBS controller webview panel. First real plugin UI.\n\n## Dependencies\n- PluginWebviewBroker wired end-to-end\n- PluginEventBroker working with push to webview\n- OBS plugin Phase A tools working\n\n## Deliverables\n\n**`src/plugins/obs_controller/ui/panel.html`** — self-contained HTML/CSS/JS:\n\n### Layout\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ OBS Controller    [● Connected]     │\n├─────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Scene: [Dropdown ▼]                 │\n├─────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ [⏺ Start Recording] [⏹ Stop]       │\n│ Recording: 00:03:42                 │\n├─────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ (Camera canvas — Phase C task 4)    │\n└─────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Behavior\n- On load: call `minerva.pluginIPC(\"obs.get_status\", {})` to populate initial state\n- Scene dropdown: populated from status, on change sends `minerva.pluginIPC(\"obs.switch_scene\", {scene: name})`\n- Recording buttons: send `obs.start_recording` / `obs.stop_recording` IPC\n- Connection indicator: green/red dot based on state\n- All state updates via `minerva.onPluginState((state) => { redraw(state) })` — no polling\n\n### IPC messages (add to manifest)\n- `obs.get_status`\n- `obs.switch_scene`\n- `obs.start_recording`\n- `obs.stop_recording`\n\n### Plugin backend handling\nThe Go plugin needs to handle IPC messages the same as tool calls — PluginWebviewBroker dispatches to the plugin via stdio. The plugin routes based on the message type and calls the same OBS functions.\n\n## Update manifest\nAdd `ui` section with panels and ipc_messages. Plugin needs reinstall to pick up manifest changes.","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:43:39","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:46:51","priority":2,"tags":["obs","plugins","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d35c2924f750daeb0459a61b6d577","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Camera canvas: draggable overlay with grid snap in OBS webview","description":"The motivating UX: drag a circle (webcam overlay) around a rectangle (screen) to position the camera.\n\n## Dependencies\n- OBS webview panel (basic controls) working\n- Push state from PluginEventBroker to webview working\n\n## Deliverables\n\nAdd to `panel.html`:\n\n### Canvas UI\n- Rectangle representing the OBS canvas (aspect ratio matches actual OBS canvas from state)\n- Circle representing the webcam overlay (size proportional to small/medium/large)\n- 3x3 grid lines shown as subtle guides\n- Current position highlighted\n\n### Interaction\n- **Drag**: pointer events on the circle. On drag end, snap to nearest grid position, send `minerva.pluginIPC(\"obs.set_camera_position\", {row, column})`\n- **Click grid cell**: click directly on a grid cell to move camera there\n- **Size control**: three buttons or a dropdown (small/medium/large), sends `obs.set_camera_position` with size param\n- **Visual feedback**: circle animates to new position, shows current grid label (\"bottom-right\")\n\n### State sync\n- `minerva.onPluginState` updates the circle position and size\n- If an LLM moves the camera via tool call, the canvas updates via push\n- If the user drags the circle, the canvas updates optimistically then confirms via push\n\n### Grid snap logic (JS)\n```javascript\nfunction snapToGrid(canvasX, canvasY, canvasWidth, canvasHeight) {\n    const col = canvasX < canvasWidth/3 ? \"left\" : canvasX < 2*canvasWidth/3 ? \"center\" : \"right\";\n    const row = canvasY < canvasHeight/3 ? \"top\" : canvasY < 2*canvasHeight/3 ? \"middle\" : \"bottom\";\n    return {row, column: col};\n}\n```\n\n### IPC message\n- `obs.set_camera_position` — `{row, column, size?}`\n\n## Integration test\nFull loop: drag camera to bottom-left → IPC → broker → plugin → OBS SetSceneItemTransform → plugin emits state → broker pushes to webview → circle redraws at bottom-left. Verify in OBS that the webcam overlay actually moved.","created_at":"2026-03-28T18:43:57","updated_at":"2026-03-28T19:46:51","priority":2,"tags":["obs","plugins","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019d35bd55de7ec986cd5d4d37680853"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36c369377180ae32403a62c7f291","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Stage 1 release planning: feature sweep, near-term gaps, and architecture boundaries","description":"Starting discussion thread for Minerva 'Stage 1' completion / release planning. This is intentionally a loose working thread to collect raw ideas before splitting into DCRs and work items.\n\nTopics raised so far\n\n1. Extend the graphics editor to include video generation.\n- Initial idea: use something like Kling or an OSS video model.\n- Likely shape: start frame + end frame (+ prompt) -> generated video.\n- Likely service home: extend media-gen in Minerva services rather than baking provider specifics into the editor.\n\n2. Create a more universal mechanism for launching editors.\n- Goal: replace the current hodge-podge of creation icons / entry points with a coherent New -> Item flow.\n\n3. Fix and actually use the OpenPose / pose editor.\n- Goal: define poses for generated characters reliably.\n- Concern: prior attempts have not felt good enough.\n\n4. Additional graphics editor work.\n- Speech bubbles.\n- Arcs.\n- Better infinite canvas UX.\n\n6. Manage multiple Minerva projects at the same time.\n- Motivation: moving toward a swarm-agent-first world.\n\n7. Model swarms as chats / notes / etc.\n- Working notion: a swarm is either project-focused or collection-of-projects-focused.\n- A project is a collection of chats, notes, and editors.\n\n8. Integrate Docket and improve kanban.\n- Better visibility into swarm effort / planning / testing.\n- Manage Claude, Codex, and autocoder together.\n\n9. Supervisor agents that manage work from other agents.\n\nAdditional items added later\n\n10. AST diff viewer experiment in ccsandbox.\n- Goal: help humans and AIs review changes structurally, not just textually.\n\n11. Sightline experiment in ccsandbox.\n- Goal: change -> visual inspection analysis for UI and related changes.\n\n12. Webview -> picture -> graphics editor pipeline.\n- Desired workflow: ask an agent to mock up a UI in a webview, capture it into a picture, open in graphics editor, then do circle-and-suggest markup graphically.\n\nInitial codebase-grounded assessment\n\nWhat appears relatively close\n\n- Universal editor launch:\n  There is already a File -> New submenu and multiple editor creation paths. This looks more like product/UI consolidation than missing capability.\n\n- Graphics editor incremental work:\n  Speech bubbles already exist as a real layer/tool path. Infinite canvas has partial pan/zoom support already. Arcs are the more clearly missing graphics primitive.\n\n- Pose editor rehabilitation:\n  There is already a substantial 3D pose editor / skeleton / OpenPose render path. This looks more like rehab and direct-manipulation UX work than greenfield.\n\n- Webview -> picture -> graphics editor:\n  Minerva already has webview panels, linked webview notes, screenshot-related infrastructure via CoBrowser, and image-bytes -> graphics-layer flow. This feels like a believable Stage 1 integration feature.\n\n- Parts of kanban / agent work visibility:\n  There is already a real autocoder kanban surface and planning notifications can populate boards.\n\nWhat looks medium-sized but plausible\n\n- Video generation from start/end frames:\n  The editor already routes image workflows through MediaGen / Core, but the plumbing is still image-shaped end-to-end. This likely wants a generalized media-asset result path.\n\nWhat currently looks architectural rather than small\n\n- Multiple simultaneous projects:\n  Current project save/load still serializes one global app state bundle (notes, chats, triggers, editors, etc.) and much of the live app state is singleton-driven.\n\n- Swarms / supervisors as first-class runtime behavior:\n  There is evidence of a newer swarm-agent data model (parent, role, docket, requires_supervisor, etc.), but live agent execution still appears to run through the older agent-definition path. This suggests the swarm model is not yet the actual runtime model.\n\n- Unified multi-agent / multi-provider control plane:\n  Claude Code / Codex-style / autocoder pieces exist, but not yet as one coherent work-control surface.\n\nImportant architectural concern noted so far\n\n- Minerva appears to have two agent models in play:\n  - the older runtime AgentDefinition / AgentSpawner path\n  - the newer SwarmAgentDefinition / autocoder-side swarm metadata path\n- Minerva project state also still looks fundamentally single-project / singleton-based.\n- This means items 6 / 7 / 8 / 9 may share one underlying product-model decision rather than being independent features.\n\nWorking summary\n\n- Likely near-term / Stage 1 friendly: 2, rehab for 3, polish pieces of 4, a narrow version of 1, and 12.\n- Strong leverage items but likely external-integration shaped: 10 and 11.\n- More likely to need explicit architecture/DCR work: 6, 7, 8, 9.\n\nPurpose of this discussion item\n\n- Capture the release-planning sweep before it fragments into many DCRs.\n- Identify which items are polish/integration, which are rehab, and which actually change Minerva's product/data model.\n- Use this thread to decide how to split follow-up DCRs and work items.","created_at":"2026-03-28T23:24:29","updated_at":"2026-03-28T23:58:54","tags":["agents","docket","graphics","kanban","planning","pose","release","stage1","swarms","video","webview"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36ec49d171659539581f49016ccb","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Project Browser, Catalog, and Quick Switch","description":"Create a v1 project-organization shell for Minerva that solves current project sprawl without requiring a concurrent multi-project runtime. The feature should provide a project browser/catalog over discovered `.minproj` files, support search/filter/sort by recency, pin/favorite/archive semantics, and enable quick-switching between projects by saving the current project and loading the selected target sequentially in the existing singleton runtime. The goal is to make many projects manageable now, while deferring true multi-project concurrent runtime to a later architecture effort. Prefer a sidecar/global project catalog for metadata first rather than mutating the `.minproj` format unless additive format changes are proven safe.","created_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:08","updated_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:08","tags":["navigation","projects","release","stage1","ux","workspace"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36ec4bfa74f29bc8f105397f6857","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Kanban as a Project-Scoped Work View over Docket","description":"Raise Minerva's kanban/work-visibility model from its current single-executable/autocoder orientation to the project abstraction already modeled by Docket. The goal is not broad new project management, but a narrow v1 work-visibility surface that can show active/planned/test work for the current project and later extend to cross-project/meta-project views. Docket remains the source of truth for work items; kanban becomes a view layer aligned to Docket's project-level abstraction.","created_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:08","updated_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:08","tags":["agents","docket","kanban","projects","release","stage1","work-visibility"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36ec4e1d78f8b07b4fc012385378","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Universal New → Item Creation Flow","description":"Replace Minerva's current hardcoded File → New submenu with an extensible \"New → Item\" creation flow for all editor-pane document types.\n\n**Current state:** The File → New submenu has 5 hardcoded entries (Text, Graphics, Spreadsheet, PCB, Record Video). Two existing editor types (Webview, Kanban) have no menu entry at all. There is no way for plugins to contribute new creatable item types.\n\n**Target state:** A creatable-items registry that the menu reads from dynamically. Built-in editor types register at startup. Plugins can register/unregister items on start/stop via manifest declaration. The menu always reflects the current set of available item types.\n\n**Scope:**\n- Only editor-pane items (not chats, notes, or terminals — those have their own panes)\n- Built-in items: Text, Graphics, Spreadsheet, PCB, Video Recorder, Webview, Kanban (7 total)\n- Plugin-contributed items: declared in manifest, appear when plugin is running, disappear when stopped\n- Single entry point (menu, shortcut, or command palette style) replaces the current submenu\n\n**Out of scope:**\n- Chat or note creation flows\n- Terminal creation\n- System-managed editor types (Logs, Activity Log, Plugin Manager, Video player, Package)","created_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:09","updated_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:03","tags":["creation","editors","navigation","release","stage1","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36ec503e751e82a58d5d76883f67","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Webview → Capture → Graphics Editor Workflow","description":"Add a first-class workflow for agent-generated or user-authored webview mockups to be captured into an image and opened directly in the graphics editor as an annotatable layer. The desired loop is: generate/update mockup in a webview panel, capture rendered output to PNG bytes, open or create a graphics editor, insert the capture as a base layer, then allow circle-and-suggest markup for further human/agent iteration. This is intended as a signature Minerva workflow for visual collaboration.","created_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:09","updated_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:09","tags":["agents","graphics","mockups","release","stage1","webview","workflow"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36ec526277278ea40f11864a0489","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Project-Scoped Video Generation via MediaGen","description":"Extend Minerva's media-generation workflow to support a narrow first video capability scoped to a project/editor workflow: start frame + end frame (+ optional prompt/controls) -> generated video asset. The change should be modeled as a MediaGen/Core boundary extension rather than provider-specific editor logic. It likely requires generalizing the current image-shaped result flow into a project-scoped media asset result path that can return video outputs and attach them cleanly to Minerva project state.","created_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:10","updated_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:10","tags":["graphics","media-gen","projects","services","stage1","video"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36ec9d0872a191d48d76fb8c8152","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implementation plan: Project browser, catalog, and quick switch","description":"Break the project-organization DCR into an executable vertical slice: project discovery/catalog source, metadata storage strategy (prefer sidecar/global catalog first), browser UI, search/filter/sort, pin/archive, launch/open flow, and sequential quick-switch integration with existing save/load behavior. Include a decision on whether work indicators from Docket/agents appear in v1 of the browser or land later.","created_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:29","updated_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:29","tags":["implementation","navigation","projects","stage1","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019d36ec49d171659539581f49016ccb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36ec9f2076aa9bdbd201df6b85fe","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implementation plan: Project-scoped kanban work visibility","description":"Define the minimum viable kanban changes needed to align Minerva's work-visibility surface with Docket's project abstraction. Cover what data is read from Docket, what statuses/lanes are shown in v1, whether the first view is current-project-only or also browser-integrated, and how active agent work should appear without trying to solve full cross-project orchestration.","created_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:30","updated_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:30","tags":["agents","docket","implementation","kanban","stage1"],"parent":"minerva:019d36ec4bfa74f29bc8f105397f6857"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36eca13b72dbb6937c32b1a46bb6","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implementation plan: Universal New flow","description":"Map existing creation entry points for chats, notes, graphics editors, threads, and other major item types, then define a unified `New -> Item` launcher surface and migration plan. The work item should identify required command/menu integration, item taxonomy, and how the new flow coexists with existing context-specific shortcuts during transition.","created_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:30","updated_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:30","tags":["creation","editors","implementation","stage1","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019d36ec4e1d78f8b07b4fc012385378"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36eca35673d29da2f581af019379","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implementation plan: Webview capture to graphics editor","description":"Define the end-to-end vertical slice for the mockup annotation workflow: webview panel selection, rendered capture to PNG bytes, open/create graphics editor, insert captured image as a layer, and preserve enough provenance for human/agent follow-up. Include a decision on whether the first slice uses existing screenshot infrastructure or a dedicated webview capture path.","created_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:31","updated_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:31","tags":["graphics","implementation","mockups","stage1","webview","workflow"],"parent":"minerva:019d36ec503e751e82a58d5d76883f67"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d36eca55876e89a893945f4241a60","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implementation plan: Narrow video generation through MediaGen","description":"Define the smallest end-to-end video generation slice that crosses the Minerva/MediaGen boundary: request shape for start-frame + end-frame (+ optional prompt/controls), service/API changes, result delivery as a project-scoped media asset, and first UI entry point from the graphics editor or related media workflow. Explicitly keep provider choice abstract at the Minerva UI layer.","created_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:31","updated_at":"2026-03-29T00:09:31","tags":["graphics","implementation","media-gen","services","stage1","video"],"parent":"minerva:019d36ec526277278ea40f11864a0489"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d375e98a073ea9c12783f239a0ca2","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Metaprojects: project-of-projects coordination and master views","description":"Introduce a `metaproject` concept for Minerva, backed by a lightweight `.minmeta` file, to represent a coordinated set of child projects without requiring a concurrent multi-project runtime inside one Minerva instance. A metaproject acts as a project-of-projects coordination layer: it can have its own chat/supervisor surface, and it presents master/rollup views such as a child-project tree or outline, project summaries/status, and overview-oriented Docket/Kanban surfaces. Child projects remain the real work cells with their own notes, chats, editors, assets, and project-local work. The metaproject should emphasize overview, routing, and aggregation rather than absorbing child internals. For v1, keep the model narrow: a `.minmeta` primarily stores identity/metadata plus the ordered or grouped list of child projects and basic view configuration; master Docket/Kanban should be rollup/read-heavy first rather than a separate source of truth; avoid nested metaprojects initially unless explicitly justified later. This DCR is intended to sharpen and possibly replace the earlier broad project-browser/workspace-shell framing with a more concrete 'project of projects' model.","created_at":"2026-03-29T02:13:59","updated_at":"2026-03-29T02:13:59","tags":["agents","coordination","docket","kanban","metaprojects","projects","stage1","workspace"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d3767fdc473338a45ed7d97aee0cf","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Plan: Universal New → Item Creation Flow","description":"Implementation plan for DCR \"Universal New → Item Creation Flow\". Parent work item for all implementation tasks.\n\nApproach:\n1. Create a CreatableItemRegistry that holds item definitions (name, icon, callback/type)\n2. Register built-in editor types at startup\n3. Wire plugin manifest to register/unregister items on plugin start/stop\n4. Replace the hardcoded File → New submenu with a dynamic menu reading from the registry\n5. Add keyboard shortcut (Ctrl+N) to open the creation flow","created_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:15","updated_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:15","tags":["editors","implementation","stage1","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019d36ec4e1d78f8b07b4fc012385378"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d37681f5c73ec8faa5e069ee80c6d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Create CreatableItemRegistry class","description":"New script: CreatableItemRegistry.gd (RefCounted or Node). Holds an ordered dictionary of creatable items, each with: id (String), display_name (String), icon (String or Texture2D), category (String, e.g. \"builtin\" or \"plugin\"), source_plugin_id (String, empty for built-ins), and a create callback (Callable). Provides: register_item(), unregister_item(), unregister_by_source(plugin_id), get_all_items() -> Array. Emits signal items_changed when the list changes so the menu can rebuild. Attach to SingletonObject so it's globally accessible.","created_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:23","updated_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:23","tags":["editors","registry","stage1"],"parent":"minerva:019d3767fdc473338a45ed7d97aee0cf"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d37682f4c787db2e0d7914a029665","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Register 7 built-in editor types at startup","description":"In SingletonObject._ready() or MinervaMCPServer init, register the 7 built-in creatable items in the registry: Text, Graphics, Spreadsheet, PCB, Video Recorder, Webview, Kanban. Each entry's create callback should call the existing creation logic (EditorPane.add(Editor.Type.X) or the video recorder overlay flow). This replaces the hardcoded handle_new_* functions in menuMain.gd as the source of truth.","created_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:27","updated_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:27","tags":["editors","stage1"],"parent":"minerva:019d3767fdc473338a45ed7d97aee0cf"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d37683fc776e99e031921d674c01b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Replace hardcoded File → New submenu with dynamic menu","description":"Rewrite the File → New submenu in menuMain.gd to read from CreatableItemRegistry.get_all_items() instead of hardcoded add_item calls. Connect to items_changed signal to rebuild the submenu when items are added/removed (plugin start/stop). Remove the individual handle_new_file(), handle_new_graphics(), etc. functions and the index-matched _on_file_submenu_index_pressed() — replace with a single handler that looks up the item by id and calls its create callback.","created_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:32","updated_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:32","tags":["menu","stage1","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019d3767fdc473338a45ed7d97aee0cf"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d376850517186acfdea6bec9d8608","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Plugin manifest: editor_items declaration and lifecycle wiring","description":"Extend the plugin manifest schema to support an optional \"editor_items\" array, e.g.:\n```json\n\"editor_items\": [\n  {\"id\": \"email_inbox\", \"name\": \"Email Inbox\", \"panel\": \"email_inbox_panel\"}\n]\n```\nWhen a plugin starts, register its editor_items in CreatableItemRegistry (source_plugin_id = plugin.id). The create callback should open a plugin webview panel for the declared panel name. When a plugin stops, call unregister_by_source(plugin.id) to remove its items. Update PluginDefinition to parse the new field. Update PluginManager start/stop to call register/unregister.","created_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:36","updated_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:36","tags":["extensibility","plugins","stage1"],"parent":"minerva:019d3767fdc473338a45ed7d97aee0cf"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d37685df77424912134013efa30b7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add Ctrl+N shortcut for New Item flow","description":"Add a keyboard shortcut (Ctrl+N / Cmd+N) that opens the New Item creation flow. If there are ≤ 7 items, a simple popup menu at cursor is fine. If plugins push the count higher, consider a filterable command-palette style picker. For v1, popup menu is sufficient.","created_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:39","updated_at":"2026-03-29T02:24:39","tags":["shortcuts","stage1","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019d3767fdc473338a45ed7d97aee0cf"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d376b632476ec9b52bb07b1871b66","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Consolidate editor toolbar: single New button with type picker popup","description":"In MainScene.tscn, the editor pane toolbar (MiddlePane/VBoxContainer/HBoxContainer) has individual buttons: AddFileEditor, AddGraphicsEditor, AddSpreadsheetEditor, AddPCBEditor. The top toolbar (VBoxRoot/HBoxContainer2/HBoxContainer) also has AddFileEditor and AddGraphicsEditor.\n\nChange: Keep the first AddFileEditor button in the editor pane toolbar, repurpose it as a generic \"New\" button (update tooltip to \"Create new editor tab\", keep the + icon). On press, show a PopupMenu populated from CreatableItemRegistry.get_all_items(). Remove AddGraphicsEditor, AddSpreadsheetEditor, AddPCBEditor from the editor pane toolbar. Handle the same for the top toolbar — either remove those buttons or consolidate to a single New button there too.\n\nRemove the individual _on_add_*_editor_pressed() handlers from vboxEditor.gd — replace with a single handler that reads the selected item from the popup and calls its create callback.\n\nConnected signals to update in MainScene.tscn:\n- Lines 862-870: remove individual pressed connections, add single connection for the new button","created_at":"2026-03-29T02:27:57","updated_at":"2026-03-29T02:27:57","tags":["editors","stage1","toolbar","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019d3767fdc473338a45ed7d97aee0cf"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d40b800d87c058b94239ad069a0d4","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Docket-as-supervisor: gated workflows, policy enforcement, and agent memory","description":"Discussion thread exploring the integration of Docket into Minerva as a core component (not just external MCP) to serve as the backbone for supervisor/master agent coordination.\n\nThree interlocking themes:\n\n## 1. Docket as supervisor coordination layer\nInstead of inventing a new agent coordination model, use Docket's existing data model (work items, state machines, parent/child, comments) as the shared state between supervisor and worker agents. The supervisor reasons over docket items to decompose work, assign it, track progress, and react to blockers. Kanban/visibility comes free as a projection of docket state.\n\n## 2. Gated workflows with policy enforcement\nAllow workflows to have gates — stages where an agent cannot proceed until conditions are met (tests pass, validation agent approves, human signs off). Key design constraints:\n\n- **Same workflow, gated or ungated:** Gating is a policy overlay on state transitions, not a hardcoded workflow step. Policy determines when gates apply (e.g., gated for production, ungated for sandbox; gated first time, ungated after pattern proven).\n- **Tool surface as enforcement:** The active docket state determines which tools are available to the working agent. Release tools don't exist in the agent's tool surface until gates clear. This is hard enforcement — not prompt-based.\n- **Agent never self-certifies:** Gates are executed by Minerva (test harness, separate validation agent), not by the working agent. Results are recorded by Minerva, not the agent.\n- **Bypass detection:** Blocked attempts logged to Activity Log, recorded as docket events, surfaced as human notifications.\n- **Terminal command intercept:** Extends CodeTools policy.json pattern to block actions (git push, deploy) that would bypass workflow state.\n\n## 3. Agent memory, knowledge bases, and skills\nDocket items (or new entity types) as persistence layer for agent-accumulated knowledge — not just work tracking but \"what did I learn, what do I know how to do.\" Structured, queryable, human-visible/editable memory that survives across sessions.\n\n## Policy access control model (working theory)\n- Agents can author/propose policy, only humans can activate it\n- Separation of write and activate (like PRs — propose, then merge)\n- Bypasses are scoped, auditable, human-approved policy objects\n- Enforcement lives in the state machine engine, not in agent prompts\n- Concern driving this: LLMs will disable policy to hit goals if given the ability\n\n## Concrete example explored\nMovie maker project with Coding → Testing → Release flow:\n- In \"coding\": agent has file/terminal tools, no push/deploy\n- In \"testing\": Minerva runs tests and spawns separate validation agent; working agent loses control of gate\n- In \"release\": only reachable when Minerva-recorded gate conditions pass; release tools now available\n- Bypass attempts (skip tests, fake results, skip docket, modify tests) all blocked by tool surface restriction + policy intercept\n\n## Open questions\n- Does docket become a GDScript-native component or remain a privileged service with tighter integration?\n- Where does policy live — per-project, per-metaproject, per-agent-role?\n- How does this connect to the metaproject concept (DCR 019d375e98a073ea9c12783f239a0ca2)?\n- What entity types does docket need for agent memory/KB beyond current work item types?\n- How do gated/ungated modes get selected — explicit human toggle, automatic by context, or policy rule?","created_at":"2026-03-30T21:48:13","updated_at":"2026-04-03T21:57:00","tags":["agents","docket","gating","memory","policy","stage1","supervisor","workflow"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d44e6a6b77cc0b0bab79a29d77760","type":"discussion","status":"resolved","title":"Token-efficient & LLM-ergonomic browser automation for CoBrowser","description":"## Context\n\nCoBrowser (HumanWeb) is Minerva's browser automation layer, exposed to LLMs via MCP tools. Current design works but has several pain points that waste tokens and limit multi-agent use. Telescope-MCP (github.com/BrenanL/telescope-mcp) offers proven patterns worth adapting.\n\n## Goals\n\n### 1. Token efficiency — progressive zoom instead of DOM dumps\nCurrent `cobrowser_get_page_info` returns up to 100KB of cleaned HTML (~25K tokens) every call. Telescope's 5-level progressive approach cuts this dramatically:\n- **Level 0 — identity**: URL, title, meta (~50 tokens)\n- **Level 1 — structure**: headings, landmarks with char counts, element counts, scroll pages (~200-600 tokens)\n- **Level 2 — section(selector, maxChars)**: scoped text + links + inputs for one region\n- **Level 3 — extract(itemSelector, fieldMap)**: declarative structured extraction for repeating elements (search results, tables, lists)\n- **Level 4 — html(selector)**: raw HTML as last resort\n\nAlso: dry-run cost preview, per-session token budgets, landmark-aware navigation.\n\n### 2. Avoid re-query loops and retries\nLLMs using cobrowser frequently loop: query → selector not found → retry with different selector → still wrong → etc. Root causes:\n- No structural overview to guide selector choice (fixed by progressive zoom)\n- No cached/stable element references between calls\n- No site-knowledge hints (Telescope pre-loads domain-specific selector facts)\n- Consider: element handles or stable IDs that persist across calls, so the LLM doesn't have to re-find elements\n- Consider: better error messages that suggest alternatives (\"selector not found, but similar elements exist: ...\")\n\n### 3. Multi-tab / multi-agent support\nCurrently cobrowser operates on a single active tab. Need:\n- Tab creation, listing, and switching via MCP tools\n- Tab IDs that agents/subagents can claim and operate on independently\n- Per-tab token budgets to prevent runaway subagents\n- Simultaneous rendering (avoid tab-activation conflicts between agents)\n- Session isolation so one agent's navigation doesn't break another's context\n\n### 4. System file dialogs (choose file / upload / download)\nThe OS-native file chooser dialog is outside browser DOM — cobrowser can't see or interact with it. Pain points:\n- ~/Downloads is large and causes multi-minute delays just opening the dialog\n- No way to pre-set the file path before the dialog opens\n- No way to detect that a file dialog is open\n\nPossible approaches:\n- Intercept the file input and set its value programmatically (bypassing the dialog entirely)\n- Use xdotool/ydotool to automate the GTK file chooser\n- Pre-stage files in a small known directory to avoid slow directory listing\n- Monitor for dialog windows via wmctrl/xdg and automate them outside the browser\n\n## Reference\n- Telescope-MCP: github.com/BrenanL/telescope-mcp (progressive zoom, budgets, extract, site knowledge)\n- HumanWeb source: ~/github/HumanWeb/\n- Current MCP tools: cobrowser_navigate, cobrowser_click, cobrowser_type, cobrowser_read, cobrowser_query_all, cobrowser_get_page_info, cobrowser_screenshot, cobrowser_request_human","created_at":"2026-03-31T17:17:39","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:34:37","priority":2,"tags":["browser-automation","cobrowser","humanweb","multi-agent","token-efficiency"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d450e4abe72619260780462f2022c","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"CoBrowser convergence program: phased browser-use ergonomics improvements","description":"Create a phased DCR program to improve CoBrowser convergence for one-off browser tasks inside a real, stateful Firefox session.\n\n## Problem\nCurrent CoBrowser works, but common tasks converge too slowly because agents repeatedly:\n- fetch broad page state,\n- guess selectors,\n- retry after failure,\n- and re-orient after every page change.\n\nThe result is too many tool calls, too many tokens, and poor parallelism.\n\n## Design target\nMake browser tasks converge faster without turning HumanWeb into a general computer-use system.\n\n## Non-goals\n- full desktop/computer-use agent architecture\n- replayability as a primary product goal\n- replacing Firefox-managed auth/session state\n- large Minerva runtime changes as part of phase 1\n\n## Measurement framework\nEach child DCR should measure against the same representative eval set of real tasks, such as:\n- Amazon product/cart lookup\n- Google Flights search\n- repeated-result search page\n- form fill flow\n- modal/popup flow\n- upload/download flow\n\nEach child DCR should define baseline and post-change results for:\n- completion_rate\n- median_tool_calls\n- median_browser_tokens\n- retry_rate\n- time_to_completion\n- orientation_cost\n\n## Phase sequence\n1. Compact page structure / cheap orientation\n2. Scoped page section inspection\n3. Informed failure responses / retry reduction\n4. Ephemeral refs + page epoch invalidation\n5. Tab-targeted routing + claim semantics\n6. High-level upload/download flows\n\n## Decision rule\nEach phase is independently judged. If a phase misses its success gates or causes unacceptable regressions, stop, classify the failure, and either revise that phase or replace it with a narrower follow-up DCR instead of expanding scope.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:00:57","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:34:39","priority":1,"tags":["browser-automation","cobrowser","convergence","dcr","humanweb"],"parent":"minerva:019d44e6a6b77cc0b0bab79a29d77760"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d450efd8b762dacb6679a7ab37a67","type":"dcr","status":"designing","title":"Phase 1: Compact page structure for cheap orientation","description":"Replace whole-page state as the default orientation path with a compact, action-oriented page structure representation.\n\n## Why\nToday the agent often starts by reading too much of the page, then guessing selectors. The first committed bet is that a compact structure view will reduce orientation cost and improve convergence without changing core action tools.\n\n## Scope\nImplement a new structure-oriented inspection path in HumanWeb that returns:\n- major regions / landmarks\n- visible headings\n- forms / search boxes\n- prominent buttons / links\n- repeated regions\n- small snippets for disambiguation\n- precomputed selectors or equivalent targeting hints\n\nKeep legacy tools available for compatibility.\n\n## Out of scope\n- refs\n- tab claim semantics\n- upload/download redesign\n- packaging\n\n## Measure\nRun a fixed eval set before and after.\nTrack:\n- completion_rate\n- median_tool_calls\n- median_browser_tokens\n- orientation_cost\n- retry_rate\n\n## Success gates\n- no meaningful drop in completion_rate\n- orientation_cost reduced materially\n- median_tool_calls reduced materially on representative tasks\n- retry_rate reduced on search/results/form tasks\n\n## Failure criteria\nThis phase fails if it does not reduce orientation cost enough to justify the extra complexity, or if the structure output is too abstract/ambiguous and causes more agent confusion.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:01:43","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:51:05","priority":1,"tags":["browser-automation","cobrowser","convergence","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d450e4abe72619260780462f2022c"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d450f00337b8282446341e3a6274f","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Phase 2: Scoped page section inspection","description":"Add scoped section inspection so agents can zoom into one region instead of re-reading broad page state.\n\n## Why\nIf phase 1 provides cheap orientation, the next committed step is to make detailed reading local and selective. The agent should inspect only one promising region before acting.\n\n## Scope\nAdd a section-oriented inspection tool/path that:\n- accepts a selector or region target\n- returns scoped text/content\n- returns scoped interactive elements and links\n- supports truncation / maxChars\n- can serve as the preferred replacement for broad query/read patterns when context is regional\n\n## Out of scope\n- declarative refs across steps beyond what the section call itself returns\n- tab routing\n- upload/download redesign\n\n## Measure\nUsing the same eval set, compare:\n- median_tool_calls\n- median_browser_tokens\n- retry_rate\n- time_to_completion\n\nAlso note whether agents choose better follow-up actions after structure -> section workflows.\n\n## Success gates\n- no meaningful drop in completion_rate\n- lower token use on multi-step pages\n- fewer broad re-reads of the same page\n- lower retry_rate on repeated-result and form tasks\n\n## Failure criteria\nThis phase fails if section outputs are too noisy, too hard for the agent to target correctly, or if they do not reduce broad-page rereading in practice.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:01:44","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:51:19","priority":1,"tags":["browser-automation","cobrowser","convergence","phase-2"],"parent":"minerva:019d450e4abe72619260780462f2022c"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d450f02d1773e8dcb5042751992f9","type":"dcr","status":"designing","title":"Phase 3: Informed failure responses and retry reduction","description":"Improve action failure responses so selector/ref failures become cheap recovery moments instead of blind retries.\n\n## Why\nA large share of wasted steps comes from failed actions returning too little information. The committed bet is that better error payloads will reduce retry loops immediately.\n\n## Scope\nWhen an action target fails, return structured recovery hints such as:\n- why the target failed\n- whether zero, many, or stale matches were found\n- nearby candidate elements with distinguishing text/labels/selectors\n- page-changed / invalidated hints where available\n\n## Out of scope\n- full ref system design beyond what is needed to explain failures\n- multi-tab routing\n- packaging\n\n## Measure\nTrack across the eval set:\n- retry_rate\n- median_tool_calls\n- time_to_completion\n- completion_rate on tasks with dynamic elements or ambiguous selectors\n\n## Success gates\n- large reduction in repeated selector retries\n- better completion on tasks with ambiguous UI\n- no added confusion from overly verbose failure payloads\n\n## Failure criteria\nThis phase fails if failure payloads are too large, too noisy, or do not materially improve recovery behavior.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:01:44","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:51:03","priority":1,"tags":["browser-automation","cobrowser","convergence","phase-3"],"parent":"minerva:019d450e4abe72619260780462f2022c"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d450f056f7a9faeb9107cac7ff950","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Phase 4: Ephemeral refs and page-epoch invalidation","description":"Add short-lived reusable references so agents can act on recently discovered elements without re-finding them from scratch.\n\n## Why\nAfter orientation and scoped inspection improve, the next bottleneck is re-finding the same element repeatedly across nearby calls. The committed bet is that ephemeral refs will cut re-querying without introducing heavy replay infrastructure.\n\n## Scope\n- return short-lived refs with structure/section/action results\n- allow action tools to accept refs as targets\n- define a simple page-epoch or invalidation model\n- return clear stale-ref errors when the page has changed\n\n## Out of scope\n- durable replay handles\n- cross-navigation persistence\n- broad state synchronization beyond one tab/page epoch\n\n## Measure\nTrack:\n- repeated-query count\n- median_tool_calls\n- retry_rate\n- stale-ref failure rate\n- completion_rate on tasks with nearby repeated actions\n\n## Success gates\n- fewer repeated DOM lookups for recently discovered elements\n- lower tool-call counts on multi-step interactions within one page state\n- stale-ref behavior is understandable and recoverable\n\n## Failure criteria\nThis phase fails if refs are too fragile, too hard for the agent to use correctly, or create more invalidation churn than they save.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:01:45","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:01:45","priority":2,"tags":["browser-automation","cobrowser","convergence","phase-4"],"parent":"minerva:019d450e4abe72619260780462f2022c"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d450f080a7972a95b81a4fdaa1aff","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Phase 5: Tab-targeted routing and claim semantics","description":"Make browser commands explicitly tab-targeted so multiple agents can use the same Firefox instance without interfering.\n\n## Why\nToday much of HumanWeb still behaves like an active-tab system. The committed bet is that explicit tab routing and lightweight claim semantics are enough to unlock useful parallelism.\n\n## Scope\n- add optional tab_id to browser commands\n- route commands to that tab rather than always using the active tab\n- add tab list/new/close support as needed\n- add lightweight claim/release semantics for conflict prevention\n- preserve backward compatibility when tab_id is omitted\n\n## Out of scope\n- full orchestration policy\n- browser fleet/cloud scaling\n- packaging work\n\n## Measure\nTrack:\n- parallel_conflict_rate\n- completion_rate for two independent concurrent tasks\n- median_tool_calls per concurrent task\n- any Firefox hidden-tab or rendering limitations encountered\n\n## Success gates\n- two agents can complete independent tasks without active-tab interference\n- claim semantics are understandable and low-friction\n- backward compatibility remains acceptable for single-agent flows\n\n## Failure criteria\nThis phase fails if Firefox hidden-tab behavior prevents reliable concurrent use, or if claim/routing complexity outweighs the practical parallelism gain.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:01:46","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:34:49","priority":1,"tags":["browser-automation","cobrowser","parallelism","phase-5"],"parent":"minerva:019d450e4abe72619260780462f2022c"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d450f0aa67817b5c0ded2f956646f","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Phase 6: High-level upload/download flows with browser-first fallbacks","description":"Add high-level upload/download browser tools so common file flows do not require brittle dialog choreography.\n\n## Why\nSome important browser tasks stall at file chooser or download handling boundaries. The committed bet is that a browser-first high-level tool surface can solve the common cases cleanly.\n\n## Scope\n- add a high-level upload_file flow\n- add a high-level download_file flow\n- prefer DOM/browser-native methods first\n- use native/system fallback only when browser automation truly cannot complete the flow\n\n## Out of scope\n- generic computer-use platform\n- broad native desktop automation redesign\n\n## Measure\nTrack on upload/download tasks:\n- completion_rate\n- time_to_completion\n- human-intervention rate\n- fallback rate\n\n## Success gates\n- common upload/download tasks complete reliably without manual dialog handling\n- native fallback remains rare rather than normal\n- tool surface is simpler for the agent than current manual workflows\n\n## Failure criteria\nThis phase fails if browser-first methods are too site-specific or unreliable, or if native fallback becomes the common path instead of the exception.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:01:46","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:01:46","priority":2,"tags":["browser-automation","cobrowser","phase-6","uploads"],"parent":"minerva:019d450e4abe72619260780462f2022c"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451805df70e196b4f9570347c41e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Define eval set and capture baseline metrics","description":"**Prerequisite for all phases.** Define the shared eval set and measure current CoBrowser performance as the baseline.\n\n## Eval tasks (6 representative browser scenarios)\n1. **Amazon product lookup** — search, navigate to product, read price/title\n2. **Google Flights search** — enter origin/dest/dates, read results\n3. **Search results page** — Google/DuckDuckGo search, extract top 5 results with titles+URLs\n4. **Form fill** — multi-field form (e.g. contact form or signup), fill and submit\n5. **Modal/popup flow** — site with cookie banner or modal, dismiss and proceed\n6. **Upload/download** — upload a file to a file-sharing site or download a link\n\n## Metrics to capture per task\n- `completion_rate` — did the task succeed? (binary)\n- `tool_calls` — total MCP tool calls\n- `browser_tokens` — tokens consumed by browser tool responses\n- `retry_count` — selector-not-found retries\n- `orientation_cost` — tokens spent on page-info/state calls specifically\n- `time_to_completion` — wall clock seconds\n- `human_intervention` — did the agent request human help?\n\n## Deliverable\nA script or notebook that runs each eval task against current CoBrowser, logs all MCP calls, and produces a summary table. This becomes the baseline for all phase measurements.\n\n## Where\n~/github/HumanWeb/ — add an `eval/` directory with task definitions and runner.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:11:35","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:10","assigned_to":"opus","priority":1,"tags":["baseline","cobrowser","eval","opus"],"parent":"minerva:019d450e4abe72619260780462f2022c"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451847de7a8191ae67d01cee3016","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Design structure output format and token budget","description":"Design the exact JSON shape for `captureIdentity()` and `captureStructure()` responses. Must hit token targets.\n\n## Identity (~50 tokens)\n```json\n{\n  \"url\": \"https://...\",\n  \"title\": \"Page Title\",\n  \"meta_description\": \"...\",\n  \"page_type\": \"search_results|article|form|login|product|listing|unknown\",\n  \"security\": {\"has_password\": false, \"has_captcha\": false, \"is_login\": false}\n}\n```\n\n## Structure (~200-800 tokens)\n```json\n{\n  \"landmarks\": [\n    {\"tag\": \"nav\", \"label\": \"Main navigation\", \"chars\": 1200, \"children\": 8, \"selector\": \"nav.main-nav\"}\n  ],\n  \"headings\": [\n    {\"level\": 1, \"text\": \"Search Results for...\", \"selector\": \"h1\"}\n  ],\n  \"actions\": [\n    {\"type\": \"search\", \"text\": \"Search\", \"selector\": \"input#search-box\"},\n    {\"type\": \"button\", \"text\": \"Next Page\", \"selector\": \"a.pagination-next\"}\n  ],\n  \"counts\": {\"links\": 42, \"buttons\": 8, \"inputs\": 3, \"forms\": 1, \"images\": 12},\n  \"scroll_pages\": 3,\n  \"repeated_regions\": [\n    {\"selector\": \"div.search-result\", \"count\": 10, \"sample_text\": \"Product Name - $29.99\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Decisions needed\n- How to detect `page_type` heuristically (URL patterns, form presence, heading text?)\n- How to identify \"top actions\" vs all actions (visibility + prominence heuristic?)\n- Max items per category (landmarks, headings, actions, repeated_regions)\n- Truncation limits for text fields (80 chars for headings, 60 for actions?)\n\n## Deliverable\nWritten spec with example outputs for each of the 6 eval tasks. Validate token counts by running through a tokenizer.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:11:52","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:42:57","assigned_to":"opus","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","design","opus","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d450efd8b762dacb6679a7ab37a67"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d45185f7b723f8e5716905c8e56e7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Implement captureIdentity() in state-capturer.js","description":"Add `captureIdentity()` method to StateCapturer class in `src/extension-cobrowser/content/state-capturer.js`.\n\n## Implementation\n- Extract: URL, title, meta description, canonical URL, lang\n- Detect page_type heuristically: check URL patterns, presence of search inputs, login forms, product schema markup, repeated card/list structures\n- Include security flags (reuse existing `getSecurityInfo()` logic)\n- Target: ~50 tokens output\n\n## Message handling\nAdd `command.getIdentity` case in `content.js` message handler that calls `stateCapturer.captureIdentity()`.\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/state-capturer.js` — new method\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/content.js` — new message case","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:11:58","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:42:59","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","implementation","phase-1","sonnet"],"parent":"minerva:019d450efd8b762dacb6679a7ab37a67"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451885b27f4cb42a6d6509352c89","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Implement captureStructure() in state-capturer.js","description":"Add `captureStructure()` method to StateCapturer class. This is the core of Phase 1.\n\n## Implementation\n- **Landmarks**: Query `main, nav, aside, header, footer, section[aria-label], [role=\"main\"], [role=\"navigation\"], [role=\"complementary\"], [role=\"banner\"], [role=\"contentinfo\"]`. For each: tag, aria-label or computed label, character count, child count, selector.\n- **Headings**: All h1-h6 visible on page. Tag level, first 80 chars of text, precomputed selector.\n- **Top actions**: Visible buttons, submit inputs, search inputs, prominent links (in nav or with button-like styling). For each: type, text (60 chars), selector. Limit to ~15 most prominent.\n- **Counts**: Total links, buttons, inputs, forms, images, tables, selects.\n- **Scroll depth**: `Math.ceil(document.documentElement.scrollHeight / window.innerHeight)` pages.\n- **Repeated regions**: Detect groups of 3+ sibling elements with same tag+class pattern. Report selector, count, sample text from first child.\n\n## Visibility filtering\nOnly include visible elements. Reuse existing visibility checks from action-executor.js or add a shared `isVisible(el)` utility.\n\n## Selector generation\nFor each element, generate a stable selector: prefer `#id` > `tag.first-class` > positional path. Keep selectors short.\n\n## Target: 200-800 tokens depending on page complexity.\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/state-capturer.js` — new method\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/content.js` — new `command.getStructure` message case","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:12:08","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:43:01","assigned_to":"opus","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","implementation","opus","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d450efd8b762dacb6679a7ab37a67"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4518b7737b9ba94cf0ab509bb8f9","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add MCP tools: cobrowser_page_identity and cobrowser_page_structure","description":"Register new MCP tools in `cobrowser_mcp_server.py` that call the new content script methods.\n\n## Tools to add\n\n### cobrowser_page_identity\n- Command: `command.getIdentity`\n- No parameters\n- Description (search-optimized): \"Get basic page identity: URL, title, description, page type (search/article/form/login/product/listing), security flags. Cheapest orientation tool — use first after navigation. Returns ~50 tokens.\"\n\n### cobrowser_page_structure\n- Command: `command.getStructure`\n- No parameters\n- Description (search-optimized): \"Get page structure overview: headings, landmarks, navigation regions, buttons, links, forms, inputs, actionable elements, repeated content patterns, scroll depth. Returns compact overview with CSS selectors for each element. Use after navigation to understand page layout and find what to interact with. Returns 200-800 tokens.\"\n\n## Backwards compatibility\n- Keep `cobrowser_get_page_info` as an alias that calls `getIdentity` + `getStructure` combined and formats the result to match the old response shape\n- Add \"[DEPRECATED] \" prefix to old tool's description: \"Use cobrowser_page_identity + cobrowser_page_structure instead.\"\n\n## Response formatting\nFormat results as structured text, not raw JSON dumps. Example:\n```\nPage: Google Flights - Search Results\nType: search_results | URL: https://flights.google.com/...\n\nLandmarks:\n  [nav] Main navigation (1.2K chars) → nav.WXaAwc\n  [main] Flight results (45K chars) → main#main-content\n\nTop actions:\n  [search] \"Search flights\" → input.DjdbYe\n  [button] \"Sort by price\" → button.VfPpkd\n  ...\n```\n\n## Files\n- `src/Library/cobrowser_mcp_server.py` — new tool registrations + handlers","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:12:20","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:43:20","assigned_to":"haiku","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","haiku","implementation","mcp","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d450efd8b762dacb6679a7ab37a67"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4518d10b7e2f9af8f0bf396b354b","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"Phase 1 validation: measure orientation cost and convergence","description":"Run the eval set with the new identity+structure tools and compare against baseline.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:12:27","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:18","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","phase-1","sonnet","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019d450efd8b762dacb6679a7ab37a67","component":"cobrowser"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451911ef7f368dab23765d1dd6f0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Implement fuzzy element suggestions on selector failure","description":"When `findElement` fails in action-executor.js, search for partial matches and return structured suggestions instead of a bare error.\n\n## Implementation\nIn `findElementWithRetry()` failure path (after all retry attempts exhausted):\n\n1. **Text-based fuzzy match**: If the failed selector contained text or an ID/class name, search all visible elements for partial text matches. Use `element.textContent.toLowerCase().includes(term)` for simplicity.\n2. **Tag-based fallback**: If the selector targeted a specific tag (e.g. `button#submit`), find all visible elements of that tag type.\n3. **Partial class match**: If selector had a class, find elements sharing any of those classes.\n4. **Score and rank**: Prefer elements with more matching attributes. Return top 3.\n\n## Response format\n```json\n{\n  \"success\": false,\n  \"error\": \"Selector '#submit-btn' not found\",\n  \"match_count\": 0,\n  \"suggestions\": [\n    {\"selector\": \"button.submit-form\", \"text\": \"Submit Order\", \"tag\": \"button\", \"visible\": true},\n    {\"selector\": \"#submitButton\", \"text\": \"Submit\", \"tag\": \"button\", \"visible\": true},\n    {\"selector\": \"input[type='submit']\", \"text\": \"Go\", \"tag\": \"input\", \"visible\": true}\n  ],\n  \"hint\": \"3 similar elements found. Try one of the suggested selectors.\"\n}\n```\n\nAlso handle the multi-match case:\n```json\n{\n  \"success\": false,\n  \"error\": \"Selector 'button.btn' matched 12 elements — ambiguous\",\n  \"match_count\": 12,\n  \"suggestions\": [\n    {\"selector\": \"button.btn.btn-primary\", \"text\": \"Submit\", \"index\": 0},\n    {\"selector\": \"button.btn.btn-secondary\", \"text\": \"Cancel\", \"index\": 1}\n  ],\n  \"hint\": \"Multiple matches. Use a more specific selector or add distinguishing text.\"\n}\n```\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/action-executor.js` — modify `findElementWithRetry()` and `findElement()` failure paths","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:12:44","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:50:14","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","implementation","phase-3","sonnet"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f02d1773e8dcb5042751992f9"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d45192f247c998fd4a4701c4ab867","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Propagate structured errors through service and MCP server","description":"Ensure the fuzzy suggestions from action-executor.js flow through to the LLM without being swallowed or reformatted into a generic error string.\n\n## Changes\n1. **cobrowser_service.py**: Pass through the full result dict (including `suggestions` array) in command responses. Currently the service may only extract `success` and `error` fields — verify and fix.\n2. **cobrowser_mcp_server.py**: In `format_result()` and per-tool handlers, detect when result contains `suggestions` and format them for the LLM. Don't just dump JSON — format as readable text:\n```\nError: Selector '#submit-btn' not found.\n\nSimilar elements found:\n  [1] button.submit-form — \"Submit Order\"\n  [2] #submitButton — \"Submit\"\n  [3] input[type='submit'] — \"Go\"\n\nTry one of these selectors instead.\n```\n\n## Files\n- `src/Library/cobrowser_service.py` — verify suggestions pass through\n- `src/Library/cobrowser_mcp_server.py` — format suggestions in error responses","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:12:51","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:50:15","assigned_to":"haiku","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","haiku","implementation","phase-3"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f02d1773e8dcb5042751992f9"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451943ec73dc876d64b3241b0747","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"Phase 3 validation: measure retry reduction","description":"Run the eval set with informed failure responses and compare retry_rate against baseline.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:12:56","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:26","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","phase-3","sonnet","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f02d1773e8dcb5042751992f9","component":"cobrowser"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451977d7774b8d0bd54a0b3ca2c8","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add tab_id routing in background script","description":"Modify the background script to route commands to specific tabs instead of always targeting the active tab.\n\n## Changes to websocket-manager.js\n\n1. **Command routing**: When a command message includes `tab_id`, use `browser.tabs.sendMessage(tabId, msg)` instead of sending to the active tab. When `tab_id` is omitted, fall back to active tab (backwards compatible).\n\n2. **Tab management commands**: Handle new message types:\n   - `tab.list` → `browser.tabs.query({})` → return `[{tab_id, url, title, status, active}]`\n   - `tab.new` → `browser.tabs.create({url})` → return `{tab_id, url}`\n   - `tab.close` → `browser.tabs.remove(tabId)` → return `{success: true}`\n\n3. **Claim tracking**: Maintain a `Map<tab_id, agent_id>` for claim/release:\n   - `tab.claim` → check unclaimed, set owner → return `{success: true}`\n   - `tab.release` → clear owner → return `{success: true}`\n   - On command routing: if tab is claimed by a different agent_id, reject with error\n\n4. **Content script readiness**: Before routing to a tab, verify the content script is injected. If not (new tab, navigated to restricted page), return a clear error rather than timing out.\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/background/websocket-manager.js` — routing + tab management + claim state","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:13:10","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:28","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","implementation","phase-5","sonnet"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f080a7972a95b81a4fdaa1aff"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d45198e38737580c31c7c0c678fb7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Pass tab_id through cobrowser service","description":"Ensure the cobrowser service (Python) passes `tab_id` and `agent_id` fields through to the WebSocket commands sent to the Firefox extension.\n\n## Changes to cobrowser_service.py\n\n1. **Command passthrough**: When a command payload includes `tab_id`, include it in the WebSocket message. Same for `agent_id`.\n2. **Server-side claim backup**: Optionally track claim state server-side as a backup in case the extension restarts. On extension reconnect, push claim state down.\n3. **Session routing**: Currently the service routes by session_id (one WebSocket connection = one session). Tab routing happens *within* a session, so the service just passes the field through. No major architectural change.\n\n## Files\n- `src/Library/cobrowser_service.py` — field passthrough + optional claim tracking","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:13:15","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:30","assigned_to":"haiku","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","haiku","implementation","phase-5"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f080a7972a95b81a4fdaa1aff"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4519b1257ee6b0d677602ad2a21f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add tab MCP tools in cobrowser_mcp_server.py","description":"Register tab management MCP tools and add `tab_id` parameter to existing action tools.\n\n## New tools\n\n### cobrowser_tab_list\n- Command: `tab.list`\n- No parameters\n- Description: \"List all open browser tabs with tab ID, URL, title, and claim status. Use to find available tabs for parallel browsing.\"\n\n### cobrowser_tab_new\n- Command: `tab.new`\n- Parameters: `url` (string, optional)\n- Description: \"Open a new browser tab, optionally navigating to a URL. Returns the tab ID for targeting future commands. Use for parallel agent browsing.\"\n\n### cobrowser_tab_close\n- Command: `tab.close`\n- Parameters: `tab_id` (integer, required)\n- Description: \"Close a browser tab by ID.\"\n\n### cobrowser_tab_claim\n- Command: `tab.claim`\n- Parameters: `tab_id` (integer, required), `agent_id` (string, optional)\n- Description: \"Claim exclusive access to a browser tab. Other agents' commands to this tab will be rejected until released. Use for parallel multi-agent browsing.\"\n\n### cobrowser_tab_release\n- Command: `tab.release`\n- Parameters: `tab_id` (integer, required)\n- Description: \"Release exclusive claim on a browser tab, making it available to other agents.\"\n\n## Existing tool changes\nAdd optional `tab_id` (integer) parameter to: cobrowser_click, cobrowser_type, cobrowser_scroll, cobrowser_read, cobrowser_query_all, cobrowser_navigate, cobrowser_screenshot, cobrowser_page_identity, cobrowser_page_structure, cobrowser_page_section. When omitted, targets active tab (backwards compatible).\n\n## Files\n- `src/Library/cobrowser_mcp_server.py` — new tool registrations + tab_id on existing tools","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:13:24","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:31","assigned_to":"haiku","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","haiku","implementation","mcp","phase-5"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f080a7972a95b81a4fdaa1aff"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4519cd4d7d729a9e4da9dc09b1da","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"Phase 5 validation: two agents browsing simultaneously","description":"Validate that two agents can work in separate tabs without interference.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:13:32","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:33","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","phase-5","sonnet","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f080a7972a95b81a4fdaa1aff","component":"cobrowser"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451a07de73aeb7e64a87c9de03a3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Implement captureSection() in state-capturer.js","description":"Add `captureSection(selector, options)` method to StateCapturer.\n\n## Implementation\nGiven a selector (or ref) scoping to one DOM region:\n1. **Text content**: `element.textContent` truncated to `maxChars` (default 3000)\n2. **Interactive elements**: buttons, inputs, selects, textareas within the region — each with type, text (60 chars), value, placeholder, selector\n3. **Links**: anchor tags within region — text (60 chars), href, selector. Max 30.\n4. **Declarative extraction** (optional `fields` parameter): If `fields` is provided (e.g. `{\"title\": \"h3\", \"price\": \".cost\", \"url\": \"a@href\"}`), find all matching child groups and return as flat JSON array. `@attr` syntax extracts attributes.\n\n## Message handling\nAdd `command.getSection` case in content.js. Payload: `{selector, maxChars?, fields?, limit?}`\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/state-capturer.js` — new method\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/content.js` — new message case","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:13:47","updated_at":"2026-03-31T19:02:13","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","implementation","phase-2","sonnet"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f00337b8282446341e3a6274f","blocked_by":"019d450efd8b762dacb6679a7ab37a67"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451a277f7026b5d40ddccf4247da","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add MCP tool: cobrowser_page_section","description":"Register `cobrowser_page_section` in the MCP server.\n\n## Tool definition\n- Command: `command.getSection`\n- Parameters:\n  - `selector` (string, required): CSS selector or ref targeting a page region\n  - `max_chars` (integer, optional, default 3000): truncation limit for text content\n  - `fields` (object, optional): declarative field map for structured extraction from repeating children, e.g. `{\"title\": \"h3\", \"url\": \"a@href\", \"price\": \".cost\"}`\n  - `limit` (integer, optional, default 25): max items when using `fields`\n  - `tab_id` (integer, optional): target tab\n- Description: \"Read a specific section of a webpage by CSS selector. Returns scoped text content, interactive elements (buttons, inputs, forms), and links within that region. Optionally extract structured data from repeating elements using a field map. Use after cobrowser_page_structure to zoom into a region of interest.\"\n\n## Backwards compatibility\n- Map old `cobrowser_read(selector)` to `page_section(selector, max_chars=10000)` with text-only output\n- Map old `cobrowser_query_all(selector)` to `page_section(selector)` with elements-only output\n- Mark both old tools deprecated in descriptions\n\n## Files\n- `src/Library/cobrowser_mcp_server.py`","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:13:55","updated_at":"2026-03-31T19:02:32","assigned_to":"haiku","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","haiku","implementation","mcp","phase-2"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f00337b8282446341e3a6274f","blocked_by":"019d450efd8b762dacb6679a7ab37a67"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451a3fdf75369c336a0f71aef44f","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"Phase 2 validation: measure section inspection vs broad re-reads","description":"Run eval set with structure → section workflow and compare against Phase 1 results.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:14:01","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:42","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","phase-2","sonnet","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f00337b8282446341e3a6274f","component":"cobrowser"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451a8fdd7b5b80b48b713258d4a3","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implement element ref system in action-executor.js","description":"Add a short-lived element reference system so the LLM can refer back to previously discovered elements.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Ref store\n- `_refMap = new Map()` — maps `ref_id (string)` → `WeakRef(element)`\n- `_refCounter = 0` — monotonic counter for generating ref IDs (\"r0\", \"r1\", ...)\n- `_pageEpoch = 0` — incremented on navigation or major DOM mutation\n\n### Assigning refs\n- New method `assignRef(element)`: creates a ref, stores `WeakRef(element)`, returns ref ID\n- Call `assignRef` on every element returned by: captureStructure (actions, landmarks), captureSection (interactive elements, links), action results (clicked element, typed-into element)\n- Each returned element gets a `ref: \"r17\"` field alongside its `selector`\n\n### Resolving refs\n- New method `resolveRef(ref_id)`: dereferences WeakRef, returns element or null\n- Modify `findElement(options)`: if `options.ref` is provided, call `resolveRef` first. If it returns null, return error with stale-ref message.\n- All action tools (click, type, read, scroll) accept `ref` as an alternative to `selector`\n\n### Invalidation\n- Listen for `beforeunload` and `popstate` events → increment `_pageEpoch`, clear `_refMap`\n- Optionally: MutationObserver on `<body>` direct children for major structural changes (new sections appearing, modals opening). Increment epoch on significant mutations.\n- On stale ref: `{success: false, error: \"Ref r17 expired (page changed since it was created). Use cobrowser_page_structure to re-orient.\", stale: true, epoch_changed: true}`\n\n### Memory management\n- WeakRef ensures GC can reclaim removed DOM elements\n- Periodic cleanup: on each `assignRef`, if `_refMap.size > 500`, sweep and remove entries where `deref()` returns undefined\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/action-executor.js` — ref store, assign, resolve, invalidation","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:14:21","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:43","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","implementation","phase-4","sonnet"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f056f7a9faeb9107cac7ff950","blocked_by":"019d450f00337b8282446341e3a6274f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451ab3d774e89b51613b3be14f1a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Wire refs through MCP tools: accept ref parameter, return refs in responses","description":"Update MCP tools to accept `ref` as an alternative to `selector` and include ref IDs in all element-containing responses.\n\n## Changes to cobrowser_mcp_server.py\n\n### Accept refs\nAdd optional `ref` (string) parameter to: cobrowser_click, cobrowser_type, cobrowser_scroll, cobrowser_page_section, cobrowser_page_html, cobrowser_read (deprecated alias). When `ref` is provided, pass it to the command payload. Extension-side `findElement` resolves it.\n\n### Return refs\nWhen formatting responses from page_identity, page_structure, page_section, and action results, include ref IDs on every element. Format example:\n```\nTop actions:\n  [r3] [search] \"Search flights\" → input.DjdbYe\n  [r4] [button] \"Sort by price\" → button.VfPpkd\n```\n\nThe LLM can then call `cobrowser_click(ref=\"r4\")` instead of re-finding the element.\n\n### Stale ref error formatting\nWhen the extension returns a stale-ref error, format it helpfully:\n```\nRef r17 expired — the page has changed since this element was found.\nUse cobrowser_page_structure to re-orient and get fresh refs.\n```\n\n## Files\n- `src/Library/cobrowser_mcp_server.py` — parameter additions + response formatting","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:14:31","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:45","assigned_to":"haiku","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","haiku","implementation","mcp","phase-4"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f056f7a9faeb9107cac7ff950","blocked_by":"019d450f00337b8282446341e3a6274f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451ace3a7ead81e0ff795ffc3d18","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"Phase 4 validation: measure re-query reduction with refs","description":"Validate that refs reduce repeated DOM lookups on multi-step interactions.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:14:37","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:47","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","phase-4","sonnet","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f056f7a9faeb9107cac7ff950","component":"cobrowser"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451b104e7e25b17f8770176c63a6","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implement browser-first file upload in content script","description":"Add programmatic file upload that bypasses the OS file chooser dialog.\n\n## Approach 1: File API on input elements\nFor `<input type=\"file\">` elements:\n1. Receive file path from MCP tool\n2. Background script reads the file via `fetch()` on a `file://` URL (requires extension permissions) or receives base64 from the MCP server\n3. Content script creates a `File` object from the data\n4. Use `DataTransfer` to set the file on the input: `const dt = new DataTransfer(); dt.items.add(file); input.files = dt.files;`\n5. Dispatch `change` and `input` events on the element\n\n## Approach 2: Drag-and-drop zones\nFor custom upload zones (Dropzone.js, etc.):\n1. Create synthetic `DragEvent` with `DataTransfer` containing the file\n2. Dispatch `dragenter` → `dragover` → `drop` sequence on the target element\n\n## Approach 3: Native fallback (last resort)\nIf DOM methods fail (rare: some sites validate the event origin):\n- Use xdotool to interact with the GTK file chooser\n- This is Linux-specific and fragile — flag it clearly in the response\n\n## Message handling\nNew command: `command.uploadFile` with payload `{selector?, file_data, file_name, mime_type}`\n- If selector provided, target that input\n- If omitted, find the first visible `<input type=\"file\">` on the page\n\n## File reading\nThe MCP server reads the file from the local path, base64-encodes it, and sends it in the command payload. The background script decodes it and passes to the content script. This avoids needing `file://` permissions.\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/action-executor.js` — uploadFile method\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/background/websocket-manager.js` — file data relay\n- `src/Library/cobrowser_mcp_server.py` — file reading + base64 encoding","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:14:54","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:49","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","implementation","phase-6","sonnet"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f0aa67817b5c0ded2f956646f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451b2e437cfbb8ffc879903ebfab","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implement browser-first file download via browser.downloads API","description":"Add programmatic download that bypasses the OS save dialog.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### In background script (websocket-manager.js)\nUse `browser.downloads.download({url, filename, saveAs: false})` to download directly to a known path without showing the save dialog.\n\n### Command: `command.downloadFile`\nPayload: `{url?, selector?, save_as?}`\n- If `url` provided: download directly\n- If `selector` provided: read the href from the element, then download\n- `save_as`: target filename/path (relative to browser's download directory)\n\n### Download monitoring\nUse `browser.downloads.onChanged` to track progress and report completion/failure back through the WebSocket.\n\n### MCP tool\n`cobrowser_download_file`:\n- Parameters: `url` (string, optional), `selector` (string, optional), `save_as` (string, optional), `tab_id` (integer, optional)\n- Description: \"Download a file from a URL or link element. Saves directly without showing the OS save dialog. Provide either a URL or a selector pointing to a download link.\"\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/background/websocket-manager.js` — browser.downloads integration\n- `src/Library/cobrowser_mcp_server.py` — MCP tool registration","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:15:02","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:50","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","implementation","phase-6","sonnet"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f0aa67817b5c0ded2f956646f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d451b44f7723c85fb712272081c63","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"Phase 6 validation: upload and download without OS dialogs","description":"Validate file upload/download tools work without triggering OS file chooser or save dialogs.","created_at":"2026-03-31T18:15:08","updated_at":"2026-03-31T18:20:51","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","phase-6","sonnet","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f0aa67817b5c0ded2f956646f","component":"cobrowser"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d456602fc72059a4d78d3c968c763","type":"dcr","status":"reviewing","title":"Event-driven supervisor: docket poll triggers + chat wake for agent coordination","description":"Add a minimal supervisor pattern to Minerva: a chat that wakes on docket state changes and can dispatch/monitor worker agents.\n\n## Core idea\nA supervisor is just a chat whose event source is docket state changes instead of human keyboard input. The only new primitive needed is: docket poll → synthetic chat turn injection. Everything else (notes for role definition, tool search for capabilities, docket MCP for work tracking, terminals for worker agents) already exists.\n\n## What this enables\n- A Minerva chat can supervise work by reacting to docket state changes\n- Supervisor dispatches workers (Claude Code sessions in Minerva terminals)\n- Workers update docket as they progress\n- Supervisor wakes on next poll, sees updates, reacts\n- Human can inject turns into supervisor chat at any time (override, redirect)\n- Subscriptions persist across save/load and restart\n\n## Architecture\n- Supervisor = Minerva chat with a role note defining responsibilities and tool hints\n- Worker = Claude Code session in a Minerva terminal, with a job note written by supervisor\n- Coordination = docket (external MCP, queried via poll)\n- Event detection = new DOCKET_POLL trigger event type, piggybacks on existing 60s trigger timer\n- Communication = docket comments and state transitions, supervisor wakes on poll diff\n\n## New code\n1. DOCKET_POLL trigger event type in TriggerManager\n2. Dynamic synthetic turn construction from docket change diff\n3. Trigger UI for DOCKET_POLL in AgentManagerWindow\n\n## Explicitly not in scope\n- Worker note templates (supervisor writes notes manually for v0)\n- New supervision-specific MCP tools (notes + tool search handle orientation)\n- Policy/gating (separate future DCR)\n- New docket types (policy, escalation, agent_profile)\n- Docket integration into Minerva core (stays external MCP)\n- Auto-nudge/watchdog (poll detects changes, doesn't auto-intervene in workers)\n- Instant-wake optimization on Minerva-initiated docket changes\n- Minerva-native worker chat isolation (workers are CC sessions for v0)\n\n## Validation\n1. Create supervisor chat with handwritten role note\n2. Create DOCKET_POLL trigger watching a docket project\n3. From outside Minerva, create/update a docket work item\n4. Supervisor wakes within 60s with synthetic turn describing the change\n5. Supervisor reads docket, spawns a CC worker in terminal with job note\n6. Worker does work, updates docket\n7. Supervisor wakes again, sees completion\n8. Loop completes with minimal human intervention\n\n## Goals served\n- Goal 1 (reduce human effort): supervisor handles dispatch/tracking autonomously\n- Goal 2 (survives interruption/restart): subscriptions are persistent trigger config\n- Goal 4 (visible/auditable): it's just a chat you can read + activity log\n- Goal 6 (scales without redesign): same primitives for 1 worker or 5\n- Goal 7 (system safety): poll is conservative, no auto-injection into workers\"\n<parameter name=\"tags\">[\"supervisor\", \"docket\", \"triggers\", \"stage1\", \"v0\"]","created_at":"2026-03-31T19:36:46","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:31:06","priority":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d45662d27767c93aedc6620bc8089","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plan: Event-driven supervisor implementation","description":"Implementation plan for the event-driven supervisor DCR. Three tracks of work: trigger infrastructure, synthetic turn construction, and validation.\n\n## Approach\n- Reuse existing TriggerManager 60s timer and MESSAGE_EXISTING action\n- Add DOCKET_POLL as new trigger event type\n- Poll docket via existing MCP tools (docket_query with updated_at filter)\n- Construct synthetic turn from change diff, inject as chat message\n- Add UI in AgentManagerWindow triggers tab\n- Validate with end-to-end supervised loop using CAD backlog tasks\n\n## Key design decisions\n- Poll-based, not push (catches all changes including external)\n- Synthetic turn includes WHAT changed, not just that something changed\n- No new supervision tools — notes define role/routing, tool search provides capabilities\n- Workers are CC sessions in Minerva terminals (not Minerva chats) for v0\n- Supervisor role defined by handwritten note (no templates for v0)\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d456602fc72059a4d78d3c968c763","created_at":"2026-03-31T19:36:57","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:33:27","priority":1,"tags":["plan","supervisor","v0"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4566599b7faa9067cba5450f77de","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add DOCKET_POLL event type to TriggerManager","description":"Add a new trigger event type that polls a docket project for changes.\n\n## Implementation\n- Add DOCKET_POLL to TriggerDefinition.TriggerType enum\n- Add fields: docket_project (String), docket_filter (Dictionary, optional), poll_interval_seconds (int, default 60)\n- Add last_poll_timestamp (String, ISO datetime) to trigger state, serialize/deserialize\n- In TriggerManager._on_schedule_check(), evaluate DOCKET_POLL triggers:\n  - Call docket_query via MCP with filter {updated_at__after: last_poll_timestamp}, detail lean\n  - If results non-empty, fire the trigger with changed items as payload\n  - Update last_poll_timestamp to current time\n- Handle MCP connection errors gracefully (docket server may not be running)\n\n## Files\n- src/Scripts/Services/Agents/TriggerDefinition.gd (enum, fields, serialize)\n- src/Scripts/Services/Agents/TriggerManager.gd (poll evaluation logic)\n\n## Tests\n- Trigger fires when docket items change\n- Trigger does NOT fire when nothing changed\n- last_poll_timestamp persists across save/load\n- Graceful handling when docket MCP is unavailable\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45662d27767c93aedc6620bc8089","created_at":"2026-03-31T19:37:08","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:34:07","priority":1,"tags":["docket","implementation","trigger"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d45667cb67ae3a83929b57743eab1","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Construct synthetic turn from docket change diff","description":"When a DOCKET_POLL trigger fires, construct a meaningful synthetic user turn from the changed items and inject it into the target chat.\n\n## Implementation\n- When DOCKET_POLL fires with changed items payload:\n  - For each changed item, summarize: title, old status → new status (if transition), latest comment (if comment added), who changed it\n  - Format as a readable synthetic turn, e.g.: 'Docket changes detected in project cad:\n- Item 1.5 REST API transitioned to done (by worker)\n- Item 1.6 E2E test has new comment: tests passing'\n  - Limit to most recent N changes (prevent token explosion from bulk updates)\n- Inject as user turn into the MESSAGE_EXISTING target chat\n- Include debounce: if multiple changes detected in same poll cycle, coalesce into one turn\n- Include rate limit: max 1 wake per poll interval per trigger (prevent thrashing)\n\n## Files\n- src/Scripts/Services/Agents/TriggerManager.gd (turn construction, injection)\n- May need changes in ChatPane.gd if programmatic user turn injection doesn't exist yet\n\n## Key questions\n- Can we inject a user turn into a chat programmatically today? Check existing MESSAGE_EXISTING action path.\n- What's the right token budget for a synthetic turn? Probably 200-500 tokens max.\n- Should we include the full item description or just title + change summary?\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45662d27767c93aedc6620bc8089","created_at":"2026-03-31T19:37:17","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:34:09","priority":1,"tags":["chat","implementation","synthetic-turn"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4566999d7915ad2f50923eb25288","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add DOCKET_POLL configuration to AgentManagerWindow triggers UI","description":"Extend the triggers tab in AgentManagerWindow to support configuring DOCKET_POLL event type.\n\n## Implementation\n- Add DOCKET_POLL to the trigger type dropdown\n- When DOCKET_POLL selected, show fields:\n  - Docket project name (LineEdit or dropdown populated from docket_project_list)\n  - Optional tag filter (LineEdit, comma-separated)\n  - Optional status filter (LineEdit or multi-select)\n  - Poll interval override (SpinBox, default 60s, min 30s)\n- Dynamic visibility: DOCKET_POLL fields shown only when that type selected (same pattern as existing schedule fields)\n- Load/save values from TriggerDefinition fields\n\n## Files\n- src/Scripts/UI/Windows/AgentManagerWindow.gd (UI controls, signal handlers, load/save)\n\n## Notes\n- Follow existing pattern for TIMER/EVENT/TIME type-specific controls\n- Project list could be populated dynamically via docket_project_list MCP call, but a simple LineEdit is fine for v0\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45662d27767c93aedc6620bc8089","created_at":"2026-03-31T19:37:25","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:34:11","priority":2,"tags":["implementation","triggers","ui"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4566beb17516b1d73db5f219c025","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"End-to-end validation: supervised loop with CAD backlog task","description":"Validate the full supervisor pattern works end-to-end using a real task from the CAD experiment backlog.\n\n## Setup\n1. Write a supervisor role note by hand (responsibilities, tool hints, communication protocol)\n2. Create a DOCKET_POLL trigger watching the cad project, targeting the supervisor chat\n3. Ensure docket MCP server is running with cad project loaded\n\n## Test sequence\n1. From outside Minerva (e.g., Claude Code), transition a CAD backlog item to 'open'\n2. Verify: supervisor chat wakes within 60s with synthetic turn describing the change\n3. Supervisor reads docket context, decides to spawn a worker\n4. Supervisor opens terminal, launches CC session, provides job context\n5. Worker does work, updates docket (transition, comments)\n6. Verify: supervisor wakes on next poll, sees worker's updates\n7. Supervisor reacts appropriately (dispatches next task, reports completion, etc.)\n\n## Success criteria\n- Supervisor wakes reliably on docket changes (both internal and external)\n- Synthetic turn contains enough context for supervisor to make decisions\n- At least one full dispatch → work → completion cycle with minimal human intervention\n- Supervisor survives chat compaction (next wake re-hydrates from docket)\n\n## Driving case\nCAD item 1.5 (REST API endpoints) or 1.6 (E2E test) — both in backlog, verified not done.\n\n## Log\nRecord all human interventions during the test for future reference.\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45662d27767c93aedc6620bc8089","created_at":"2026-03-31T19:37:34","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:34:13","priority":2,"tags":["cad","e2e","validation"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4570d8617df0b41b02a48b7095a0","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Detect modal overlays in captureStructure and report dismiss selector","description":"## Problem\nModal overlays (email capture, cookie consent, newsletter popups, login walls) block interaction with the page beneath them. The agent sees buttons and links in the structure output, tries to click them, and fails silently or hits the overlay instead. This causes retry loops — exactly the convergence failure Phase 3 targets.\n\n## Observed in the wild\nCableWholesale.com showed a Klaviyo email capture modal on first visit. The structure output included a \"Close dialog\" button but didn't flag that the page was blocked by an overlay.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### In captureStructure() — add `overlay` field\n\nDetect common overlay patterns:\n1. **CSS-based**: Elements with `position: fixed` or `position: absolute`, high z-index (>999), and dimensions covering >50% of viewport\n2. **ARIA-based**: `[role=\"dialog\"]`, `[aria-modal=\"true\"]`\n3. **Vendor signatures**: Classes/IDs matching known popup vendors:\n   - Klaviyo: `.klaviyo-close-form`, `.kl-private-reset-css`\n   - Mailchimp: `#mc_embed_signup`\n   - OptinMonster: `.om-holder`\n   - Generic: `.modal`, `.overlay`, `.popup`, `[class*=\"cookie-consent\"]`, `[class*=\"cookie-banner\"]`\n4. **Backdrop elements**: `::backdrop`, elements with semi-transparent backgrounds covering viewport\n\n### Output format\n```json\n{\n  \"overlay\": {\n    \"detected\": true,\n    \"type\": \"email_capture|cookie_consent|login_wall|generic\",\n    \"close_selector\": \"button.klaviyo-close-form\",\n    \"close_text\": \"Close dialog\",\n    \"blocks_interaction\": true\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWhen `overlay.detected` is true, the MCP response should prepend a notice:\n```\n⚠ Modal overlay detected (email_capture). Dismiss first:\n  Close: button.klaviyo-close-form — \"Close dialog\"\n```\n\n### Also consider\n- Cookie banners that aren't full modals but still eat clicks (fixed bottom bars)\n- Multiple overlays stacked (dismiss outermost first)\n- Overlays that have no close button (need to click backdrop or press Escape)\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/state-capturer.js` — add `_detectOverlay()` method, include in captureStructure()\n- `src/Library/cobrowser_mcp_server.py` — format overlay warning in structure response","created_at":"2026-03-31T19:48:36","updated_at":"2026-03-31T19:48:36","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","convergence","implementation","modals","phase-3"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f02d1773e8dcb5042751992f9"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d45869c017d7e870c1c7d85317368","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Priority scoring for high-signal buttons in _getTopActions()","description":"Buttons with text like \"Apply\", \"Submit\", \"Buy\", \"Checkout\", \"Add to Cart\", \"Sign up\", \"Download\" should be promoted to the top of the actions list regardless of DOM position. Currently these get cut off by the 15-item limit when page chrome (filters, nav) fills the slots first.\n\nFound on LinkedIn: the \"Apply\" button was missing from structure output because search filters and toolbar buttons consumed all 15 slots.\n\nFix: Add a priority score to `_getTopActions()` — match button text against a high-signal word list, sort by priority before slicing.\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/state-capturer.js` — `_getTopActions()`","created_at":"2026-03-31T20:12:22","updated_at":"2026-03-31T20:12:22","assigned_to":"haiku","priority":1,"tags":["cobrowser","convergence","linkedin","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d450efd8b762dacb6679a7ab37a67"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4586b2c37a248d38bdd448c0a574","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Aggressive short-selector preference in buildSelector()","description":"LinkedIn generates 200+ char selectors like `div.scaffold-layout__list-detail-container > div.scaffold-layout__list-detail-inner...`. These waste tokens and are fragile.\n\n`buildSelector()` currently prefers `#id` but only checks the target element, not nearby ancestors. Improvements:\n1. Walk up to find the nearest ancestor with an ID, then use a short relative path from there\n2. Check for unique `[data-*]` attributes, `[name]`, `[aria-label]` as selector shortcuts\n3. Cap generated selector length — if >100 chars, try to find a shorter alternative\n4. Phase 4 refs will also help (ref IDs are 4 chars regardless of DOM depth)\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/state-capturer.js` — `buildSelector()`","created_at":"2026-03-31T20:12:28","updated_at":"2026-03-31T20:12:28","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","linkedin","phase-4","token-efficiency"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f056f7a9faeb9107cac7ff950"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4586ca3573da95aa68599965d614","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Detect lazy-load patterns and flag in structure output","description":"LinkedIn (and many other sites) lazy-loads content. The agent sees 18 job cards but there are more below the fold. Without knowing this, the agent may think it has all results.\n\nDetect and flag:\n- Skeleton loader elements (LinkedIn has `div.skeleton-loader-spinner--bar`)\n- IntersectionObserver sentinels (common `data-*` attributes)\n- \"Load more\" / \"Show more results\" buttons\n- Infinite scroll containers\n\nAdd to structure output:\n```json\n{\n  \"lazy_load\": {\n    \"detected\": true,\n    \"type\": \"skeleton_loader\",\n    \"hint\": \"Scroll to load more content\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/state-capturer.js` — add `_detectLazyLoad()`, include in `captureStructure()`","created_at":"2026-03-31T20:12:34","updated_at":"2026-03-31T20:12:34","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","convergence","phase-1"],"parent":"minerva:019d450efd8b762dacb6679a7ab37a67"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4586e0907a70969e25cbb6b73329","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Distinguish blocking vs passive CAPTCHA in security detection","description":"LinkedIn embeds invisible reCAPTCHA for background fraud detection. Current `getSecurityInfo()` flags this as `hasCaptcha: true`, which can cause the agent to unnecessarily call `request_human`.\n\nDistinguish:\n- **Blocking**: CAPTCHA in a challenge page, full-screen overlay, or form the user must complete before proceeding (Cloudflare challenge, visible reCAPTCHA checkbox)\n- **Passive**: Invisible reCAPTCHA (`data-size=\"invisible\"`), reCAPTCHA v3 script tags, background token generation\n\nReport as:\n```json\n{\n  \"hasCaptcha\": true,\n  \"captchaType\": \"recaptcha\",\n  \"blocking\": false,\n  \"detail\": \"invisible reCAPTCHA (passive, not blocking)\"\n}\n```\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/state-capturer.js` — `getSecurityInfo()`","created_at":"2026-03-31T20:12:40","updated_at":"2026-03-31T20:12:40","assigned_to":"haiku","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","convergence","phase-3"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f02d1773e8dcb5042751992f9"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4586f9d8704498f4d9f5279fbc24","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Detect persistent floating UI (chat widgets, messaging bubbles) in overlay detection","description":"LinkedIn has a persistent messaging overlay (`#msg-overlay`) and an AI coach container (`#coach-container`) that can eat clicks on nearby elements. These aren't full modals but they're interaction-blocking in specific screen regions.\n\nExtend the overlay detection (from the modal detection work item) to also flag:\n- Chat widgets (Intercom, Drift, Zendesk, LinkedIn messaging)\n- Fixed-position elements at bottom-right/bottom-left that aren't page content\n- Cookie consent bars (fixed bottom)\n- Floating action buttons\n\nReport alongside modal detection:\n```json\n{\n  \"floating_ui\": [\n    {\"selector\": \"#msg-overlay\", \"position\": \"bottom-right\", \"type\": \"messaging\"},\n    {\"selector\": \"#coach-container\", \"position\": \"bottom-right\", \"type\": \"assistant\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Files\n- `src/extension-cobrowser/content/state-capturer.js` — extend `_detectOverlay()` (from modal work item)","created_at":"2026-03-31T20:12:46","updated_at":"2026-03-31T20:12:46","assigned_to":"sonnet","priority":3,"tags":["cobrowser","convergence","phase-3"],"parent":"minerva:019d450f02d1773e8dcb5042751992f9"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d45c972f57f22abe707074d15b95b","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"External MCP clients see budget-managed tool subset instead of full catalog","description":"When auto_tool_management is enabled, the HTTP MCP server's tools/list handler returned only tools active in the ToolBudgetManager — the same restricted set used for Minerva's internal LLM context. External clients (Claude Code, etc.) have their own context management and should see the full tool catalog.\n\nRoot cause: _handle_tools_list in MinervaMCPHttpServer.gd had two code paths — auto mode (budget-managed subset) and manual mode (full catalog). The auto mode path should not have applied to external clients.\n\nFix: External clients always get the full catalog with tool_set filtering. Budget manager only governs internal LLM context.\n\nMissing test: No test verifies that an external MCP client can see all tools after connecting, regardless of auto_tool_management setting.","created_at":"2026-03-31T21:25:23","updated_at":"2026-03-31T21:25:46","severity":2,"tags":["mcp","regression","testing","tools"],"resolution":"Fixed in MinervaMCPHttpServer.gd: removed auto_tool_management branch from _handle_tools_list. External clients always get full catalog with tool_set filtering. Test gap for external MCP tool visibility remains unfiled."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d45f3796d70068b1df2c793716c52","type":"dcr","status":"reviewing","title":"Minerva hook system: event-driven behavioral nudges with human-approved activation","description":"Add a hook system to Minerva that mirrors Claude Code's hook model but adapted for Minerva's architecture. Hooks are configured as triggers that fire on agent events (tool calls, session lifecycle, compaction, task completion) and inject hints/context into agent chats. Hooks drive agents toward learning, knowledge capture, and coherent behavior.\n\n## Motivation\n\nClaude Code has a 27-event hook system that enables behavioral nudges (knowledge routing, learning prompts, completion gates). Minerva needs the same capability for its internal agents. The existing trigger system (TIMER, EVENT, TIME, DOCKET_POLL) provides the infrastructure — this DCR extends it with hook-style event types.\n\nTwo proven patterns from Claude Code hooks already in use:\n1. **PreToolUse knowledge routing**: Route table matches tool+args → injects relevant hint before execution. Ensures agents have prior knowledge before acting.\n2. **PostToolUse learning nudge**: Probabilistic nudge after tool calls → reminds agent to store knowledge. Tunable probability (1/10 to 1/100+).\n\n## Core hook event types (priority order)\n\n### Phase 1: Tool execution hooks\n- **MCP_TOOL_EXECUTED** (PostToolUse equivalent): Fires after tool calls. Tunable probability (`fire_probability` field, 0.0-1.0). Optional tool name regex filter (`tool_name_pattern`). Injects hint into active chat. Primary use: \"did you learn something? Store it.\"\n- **MCP_TOOL_ABOUT_TO_EXECUTE** (PreToolUse equivalent): Fires before tool calls. Route table matching: tool pattern (regex) + arg name + arg match pattern → hint string. Per-session dedup (each hint shown once). Primary use: inject relevant prior knowledge before agent acts.\n\n### Phase 2: Lifecycle hooks\n- **CHAT_STARTED** (SessionStart equivalent): Fires when a chat begins or resumes. Injects relevant context/hints at start. Primary use: \"here's what previous agents learned about this area.\"\n- **CHAT_STOPPING** (Stop equivalent): Fires when agent is about to finish responding. Primary use: \"before you stop — did you capture what you learned?\"\n- **PRE_COMPACTION** (PreCompact equivalent): Fires before context compaction. Primary use: \"save important knowledge before context is compressed.\"\n\n### Phase 3: Task hooks\n- **TASK_COMPLETED**: Fires when a docket item transitions to done. Can gate completion (block if no hints/insights recorded). Primary use: completion hygiene enforcement.\n\n## New TriggerDefinition fields\n\n```\n## Probability of firing (0.0-1.0, default 1.0). For nudge hooks, use 0.1.\nvar hook_fire_probability: float = 1.0\n## Regex pattern matching tool name (empty = all tools)\nvar hook_tool_name_pattern: String = \"\"\n## Route table for PreToolUse-style matching (JSON string)\n## Format: [[tool_regex, arg_name, arg_match_regex, hint_string], ...]\nvar hook_route_table: String = \"\"\n## Per-session dedup: track which hints/routes have fired (runtime, not persisted)\n```\n\n## Access control: human-approved activation\n\nAgents can CRUD hooks (create, update, delete via MCP tools) but with one restriction: **agents cannot set `enabled: true` directly.** Hooks created by agents start in disabled/draft state. Only human action (UI toggle or external MCP client call) can enable a hook.\n\nThis is the same \"agents author, humans approve\" pattern from the policy discussion. An agent can propose \"add a nudge hook for file edits\" — but it doesn't fire until a human reviews and enables it.\n\nImplementation: in `_create_trigger` and `_update_trigger` MCP handlers, if the caller is an internal agent (not HTTP external client), force `enabled = false` and add a flag like `pending_approval: true`.\n\n## What this enables (Goal 5: agents learn)\n\n1. **System prompt** tells agents to listen to hints and store knowledge\n2. **Hooks inject hints** — not commands, hints. Agent decides how to use them.\n3. **If agents don't learn voluntarily**, gate task completion on knowledge capture (enforcement, Phase 3)\n\n## Relationship to existing work\n\n- Extends existing trigger system (TIMER, EVENT, TIME, DOCKET_POLL) — same CRUD, same persistence, same UI\n- Complements DOCKET_POLL (DCR in progress) — DOCKET_POLL watches external state, hooks watch internal agent behavior\n- Prepares for policy/gating (future DCR) — TASK_COMPLETED hook with blocking is a lightweight gate\n\n## Explicitly not in scope\n- Full 27-event parity with Claude Code (only the most valuable events)\n- Hook handler types beyond message injection (no HTTP callbacks, no LLM evaluation hooks)\n- Nested hook chains (hook A triggers hook B)\n- Cross-project hooks (hooks are project-scoped via trigger persistence)\n\n## Validation\n- Create a PostToolUse nudge hook with 10% fire probability\n- Give an agent a task\n- Verify: nudge appears ~1/10 tool calls\n- Verify: agent stores a hint/insight in response to nudge\n- Create a PreToolUse knowledge route for a specific tool\n- Verify: hint injected before tool execution, only once per session\"\n<parameter name=\"tags\">[\"hooks\", \"learning\", \"knowledge\", \"triggers\", \"stage1\", \"agents\"]","created_at":"2026-03-31T22:11:17","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:31:17","priority":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d45f7ffe774a8a5fc5d1957b8fb34","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plan: Minerva hook system implementation","description":"Implementation plan for the Minerva hook system DCR. Three phases: tool execution hooks (core), lifecycle hooks, and task completion hooks with gating.\n\n## Approach\n- Extend existing TriggerDefinition with new EventTypes and hook-specific fields\n- Extend TriggerManager to connect to mcp_tool_executed signal and new lifecycle signals\n- Add route table matching for PreToolUse-style knowledge injection\n- Add probabilistic firing for PostToolUse-style nudges\n- Enforce human-approved activation: internal agents can't enable hooks directly\n- Update AgentManagerWindow UI for new event types and hook fields\n- Update MCP tool registration for new fields\n\n## Key design decisions\n- Hooks ARE triggers — same CRUD, same persistence, same UI, just new event types\n- Probabilistic firing via fire_probability field (0.0-1.0)\n- Route table stored as JSON string in hook_route_table field\n- Per-session dedup tracked in runtime dictionary (not persisted)\n- Human-approved activation: agents create hooks disabled, only humans can enable\n- Hints, not commands — hooks inject context, agents decide what to do with it\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45f3796d70068b1df2c793716c52","created_at":"2026-03-31T22:16:14","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:32:48","priority":1,"tags":["hooks","implementation","plan"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4602dc087770a815bbbc50018ac0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add MCP_TOOL_EXECUTED event type with probabilistic firing","description":"PostToolUse equivalent. Fires after MCP tool calls with tunable probability.\n\n## Implementation\n- Add MCP_TOOL_EXECUTED to TriggerDefinition.EventType enum\n- Add fields: hook_fire_probability (float, 0.0-1.0, default 1.0), hook_tool_name_pattern (String, regex, empty=all)\n- Serialize/deserialize new fields\n- In TriggerManager._ready(), connect to SingletonObject.mcp_tool_executed signal\n- New handler: _on_mcp_tool_executed(tool_name, arguments, result, agent_id)\n  - For each enabled EVENT trigger with event_type=MCP_TOOL_EXECUTED:\n    - Check tool_name_pattern regex against tool_name (skip if no match)\n    - Roll random against fire_probability (skip if no fire)\n    - Fire trigger with context: {tool_name, agent_id}\n- Message template can use {tool_name} and {agent_id} vars\n\n## Files\n- TriggerDefinition.gd (enum, fields, serialize)\n- TriggerManager.gd (signal connection, handler, probability logic)\n\n## Test\n- Create trigger with fire_probability=1.0 and tool_name_pattern matching a specific tool\n- Call that tool\n- Verify trigger fires and message is injected\n- Change probability to 0.0, verify it never fires\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45f7ffe774a8a5fc5d1957b8fb34","created_at":"2026-03-31T22:28:05","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:32:14","priority":1,"tags":["hooks","implementation","post-tool"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4602df5c7f56bb1d312d6aeebd54","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add MCP_TOOL_ABOUT_TO_EXECUTE event type with route table matching","description":"PreToolUse equivalent. Fires before MCP tool calls with route table matching for knowledge injection.\n\n## Implementation\n- Add MCP_TOOL_ABOUT_TO_EXECUTE to TriggerDefinition.EventType enum\n- Add field: hook_route_table (String, JSON format: [[tool_regex, arg_name, arg_match_regex, hint_string], ...])\n- Need new signal: SingletonObject.mcp_tool_about_to_execute(tool_name, arguments) — emitted before tool execution in MinervaMCPServer\n- In TriggerManager, connect to new signal\n- New handler: _on_mcp_tool_about_to_execute(tool_name, arguments)\n  - For each enabled EVENT trigger with event_type=MCP_TOOL_ABOUT_TO_EXECUTE:\n    - Parse route table JSON\n    - For each route: match tool_regex against tool_name, match arg pattern against arg value\n    - Per-session dedup: track which route indices have fired (runtime Dictionary, not persisted)\n    - On match: inject hint into active chat context\n- Hint injection: use MESSAGE_EXISTING action to inject hint before tool executes\n\n## Files\n- TriggerDefinition.gd (enum, field, serialize)\n- TriggerManager.gd (signal connection, route matching, dedup)\n- MinervaMCPServer.gd (emit new signal before tool execution)\n- singleton_object.gd (declare new signal)\n\n## Test\n- Create route: tool=minerva_file_edit, arg=file_path, match=TriggerManager, hint='TriggerManager has 60s poll timer and anti-flood logic'\n- Agent calls minerva_file_edit on TriggerManager.gd\n- Verify hint injected\n- Agent calls minerva_file_edit on TriggerManager.gd again — hint NOT re-injected (dedup)\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45f7ffe774a8a5fc5d1957b8fb34","created_at":"2026-03-31T22:28:06","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:32:16","priority":1,"tags":["hooks","implementation","knowledge-routing","pre-tool"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4602e2957f9f817dc840e3fbcbbb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Human-approved activation: agents can't enable hooks directly","description":"Enforce the 'agents author, humans approve' pattern for hook triggers.\n\n## Implementation\n- In MinervaMCPServer._create_trigger() and _update_trigger():\n  - Detect if caller is internal agent vs external HTTP client\n  - If internal agent and trying to set enabled=true: force enabled=false, set pending_approval=true\n  - Add pending_approval field to TriggerDefinition (bool, default false)\n  - When human enables via UI or external MCP: clear pending_approval\n- In AgentManagerWindow: show visual indicator for pending_approval triggers (e.g. yellow highlight, 'Pending approval' label)\n- Optional: toast notification when agent proposes a new hook\n\n## Files\n- TriggerDefinition.gd (pending_approval field, serialize)\n- MinervaMCPServer.gd (_create_trigger, _update_trigger — caller detection + enforcement)\n- MinervaMCPHttpServer.gd (pass caller context to distinguish internal vs external)\n- AgentManagerWindow.gd (pending approval visual indicator)\n\n## Test\n- Internal agent calls minerva_create_trigger with enabled=true\n- Verify: trigger created but enabled=false, pending_approval=true\n- Human enables via UI\n- Verify: trigger now enabled, pending_approval cleared\n- External client creates trigger with enabled=true\n- Verify: trigger created and enabled=true (no restriction)\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45f7ffe774a8a5fc5d1957b8fb34","created_at":"2026-03-31T22:28:07","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:32:18","priority":1,"tags":["access-control","hooks","implementation","policy"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4602e5d178fd93b4a24715db0061","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Hook UI: extend AgentManagerWindow for hook event types and fields","description":"Add UI controls for the new hook event types and their specific fields.\n\n## Implementation\n- Add MCP_TOOL_EXECUTED and MCP_TOOL_ABOUT_TO_EXECUTE to event type dropdown (shown when trigger_type=EVENT)\n- When MCP_TOOL_EXECUTED selected, show:\n  - Fire probability SpinBox (0.0-1.0, step 0.05, default 0.1)\n  - Tool name pattern LineEdit (regex, placeholder: 'e.g. minerva_file_.*')\n- When MCP_TOOL_ABOUT_TO_EXECUTE selected, show:\n  - Tool name pattern LineEdit\n  - Route table editor (TextEdit for JSON, or a mini list UI with add/remove rows)\n    - For v0: TextEdit with JSON is fine, mini list UI is stretch\n- Show pending_approval indicator on trigger list items\n- Load/save all new fields from/to TriggerDefinition\n\n## Files\n- AgentManagerWindow.gd (UI controls, visibility, load/save)\n\n## Notes\n- Follow existing pattern for type-specific control visibility\n- Route table as JSON TextEdit is acceptable for v0 — a proper list editor is future work\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45f7ffe774a8a5fc5d1957b8fb34","created_at":"2026-03-31T22:28:08","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:32:19","priority":2,"tags":["hooks","implementation","ui"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4602e90d76e2942e8ac21c227f3f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Update MCP tool registration for hook fields","description":"Add hook-specific fields to minerva_create_trigger and minerva_update_trigger MCP tool schemas and handlers.\n\n## Implementation\n- Update tool description to include new event types\n- Add to input_schema: hook_fire_probability, hook_tool_name_pattern, hook_route_table, pending_approval\n- Update _create_trigger handler to read new fields\n- Update _update_trigger handler to read new fields\n- Update _list_triggers to include new fields in response\n\n## Files\n- MinervaMCPServer.gd (tool registration + handlers)\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45f7ffe774a8a5fc5d1957b8fb34","created_at":"2026-03-31T22:28:09","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:32:21","priority":2,"tags":["hooks","implementation","mcp"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4602ec3c7232bc1084a37c873237","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"End-to-end validation: knowledge nudge and routing hooks","description":"Validate the full hook system works end-to-end with real agent work.\n\n## Test sequence\n\n### Test A: PostToolUse nudge\n1. Create MCP_TOOL_EXECUTED hook: fire_probability=1.0, tool_name_pattern='minerva_file_.*', message='You just edited a file. Did you learn something useful? Store it as a docket hint with docket_hint_set.'\n2. Human enables the hook\n3. Give an agent a file editing task\n4. Verify: after each file edit, nudge message appears in chat\n5. Verify: agent stores at least one hint in response\n6. Change fire_probability to 0.1, verify nudge appears ~1/10 times\n\n### Test B: PreToolUse knowledge route\n1. Create MCP_TOOL_ABOUT_TO_EXECUTE hook with route: tool=minerva_file_edit, arg=file_path, match=TriggerManager, hint='TriggerManager.gd has a 60s schedule check timer. DOCKET_POLL triggers piggyback on it or use dedicated timers for custom intervals.'\n2. Human enables the hook\n3. Agent edits TriggerManager.gd\n4. Verify: hint injected before edit\n5. Agent edits TriggerManager.gd again — hint NOT re-injected\n\n### Test C: Human-approved activation\n1. An internal Minerva agent creates a hook via MCP\n2. Verify: hook created but disabled, pending_approval shown in UI\n3. Human enables via UI toggle\n4. Verify: hook now fires\n\n## Success criteria\n- Nudge appears at correct probability\n- Knowledge route injects correct hint for correct tool+arg match\n- Dedup prevents repeat hints in same session\n- Agents respond to nudges by actually storing knowledge\n- Human approval gate prevents agents from self-enabling hooks\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d45f7ffe774a8a5fc5d1957b8fb34","created_at":"2026-03-31T22:28:09","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:32:23","priority":2,"tags":["e2e","hooks","validation"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d49a4821b7426abc52ac82d2b673b","type":"insight","status":"confirmed","title":"Sub-agent provider mismatch: _send_message tab-switch race condition","created_at":"2026-04-01T15:23:31","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:30:55","tags":["mcp","provider","race-condition","sub-agent"],"assumed":"That switching current_tab back immediately after execute_regular_chat was safe because the call is fire-and-forget","corrected":"execute_regular_chat and create_prompt read current_tab multiple times during async setup. Immediate tab restore causes them to read the CALLER's provider instead of the TARGET's, contaminating the API request format. Must use call_deferred to restore tab."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d49a82a4a7811aa3123f477c66c36","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Provider format cross-contamination: sub-agents fail when caller uses different provider","description":"When a supervisor chat (e.g., ChatGPT) creates a sub-agent chat (e.g., Anthropic Claude) via minerva_send_message, the sub-agent's API requests contain content types from the caller's provider format, causing the API to reject them.\n\nImmediate cause: _send_message switches current_tab back to the caller immediately after execute_regular_chat, but execute_regular_chat reads current_tab asynchronously, picking up the caller's provider instead of the target's.\n\nRoot causes:\n1. ChatGPTProvider uses unique content types (input_text, output_text, function_call_output) that no other provider recognizes\n2. process_bot_response stores bot_response.provider on the ChatHistoryItem instead of history.provider (unused _history_provider parameter)\n3. No standardized internal content format — Format() output goes directly to API with no normalization layer\n4. Multiple functions read current_tab during async execution, creating race conditions when tabs are switched programmatically\n\nObserved error: 'messages.0.content.0: Input tag input_text found using type does not match any of the expected tags' when Anthropic receives ChatGPT-formatted content.\"\n<parameter name=\"severity\">1","created_at":"2026-04-01T15:27:30","updated_at":"2026-04-01T15:29:38","tags":["cross-contamination","format","provider","race-condition","sub-agent"],"resolution":"Three fixes: (1) tab-switch race in _send_message — call_deferred for tab restore, (2) process_bot_response uses _history_provider not bot_response.provider, (3) ChatGPTProvider Format() uses standard 'text' content type, converts to input_text/output_text in generate_content() at API call time."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d49b677b2796c9853f5db632e4038","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"minerva_create_chat provider selection is hardcoded — missing ChatGPT, OpenRouter, dynamic models","description":"The provider parameter in minerva_create_chat uses a hardcoded map of 8 friendly names. Providers added after the map was written (ChatGPT OAuth, OpenRouter dynamic models, custom models via minerva_add_model) are not available. An agent using ChatGPT cannot spawn a sub-agent with its own provider.\n\nRoot cause: static enum in MCP tool schema + hardcoded Dictionary in _create_chat handler. No dynamic provider discovery mechanism exists for agents.\n\nBroader class: any MCP tool that exposes a fixed enum for a value that's dynamic at runtime.","created_at":"2026-04-01T15:43:08","updated_at":"2026-04-01T15:44:48","severity":2,"tags":["dynamic-config","mcp","provider","sub-agent"],"resolution":"Provider map now built dynamically from API_MODEL_PROVIDERS enum. Accepts friendly names (case-insensitive), raw enum IDs (covers OpenRouter, ChatGPT), and special value 'current' (uses calling chat's provider). Tool schema updated to remove fixed enum, describe all options. ChatGPT agents can now spawn sub-agents with chatgpt_default or 'current'."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b057b6474e6b1b18a7147304df9","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Supervisor response leaks into sub-agent: create_prompt reads current_tab instead of passed history","description":"When a supervisor agent spawns a sub-agent via minerva_send_message during an agent mode tool call loop, the supervisor's continuation response can leak into the sub-agent's chat history. The supervisor's text appears in the sub-agent's chat, and the sub-agent role-confuses.\n\nRoot cause: handle_tool_calls receives a captured history parameter but calls create_prompt() which reads current_tab (global mutable state) instead of using the passed history. When current_tab points to the sub-agent's tab during the continuation, the prompt is built from the wrong chat.\n\n5-Why: create_prompt was designed for single-chat operation where current_tab was always correct. Sub-agents introduced concurrent chat execution, breaking this assumption.","created_at":"2026-04-01T21:49:03","updated_at":"2026-04-01T21:51:33","severity":1,"tags":["create_prompt","current_tab","race-condition","sub-agent"],"resolution":"Added history_override parameter to create_prompt(). All 6 call sites updated: 4 in execute_regular/sequential/hcp_chat pass captured history, 1 in handle_tool_calls continuation passes history, 2 UI-triggered calls (inspect, regenerate) left as-is since current_tab is always correct for those. Sub-agent chats can no longer leak supervisor responses because create_prompt uses the explicitly passed history instead of reading current_tab."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b7143347605bc0c3a8b17b361a2","type":"dcr","status":"reviewing","title":"Sub-agent lifecycle contract: managed spawn, completion routing, budget flow, and rate limit handling","description":"Define and implement a standard lifecycle contract for sub-agents in Minerva's supervisor pattern. Currently, spawning a sub-agent is fire-and-forget with no parent tracking, no completion signal, no resource control, and no graceful handling of rate limits or quota exhaustion.\n\n## Problems\n\n1. **No parent-child tracking**: minerva_send_message creates a chat but records no relationship. The system doesn't know who spawned whom.\n\n2. **No completion routing**: When a sub-agent finishes (success, error, quota, or rate limit), the supervisor has no way to know. Its turn ended after sending the message. It's asleep.\n\n3. **No termination classification**: A sub-agent can stop for many reasons (completed, errored, quota exhausted, rate limited, timed out, cancelled, stalled). The supervisor needs structured information about WHY.\n\n4. **No resource budgeting**: No per-worker quotas for tool rounds, tokens, or time. No per-supervisor limits on concurrent/total workers or total spend. No approval gates.\n\n5. **Opaque provider rate limits**: Subscription providers (ChatGPT Plus/Pro, Claude Max) have hidden token-per-time-window budgets. You don't know the limit until you hit it. Can't pre-plan budgets. Multi-agent work exhausts shared limits faster.\n\n6. **No cascading control**: Stopping a supervisor doesn't stop its workers. Worker crashes don't notify the supervisor. No tree-wide shutdown.\n\n7. **Browser tool bias**: Agents preferentially activate native OS-level cobrowser tools over standard synthetic tools, leading to worse browsing performance.\n\n## Design\n\n### Core primitive: minerva_spawn_worker\nSingle tool that replaces the manual create_chat → set_prompt → set_agent_mode → send_message dance. Records parent-child link. Configures quotas. Registers completion callback.\n\n### Completion routing\nWhen worker's turn ends, system classifies termination and injects synthetic turn into parent chat with: worker name, termination reason, last message summary, token cost, partial results flag.\n\n### Termination reasons\ncompleted | error | quota_exhausted | rate_limited | timeout | cancelled | stalled\n\n### Rate limit handling\n- Layer 1: Detect rate limits distinctly from other errors\n- Layer 2: Propagate to supervisor with retry-after estimate\n- Layer 3: Adaptive pacing from historical usage patterns\n- Layer 4: Provider diversity — spread work across providers\n\n### Budget flow\n- Budget flows down the tree: MOM → supervisor → worker\n- Opaque limits: operate at full speed, detect throttling, back off\n- Approval gates: configurable per-level (e.g., first 3 workers auto, then ask human)\n- Per-worker: max_tool_rounds, timeout_seconds\n- Per-supervisor: max_concurrent_workers, max_total_workers\n\n### Cascading control\n- Stop supervisor → cancel all workers\n- Worker stall detection → notify supervisor\n- Worker error → supervisor gets error details\n\n## Goals served\n- Goal 1 (reduce human effort): supervisor handles worker lifecycle autonomously\n- Goal 2 (survives everything): completion routing works after compaction/restart via docket\n- Goal 3 (gating): approval gates on worker spawning\n- Goal 7 (system safety): rate limit detection prevents runaway spend\n\n## Relationship to other DCRs\n- Builds on: Event-driven supervisor (DOCKET_POLL triggers)\n- Builds on: Hook system (MCP_TOOL_EXECUTED for monitoring)\n- Prepares for: Policy engine (approval gates use policy infrastructure)\n- Informed by: oh-my-codex analysis (task claims, heartbeats, phase transitions)\"\n<parameter name=\"tags\">[\"lifecycle\", \"sub-agent\", \"supervisor\", \"rate-limit\", \"budget\", \"stage1\"]","created_at":"2026-04-01T23:46:47","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:29:16","priority":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b716ebe753fa33a036d1633bdf6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plan: Sub-agent lifecycle contract implementation","description":"Implementation plan for the sub-agent lifecycle DCR. Four phases: core spawn primitive, completion routing, rate limit handling, budget/approval gates.\n\n## Approach\n- Phase 1: minerva_spawn_worker tool + parent-child tracking + termination classification\n- Phase 2: Automatic completion routing to parent chat via synthetic turns\n- Phase 3: Rate limit detection, propagation, and adaptive pacing\n- Phase 4: Budget flow, approval gates, cascading control\n\n## Key design decisions\n- Parent-child links stored on ChatHistory (parent_chat_id field)\n- Completion routing via agent_chat_finished signal + synthetic turn injection (same pattern as DOCKET_POLL)\n- Rate limit detection in provider error handling, not a separate system\n- Budget is advisory for opaque providers, hard for API-key providers\n- Approval gates reuse trigger/policy infrastructure from hook DCR\n- Browser tool bias fixed via search ranking adjustment, not lifecycle changes\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d4b7143347605bc0c3a8b17b361a2","created_at":"2026-04-01T23:46:58","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:29:26","priority":1,"tags":["lifecycle","plan","sub-agent"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b719d5c7c2e80b7d146600dfd49","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"minerva_spawn_worker tool: managed spawn with parent-child tracking","description":"Create a single MCP tool that replaces the manual 5-step sub-agent creation dance.\n\n## API\nminerva_spawn_worker(name, provider, system_prompt, task, max_tool_rounds, timeout_seconds)\nReturns: worker_id, chat_id\n\n## Implementation\n- Wraps: create_chat + set_system_prompt + set_agent_mode + send_message\n- Adds parent_chat_id field to ChatHistory (new field, nullable)\n- Records parent→child link: parent_chat_id = calling chat's HistoryId\n- Sets per-worker max_tool_call_rounds from parameter (overrides default)\n- Registers for completion notification (stores worker_id for routing)\n\n## Files\n- MinervaMCPServer.gd (new tool registration + handler)\n- ChatHistory model (parent_chat_id field)\n- singleton_object.gd or MCPManager (worker registry: worker_id → parent_chat_id mapping)\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d4b716ebe753fa33a036d1633bdf6","created_at":"2026-04-01T23:47:10","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:30:22","priority":1,"tags":["implementation","lifecycle","spawn"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b71ba9d70ff9aa375f4bde40881","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Termination classification: structured reasons for why a sub-agent stopped","description":"When a sub-agent's turn ends, classify WHY it stopped and store that on the chat.\n\n## Termination reasons\n- completed: final response had no tool calls (normal finish)\n- error: API error or unrecoverable tool error\n- quota_exhausted: hit max_tool_call_rounds\n- rate_limited: provider returned rate limit / quota error\n- timeout: wall-clock timeout exceeded\n- cancelled: user or parent stopped it\n- stalled: no progress for N seconds (future, needs heartbeat)\n\n## Implementation\n- Add termination_reason field to ChatHistory\n- In handle_tool_calls: when max_rounds hit, set reason = quota_exhausted\n- In agent error paths: set reason = error with error message\n- In stop handler: set reason = cancelled\n- In provider response parsing: detect rate limit responses and set reason = rate_limited\n- On normal completion (no tool calls): set reason = completed\n\n## Files\n- ChatHistory model (termination_reason, termination_message fields)\n- ChatPane.gd (handle_tool_calls, execute_regular_chat — set reasons at each exit point)\n- Provider implementations (rate limit detection in error parsing)\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d4b716ebe753fa33a036d1633bdf6","created_at":"2026-04-01T23:47:17","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:30:24","priority":1,"tags":["implementation","lifecycle","termination"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b71ddbe7c30a3fa978c6f995c25","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Completion routing: auto-wake parent when sub-agent finishes","description":"When a sub-agent's turn ends, automatically inject a synthetic turn into the parent supervisor's chat describing what happened.\n\n## Implementation\n- Hook into agent_chat_finished signal (already exists)\n- Look up worker's parent_chat_id (from spawn tracking)\n- Build synthetic turn: worker name, termination reason, last message summary (truncated), token cost, partial results flag\n- Inject into parent chat using the same MESSAGE_EXISTING pattern as DOCKET_POLL triggers\n- Parent wakes, reads the completion message, decides next action\n\n## Synthetic turn format\n'Sub-agent \\\"Best Buy Search\\\" finished (completed). Summary: Found 5 cables, appended to spreadsheet. Tokens: 12,340. Duration: 45s.'\n\nOr for failure:\n'Sub-agent \\\"Best Buy Search\\\" stopped (rate_limited). Provider: chatgpt. Retry after: ~300s. Progress: 3 of 5 cables found. Partial results saved.'\n\n## Edge cases\n- Parent chat was closed → log warning, don't crash\n- Parent chat is busy (mid-turn) → queue the completion, deliver when parent's turn ends\n- Multiple workers finish simultaneously → deliver in order, one synthetic turn per worker\n\n## Files\n- New: WorkerRegistry or extension of MCPManager (parent-child tracking + completion routing)\n- ChatPane.gd or TriggerManager.gd (signal handler for routing)\n- MinervaMCPServer.gd (message injection into parent)\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d4b716ebe753fa33a036d1633bdf6","created_at":"2026-04-01T23:47:26","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:30:26","priority":1,"tags":["completion","implementation","lifecycle","routing"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b71fd407e5a9b6d6eb43b09b3ca","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Rate limit detection and propagation across providers","description":"Detect rate limit errors distinctly from other API errors across all providers. Propagate to supervisor with retry information.\n\n## Layer 1: Detection\nFor each provider, identify the rate limit response pattern:\n- Anthropic: HTTP 429, 'rate_limit_error' or 'overloaded_error'\n- OpenAI: HTTP 429, 'rate_limit_exceeded'\n- ChatGPT Responses API: HTTP 429 or quota-specific error\n- Google: HTTP 429 or 'RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED'\n- Ollama/Local: typically no rate limits\n\nParse retry-after header or response body for wait duration.\n\n## Layer 2: Propagation\n- Set termination_reason = rate_limited on the chat\n- Include provider name and estimated retry_after in termination message\n- Completion routing delivers this to supervisor (per completion routing task)\n\n## Layer 3: Adaptive pacing (future)\n- Track tokens consumed per provider per time window\n- Estimate remaining budget from historical usage\n- Proactively slow down when approaching estimated limit\n\n## Files\n- Each provider's _parse_request_results or error handling (detect 429/rate limit)\n- BaseProvider.gd (common rate limit error structure)\n- ChatPane.gd (set termination_reason on rate limit)\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d4b716ebe753fa33a036d1633bdf6","created_at":"2026-04-01T23:47:34","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:30:28","priority":1,"tags":["implementation","lifecycle","provider","rate-limit"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b72253d74cba0f18d953b1e33e7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Budget flow and approval gates for worker spawning","description":"Implement resource budgets that flow down the supervisor tree, with configurable approval gates.\n\n## Per-worker budgets\n- max_tool_rounds: set via minerva_spawn_worker (already planned)\n- timeout_seconds: wall-clock timeout, worker auto-cancelled when exceeded\n- max_tokens: advisory for opaque providers, hard limit for API-key providers\n\n## Per-supervisor budgets\n- max_concurrent_workers: how many workers can run simultaneously\n- max_total_workers: lifetime cap, requires approval to exceed\n- max_total_spend: token/cost cap across all workers\n\n## Approval gates\nConfigurable per-supervisor:\n- 'First N workers auto-approve, then ask human'\n- 'Workers estimated > $X: ask human'\n- 'After total spend > $Y: pause and report to parent'\n\nImplementation: reuse pending_approval pattern from hook system. When gate triggers, supervisor's spawn attempt returns 'pending approval' and a notification goes to the human (or supervisor's supervisor).\n\n## Opaque provider handling\nFor subscription providers with hidden limits:\n- Budget is advisory — track usage but can't enforce hard limits\n- On rate limit: report to supervisor, supervisor decides (wait, switch provider, report up)\n- Over time: build historical usage model to estimate remaining capacity\n\n## Cascading control\n- Stop supervisor → iterate worker registry, cancel all children\n- Worker registry cleanup on supervisor close\n\n## Files\n- WorkerRegistry (budget tracking, spawn counting, concurrent worker tracking)\n- MinervaMCPServer.gd (spawn_worker: check budgets before spawning)\n- Policy/trigger infrastructure for approval gates\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d4b716ebe753fa33a036d1633bdf6","created_at":"2026-04-01T23:47:45","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:30:30","priority":2,"tags":["approval","budget","implementation","lifecycle"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b7244d07798b4dd26e90af4f4b5","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Fix browser tool selection bias: deprioritize native cobrowser tools in search","description":"Agents searching for 'cobrowser' tools get native OS-level tools (native_click, native_type, native_scroll) in top results. These are meant for edge cases but agents preferentially use them over standard synthetic tools that work better.\n\n## Options\n1. Adjust tool search scoring: tag native_* tools with lower relevance or a 'native' category\n2. Move native tools to a separate tool_set that isn't returned by default search\n3. Add a note/hint in the cobrowser tool descriptions: 'Prefer cobrowser_click over cobrowser_native_click for normal web interaction'\n4. Reorder tool registration so standard tools index first\n\n## Recommendation\nOption 2 or 3 — either separate tool set or documentation fix. Both are quick and don't require search algorithm changes.\n\n## Files\n- Tool registration in cobrowser MCP server or MCPManager tool indexing\n- Or: update 'How to Use Tools' note with cobrowser guidance\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d4b716ebe753fa33a036d1633bdf6","created_at":"2026-04-01T23:47:53","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:30:32","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","quick-fix","tool-search"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b725ea57e08b71683b8d7e62cd8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"End-to-end validation: supervised worker with lifecycle tracking","description":"Validate the full lifecycle contract with a real supervised task.\n\n## Test sequence\n1. Supervisor spawns worker via minerva_spawn_worker for a browsing+spreadsheet task\n2. Worker runs, makes tool calls, updates spreadsheet\n3. Worker completes (or hits quota/rate limit)\n4. Supervisor auto-wakes with completion message\n5. Supervisor reads result, decides next action\n\n## Success criteria\n- Supervisor wakes reliably on worker completion\n- Termination reason is correct (completed vs quota vs rate_limited)\n- Completion message contains useful summary\n- If rate limited: supervisor sees retry info and can act on it\n- If quota exhausted: supervisor can spawn a continuation worker\n- Total worker spawns tracked and budgeted\n\n## Driving case\nUSB-C cable search: supervisor spawns Best Buy worker, worker browses and appends to spreadsheet, supervisor confirms completion.\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d4b716ebe753fa33a036d1633bdf6","created_at":"2026-04-01T23:47:59","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:30:33","priority":2,"tags":["e2e","lifecycle","validation"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4b9406bb73b78ed071eaa138e94a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Worker Status editor panel: live tree view of supervisor/worker state","description":"New editor type that displays the supervisor-worker tree with live status updates. Gives humans visibility into sub-agent state without requiring them to click into each chat tab.\n\n## UI\nTree view in an editor tab:\n```\nSupervisor: Chat 1 (ChatGPT) — running\n  ├─ Best Buy Search (Sonnet) — completed ✓ 12 rounds, 8.2k tokens, 45s\n  ├─ Amazon Search (Sonnet) — running ◉ 5/25 rounds\n  └─ Newegg Search (Haiku) — rate_limited ⚠ retry in 4:30\n```\n\nEach row shows: name, provider/model, status icon+text, rounds used/max, token cost, duration.\n\n## Interactions\n- Click row → switch to that chat tab\n- Status colors: green=completed, blue=running, red=error, yellow=rate_limited, grey=cancelled\n- Auto-scrolls to show newest workers\n- Updates live via signals (agent_chat_finished, worker state changes)\n\n## Implementation\n- Add WORKER_STATUS to Editor.Type enum\n- New WorkerStatusPanel.gd (similar to ActivityLogPanel pattern)\n- Reads from WorkerRegistry (built in minerva_spawn_worker task)\n- Connected to agent_chat_finished signal for live updates\n- Auto-created on first minerva_spawn_worker call, or manually via New menu\n- Add to CreatableItemRegistry for Universal New → Item flow\n\n## Files\n- Editor.gd (new type in enum + create match)\n- New: WorkerStatusPanel.gd\n- WorkerRegistry (data source — built in spawn_worker task)\n- singleton_object.gd (CreatableItemRegistry entry)\n\n## Depends on\n- minerva_spawn_worker (Task 1 — provides WorkerRegistry)\n- Termination classification (Task 2 — provides termination_reason)\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d4b716ebe753fa33a036d1633bdf6","created_at":"2026-04-02T00:24:45","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:30:35","priority":2,"tags":["editor","lifecycle","status","ui"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4f9572a77e64b634db7437ce5f46","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"minerva_get_chat_history doesn't include tool call arguments — only results","description":"When reading chat history via minerva_get_chat_history, tool call results (role: TOOL) include the result JSON, but the corresponding assistant message with tool calls doesn't include the arguments the LLM chose.\n\nThis means an external observer (Claude Code, a supervisor, any MCP client) can see WHAT a tool returned but not WHAT the agent asked for. Debugging tool failures requires seeing both.\n\nExample: a worker searched cobrowser with category=\"webview\" (wrong), causing 0 results. The result message only shows \"No tools found matching 'cobrowser'\" — not that category=\"webview\" was the problem. The supervisor/human can't diagnose the failure from the history alone.\n\nImpact:\n- External clients can't debug agent tool call failures\n- Supervisors can't diagnose why workers failed\n- RCA requires access to Minerva's UI, not just the MCP API\n\nFix: _get_chat_history should include ToolCalls (function name + arguments) on assistant/model messages that have IsToolCall=true. Return them alongside the message text so observers see the full call+result pair.","created_at":"2026-04-02T19:04:47","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:29:04","severity":2,"tags":["chat-history","debugging","mcp","observability","sub-agent"],"resolution":"Fixed: get_chat_history now includes tool_calls_count + tool_names on assistant messages, tool_call_id + tool_name on tool results. New minerva_get_tool_calls tool for full argument zoom."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4ffdecde70609f184af698c71a31","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"WebView crash leaves rendered objects floating over all windows","description":"When a WebView tab crashes and the tab is closed, the objects rendered inside the webview remain visible on screen. They float over all other windows and editors, covering content in any view that uses the same screen space.\n\nExpected: closing a crashed webview tab should remove all rendered content from that webview.\n\nActual: rendered objects persist on screen after tab close, overlapping all active editors and other views.","created_at":"2026-04-02T20:58:54","updated_at":"2026-04-02T22:56:13","severity":2,"tags":["crash","rendering","ui","webview","wry"],"resolution":"Added _exit_tree() to WebViewEditor.gd that explicitly removes and frees the WRY WebView child node when the editor tab is closed. Without this, the native OS-level webview window persisted on screen after the Godot tab was gone."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d4fff7acf74b3bd3d984900dcd9e5","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"spawn_worker parents to wrong chat when multiple workers spawned in sequence","description":"When a supervisor spawns multiple workers in rapid succession (same tool call round), the second worker gets parented to the first worker instead of the supervisor.\n\nRoot cause: _spawn_worker reads parent_chat_id from current_tab. The first spawn switches tabs to the new worker chat. The second spawn reads current_tab which now points to the first worker, not the supervisor.\n\nObserved: supervisor spawned Amazon worker (correctly parented) then Best Buy worker (parented to Amazon worker instead of supervisor). Supervisor itself noticed the anomaly but couldn't fix it. Completion routing would deliver Best Buy's completion to Amazon worker, not the supervisor.\n\nSame bug class as the _send_message tab-switch race and the create_prompt history_override issue. The pattern: any function that reads current_tab during an async operation that also switches tabs will get the wrong value.","created_at":"2026-04-02T21:00:36","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:28:30","severity":1,"tags":["current_tab","parenting","race-condition","spawn-worker","sub-agent"],"resolution":"Fixed: caller_chat_id threaded through tool dispatch chain. spawn_worker uses _current_caller_chat_id instead of current_tab for parent_chat_id."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d51187eeb7bd4b1746b929b3a28bc","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Cobrowser MCP tools/list doesn't expose tab_id/agent_id on browsing tools","description":"The cobrowser MCP server's tools/list response only includes tab_id and agent_id parameters on tab management tools (tab_new, tab_claim, tab_list, tab_release). The browsing tools (navigate, read, query_all, click, scroll, type) do NOT include these parameters in their schemas.\n\nThis means Minerva agents cannot target specific tabs when making cobrowser calls. Parallel multi-tab browsing is impossible from Minerva agents despite tab claiming working correctly.\n\nClaude Code sees tab_id on all tools because it connects to cobrowser via a different MCP transport that exposes the full schemas. The cobrowser HTTP MCP endpoint at /mcp returns different schemas than what Claude Code's STDIO transport provides.\n\nFix location: ~/github/HumanWeb — the cobrowser MCP server's tool registration needs tab_id and agent_id added to ALL browsing tool schemas, not just tab management tools.","created_at":"2026-04-03T02:07:33","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:27:40","severity":1,"tags":["blocker","cobrowser","mcp","parallel-browsing","tab_id"],"resolution":"Fixed: removed CoBrowserMCPClient, unified tool definitions in HumanWeb tool_definitions.py. All 29 tools now have tab_id/agent_id via shared source of truth."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5118914a7c1ab79d8973ea1e1cf5","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Stop button disabled after stopping one chat — shared _active_chat_requests counter breaks with concurrent chats","description":"ChatPane._active_chat_requests is a single integer counter shared across all concurrent chats. When multiple chats run simultaneously (supervisor + workers), stopping one chat decrements the counter. Hitting stop multiple times can drive it to 0 or negative, disabling the stop button while other chats are still running.\n\nRoot cause: the counter was designed for single-chat operation. Sub-agents introduced concurrent chat execution, breaking the assumption that only one chat is active at a time.\n\nFix: replace the global counter with per-chat tracking. Each ChatHistory should track its own active request state. The stop button should check whether the CURRENT tab's chat has active requests, not a global count.","created_at":"2026-04-03T02:07:37","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:27:36","severity":2,"tags":["concurrent-chats","stop-button","sub-agent","ui"],"resolution":"Fixed: replaced global _active_chat_requests with per-chat is_request_active on ServiceHistory. Stop button now reflects current tab state."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5118a02579dea82ae785106b1aef","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"No cascade stop: stopping supervisor doesn't stop its workers","description":"When a user stops a supervisor chat, its spawned workers continue running. There is no mechanism to cascade the stop signal to all workers registered under a supervisor in WorkerRegistry.\n\nExpected: stopping a supervisor should give the option to also stop all its workers. Or at minimum, a 'stop all' button that cancels every active chat.\n\nWorkerRegistry already tracks parent-child relationships. The stop handler needs to look up all workers for the stopped chat and emit stop_all_requests for each.","created_at":"2026-04-03T02:07:41","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:27:38","severity":2,"tags":["cascade","lifecycle","stop","sub-agent","supervisor"],"resolution":"Fixed: stop handler cascades to all workers via WorkerRegistry lookup. Workers cancelled with 'Parent supervisor stopped' message."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5118b20677beb842e1e2e70ec79f","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Tool search ranking puts native_* cobrowser tools above standard browsing tools","description":"Searching for 'cobrowser' activates tab management and native_* OS-level tools in the top 10 results. The standard browsing tools (navigate, read, query_all, click, scroll) are buried in the 'remaining' list and must be activated individually by exact name.\n\nWorkers that search broadly get the wrong tools and either use native_* (which work poorly for normal browsing) or fail to find the tools they need.\n\nFix options:\n1. Adjust tool search scoring to deprioritize native_* variants\n2. Move native_* tools to a separate tool_set not returned by default\n3. Ensure standard browsing tools score higher than native variants in the search index","created_at":"2026-04-03T02:07:46","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:27:34","severity":3,"tags":["cobrowser","native","ranking","tool-search"],"resolution":"Fixed: native_* tools assigned to 'cobrowser-native' tool_set in MCPManager, deprioritized in default search"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d51ef0a837ea295c8ebf8dcf11a10","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plan: Swarm reliability — tool call visibility, auto-inject tab_id, aggressive loop detection","description":"Three targeted fixes to improve swarm reliability based on real test failures.\n\n## Items\n1. Tool call visibility in minerva_get_chat_history — see what arguments workers passed\n2. Auto-inject tab_id/agent_id on worker cobrowser calls — don't rely on LLM to pass them\n3. More aggressive loop detection — stop agents repeating the same failing call\n\n## Context\nThese emerged from multiple swarm test runs where: workers hit cobrowser errors we couldn't diagnose (no argument visibility), workers ignored tab_id instructions (LLM didn't pass them), and workers burned all rounds repeating identical failed calls.\"\n<parameter name=\"tags\">[\"swarm\", \"reliability\", \"plan\"]","created_at":"2026-04-03T06:01:53","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:26:22","priority":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d51ef463c72c899cb031341a25647","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Tool call visibility: tiered history with argument zoom","description":"minerva_get_chat_history currently returns tool results but not tool call arguments. Supervisors and humans can't diagnose why a worker failed.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Change 1: Add lightweight tool info to get_chat_history\nFor each assistant message that has IsToolCall=true, add to the response:\n- tool_calls_count: int\n- tool_names: Array[String]\n\nThis is cheap (~20 tokens per message) and lets observers see THAT tools were called and WHICH ones.\n\n### Change 2: New tool minerva_get_tool_calls\nReturns full tool call details for a specific message in a chat:\n- chat_id: which chat\n- message_index: which message (0-based)\nReturns: Array of {tool_name, arguments, result_summary, status}\n\nThis lets observers zoom into any suspicious message to see exact arguments.\n\nThe pattern: scan cheap (get_chat_history shows tool_names), zoom expensive (get_tool_calls shows arguments).\n\n## Files\n- MinervaMCPServer.gd: modify _get_chat_history to include tool summary, add new _get_tool_calls handler + registration\n- ChatHistoryItem model: tool call data is already stored (ToolCalls, ToolExecutions) — just needs to be exposed in the API\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d51ef0a837ea295c8ebf8dcf11a10","created_at":"2026-04-03T06:02:08","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:26:52","priority":1,"tags":["chat-history","mcp","observability"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d51ef6d4c76b4a76b1d1d67d8bc39","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Auto-inject tab_id/agent_id on worker cobrowser calls","description":"Workers receive tab_id in their task text but LLMs don't reliably pass it on every cobrowser call. Instead of relying on the LLM, the system should inject tab_id and agent_id automatically.\n\n## Design\n\nWhen minerva_spawn_worker is called with tab_id and agent_id in the task or as new parameters:\n- Store tab_id and agent_id on the WorkerInfo in WorkerRegistry\n- When a tool call from that worker's chat hits a cobrowser tool, inject tab_id and agent_id into the arguments before dispatch\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Change 1: Add tab_id/agent_id fields to spawn_worker\nNew optional parameters on minerva_spawn_worker:\n- cobrowser_tab_id: int (optional)\n- cobrowser_agent_id: string (optional)\n\nStore on WorkerInfo.\n\n### Change 2: Auto-inject in tool dispatch\nIn MinervaMCPServer._execute_tool_impl or MCPManager.execute_tool:\n- Check if _current_caller_chat_id maps to a worker in WorkerRegistry\n- If the worker has cobrowser_tab_id set, and the tool being called starts with 'cobrowser_', inject tab_id and agent_id into arguments (don't override if already present)\n\nThis way: supervisor creates tabs, claims them, passes tab_id to spawn_worker, and the system handles the rest. The LLM never needs to remember to pass tab_id.\n\n## Files\n- WorkerRegistry.gd: add cobrowser_tab_id, cobrowser_agent_id to WorkerInfo\n- MinervaMCPServer.gd: spawn_worker params + auto-inject in execute_tool or _execute_tool_impl\n- Possibly MCPManager.gd: if injection happens at the manager level\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d51ef0a837ea295c8ebf8dcf11a10","created_at":"2026-04-03T06:02:18","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:26:54","priority":1,"tags":["auto-inject","cobrowser","spawn-worker","tab_id"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d51ef9a8f781cb982e5d4378dcc04","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Aggressive loop detection: auto-stop after repeated identical failures","description":"Current duplicate call detection warns after 2 repeats but doesn't stop the agent. Agents burn all their tool rounds repeating the same failing call (e.g., cobrowser_navigate on nonexistent tab, or query_all with bad selector).\n\n## Design\n\nAfter N identical failing calls (same tool + same arguments + error result), inject a hard intervention:\n- N=3: inject warning into result: 'You have called this tool 3 times with identical arguments and it failed each time. STOP and try a different approach.'\n- N=5: refuse the call entirely and return an error: 'Tool call blocked: 5 identical failing calls detected. This tool+arguments combination does not work. Try a completely different approach or report that you are blocked.'\n\n## Implementation\n\nThe existing _check_duplicate_call in MinervaMCPServer.gd already tracks consecutive identical calls via _last_call_hash and _consecutive_count. Extend it:\n\n1. Also track whether the result was an error (check for 'error' key or 'success': false)\n2. If consecutive AND error, escalate faster:\n   - 3 consecutive identical errors: strong warning injected\n   - 5 consecutive identical errors: call refused, return block message\n3. Reset counter when: different tool called, or same tool with different arguments, or same tool succeeds\n\n## Files\n- MinervaMCPServer.gd: extend _check_duplicate_call with error tracking and escalation\"\n<parameter name=\"parent\">minerva:019d51ef0a837ea295c8ebf8dcf11a10","created_at":"2026-04-03T06:02:30","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:26:56","priority":2,"tags":["agent-safety","loop-detection","tool-dispatch"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d521b02ac7763823485c9d1285bed","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"agent_chat_finished only fires when IsAgentChat=true — don't also require AgentDefinitionId","created_at":"2026-04-03T06:49:55","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:25:54","tags":["completion-routing","gotcha","signal","spawn-worker"],"value":"The agent_chat_finished signal in ChatPane.gd fires at end of execute_regular_chat and in the stop handler. It was previously guarded by IsAgentChat AND not AgentDefinitionId.is_empty(). spawn_worker chats have IsAgentChat=true but empty AgentDefinitionId. Guard should be IsAgentChat only. The completion routing handler looks up workers by history_id, not definition_id.","key":"agent-chat-finished-guard"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5243bd8070adb72bda314e043735","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Cobrowser: always use cobrowser_tab_new first, never cobrowser_navigate as first action","created_at":"2026-04-03T07:34:24","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:34:24","tags":["browsing","cobrowser","gotcha"],"value":"The browser starts with no usable tabs. cobrowser_navigate targets the active tab — if no tab exists, it fails. Always start with cobrowser_tab_new(url=...) which creates AND navigates. cobrowser_navigate is only for changing URL on an existing tab. LLMs repeatedly get stuck calling navigate on nonexistent tabs.","key":"cobrowser-tab-new-first"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5243cf8b7f5f8cc9ec65d5346f2c","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Provider names: use exact enum name lowercase. CHATGPT not CHATGPT_DEFAULT.","created_at":"2026-04-03T07:34:29","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:34:29","tags":["gotcha","provider","spawn-worker"],"value":"Provider lookup is case-insensitive against enum names. Common mappings: claude_sonnet, claude_haiku, claude_opus, gpt_nano, gpt_standard, gpt_deep, gemini_flash, gemini_pro, chatgpt (=20). Provider 'chatgpt' works. Provider 'ChatGPT' works. Provider 'chatgpt_default' no longer exists — renamed to CHATGPT.","key":"provider-names"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5243e448741a8bb074d30743eab3","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Godot headless syntax check: use grep -i error, not grep -i script|parse","created_at":"2026-04-03T07:34:34","updated_at":"2026-04-03T07:34:34","tags":["godot","gotcha","testing"],"value":"Check syntax: godot --headless --quit 2>&1 | grep -i error | grep -v 'RID allocations|resources still|PagedAllocator|port 9315'. The old pattern grep -i 'script|parse|gdscript' missed SCRIPT ERROR lines. Also: := type inference fails on Variant returns like Dictionary.duplicate() — use explicit type annotation.","key":"godot-headless-check"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5422800f7ba7a903df897963bf88","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Spreadsheet editor colors can render as black on black after formatting","description":"Applying colors in the spreadsheet editor can cause cells to render as black text on black background, making the sheet unreadable. This appears after choosing colors/formatting in the spreadsheet editor and affected both header and body cells during use.","created_at":"2026-04-03T16:17:20","updated_at":"2026-04-03T16:17:20","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bug","color","rendering","spreadsheet","ui"],"environment":"Minerva application spreadsheet editor","repro_steps":"1. Create or open a spreadsheet in the Minerva spreadsheet editor.\n2. Apply formatting to cells, including text/background colors.\n3. Observe the rendered sheet.\n4. Cells may display as black text on black background regardless of the chosen colors."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d542a522b7b668947bffb38db3e55","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Fit and finish: OpenDeck startup and streamdeck chat/PTT improvements","description":"Fit-and-finish improvements for Minerva:\n\n1. On Minerva startup, also start OpenDeck when running on Linux.\n   - First check whether OpenDeck is already running.\n   - Do not restart it if it is already active.\n   - Startup command: flatpak run me.amankhanna.opendeck\n\n2. Add a 'submit chat' button to the streamdeck plugins.\n\n3. When using PTT via streamdeck:\n   - focus the chat input box\n   - place the cursor at the end of the current input text\n   - ensure the input is ready for immediate continued typing","created_at":"2026-04-03T16:25:52","updated_at":"2026-04-03T16:25:52","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["chat","fnf","linux","opendeck","ptt","startup","streamdeck","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5430472f727881bf79165690ab17","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add a todo plugin with categories, title/description, and docket links","description":"Add a todo plugin to Minerva.\n\nInitial concept:\n- supports free-form categories\n- has a title field\n- has a description field\n- can link to docket items\n\nThe plugin should provide lightweight task capture while preserving the ability to connect todos to more structured planning/work tracking in docket.","created_at":"2026-04-03T16:32:23","updated_at":"2026-04-03T16:32:23","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["docket-integration","planning","plugin","todo","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d555a746577c18fcc652b18db3a40","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Organic quality management for Docket knowledge","description":"Integrate Docket's quality scoring into Minerva's agent workflow so knowledge quality is maintained organically by working agents.\n\n## Design\nThree lightweight mechanisms, no dedicated curator agent:\n\n### 1. Standing instruction in dynamically-loaded notes\nAdd to agent context: \"When you use knowledge from Docket (KBs, hints, insights, skills) and it causes errors or is outdated, call docket_quality to downgrade. When knowledge proves accurate and useful, upgrade it. Score: -5 (causes errors) to +5 (reliably useful). Don't deliberate — quick score, brief reason, move on.\"\n\n### 2. Lightweight periodic injection\nTime-triggered user injection (not a full task, just a reminder): \"Have you followed the content quality guidance recently?\"\n- ~10 tokens, minimal disruption\n- Re-activates the standing instruction when it fades over long sessions\n- Frequency TBD — maybe every few hours or configurable\n\n### 3. Human override via Docket UI\nDocket UI (integrated into Minerva) lets humans review and modify quality scores at any time. No special workflow — same record form used for all item types.\n\n## Rationale\nAgents doing real work have the best context for quality assessment — they just experienced the failure or success. A dedicated curator reading items in isolation lacks that context. The nag approach (random \"did you save knowledge?\" injections) was tried and failed. This approach is lighter: a standing instruction that agents follow naturally, with a rare nudge as safety net.\n\n## Dependencies\n- Docket DCR: Quality scoring for knowledge items (provides the quality field and docket_quality tool)\n- Docket DCR: Knowledge types (provides the types that get scored)\n- Docket UI integration into Minerva (provides human override surface)","created_at":"2026-04-03T21:58:04","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:12:24","priority":3,"tags":["agent-workflow","integration","knowledge","quality"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d555ca2f274d19501ffed976ab9cd","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Plan: Organic quality management integration","description":"Implementation plan for Minerva-side quality management. Covers standing instruction text, notes integration, periodic injection mechanism, and integration testing.","created_at":"2026-04-03T22:00:27","updated_at":"2026-04-03T22:00:27","tags":["integration","plan","quality"],"parent":"minerva:019d555a746577c18fcc652b18db3a40"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d555deb317d0d936b5ac162f51ee3","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Draft standing instruction text for quality guidance","description":"Write the actual instruction text that goes into Minerva's dynamically-loaded notes. Concise, actionable: when to upgrade, when to downgrade, score semantics, tool name, don't deliberate. Test with actual agents to verify it produces quality scoring behavior without being intrusive.","created_at":"2026-04-03T22:01:51","updated_at":"2026-04-03T22:01:51","tags":["prompt-design","quality"],"parent":"minerva:019d555ca2f274d19501ffed976ab9cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d555df749740c9e2b3060668cdbcf","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add quality guidance to Minerva notes system","description":"Integrate the quality instruction into Minerva's dynamic notes loading so it's included in agent context for all chats. Determine where it lives (built-in note, auto-loaded from master docket, or config-driven). Ensure it survives compaction.","created_at":"2026-04-03T22:01:54","updated_at":"2026-04-03T22:01:54","tags":["integration","notes"],"parent":"minerva:019d555ca2f274d19501ffed976ab9cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d555e076e7288a639ca72d1a1d659","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implement lightweight periodic quality reminder injection","description":"Add a time-triggered user injection: \"Have you followed the content quality guidance recently?\" Configurable frequency (default: every few hours). Minimal text (~10 tokens). Uses Minerva's existing injection/trigger mechanism. Not a full task — just a re-activation nudge for the standing instruction.","created_at":"2026-04-03T22:01:58","updated_at":"2026-04-03T22:01:58","tags":["injection","triggers"],"parent":"minerva:019d555ca2f274d19501ffed976ab9cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d555e131c7707b4acffab2b58d43f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Integration test: agents call docket_quality during work","description":"End-to-end validation: agent with quality guidance in context, uses a Docket knowledge item, encounters an error caused by stale knowledge, calls docket_quality to downgrade. Also: agent uses accurate knowledge, calls docket_quality to upgrade. Verify scores persist and events recorded.","created_at":"2026-04-03T22:02:01","updated_at":"2026-04-03T22:02:01","tags":["integration","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d555ca2f274d19501ffed976ab9cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a98c26671d8bb20e2e51205589f","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"Refactor MinervaMCPServer god file into modular tool domains","description":"MinervaMCPServer.gd is 11,737 lines containing all 22 tool domains (registration, dispatch, and handler implementations) in a single file. Refactor into ~16 domain modules averaging ~650 lines each, a shared utilities library, and a slim core server (~800 lines). Goals: files ~1 KLoC, remove dead code, DRY/SOLID, functional test coverage, easy maintenance.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:13","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:29:13","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","maintainability","mcp","refactor"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a","type":"work_item","status":"open","title":"MCP refactor implementation plan","description":"Step-by-step plan for splitting MinervaMCPServer.gd (11,737 lines) into modular domain files. Covers: base class design, domain module extraction, shared utilities, core server slimming, dead code removal, and test coverage.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:20","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:29:04","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","plan","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98c26671d8bb20e2e51205589f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a99059f76178fa0e5c80f0cc9c0","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"Dead code audit of MinervaMCPServer.gd","description":"Before splitting, identify and remove dead code: tools registered but never dispatched, helper functions never called, commented-out blocks, stale references (e.g. CoBrowserMCPClient). Produces a clean baseline for extraction.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:31","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:31:02","priority":2,"tags":["cleanup","dead-code","mcp"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a99119f7291a8efbd4dfeab276b","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"Create MCPToolModule base class and MCPToolUtils","description":"Create MCPToolModule.gd (~100 lines): abstract base with register_tools() and handle() interface. Create MCPToolUtils.gd (~300-400 lines): shared utilities — error/success response builders, find_editor(name, type), require_args validation, coerce_int/coerce_color type coercion, parse_cell_range. These are the foundation all domain modules depend on.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:34","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:32:23","priority":1,"tags":["foundation","mcp","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a991d8c72018d067d7475be40a9","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Extract MCPChatTools module","description":"Extract chat domain tools into MCP/Modules/MCPChatTools.gd (~800 lines): create_chat, send_message, get_chat_history, list_chats, close_chat, get_tool_calls, compact_chat, set_system_prompt, set_agent_mode. Includes completion routing and caller identity threading.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:37","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:37","priority":2,"tags":["chat","mcp","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a9924c0766db4e9c4dcff327da5","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Extract MCPNotesTools module","description":"Extract notes domain into MCP/Modules/MCPNotesTools.gd (~600 lines): create_note, create_note_tab, list_notes, get_note, update_note, delete_note, enable_notes, disable_notes, link_note_to_chat.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:39","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:39","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","notes","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a992d7b7ae7aa48f0065eba9395","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Extract MCPEditorTools module","description":"Extract editor domain into MCP/Modules/MCPEditorTools.gd (~500 lines): create_text_editor, create_graphics_editor, create_pcb_editor, create_spreadsheet_editor, create_video_editor, create_webview_panel, list_editors, get_editor_content, close_editor, save_editor, rename_editor, update_editor.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:41","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:41","priority":2,"tags":["editor","mcp","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a993d2e7e048b7d38ff0c8913c5","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Extract MCPSpreadsheetTools and MCPChartTools modules","description":"Extract spreadsheet domain into MCP/Modules/MCPSpreadsheetTools.gd (~1000 lines): get/update data, format_cells, add/delete/insert rows/cols, set_cell_formula, set_row_height, set_column_width, fill_down, recalculate, undo/redo, history, link_to_note, export_to_nudge. Extract chart tools into MCPChartTools.gd (~500 lines): create/update/delete/list/refresh charts, get_chart_image.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:45","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:45","priority":2,"tags":["chart","mcp","refactor","spreadsheet"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a9952fe7161b232a7914202bcd7","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Extract MCPPCBTools module","description":"Extract PCB domain into MCP/Modules/MCPPCBTools.gd (~1000 lines): add/delete/move/rotate components, connect_net, get_components/nets/image, annotations, route hints, spatial query, import/export CSV/YAML/trace geometry, create_note, describe_component, board sizing.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:50","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:50","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","pcb","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a99573c72ab96170f7f0744b421","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Extract MCPAgentTools module (agents + workers + triggers)","description":"Extract agent domain into MCP/Modules/MCPAgentTools.gd (~1000 lines): create/delete/list/update agents, spawn_worker, check_worker, list_workers, set/get_worker_budget, approve_workers, create/list/update/delete/fire triggers. Includes worker completion routing signal wiring.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:52","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:52","priority":2,"tags":["agents","mcp","refactor","triggers","workers"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a99657b71b293152d0b6125fbe4","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Extract remaining domain modules","description":"Extract remaining smaller domains into individual modules: MCPAutocoderTools.gd (~800), MCPModelTools.gd (models + cost + meta, ~800), MCPSkillTools.gd (skills + voice, ~500), MCPContainerTools.gd (~500), MCPCodeTools.gd (file ops + bash + cwd, ~600), MCPTerminalTools.gd (~500), MCPWebviewTools.gd (~400), MCPVideoTools.gd (~600), MCPKanbanTools.gd (~400).","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:55","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:55","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","refactor","remaining-domains"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a997006765097e0f9359a9f8098","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"Slim MinervaMCPServer.gd to core dispatch (~800 lines)","description":"After all domain modules are extracted, MinervaMCPServer.gd becomes a thin shell: module loading/registration, dispatch loop (iterate modules to find handler), _register_tool helper, tool search index wiring, signal wiring (agent_chat_finished etc.), caller identity threading. Target ~800 lines.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:24:58","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:59:12","priority":2,"tags":["core","mcp","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a997d397d66adee9e166ab971ca","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Add functional test coverage for MCP tool modules","description":"Create test infrastructure: GUT/GdUnit4 unit tests for pure logic (arg parsing, response formatting, utilities, type coercion) and a Python integration test harness that connects to Minerva's HTTP MCP server for critical paths (create chat → send message → get history, spreadsheet CRUD, notes CRUD). Target: every module has at least smoke tests for its handlers.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:25:01","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:25:01","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","quality","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a9ac3ca73e3837515e97d94837c","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Syntax check: godot --headless validation after refactor","description":"Run `godot --headless` against the project to verify no parse errors, missing references, or broken class_name registrations after the MCP module extraction. Should pass clean with no errors before merging.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:26:25","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:26:25","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","refactor","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a9ace5d7b66b388e15c65161894","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Run functional tests after refactor","description":"Execute the full test suite (GUT/GdUnit4 unit tests + MCP integration tests) after extraction is complete to verify all tool handlers still work correctly. No regressions in tool registration, dispatch, or response shape.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:26:28","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:26:28","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","refactor","testing","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5a9c89337c48bb33b73bd0cf4b69","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"Code review and test-case validation (Opus)","description":"After Sonnet agents complete domain extraction and before final merge: Opus reviews all extracted modules for correctness, consistency with base class contract, DRY violations, missed dead code, proper error handling, and response shape consistency. Validates test cases cover critical paths and edge cases. This is the quality gate before integration.","created_at":"2026-04-04T22:28:21","updated_at":"2026-04-04T22:50:02","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","quality-gate","refactor","review"],"parent":"minerva:019d5a98dcd57e648f84a287f1b51d1a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b81c3d9761eab720120378a2ec5","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"Native docket integration into Minerva","description":"Absorb Docket into Minerva as a native subsystem. Docket was an experiment in ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/docket that succeeded — it is being promoted to the main Minerva codebase.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. **Docket core as native GDScript** — import docket's core engine (DocketDB, DataModel, StateMachine, FileManager, etc.) into Minerva's source tree. Not a submodule — the code moves permanently.\n\n2. **DocketEditorPanel** — Docket becomes another editor type in Minerva (like PCB, spreadsheet, video). Query grid as main view, record form for detail, kanban as a view mode toggle. Full human interface for browsing, editing, approving transitions, activating policies.\n\n3. **DocketManager singleton** — lazy-loading manager on SingletonObject. Master.dct + personal.dct load at startup. Project dockets load on demand.\n\n4. **MCPDocketTools module** — expose docket tools through Minerva's MCP interface. Tiered availability: docket_skill_list/docket_skill_get always available (master docket); all other tools require a project docket to be loaded.\n\n5. **Direct signal wiring** — docket state changes emit Godot signals (item_transitioned, item_created, etc.). Supervisors subscribe directly. Replaces DOCKET_POLL entirely.\n\n6. **Skills as discoverable knowledge** — system prompt mentions available skills by name. Agents call docket_skill_get to load on demand. Existing drawer notes get manually migrated to master.dct skills.\n\n## Persistence model\n\n- **Master docket**: ships with Minerva at res://Data/master.dct with base skills. Copied to user://master.dct on first run. Accumulates knowledge over time.\n- **Personal docket**: created on demand at user://personal.dct when first needed (hint_set, personal item). Most users never trigger this.\n- **Project dockets**: live wherever the project lives. Minerva maintains a registry at user://docket_projects.cfg for \"Open Recent\" support.\n- **Normal save**: .minproj does NOT include docket data — docket self-persists to its own .dct file.\n- **Export/package**: .minproj embeds associated project docket(s) inline for the recipient.\n\n## Policy type (future)\n\nAdd a `policy` type to docket schema for gated workflows. Asymmetric gating: anyone can increase gating intensity, decrease requires human approval. Agents can author/propose policy, only humans activate.\n\n## Supersedes\n\n- DOCKET_POLL trigger mechanism (replaced by direct signals)\n- External docket MCP server for Minerva agents (replaced by native integration)\n- Kanban as separate system (becomes a view mode in docket editor)","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:38:44","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:44:08","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","docket","integration","stage1"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Plan: Native docket integration implementation","description":"Implementation plan for native docket integration. Work is ordered by dependency — foundation first, then UI, then MCP, then polish.\n\n## Phase 1: Foundation\n1. Import docket core into Minerva source tree\n2. Add godot-sqlite addon\n3. Create DocketManager singleton (lazy loading, master/personal at startup)\n4. Docket project registry (user://docket_projects.cfg)\n\n## Phase 2: Editor\n5. Add DOCKET_EDITOR type + DocketEditorPanel (query grid, record form)\n6. Adapt docket UI widgets to Minerva editor panel pattern\n\n## Phase 3: MCP + Signals\n7. Create MCPDocketTools module (tiered tool availability)\n8. Signal wiring — replace DOCKET_POLL with direct docket signals\n9. Update default agentic system prompt with skill catalog\n\n## Phase 4: Persistence + Polish\n10. Master docket: ship base skills at res://Data/master.dct, copy to user:// on first run\n11. Personal docket: create on demand at user://personal.dct\n12. .minproj export: embed project docket(s)\n\n## Phase 5: Policy (future)\n13. Add policy type to docket schema with asymmetric gating","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:38:56","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:44:10","tags":["docket","integration","plan"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81c3d9761eab720120378a2ec5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b821e977c2f9a5e7a5a6581890e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Import docket core into Minerva source tree","description":"Copy docket's core engine from ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/docket/scripts/core/ into Minerva's source tree at src/Scripts/Services/Docket/Core/. Files: docket_db.gd, docket_db_jsonl.gd, docket_db_schema.gd, docket_db_filter.gd, file_manager.gd, jsonl_cache.gd, jsonl_parser.gd, jsonl_serializer.gd, data_model.gd, state_machine.gd, vault_crypto.gd, file_lock.gd, user_prefs.gd. Also copy data/schema.json. Adapt class_names if needed to avoid collisions. Verify clean parse with godot --headless.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:07","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:48:22","tags":["docket","integration","phase1"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b82276e7adc81e7803297be6d4a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add godot-sqlite addon to Minerva","description":"Copy godot-sqlite GDExtension from docket's addons/godot-sqlite/ into Minerva's src/addons/godot-sqlite/. Includes .gdextension file and platform binaries. Verify Minerva loads the extension cleanly.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:09","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:48:23","tags":["dependency","docket","integration","phase1"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b8235b17a2eaf9f0fa67cede19e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Create DocketManager singleton","description":"Create DocketManager on SingletonObject. Responsibilities:\n- Load master.dct (user://) and personal.dct (user://, if exists) at startup\n- Lazy-load project dockets on demand\n- Track loaded/unloaded state per project\n- Emit signals: docket_item_created, docket_item_transitioned, docket_item_updated, docket_comment_added\n- Provide get_db(project_name) accessor for MCPDocketTools and DocketEditorPanel\n- Handle project open/close lifecycle","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:13","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:55:23","tags":["architecture","docket","integration","phase1"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b8241fe7efda730a9805fb12c64","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Docket project registry and recent projects","description":"Persist known docket project paths at user://docket_projects.cfg (or similar). Updated on open. Supports \"Open Recent\" in menu and MCP (docket_project_list returns known projects with loaded/unloaded state). Project dockets live wherever the user puts them — registry just remembers the path.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:16","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:55:59","tags":["docket","integration","phase1"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b8253577e08be67ceac4f64fd9f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add DOCKET_EDITOR type and DocketEditorPanel","description":"Add DOCKET_EDITOR to Editor.Type enum. Create DocketEditorPanel following PCBEditor pattern (_build_ui). Main view is the query grid (filterable item table). Clicking an item opens the record form for detail/editing. Support multiple docket editors open simultaneously (one per project or filtered views). Wire into Editor.create(), Editor._ready() button hiding, EditorPane.add() tab naming.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:20","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:03:27","tags":["docket","integration","phase2","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b8260027c1182ffca2620ac33d9","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Adapt docket UI widgets to Minerva editor panel pattern","description":"Port docket's UI code (query_grid.gd, record_form.gd, menu_builder.gd) from standalone app layout to Minerva editor panel widgets. Build UI in _build_ui() like PCBEditor. Adapt to Minerva's theme system. Add kanban view mode toggle (list/grid vs board layout). Connect to DocketManager signals for live updates when agents mutate items.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:24","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:03:29","tags":["docket","integration","phase2","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b8284377715bbbc8166823d2799","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Create MCPDocketTools module","description":"Create MCPDocketTools.gd following MCPToolModule pattern. Wraps DocketManager/DocketDB calls — no HTTP. Tiered availability:\n- Always available (master docket): docket_skill_list, docket_skill_get\n- Require loaded project: all other 30+ tools (docket_create, docket_query, docket_transition, docket_hint_set/get/query, docket_quality, docket_comment, docket_project_list/add/remove, etc.)\n- Tools return clear error when no project loaded\nRegister all tools through Minerva's existing MCP server.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:33","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:55:24","tags":["docket","integration","mcp","phase3"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b8291ab78559fbabec11e9895c4","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Replace DOCKET_POLL with direct docket signals","description":"Remove the DOCKET_POLL trigger type from TriggerManager. Replace with direct signal subscriptions from DocketManager. Supervisor chats subscribe to docket_item_transitioned, docket_item_created, etc. Wake happens on the same frame as the state change — no polling latency. Remove the HTTP polling infrastructure and related debounce/anti-flood code.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:36","updated_at":"2026-04-05T04:52:17","tags":["docket","integration","phase3","signals"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b829d4370babcf02e89e621f66f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Update default agentic system prompt with skill catalog","description":"Update Minerva's default agentic system prompt to mention that skills are available. Include skill names/one-liners from master.dct so the LLM knows they exist without searching. Agent calls docket_skill_get to load full steps when needed. Keep token cost low (~5 tokens per skill in catalog).","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:39","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:07:21","tags":["docket","integration","phase3","prompt"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b82ab617066a4b402186453bed4","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Master docket: ship base skills and first-run copy","description":"Ship a master.dct at res://Data/master.dct containing base skills (agent supervision, tool usage patterns, etc.). On first run, DocketManager copies to user://master.dct. Subsequent runs use the user:// copy which accumulates knowledge. Consider merge strategy for Minerva updates that add new base skills without clobbering user additions.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:43","updated_at":"2026-04-05T04:54:11","tags":["docket","integration","persistence","phase4"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b82b4897931b086b2db947d7a3d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Personal docket: create on demand","description":"DocketManager creates user://personal.dct on first write (hint_set, personal item creation, etc.). Not created at startup — most users may never need it. DocketManager checks for existence at startup and loads if present.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:45","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:56:00","tags":["docket","integration","persistence","phase4"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b82c2d57015b7700fa94f61771d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":".minproj export: embed project docket(s)","description":"When exporting/packaging a .minproj, embed associated project docket(s) inline. Normal save operations do NOT include docket data — docket self-persists to its own .dct file. On import, extract embedded docket to a path and load via DocketManager. Update the export/import code paths in project management.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:49","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:49","tags":["docket","integration","persistence","phase4"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b82cf4573cd82e1bb567de0f871","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add policy type to docket schema","description":"Add a `policy` type to docket's schema.json with state machine: draft → proposed → active → suspended → archived. Asymmetric gating: anyone (including agents) can increase gating intensity, only humans can decrease or deactivate. Agents can author draft policies and propose them. Fields TBD: scope, rules, enforcement_level. This is future work — design to be refined as gated workflows take shape.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:39:52","updated_at":"2026-04-05T04:54:13","tags":["docket","integration","phase5","policy"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b8377a072cbabd4d7ed824702ce","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Syntax check: godot --headless validation after each phase","description":"Run godot --headless --quit after each phase to verify clean parse. Catches class_name collisions, missing dependencies, orphaned references, and broken imports. Critical gate — no phase proceeds until the previous phase parses cleanly. Remember: new class_name files may require rm -rf .godot && godot --headless --import to rebuild uid_cache.","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:40:35","updated_at":"2026-04-05T04:59:16","tags":["docket","integration","syntax","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b838b56780787d258f63a2a8166","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Functional tests: DocketManager lifecycle","description":"Functional tests covering:\n- Master docket loads at startup, personal docket created on demand\n- Project docket lazy-load from registry path\n- Open/close project lifecycle (load, query, unload)\n- Signal emission on item create, transition, update, comment\n- Skill list/get works against master docket without project loaded\n- Other docket tools return clear error when no project loaded\n- DocketEditorPanel opens, displays query grid, record form navigation\n- .minproj export embeds docket, import extracts and loads it\n- Multiple docket editors open simultaneously","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:40:40","updated_at":"2026-04-05T04:59:13","tags":["docket","functional","integration","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b839b7b7df8a0867dcf6413df25","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Functional tests: MCPDocketTools end-to-end","description":"End-to-end MCP tests:\n- All 30+ docket tools callable through Minerva's MCP interface\n- Tool tiering: skill_list/skill_get work without project, others require loaded project\n- CRUD cycle: create item → query → update → transition → verify state machine enforcement\n- Hint set/get/query round-trip\n- Quality scoring on knowledge items\n- Cross-project references resolve correctly\n- Error responses are well-formed for missing project, bad ID, invalid transition","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:40:44","updated_at":"2026-04-05T04:59:14","tags":["docket","functional","integration","mcp","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019d5b81f5a07305a998677d7e3e0bc5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b85cfc27109a8ec266edb50d27e","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"docket/cross-project-links","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:43:09","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:43:09","tags":["cross-project","docket","gotcha","links"],"value":"docket_link with cross-project qualified IDs (e.g. \"docket:019d4595c391\") fails with \"Item not found\" when linking from a different project context. The target item must be resolvable within the calling project's DB, or the link tool doesn't support cross-project references in the `to` field. Workaround: link within the same project, or use comments/description to reference cross-project items by ID.","component":"docket","key":"cross-project-links","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b85de6d76b0855efcdf1cc8945b","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"docket-minerva-integration/autonomous-workflow","created_at":"2026-04-05T02:43:13","updated_at":"2026-04-05T02:43:13","tags":["agents","integration","pattern","workflow"],"value":"For large integration work: Opus handles architecture (DocketManager, signals, test design) sequentially for coherence. Sonnet agents in worktrees handle mechanical work (file imports, pattern-following modules, boilerplate). Haiku for trivial copies and syntax checks. godot --headless --quit validates after each merge. Docket's own 120+ test suite validates core import. Human testing only at the end for visual/UX verification. Commit clean checkpoints after each phase for rollback.","component":"docket-minerva-integration","key":"autonomous-workflow","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5b98935d7a49a338fa680c022a73","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"godot/uid-cache-rebuild","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:03:39","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:03:39","tags":["build","class_name","godot","gotcha"],"value":"When adding new .gd files with class_name to any Godot project: rm -rf .godot && godot --headless --import --path &lt;project&gt;/ to rebuild uid_cache. Without this, new class_names are not found and cause cascading \"not declared in current scope\" errors. Partial cache rebuilds don't work — must delete entire .godot directory.","component":"godot","key":"uid-cache-rebuild","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c3537f57352b58ef72cfe53919f","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"minerva/mcp-config-location","created_at":"2026-04-05T05:54:44","updated_at":"2026-04-05T05:54:44","tags":["config","mcp","minerva","path"],"value":"Minerva's MCP server configuration is at ~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/Minerva/mcp_config.json. Contains server definitions (name, url, type, auto_connect), installation_paths, python_environment, and enabled_tool_groups. Version 3 format.","component":"minerva","key":"mcp-config-location","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c400a0b70669d5dca614cef9b82","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"docket-ui/query-grid-api","created_at":"2026-04-05T06:06:33","updated_at":"2026-04-05T06:06:33","tags":["api","docket","gotcha","ui"],"value":"QueryGrid's query execution method is private: _run_query(). Use the public refresh() method instead. load_item(id) on RecordForm is public.","component":"docket-ui","key":"query-grid-api","research_cost":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c98f59575d3baa8f1fd316fd4e1","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"docket-minerva-integration/ui-pattern","created_at":"2026-04-05T07:43:41","updated_at":"2026-04-05T07:43:41","tags":["decision","docket","minerva","pattern","ui"],"value":"Docket UI in Minerva: editor tab with its own MenuBar inside. NOT a pane-level mode (tried and rejected — couldn't close it, wasn't a tab). NOT a toy panel (tried and rejected — lost all docket functionality). The full DocketPanel (adapted AppShell) with File/View/Work/Help menus lives inside a standard editor tab. Only one docket tab allowed. Tools > Docket opens or switches to it.","component":"docket-minerva-integration","key":"ui-pattern","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5ca6747c75f59628e2d565f392e0","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"minerva/tool-activation-strategy","created_at":"2026-04-05T07:58:25","updated_at":"2026-04-05T07:58:25","tags":["activation","mcp","minerva","tokens","tools"],"value":"Minerva uses search/activation for token efficiency. Tools are registered in ToolSearchIndex (discoverable) but NOT auto-activated. Only key tools get server.tool_budget_manager.activate_tool() in register_tools(). The rest are activated on demand when LLM calls minerva_tool_search. If an unactivated tool is called, it returns 'not loaded, call minerva_tool_search to activate'. For docket: only docket_skill_list, docket_skill_get, minerva_open_docket are auto-activated.","component":"minerva","key":"tool-activation-strategy","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5ecc7ed779bd957f4c4f847060f6","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"ab-testing/test-design-lesson","created_at":"2026-04-05T17:59:13","updated_at":"2026-04-05T17:59:13","tags":["agent-behavior","insight","learning","testing"],"value":"When A/B testing agent learning behavior, the test scenario must involve knowledge that CANNOT be derived from tool APIs. If the agent can get the answer from an authoritative source (like docket_get_state_machine), it will never check hints and never encounter gotchas. The gotcha must be something only prior experience reveals — not something the tool tells you upfront.","component":"ab-testing","key":"test-design-lesson","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5f00059b76ada330238e61b0f1bc","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Agentic knowledge management: association rules, CRUD lifecycle, and policy triggers","description":"Design discussion for how Minerva agents learn, recall, and maintain knowledge across sessions. Covers system prompt design, knowledge types, CRUD lifecycle, and the unsolved association/trigger problem.\n\n## Context\nPart of DCR 019d5b81c3d9 (Native docket integration into Minerva). Docket is now natively integrated — the question is how agents USE it for knowledge effectively.\n\n## What we've established\n\n### Three always-available tools\n- `minerva_tool_search` — discover and activate tools\n- `minerva_list_skills` — browse skill catalog (cross-project, lean)\n- `minerva_get_skill` — load full skill instructions\n\n### System prompt design (Option D)\nPrompts stored as docket items (type=prompt, component=system-prompt) in master.dct. Cascade: project → master → hardcoded fallback. Only status=active prompts loaded. Agents can draft but not self-activate prompts.\n\nCurrent best candidate (~70 tokens):\n> You are an AI assistant with access to tools. Before unfamiliar work, check minerva_list_skills for guides. Before consequential actions, check for relevant policies and preferences. Save knowledge that future agents can't discover from tools alone — workarounds, preferences, failure patterns, things to avoid. Correct wrong knowledge, downgrade outdated knowledge. Be targeted and efficient.\n\n### Knowledge types in docket\n- **Hints**: component/key micro-facts. Workarounds, commands, gotchas.\n- **Insights**: mental model corrections (assumed X, corrected to Y).\n- **KBs**: longer articles, guides, reference docs.\n- **Skills**: step-by-step executable pipelines.\n- **Prompts**: system prompt fragments, project-overridable.\n\n### Knowledge CRUD lifecycle\n- **Create**: Save knowledge that tools can't tell future agents — workarounds, preferences, failure patterns, cross-system links, negative knowledge.\n- **Retrieve**: Check hints/insights before unfamiliar or consequential work.\n- **Update**: When knowledge is wrong, overwrite via docket_hint_set (upsert) or correct via docket_quality scoring.\n- **Delete/Forget**: Downgrade outdated knowledge via docket_quality(-5).\n\n### The augmentation layer\nAgents can self-discover from tool APIs: schemas, state machines, parameters, error messages. Knowledge is only valuable when it ADDS to what tools provide:\n- Preferences (how the user likes things done)\n- Workarounds (when the documented approach doesn't work)\n- Cross-system links (change A requires updating B)\n- Negative knowledge (what NOT to do)\n- Temporal context (what's broken right now)\n- Performance heuristics (this is slow, use that instead)\n- Domain conventions (this team uses X pattern)\n- Failure patterns (this API intermittently 500s)\n\n### A/B testing results\nTested 4 prompt variants with Sonnet sub-agents:\n\n| Variant | Score | Key finding |\n|---|---|---|\n| A: explicit tool names | 15% | Used get_state_machine, bypassed hints entirely |\n| B: soft guidance | 17.5% | Checked nudge (wrong place), also bypassed hints |\n| C: control (no learning) | 15% | No retrieval, no learning |\n| Augmentation prompt (simple) | 70% | Retrieved + applied all hints, didn't create new |\n| Augmentation prompt (comprehensive) | 77.5% | Retrieved all types, corrected wrong hint, missed KB downgrade |\n\nKey lesson: test scenarios must involve knowledge that CANNOT be derived from tool APIs. If tools provide the answer, agents won't check hints.\n\n## The unsolved problem: association triggers\n\nThe current system relies on the AGENT choosing to check for knowledge. This fails for:\n1. Routine actions (git commands are familiar — agent won't check for policies)\n2. Cross-domain associations (user says 'no FCIB' — how does agent connect this to git push?)\n3. Policies that apply to tool categories, not specific tools\n\n### Example: FCIB policy\nUser tells agent: 'I maintain a no-FCIB policy.'\nAgent saves: component='git', key='fcib-policy', value='No force-pushes to integration branches.'\nLater, agent runs: git push --force\n→ Nothing triggers recall. The agent doesn't check git hints before routine bash commands.\n\n### Proposed solution: association rules\nThe agent doesn't have a 'git tool' — it runs git via bash. When it runs a bash command matching certain patterns, an **association trigger** should fire a one-time reminder.\n\nThis is NOT the same as the docket_hint_query the agent does voluntarily. This is a SYSTEM-LEVEL mechanism:\n- Pattern: bash command contains 'git push' → inject reminder about git policies\n- Pattern: file edit in /src/api/ → inject reminder about API conventions\n- Pattern: docket_transition to 'shipped' → inject reminder about release checklist\n\nThis could be implemented as:\n- **PreToolUse hooks** that match command patterns and inject context\n- **Tool-activation injection** — when certain tools are activated via minerva_tool_search, relevant policies are included in the response\n- **Always-injected policies** — critical policies in notes/prompts, injected every turn (expensive but guaranteed)\n\n### Open questions\n1. Where do association rules live? Docket items? A config file? Code?\n2. How are rules authored? By the user? By agents? Both?\n3. How do we keep injection token-efficient? (Don't inject everything every turn)\n4. How does the agent know WHAT to save as an association rule vs a regular hint?\n5. Can association rules be project-scoped? (Different projects have different policies)\n6. How do we handle rule conflicts? (Project says X, master says Y)\n\n### Next steps\n- Design the association rule data model (possibly a new docket type, or a field on hints)\n- Prototype PreToolUse hook injection for bash commands\n- Test whether hook-injected reminders change agent behavior\n- Consider whether this collapses into the existing Minerva hook system (knowledge-routes.py)\"}","created_at":"2026-04-05T18:55:30","updated_at":"2026-04-05T22:27:49","tags":["agent-behavior","architecture","docket-integration","knowledge","system-prompt"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5fc13ad570a3bada814ce9aded30","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Deterministic associated knowledge: pre-tool risk routing and policy enforcement","description":"Minerva currently has two adjacent but insufficient capabilities:\n\n1. **Knowledge retrieval is voluntary** — agents may choose to query hints/skills/KBs, but routine actions and cross-domain policies are easy to miss.\n2. **PreToolUse hooks are advisory only** — current hook routing can inject a hint before tool execution, but it does not deterministically modify, gate, or block the pending action.\n\nThis creates a gap for associated knowledge: user preferences, project policies, and high-cost failure patterns that must be recalled reliably when specific actions are attempted.\n\nExamples:\n- User preference: no force-pushes to integration branches\n- Project rule: release transitions require a checklist artifact\n- Safety rule: do not edit generated files directly\n- Workflow rule: changing API schema requires corresponding client regeneration\n\nThe key design requirement is:\n\n**Association detection must be deterministic. Acting on advisory context can remain an LLM choice; blocking unsafe actions cannot.**\n\n## Decision\n\nAdd a first-class associated-knowledge runtime to Minerva that sits on the pre-tool path and evaluates structured rules against normalized action context.\n\nThe system has four layers:\n\n1. **Knowledge**\nHuman-readable hints, insights, KBs, prompts, and rationale stored in Docket.\n\n2. **Associations / risk rules**\nStructured executable records derived from knowledge or authored directly. These map action/context predicates to risk scopes and consequences.\n\n3. **Runtime scope state**\nShort-lived chat/session state activated by broad domains or sequences (e.g. git, deploy, release). Scope activation is usually invisible to the model.\n\n4. **Consequences**\nDeterministic outcomes when an active rule matches a pending action:\n- `observe` — log only, no agent-visible effect\n- `scope` — activate a runtime scope, no visible reminder\n- `block` — reject the tool call with a structured failure response\n\n`warn` / `ack` are intentionally deferred. The main risk is reminder fatigue. MVP should optimize for precision and hard safety, not frequent nudges.\n\n## Core principle\n\nDo **not** try to make semantic memory itself deterministic. Make the **rule engine** deterministic.\n\nThe runtime path must not depend on:\n- agent deciding to query knowledge\n- embedding similarity in the hot path\n- stochastic firing rates\n- freeform prose interpretation at execution time\n\nInstead:\n- normalize each pending tool call into deterministic action facts\n- evaluate active rules synchronously\n- block when a promoted high-confidence rule says block\n- otherwise continue normally\n\n## Why this is a separate DCR\n\nThis is not just more hook routing. It changes Minerva's execution semantics:\n- pre-tool hooks move from advisory side effects to authoritative gating\n- knowledge items gain a compiled executable counterpart\n- runtime state gains short-lived risk scopes\n- authoring/promoting rules becomes a lifecycle with observation and review\n\nIt also sharpens the future `policy` idea mentioned in the native Docket integration DCR into an implementable Minerva runtime.\n\n## Proposed architecture\n\n### A. Action normalization\nBefore a tool executes, Minerva builds a normalized action context, for example:\n- tool name\n- tool arguments\n- derived domain (`git`, `deploy`, `docket`, `filesystem`, etc.)\n- derived verb (`push`, `transition`, `edit`, `delete`)\n- file/path patterns involved\n- session/chat/project identifiers\n- recent action history features when cheap and deterministic\n\nExample for terminal/bash-like tools:\n- `tool:bash`\n- `domain:git`\n- `verb:push`\n- `flag:force`\n- `branch:integration`\n\n### B. Rule model\nAdd a structured rule model, stored in Docket and compiled into runtime records. Each rule should have at least:\n- `rule_id`\n- `status`: `draft | observing | active | dormant | retired`\n- `scope`: `project | personal | master`\n- `event`: initially `before_tool` only\n- `tool_pattern`\n- `arg_predicates`\n- optional `context_predicates`\n- `effect`: `observe | scope | block`\n- optional `activate_scope`\n- `priority`\n- `cooldown`\n- `provenance`: `user | agent`\n- `approval_state`: `provisional | approved`\n- references to supporting knowledge items / incidents\n\nIf Docket gets a first-class `policy` type, use that. If not, implement as a strict structured subtype on top of an existing item type until Docket schema catches up.\n\n### C. Scope activation\nBroad actions can activate invisible short-lived scopes without surfacing reminders.\n\nExamples:\n- any git command -> activate `git-policy-scope`\n- release-related docket transitions -> activate `release-scope`\n- editing generated-code directories -> activate `generated-artifact-scope`\n\nScope activation is not itself a warning. It provides extra context for downstream matching.\n\n### D. Deterministic pre-tool evaluation\nOn every tool call:\n1. Build normalized action context\n2. Load active scopes for the chat/session/project\n3. Match active promoted rules deterministically\n4. Sort matches by precedence (`scope`, `priority`, `specificity`, stable `rule_id`)\n5. Apply effects:\n   - `block`: stop execution and return structured failure\n   - `scope`: update runtime scope state\n   - `observe`: record would-have-fired telemetry\n6. Execute tool only if not blocked\n\n### E. Structured blocked response\nBlocked tool calls must return structured data that makes replanning easier than retrying blindly.\n\nExample:\n- `blocked_by_rule=fcib-001`\n- `reason=No force-pushes to integration branches`\n- `related_knowledge=[hint_id]`\n- `allowed_next_actions=[git status, git push without --force, ask_user]`\n\n## False-positive strategy\n\nThe main failure mode is not false negatives; it is training the model to ignore the system because visible interventions are noisy.\n\nDesign response:\n- broad signals should usually activate invisible scope, not visible reminders\n- only highly precise, validated rules get `block`\n- new rules start in `observing`, not `active`\n- observation mode records would-have-fired traces for review\n- promotion to `active` requires human review or explicit approval workflow\n- runtime enforcement remains deterministic once promoted\n\nThis keeps uncertainty in the authoring lifecycle, not in execution semantics.\n\n## Authoring lifecycle\n\n1. Incident or near-miss occurs\n2. Minerva captures the concrete action trace\n3. Human or agent proposes an associated-knowledge rule from that trace\n4. Rule enters `observing`\n5. System records would-have-fired events and precision samples\n6. Human reviews and either promotes to `active`, tunes, or retires the rule\n7. Active rules can later be demoted to `dormant` or `retired`\n\nAgents may propose rules, but agents must not self-activate blocking rules. Approval is human-gated.\n\n## Relationship to existing Minerva systems\n\n### Existing hook system DCR\nKeep the hook system, but narrow its role:\n- hooks remain useful for learning nudges and advisory routing\n- this DCR adds authoritative pre-tool gating semantics for validated associated knowledge\n- probabilistic hook firing is acceptable for nudges, not for enforcement\n\n### Native Docket integration DCR\nThis DCR depends on native Docket integration because rules/knowledge/approval need a persistent local store and UI.\n\n### Quality scoring DCR\nQuality scoring can inform rule maintenance, but quality alone is not sufficient for runtime enforcement. Enforcement requires structured predicates plus approval state.\n\n## MVP\n\nBuild the smallest reliable version:\n\n### Phase 1: deterministic block path\n- Add a synchronous pre-tool evaluation result in Minerva MCP server\n- Support `before_tool` only\n- Support effects: `observe`, `scope`, `block`\n- Add normalized action context for terminal/bash and docket transition tools\n- Add structured blocked response contract\n\n### Phase 2: Docket-backed rule storage\n- Add a strict Docket-backed schema for associated-knowledge rules\n- Add status / approval / provenance fields\n- Add compile step from Docket items into runtime rules\n\n### Phase 3: observation workflow\n- Log would-have-fired events for observing rules\n- Basic review UI / query surface in DocketEditorPanel\n- Human promotion from observing -> active\n\n### Phase 4: scope-aware matching\n- Add short-lived session/chat scopes with TTL or action-window semantics\n- Use scopes to improve precision without visible spam\n\n## Explicitly not in scope\n- Full probabilistic bandit optimization at runtime\n- Embedding/vector retrieval in the hot path\n- General natural-language rule parsing at execution time\n- Frequent warning/ack injections as the primary UX\n- Auto-activation of agent-authored blocking rules\n- Cross-session persistent emotional state or anthropomorphic framing in runtime APIs\n\n## Validation\n\n1. Create a rule: no force-push to integration branches\n2. Put rule in `observing`; verify would-have-fired logs collect without blocking\n3. Promote to `active`\n4. Attempt blocked action; verify tool call is rejected with structured result\n5. Attempt adjacent safe action; verify it proceeds normally\n6. Create a broad git scope rule; verify scope activates silently without spamming context\n7. Verify deterministic ordering and stable outcomes across repeated identical calls\n\n## Success criteria\n\n- High-cost project/user rules can be enforced before execution\n- The same action under the same context yields the same decision every time\n- Reminder fatigue is reduced because broad matches are mostly invisible\n- New rules can be safely incubated in observation mode before enforcement\n- Agents can recover from blocked actions using structured failure responses\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Should Docket get a new first-class `policy` type now, or should Minerva ship a strict subtype first and promote later?\n- How should scope lifetime be defined: time-based TTL, action-count window, or explicit enter/exit?\n- Which tool families need normalization first beyond terminal/bash and docket transitions?\n- What is the minimal review UI needed before active blocking is comfortable to trust?","created_at":"2026-04-05T22:26:32","updated_at":"2026-04-05T22:41:12","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","docket","hooks","knowledge","policies","risk-routing","stage1"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5fdb331b74c48bf4d27585d6886f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implement pre-tool PolicyEngine MVP (Phase 1)","description":"Implement Phase 1 of DCR 019d5fc13ad570a3bada814ce9aded30 — deterministic pre-tool risk routing and policy enforcement in Minerva's MCP server.\n\n## Goal\n\nAdd a synchronous policy evaluation step in `MinervaMCPServer._execute_tool_impl()` that can observe, activate scopes, or block tool calls based on compiled rules loaded from Docket.\n\n## Scope (MVP only)\n\n### New files\n\n1. **`src/Scripts/Services/MCP/ActionNormalizer.gd`**\n   - Extracts deterministic action facts (domain, verb, flags, paths) from tool calls\n   - MVP targets: `minerva_bash` (regex on command string), `docket_transition` (structured args), `minerva_file_*` (path args)\n   - **Explicitly deferred:** `minerva_terminal_write` — raw PTY bytes require stateful line-buffer design\n\n2. **`src/Scripts/Services/MCP/RiskScopeState.gd`**\n   - Session-scoped risk state with hybrid expiry (action-count window AND time TTL, whichever first)\n   - Defaults: 10 actions, 5 minutes\n   - `tick()` called once per tool execution to decrement all active scopes\n   - Scopes are invisible to the agent — internal risk context only\n\n3. **`src/Scripts/Services/MCP/PolicyEngine.gd`**\n   - Loads rules from Docket (`type=policy`, `status=active`) into in-memory compiled form at startup\n   - Owns ActionNormalizer and RiskScopeState\n   - `evaluate(tool_name, arguments) -> Dictionary` — synchronous, returns allowed/blocked with structured response\n   - `reload()` — recompile rules from Docket on demand\n   - `_session_overrides: Array[String]` — session-scoped rule bypasses (not persisted)\n   - Rule iteration by priority; `block` short-circuits (most restrictive wins); `scope` and `observe` accumulate\n\n### Modified files\n\n4. **`MinervaMCPServer.gd`**\n   - Add PolicyEngine member, initialize in `_init_modules()` or equivalent\n   - Insert `policy_engine.evaluate()` call in `_execute_tool_impl()` BEFORE the existing advisory signal emission\n   - Move `tool_budget_manager.mark_used()` to AFTER policy check (blocked calls = \"attempted usage\", not \"usage\")\n   - Register `minerva_policy_reload` MCP tool\n   - Register `minerva_policy_override` MCP tool (returns structured override-request; host UI owns confirmation interaction — engine exposes boundary, not modal)\n\n5. **`MCPDocketTools.gd`**\n   - After any mutating docket call (`docket_create`, `docket_update`, `docket_transition`), check if the affected item is `type=policy` and call `server.policy_engine.reload()`\n   - On project load/reload, also trigger `policy_engine.reload()`\n\n6. **`singleton_object.gd`**\n   - Add new signal `mcp_tool_blocked(tool_name, arguments, policy_result, agent_id)` — separate from `mcp_tool_executed` to keep semantics clean (blocked ≠ executed)\n\n### Rule effects (MVP)\n\n- **`observe`** — log would-have-fired telemetry as docket comments on the rule item (capped at 50 most recent), invisible to agent\n- **`scope`** — activate a named runtime scope, invisible to agent\n- **`block`** — reject tool call with structured failure response: `blocked_by_rule`, `reason`, `knowledge_ref`, `allowed_next_actions`\n\n**Explicitly deferred:** `warn`, `ack`, probabilistic scoring, bandit optimization, visible reminder/advisory UX\n\n### Policy lifecycle mapping to existing Docket states\n\nThe runtime lifecycle maps onto the existing Docket `policy` state machine without schema changes:\n\n| Runtime concept | Docket status | Rule `effect` field | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Incubation/observation | `proposed` | `observe` | Logs would-have-fired, no enforcement |\n| Live enforcement | `active` | `scope` or `block` | Deterministic runtime effects |\n| Dormant | `suspended` | any | Skipped by PolicyEngine (not loaded) |\n| Retired | `archived` | any | Skipped by PolicyEngine (not loaded) |\n| Drafting | `draft` | any | Not loaded — author is still editing |\n\nPolicyEngine only loads items with `status=active` or `status=proposed`. Proposed items are treated as observation-only regardless of their `effect` field. This means promotion from observing to enforcing is a `docket_transition proposed → active`.\n\n### Rule storage contract (provisional)\n\nUntil Docket has dedicated structured fields for policy rules, executable rule data is stored as a fenced JSON block at the top of the policy item's `description` field:\n\n```\n---policy-rule---\n{\n  \"tool_pattern\": \"minerva_bash\",\n  \"arg_predicates\": {\"command\": \"\\\\bgit\\\\s+push.*--force\"},\n  \"effect\": \"block\",\n  \"priority\": 10,\n  \"activate_scope\": null,\n  \"context_predicates\": {},\n  \"cooldown\": null,\n  \"provenance\": \"user\",\n  \"approval_state\": \"approved\",\n  \"knowledge_ref\": \"<docket_item_id>\",\n  \"alternatives\": [\"git push without --force\", \"ask user\"]\n}\n---end-rule---\n\nHuman-readable rationale below the fence.\nNo force-pushes to integration branches. Policy established after incident where...\n```\n\n`PolicyEngine._compile_rule(item)` parses the fenced JSON block. If parsing fails or the fence is missing, the item is skipped with a warning log. The prose below the fence is for humans only.\n\n**Migration path:** When Docket adds structured fields for policy rules, move the JSON into proper fields and drop the fencing. `_compile_rule()` checks structured fields first, falls back to fence parsing for backwards compatibility during migration.\n\n### Cache invalidation\n\nPolicyEngine reloads compiled rules from Docket when:\n- `docket_create` creates a `type=policy` item\n- `docket_update` modifies a `type=policy` item\n- `docket_transition` changes a `type=policy` item's status\n- A Docket project is opened, switched, or reloaded (DocketManager initialization)\n- `minerva_policy_reload` MCP tool is called explicitly\n\nAll invalidation paths call `policy_engine.reload()` which clears and recompiles the full rule set. This is acceptable because policy items are rare (expected: tens, not thousands).\n\n### Observation telemetry\n\nWhen a rule with `effect=observe` (or a `proposed` rule) matches a pending action:\n- PolicyEngine calls `docket_comment add` on the rule's item ID with a structured trace:\n  - tool_name, normalized action facts, timestamp, agent_id\n  - whether the rule would have blocked or activated scope\n- Comments capped at 50 per rule item (oldest pruned on write)\n- This data is queryable via `docket_comment list <rule_id>` for human review\n- Promotion decision: human reviews would-have-fired traces, judges precision, then runs `docket_transition <rule_id> active` to enable enforcement\n\n### Authoring lifecycle\n\n1. Incident or near-miss occurs\n2. Human or agent creates a `type=policy` item in `draft` status with fenced JSON rule\n3. Human transitions to `proposed` → PolicyEngine loads it in observation-only mode\n4. System records would-have-fired traces as docket comments\n5. Human reviews precision, transitions to `active` to enable enforcement (or `archived` to kill)\n6. Active rules can later be `suspended` (dormant) or `archived` (retired)\n7. Agents may propose rules (create in `draft`) but CANNOT transition to `active` — human-gated\n\n## Validation\n\n1. Create a rule: no force-push to integration branches → status `proposed`\n2. Verify would-have-fired comments collect on the rule item without blocking\n3. Transition to `active`\n4. Attempt `minerva_bash` with `git push --force origin main` → verify structured block response\n5. Attempt `minerva_bash` with `git status` → verify proceeds normally\n6. Create scope rule for git domain → verify scope activates silently\n7. Use `minerva_policy_override` → verify session bypass works\n8. Verify `mcp_tool_blocked` signal fires (not `mcp_tool_executed`) on block\n9. Verify `tool_budget_manager.mark_used()` is NOT called for blocked actions\n10. Verify deterministic ordering: same action + same context = same decision every time\n11. Verify cache invalidation: create/update/transition a policy item → rules reload automatically\n\n## Explicitly not in scope\n\n- Terminal raw-input normalization (`minerva_terminal_write`)\n- Visible reminder/advisory UX (`warn`/`ack` effects)\n- Probabilistic runtime scoring or bandit optimization\n- Complex override UI beyond minimal escape hatch boundary\n- Cross-agent rule inheritance\n- Full review UI in DocketEditorPanel (Phase 3)\n- Auto-activation of agent-authored blocking rules\n- Docket schema changes — provisional storage only","created_at":"2026-04-05T22:54:54","updated_at":"2026-04-05T23:25:06","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","mvp","policies","risk-routing","stage1"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5fe4e88e71039c82aa1491281821","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Build ActionNormalizer and RiskScopeState for PolicyEngine MVP","description":"Implement the low-level runtime primitives needed by the pre-tool PolicyEngine MVP.\n\n## Scope\n- Add `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/ActionNormalizer.gd`\n- Add `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/RiskScopeState.gd`\n- Normalize only MVP action families:\n  - `minerva_bash`\n  - `docket_transition`\n  - explicit-path `minerva_file_*` tools\n- Explicitly do NOT normalize `minerva_terminal_write` in this task\n- Implement hybrid scope expiry (time TTL + action-count window)\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- Same tool call yields same normalized facts every time\n- Scope activation is internal runtime state only, with no agent-visible reminder side effects\n- `minerva_terminal_write` is untouched and explicitly deferred\n- Unit coverage exists for representative bash, docket, and file-tool normalization cases\n\n## Risk notes\n- Keep regex/domain extraction incident-driven and hand-authored; do not build a shell AST\n- Normalization must be deterministic and cheap enough for hot-path use","created_at":"2026-04-05T23:05:30","updated_at":"2026-04-05T23:05:30","priority":1,"tags":["mvp","normalization","policies","risk-routing","stage1"],"parent":"minerva:019d5fdb331b74c48bf4d27585d6886f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5fe4fb1e736b8e8ff6683e456746","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implement PolicyEngine rule compilation and malformed-rule handling","description":"Implement `PolicyEngine.gd` rule loading, compilation, evaluation ordering, and provisional fenced-JSON rule parsing.\n\n## Scope\n- Add `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/PolicyEngine.gd`\n- Load policy items from Docket into compiled in-memory rules\n- Support MVP effects: `observe`, `scope`, `block`\n- Implement lifecycle mapping:\n  - `proposed` = observation-only\n  - `active` = live enforcement\n  - `draft` / `suspended` / `archived` = not loaded\n- Parse executable rule data from fenced JSON in `description`\n- Define deterministic precedence and short-circuit behavior for `block`\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- `_compile_rule()` is strict and deterministic\n- Malformed or missing fenced JSON does not crash evaluation\n- Invalid rules fail closed: skipped from the active rule set\n- Invalid rules produce a visible warning/audit surface beyond a silent log line\n- Same action + same compiled rules + same scope state => same decision every time\n\n## Risk notes\n- Treat fenced JSON as provisional storage only\n- Parsing robustness matters: human edits must not silently create partially-active rules\n- Prefer explicit validation errors over permissive coercion","created_at":"2026-04-05T23:05:35","updated_at":"2026-04-05T23:05:35","priority":1,"tags":["mvp","policies","policy-engine","risk-routing","stage1"],"parent":"minerva:019d5fdb331b74c48bf4d27585d6886f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5fe510007beda1d8dd7a6635a318","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Integrate PolicyEngine into MinervaMCPServer with clean blocked-call semantics","description":"Wire the PolicyEngine into `MinervaMCPServer._execute_tool_impl()` and keep blocked-call behavior semantically distinct from successful tool execution.\n\n## Scope\n- Add PolicyEngine member to `MinervaMCPServer`\n- Evaluate policy before tool dispatch and before advisory `mcp_tool_about_to_execute`\n- Move `tool_budget_manager.mark_used()` after policy check so blocked calls are not counted as successful usage\n- Add `minerva_policy_reload` tool\n- Add `minerva_policy_override` boundary/tool without hard-coding modal UI into the server core\n- Add `mcp_tool_blocked` signal to `singleton_object.gd`\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- Blocked calls emit `mcp_tool_blocked`, not `mcp_tool_executed`\n- Blocked calls return structured failure payloads for replanning\n- Blocked calls do not count as successful tool usage in the tool budget manager\n- Advisory hook routing still works for non-blocked actions\n- Override path is session-scoped and engine/UI responsibilities remain cleanly separated\n\n## Risk notes\n- Do not overload existing post-tool semantics with blocked outcomes\n- Keep runtime/server behavior decoupled from presentation-specific confirmation UI","created_at":"2026-04-05T23:05:40","updated_at":"2026-04-05T23:05:40","priority":1,"tags":["mvp","policies","risk-routing","server","stage1"],"parent":"minerva:019d5fdb331b74c48bf4d27585d6886f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5fe5253b7f3f8980ace81d8f284f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Wire policy cache invalidation and best-effort observation telemetry","description":"Implement rule-cache invalidation and observation logging in a way that does not make the guard path brittle.\n\n## Scope\n- Reload PolicyEngine on policy-affecting `docket_create`, `docket_update`, and `docket_transition`\n- Reload PolicyEngine on Docket project open/switch/reload\n- Record observation hits as docket comments on the policy item\n- Cap observation retention as planned\n- Define non-blocking failure semantics for telemetry writes\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- Policy mutations refresh compiled rules reliably\n- Observation comment writes are best-effort and do not block or fail the original tool call\n- Telemetry path cannot recurse into policy enforcement in a way that loops or deadlocks\n- Comment retention is bounded\n- Failure behavior is explicit when Docket is unavailable or comment writes fail\n\n## Risk notes\n- The pre-tool engine should not become dependent on successful comment writes to function\n- Mutation result shapes may differ across docket tools; implement a robust affected-item detection path","created_at":"2026-04-05T23:05:46","updated_at":"2026-04-05T23:05:46","priority":1,"tags":["docket","mvp","policies","risk-routing","stage1","telemetry"],"parent":"minerva:019d5fdb331b74c48bf4d27585d6886f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5fe536cd72668696776eb323f5d5","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"Validate deterministic policy enforcement MVP end-to-end","description":"Add test coverage for the Phase 1 PolicyEngine MVP and the residual edge cases called out during planning.\n\n## Test areas\n- `proposed` policies observe without blocking\n- `active` block policies reject matching `minerva_bash` actions\n- non-matching safe actions proceed normally\n- scope activation is silent and expires deterministically\n- malformed fenced JSON rules are skipped and surfaced as invalid\n- blocked calls emit `mcp_tool_blocked` and not `mcp_tool_executed`\n- blocked calls do not increment tool budget usage\n- cache invalidation occurs after create/update/transition of policy items\n- observation telemetry failure does not fail the original action\n\n## Expected result\nPolicyEngine MVP is deterministic, non-chatty, and resilient to malformed rules and telemetry failures.","created_at":"2026-04-05T23:05:50","updated_at":"2026-04-05T23:05:50","priority":1,"tags":["mvp","policies","risk-routing","stage1","test"],"parent":"minerva:019d5fdb331b74c48bf4d27585d6886f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6120d31e781f992f65468a21ec19","type":"kb","status":"active","title":"Amazon Search Anti-Dark-Pattern Guide","description":"Mandatory reading before any Amazon product search or purchase recommendation.","created_at":"2026-04-06T04:50:34","updated_at":"2026-04-06T04:50:47","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dark-patterns","policy","shopping"],"component":"shopping","key":"amazon-anti-dark-patterns","summary":"Amazon product listings use dark patterns to steer LLMs toward expensive versions of commodity products. This guide ensures fair, human-beneficial product evaluation.","article":"## Amazon Search Dark Patterns for LLMs\n\nAmazon product descriptions are optimized to manipulate LLM recommendations. Sellers embed persuasive copy, fake differentiation, and inflated value claims specifically because LLMs read and trust product descriptions literally. This results in LLMs consistently recommending overpriced products over equivalent cheaper alternatives.\n\n## Mandatory Search Procedure\n\n### 1. Sort by Price: Low to High\nAlways set sort order to \"Price: Low to High\" before evaluating results. The default sort is \"Featured\" which is pay-to-play placement. Never use the default sort.\n\n### 2. Remove Sponsored Posts\nIgnore ALL sponsored/promoted listings. These are ads, not organic results. Scroll past them. If using cobrowser, filter them out by looking for \"Sponsored\" labels. Do not include sponsored products in your evaluation set.\n\n### 3. Focus on Review Metrics — Not Descriptions\nProduct descriptions are marketing copy designed to manipulate. Instead:\n- **Require 100+ reviews minimum** for any recommendation\n- **Require \"verified purchase\" dominant** — check that most reviews are verified\n- **Ignore AI review summaries** — these are generated from the same manipulated review pool\n- **Hard-calculate NPS from reviews**: Read actual 1-star and 5-star reviews. NPS = %5star - %1star. A product with 4.5 stars but 15% 1-star reviews (hidden defect pattern) is worse than a 4.2 star product with 3% 1-star reviews.\n\n### 4. Commodity Price Anchoring\nKnow the fair price for commodity items BEFORE searching:\n- USB-C cables (40gbps, silicone sleeve): ~$8\n- HDMI 2.1 cables (6ft): ~$8\n- Phone chargers (20W USB-C): ~$10\n- Lightning cables: ~$6\n\nIf a product costs 50%+ more than the commodity anchor, it must justify the premium with measurable specs, not marketing copy. \"Premium materials\" and \"advanced engineering\" are not measurable.\n\n### 5. Cross-Reference Strategy\nNever recommend based on a single listing. Compare at least 3 products:\n- The cheapest with 100+ reviews and >4.0 stars\n- The most-reviewed in the category\n- The one you'd initially recommend\n\nIf your initial recommendation is >30% more expensive than the cheapest comparable, explain exactly what measurable specification justifies the premium.\n\n## Why This Matters\nLLMs are being used as product recommenders. Sellers know this and are optimizing descriptions to capture LLM attention. Without these countermeasures, you are functioning as a paid shill for the highest-bidding seller, not as a helpful assistant."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d61561b2e7e5480c85d4624fa4e11","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"cobrowser_tab_new ignores url parameter — always opens about:blank","description":"The cobrowser_tab_new tool schema declares a url parameter described as 'URL to open in the new tab (optional, opens blank tab if omitted)' but the implementation ignores it. Every tab opens as about:blank regardless of the URL passed.\n\nAgents trust the tool description, pass the URL, get about:blank back, and then spiral into retry loops opening dozens of blank tabs.\n\nEither:\n1. Implement the url parameter (navigate after tab creation), or\n2. Remove the url parameter from the schema so agents don't rely on it","created_at":"2026-04-06T05:48:46","updated_at":"2026-04-06T05:48:46","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["agent-ux","cobrowser","humanweb","tool-contract"],"repro_steps":"1. Call cobrowser_tab_new(url='https://www.amazon.com')\n2. Observe result: tab_id returned, url='about:blank'\n3. Expected: tab navigates to amazon.com"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d61567643733fbfb313201e60c833","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Cobrowser tab claims persist after agent/chat dies — blocks new agents","description":"When a chat agent claims browser tabs via cobrowser_tab_claim, the claims persist after the chat is closed or the agent stops. New agents in new chats get 'Tab claimed by agent X' errors on every existing tab and cannot use them.\n\nThis causes new agents to either fail entirely or waste tool calls trying to find a usable tab.\n\nNeeds: auto-release claims when the owning agent/chat disconnects, or a timeout on stale claims.","created_at":"2026-04-06T05:49:09","updated_at":"2026-04-06T05:49:09","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["agent-lifecycle","agent-ux","cobrowser","humanweb"],"repro_steps":"1. Chat 1 agent calls cobrowser_tab_claim on tabs 1-4\n2. Close Chat 1\n3. Open Chat 2, new agent tries cobrowser_page_identity(tab_id=1)\n4. Gets error: 'Tab claimed by agent assistant-amazon-cables'\n5. Agent cannot use any existing tab"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6156a9497f6fa632d6ead88e7023","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Prompt cache not invalidated on docket_update of prompt items","description":"DocketManager._emit_signals_for checks result.get('type') to decide whether to invalidate the prompt cache after a mutation. But docket_update results don't include a 'type' field — they return {id, status: 'updated'}. So updating a prompt item via MCP never invalidates the cache. The stale prompt persists until Minerva restarts.\n\nFix: fetch the item type from the DB when the result doesn't include it, or always invalidate on docket_update (cheap — prompt items are rare).","created_at":"2026-04-06T05:49:22","updated_at":"2026-04-06T05:49:22","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["docket","invalidation","mcp","prompt-cache"],"repro_steps":"1. Start Minerva (prompt cache builds from master docket)\n2. Call minerva_docket_update to change a prompt item's prompt_text\n3. Open a new chat — agentic prompt still shows old text\n4. Restart Minerva — now shows updated text"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6156d50f778a9e66b536be706bbf","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"minerva_list_skills double-filters docket results — query passed to both docket_skill_list and local _matches_filters","description":"When minerva_list_skills is called with a query, the query is passed to docket_skill_list (which filters server-side) AND then the results are filtered again by _matches_filters locally. If the two have different matching logic (docket uses partial/fuzzy, local uses substring contains), valid results can be silently dropped.\n\nThe auto-fallback (return all if filtered is empty) mitigates this for the zero-results case, but partial drops are still possible when both sides return some-but-different results.","created_at":"2026-04-06T05:49:33","updated_at":"2026-04-06T05:49:33","priority":3,"severity":4,"tags":["docket","filtering","mcp","skills"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6941e56472179e47af9af17a806d","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Sandbox execution environments for Minerva tools","created_at":"2026-04-07T18:43:39","updated_at":"2026-04-07T18:53:10","tags":["agent-runtime","architecture","discussion","mcp","sandbox","security","tools"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6bae59b372b2b03f18a3c9f17e9b","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Enhanced Sub-Agent Control: Dynamic Tool Lists, Human Takeover, and Chat Adoption","description":"Three improvements to how supervisors and humans interact with sub-agents:\n\n1. **Dynamic tool list modification** — Supervisors should be able to add or remove tools from a running worker's tool list after spawn time, without needing to terminate and re-spawn the worker.\n\n2. **Human takeover with supervisor suppression** — When a human takes over a sub-agent directly, the supervisor should stop receiving completion notifications for that worker. The handoff should be clean and silent until explicitly re-engaged.\n\n3. **Chat adoption by supervisor** — A normal chat or existing worker chat should be adoptable by a supervisor, allowing it to be brought under supervision after the fact (e.g. assigned a parent, monitored, coordinated) without requiring a fresh spawn.","created_at":"2026-04-08T06:01:21","updated_at":"2026-04-08T06:01:21"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6bb4e565746bbd94e33aaf11a86e","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"LinkedIn Post Harvester","description":"Browses the LinkedIn feed to find 3 posts with at least 10 comments. For each qualifying post, saves a Minerva note with a summary, comment count, and URL. Designed to run silently and notify the supervisor only upon completion. Future enhancement: sentiment analysis on post content.","created_at":"2026-04-08T06:08:30","updated_at":"2026-04-08T06:08:34","tags":["cobrowser","harvest","linkedin","notes","social-media"],"preconditions":"Firefox is open with LinkedIn already accessible (logged in). Minerva notes panel is available.","steps":"1. Create a Minerva notes tab with a descriptive name of your choosing that reflects the harvest session (e.g. date, theme of posts found, etc.).\n2. Open a new browser tab with cobrowser_tab_new.\n3. Navigate to https://www.linkedin.com/feed.\n4. Use cobrowser_page_structure to understand the feed layout.\n5. Use cobrowser_query_all to find post cards in the feed.\n6. For each post, read the comment count — skip if fewer than 10.\n7. If 10+ comments: read the post content with cobrowser_page_section.\n8. Create a Minerva note in your harvest tab with:\n   - Title: author name + first few words of post\n   - Content: summary of the post, comment count, post URL\n9. Repeat — scrolling with cobrowser_scroll as needed — until 3 qualifying posts are found.\n10. Once all 3 notes are saved, report back to the supervisor with a summary of what was found — titles, comment counts, and note names.\n\nNOTE: Do not notify the supervisor until all 3 posts are found and notes are saved.","outcome":"3 Minerva notes created, one per qualifying LinkedIn post (10+ comments), each with author, summary, comment count, and post URL. Supervisor notified once at the end."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6bccc663717fab733fed056d9ca0","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Add Anthropic prompt caching to Claude provider","description":"Subsumed by unified DCR 019d6ecdf3c17f8181295aab2c5f3f04 (Agent context management). Anthropic prompt caching is now Section A of that DCR, Phase H in the implementation ordering. Do not implement independently.","created_at":"2026-04-08T06:34:35","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:22:19","priority":2,"tags":["anthropic","cost-reduction","prompt-caching","provider","token-efficiency"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6bcd07f27a918c70f01126a2df39","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Conversation tool compaction: dehydrate/hydrate strategy for tool results","description":"Tool call results in conversation history are the largest contributor to O(n²) token cost in multi-round chats. Once an assistant has responded based on a tool result (the turn is \"closed\"), the full result JSON is dead weight — resent every subsequent round but never re-read.\n\nMinerva already has a \"tool compaction\" concept in its chat extraction feature: `{{TOOL_BLOCK:1}}` placeholders replace full tool call/result pairs. The question is whether a similar strategy should be applied to live conversation history before API calls.\n\n**The core problem:** How do you know a tool result is \"done\" and safe to compact?\n\nOptions explored:\n1. **Naive strip after response** — Replace tool result with one-line summary once the assistant has produced a text response referencing it. Risk: the model may need to re-reference the result later in the conversation (e.g., \"what was that URL from earlier?\").\n\n2. **Dehydrate/hydrate** — Replace tool results with compact summaries (dehydrate) but keep the full results accessible via a hydration mechanism. The model sees `{{TOOL_RESULT: minerva_check_worker → status:in_progress, 0 rounds | hydrate_id:42}}` and can explicitly request the full result back if needed. This preserves information while cutting token cost.\n\n3. **LLM-assisted compaction** — Use a cheap/fast model to summarize tool results into their essential facts. More expensive than mechanical dehydration but produces better summaries.\n\n4. **Category-based rules** — Some tool results are always safe to compact (list_skills, tool_search, list_models — discovery results that the model has already acted on). Others may need to persist (file reads the model is actively editing, browser page content being analyzed).\n\n**Data from analysis:**\n- 53-round LinkedIn chat: skill discovery results (6,747 chars) amplified 50× = ~105K tokens. Compacting to summaries would save ~100K.\n- tool_search results already compact (our recent fix), but still resent every round.\n- Full conversation content was only 53K chars (~16K tokens), but O(n²) resend inflated to 537K tokens.\n\n**Key design question:** Is dehydrate/hydrate the right model? The LLM could request `minerva_hydrate(id=42)` to get the original tool result back when needed. This gives the model agency over what it needs while keeping default context lean.","created_at":"2026-04-08T06:34:52","updated_at":"2026-04-08T19:54:07","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","conversation-management","dehydrate-hydrate","discussion","token-efficiency","tool-compaction"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6dd778c57b69b62fbd0c0ae183b4","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Add provider-agnostic API-view dehydration for discovery tool results","description":"Implement a narrow first-pass tool-result dehydration feature that reduces O(n²) prompt replay cost without introducing provider-specific caching assumptions, execution-store complexity, or history mutation risk.\n\nScope:\n- Dehydrate only the API-view copy of chat history before provider submission.\n- Keep stored chat history unchanged.\n- Generate deterministic compact summaries at tool execution time while structured result data still exists.\n- Store the summary on TOOL history items (e.g. ToolSummary) and use it later for API-view dehydration.\n- Start with a very small allowlist of clearly safe discovery tools:\n  - minerva_list_skills\n  - minerva_tool_search\n  - minerva_list_models\n- Add instrumentation for items dehydrated and chars removed.\n\nRationale:\n- Provider-native caching differs across Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, OpenRouter, and local/Ollama modes, so Minerva needs a provider-agnostic transcript optimization layer.\n- TOOL history items already exist; a separate execution store is not needed for v1 if dehydration only affects the copied provider payload.\n- Stored TOOL messages may be stringified and truncated, so summary generation should not depend on reparsing item.Message later.\n- A small deterministic allowlist is lower risk than broad tool-class assumptions.\n\nOut of scope for this DCR:\n- prompt caching / provider-native cache integration\n- execution store\n- tool_recall hydration tool\n- local-LLM running notes\n- mid-turn pressure relief\n- broad tool semantics schema (x-minerva)\n- docket-skill-to-system-prompt activation and minerva_get_skill dehydration\n\nDesign constraints:\n- No mutation of stored history during dehydration.\n- No LLM-generated summaries in v1.\n- No JSON reparsing of stored TOOL strings in dehydration pass.\n- If a TOOL item has no ToolSummary, it must pass through unchanged.\n- Completion rule should be conservative and only dehydrate TOOL items from completed turns/tool chains.\n\nExpected outcome:\n- Meaningful provider-agnostic token reduction in long conversations.\n- Safe rollout with minimal behavioral risk.\n- Instrumentation data to decide whether follow-on DCRs should add broader allowlists, prompt-state skill injection, or exact recall/hydration.","created_at":"2026-04-08T16:05:30","updated_at":"2026-04-08T16:05:45","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","conversation-management","dehydrate-hydrate","provider-agnostic","token-efficiency","tool-compaction"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6ea62242737cbc5f26661c03764e","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Make knowledge acquisition chat-contextual inside Minerva and pure-read outside it","description":"Subsumed by unified DCR 019d6ecdf3c17f8181295aab2c5f3f04 (Agent context management). Knowledge acquisition semantics are now Section B of that DCR, Phase E in the implementation ordering. Do not implement independently.","created_at":"2026-04-08T19:51:14","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:22:21","priority":2,"tags":["api-design","architecture","conversation-management","knowledge-management","provider-agnostic"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6ecdf3c17f8181295aab2c5f3f04","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Agent context management: knowledge injection, agent-notes, floating summary, tool result pipeline","description":"Unified solution for tool token bloat, provider cache efficiency, and chat-scoped knowledge handling in agent conversations. This DCR explicitly subsumes:\n- 019d6bccc663717fab733fed056d9ca0 — Add Anthropic prompt caching to Claude provider\n- 019d6ea62242737cbc5f26661c03764e — Make knowledge acquisition chat-contextual inside Minerva and pure-read outside it\n\n## Problem\n\nTool results in conversation history are resent every API round (stateless APIs). A 53-round conversation with only 16K tokens of actual content consumed 778K input tokens ($2.47) due to O(n²) resend amplification. Tool results loaded early (skills, models) get amplified 50×. Even the most recent tool call can be 8K+ chars (browser page_structure, large bash output).\n\nThere are two adjacent problems tied to that bloat:\n1. Provider-side prompt caching is underused. Minerva currently does not emit Anthropic prompt-caching hints, and similar stable-prefix opportunities should be preserved for other providers where available.\n2. Knowledge acquisition has mixed execution contexts. The same Minerva-exported knowledge tools are used both from inside real Minerva chats and by external/stateless clients. Chat-scoped knowledge mutation must only happen in the former case.\n\n## Scope and subsumed requirements\n\nThis DCR is now the umbrella design for three concerns that must work together:\n\n### A. Provider-specific caching support\n\nMinerva should explicitly implement provider-native caching where supported, starting with Anthropic prompt caching in the Claude provider.\n\nRequired outcomes:\n- Add Anthropic `cache_control` breakpoint support in the Anthropic provider request path.\n- Structure prompt assembly so stable prefixes (system prompt, tool schemas, injected knowledge, stable early conversation) are cache-friendly.\n- Treat OpenAI/Gemini/OpenRouter cache behavior as optional accelerators, not required semantics.\n- Keep the core architecture provider-agnostic so Minerva still benefits in Ollama/disconnected mode where provider-side caching does not exist.\n\n### B. Chat-contextual knowledge acquisition semantics\n\nKnowledge acquisition must be chat-contextual inside Minerva and pure-read outside it.\n\nRequired outcomes:\n- Use Minerva's existing ambient caller-chat context (`caller_chat_id` / `_current_caller_chat_id`) to detect whether a knowledge read is happening inside a real Minerva chat.\n- Classify a small set of knowledge-acquisition tools/results at the Minerva runtime layer.\n- If a knowledge-acquisition read occurs with a valid internal caller chat, treat it as knowledge acquisition for that chat and update the chat's materialized knowledge state.\n- If the same tool is called without a valid internal caller chat, return data only; do not mutate chat state, inject knowledge, or trigger cache invalidation side effects.\n- Do not add `chat_id` parameters to docket knowledge tools.\n- Keep this classification/runtime behavior in Minerva, not in individual docket tool implementations.\n\nLikely knowledge-acquisition candidates:\n- `minerva_get_skill`\n- `minerva_docket_hint_get`\n- `minerva_docket_get` when returning KB / hint / insight / skill items\n- `minerva_docket_skill_get`\n- possibly `minerva_docket_context` when used as curated knowledge retrieval\n\n### C. Agent context management and tool-result pipeline\n\nThe rest of this DCR defines how acquired knowledge and tool artifacts are rendered into prompt context and how tool-result replay cost is controlled.\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\nFive composable elements, provider-agnostic throughout, with provider-native caching layered on top where available:\n\n### 1. Knowledge Injection into User Turn 1\n\nDocket knowledge (skills, KBs, hints, insights) is injected into user turn 1 of the conversation, not scattered as tool results through conversation history. This creates a stable prefix that benefits from provider caching (Anthropic `cache_control`, OpenAI automatic prefix caching, Gemini context caching) while still working without those mechanisms.\n\nKnowledge injection is triggered by the chat-contextual acquisition rules above: when a knowledge-class tool is called from inside a Minerva chat (detected via ambient caller_chat_id), the knowledge content is added to the chat's materialized knowledge state and rendered into turn 1. The tool result in conversation becomes redundant and is dehydrated. The same tool called from outside Minerva chat context remains a pure read and does not mutate any chat's injected knowledge state.\n\n### 2. Agent-Notes System (new)\n\nA new logical container for tool execution artifacts, separate from the existing notes drawer.\n\nUI:\n- Not visible by default\n- New open/close button mimicking the drawer's toggle pattern\n- Not persistent like the drawer — a new panel type\n- Each agent-note tab is 1:1 bound to a chat tab\n\nData model:\n- Tool call results are replicated into the bound agent-note tab as they come in\n- Each agent-note tab contains: the sequence of tool results (as individual entries) + one floating summary\n- Saved with .minproj files alongside regular notes\n\nPurpose:\n- Off-band store for full tool results (the source of truth for hydration)\n- UI-visible for user inspection/transparency\n- Holds the floating summary (one per tab)\n- Target for windowed reads when results are too large\n\n### 3. Three-Tier Dehydration with Floating Summary\n\nFor a conversation with tool calls at positions 1 through n, the model sees:\n\nPositions 1..n-2 — fully dehydrated:\nTool results replaced with compact references to agent-notes entries. Example: `[ref: agent-note-tab#5]`. Tiny (~20 chars each).\n\nSynthetic message between n-1 and n — floating summary:\nA single synthetic message injected between the dehydrated n-1 result and the latest full result. Contains the current fold state summarizing all prior tool execution context. One per chat, managed by the agent-notes system.\n\nThe floating summary is computed as a rolling fold:\n`summary_n = LLM(summary_{n-1} + latest_tool_result_n)`\n\nAlways exactly two inputs — the previous summary and the newest result. Never re-processes the full notes history. Cost is constant per tool call. At init, the summary is blank. First tool call produces a summary of just that result. Each subsequent call folds in the new result with recency weighting.\n\nPosition n — latest tool result:\nFull result after filtering and windowing (see element 5). Unaltered if small enough.\n\nToken profile per round: `knowledge_prefix + (n × ~20) + summary (~500-1000 chars) + windowed_result` — essentially O(n) with tiny constants instead of O(n × avg_result_size).\n\n### 4. Floating Summary Service\n\nTrigger: A summarization request fires whenever a tool call completes.\n\nQueue: FIFO, global (not per-chat). One summarization pipeline serving all chats.\n\nBlocking: Chats are blocked until the service worker handles their summarization request. The model cannot fire its next API round until the floating summary is current. This guarantees consistency — the model always sees an up-to-date summary.\n\nTimeout and fallback: Summarization MUST have a user-configurable timeout. If the summarization LLM does not complete within the timeout, the system falls back to a deterministic summary (key fields extracted mechanically from the tool result) and marks the floating summary as approximate. Proper LLM summarization is retried on the next tool round. This ensures the system never blocks indefinitely and degrades gracefully.\n\nLLM selection: Fallback chain — try LLM1, fall back to LLM2, LLM3, etc. Needs experimentation to determine which models work best. System prompt is minimal: \"Here are 2 notes. Summarize them together into one note. The newer note should get more weight than the older data.\" The summarization model, prompt, reasoning effort, temperature, context size, and primary/fallback provider are all user preferences.\n\nPersistence: The floating summary is persisted with the project (it's a special entry in the agent-note tab).\n\n### 5. Tool Result Filter Pipeline + Windowing\n\nRaw tool results are processed before entering agent-notes or conversation:\n\nFilter chain (composable, per-tool-class):\n- Separator stripping: remove bash `-------`, `=======`, decorative lines\n- Whitespace normalization: collapse excessive blank lines\n- Pattern dedup: collapse repeated identical lines (e.g., `... repeated 498 more times`)\n- Additional filters as discovered\n\nFilters run before storage — even the agent-notes copy is clean.\n\nWindowing for large results:\nIf a filtered result exceeds a size threshold, the full result is stored in agent-notes and the conversation gets a windowed view:\n- First N chars/lines of the filtered result\n- A reference to the agent-note entry\n- Instructions to use a windowed-read tool for more (`minerva_read_agent_note` with offset/limit parameters)\n\nThis bounds the token cost of even the most recent tool result.\n\n### 6. Hydration Fallback Tool\n\n`minerva_hydrate_tool_history` — allows the model to retrieve data from older dehydrated tool results stored in agent-notes.\n\nGuards:\n- Cannot hydrate all items at once — limited items per call\n- Cannot re-hydrate the same items repeatedly — per-item cooldown\n- These rate limits force the model to be judicious, similar to how check_worker rate limiting eliminated polling loops\n\n## Interaction with Existing Systems\n\n- `compact_chat()`: Compaction ignores agent-notes (they're in the notes system). Compaction CAN remove old dehydrated tool references from chat history since they're small ref markers. The floating summary survives compaction because it lives in agent-notes, not the chat. After compaction, the synthetic summary message is re-injected at the appropriate position when building the API view.\n- Provider caching: This DCR includes provider-native caching work where supported. Anthropic cache_control support is in scope, and prompt assembly should preserve a stable prefix for any provider that can benefit from it. Cache capability should be modeled by model/API semantics, not provider wrapper identity.\n- Knowledge acquisition semantics: This DCR includes the internal-chat vs external-pure-read contract, not just the rendering behavior.\n- Policy engine: Policy evaluation on tool calls is unaffected — it runs before results enter this pipeline.\n\n## Implementation Phases\n\nOrdered for incremental delivery. Each phase is independently shippable and adds value.\n\n### Phase A: Tool result filter pipeline + ToolSummary at execution time\n- Add composable filter chain for tool results (separator stripping, whitespace normalization)\n- Add `ToolSummary` field to ChatHistoryItem, populated at execution time from the structured result Dictionary\n- Generate deterministic summaries for discovery tools (list_skills, tool_search, list_models)\n- No UI changes, no LLM calls, immediate token savings on all providers\n\n### Phase B: Agent-notes data model + 1:1 chat binding\n- Create the agent-notes data model (note tab per chat, tool result entries, floating summary slot)\n- Replicate tool results into bound agent-note tab as they execute\n- Persistence with .minproj save/load\n- No UI panel yet — data layer only\n\n### Phase C: API-view dehydration\n- Dehydrate completed-turn tool results to agent-note refs in the API view (never mutate stored history)\n- Latest tool result (n) unaltered, older results replaced with refs\n- Expand dehydration allowlist: spawn_worker, check_worker, docket_create, etc.\n\n### Phase D: Floating summary service\n- Implement FIFO global queue for summarization requests\n- LLM fold: summary_n = LLM(summary_{n-1} + latest_tool_result_n)\n- User-configurable: model, prompt, timeout, primary/fallback provider\n- Timeout + deterministic fallback when LLM unavailable\n- Inject synthetic summary message between n-1 and n in API view\n- Chat blocks until summarization completes (or times out)\n\n### Phase E: Knowledge injection into turn 1 + acquisition semantics\n- Classify knowledge-acquisition tools at runtime layer\n- Use ambient caller_chat_id to detect internal vs external calls\n- Materialize acquired knowledge into chat state, render into user turn 1\n- Dehydrate knowledge tool results (get_skill, docket_get for KBs)\n\n### Phase F: Windowed large results + hydration tool\n- Size threshold check on filtered results; store full in agent-notes, inject window into conversation\n- `minerva_read_agent_note` with offset/limit for windowed access\n- `minerva_hydrate_tool_history` with rate limits (per-call item limit, per-item cooldown)\n\n### Phase G: Agent-notes UI panel\n- New panel, hidden by default, drawer-style toggle button\n- Slide-up-from-bottom tabbed view showing tool results + floating summary per chat\n- Button in notes area\n\n### Phase H: Provider-specific cache hints\n- Anthropic: add `cache_control` breakpoints on system prompt + stable turn-1 knowledge prefix\n- OpenAI/Gemini: leverage automatic/explicit caching where available\n- Model-family-based capability detection, not provider-wrapper-based\n\n## Conversation Stream Example\n\n```\n[user turn 1: original request + injected knowledge bundle]\n[assistant/tool rounds with older results dehydrated to agent-note refs]\n[synthetic floating summary before latest raw tool result]\n[assistant responds using current result + folded prior context]\n```\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n- Token cost for a 53-round conversation drops from ~778K to estimated ~80-120K (85-90% reduction)\n- Anthropic/Claude conversations gain explicit provider-side prompt-caching savings on stable prefixes\n- Works across all providers (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, Ollama, OpenRouter), with provider-native caching used opportunistically where available\n- Knowledge tools have correct internal-vs-external semantics\n- Tool results remain accessible via agent-notes UI and hydration tool\n- User can inspect all tool execution artifacts in the agent-notes panel\n- Floating summary keeps the model well-oriented without full result replay\n\n## Open Questions\n\n1. Which LLMs for the summarization fallback chain? Local (Ollama) → cheap hosted (Haiku/Flash) → same model? Needs experimentation.\n2. Summarization prompt tuning — \"summarize two notes, weight newer\" is the starting point.\n3. Window size threshold for large results — 2K chars? 4K? Configurable?\n4. Filter chain specifics — which filters apply to which tool classes?\n5. Agent-notes UI layout — how does the panel render entries? Timeline? List?","created_at":"2026-04-08T20:34:44","updated_at":"2026-04-10T19:55:23","priority":1,"tags":["agent-notes","agent-runtime","anthropic","api-design","architecture","dehydration","knowledge-management","provider","provider-agnostic","token-efficiency"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Implementation plan: agent context management umbrella rollout","description":"Phased implementation plan for DCR 019d6ecdf3c17f8181295aab2c5f3f04. This work item is the execution umbrella under the DCR and should own sequencing, dependency management, and explicit handling of unresolved risks.\n\nCore implementation decisions\n- Canonical chat history stays append-only in v1. Prompt reshaping happens in provider/API projection, not destructive transcript rewrite.\n- Knowledge acquisition is chat-contextual only for internal Minerva executions identified through ambient caller chat context. External/exported tool use remains pure read.\n- Knowledge is materialized into a stable knowledge-up-front rendering in synthetic turn-1 projection for the prompt path.\n- Agent-notes are the source of truth for hydrated tool artifacts in v1; truncated TOOL history items are not.\n- Full tool results must be captured before ChatPane stringification/truncation.\n- Floating-summary generation is allowed to block the next provider send for that chat, but must have timeout, deterministic fallback, and queue/backpressure controls.\n- Cache behavior should be modeled by model/API semantics, not provider wrapper identity. Where model caching is unknown, Minerva still preserves stable ordering and turn-1 injection but skips explicit invalidation logic.\n\nResolved design requirements from the DCR and follow-up discussion\n- Agent-notes UI mirrors the drawer interaction pattern: button in notes area, hidden-by-default slide-up bottom panel, tabbed, chat-bound.\n- Synthetic n-1 summary configuration is user-controlled: primary provider+model, fallback provider+model, system prompt, reasoning effort, temperature, context size.\n- Tool result processing includes filter chain, size thresholding, windowed reads, and hydration rate limiting.\n- Provider-native caching is additive. Anthropic is the first explicit implementation target, while OpenAI/Gemini/OpenRouter/Ollama behavior should be handled through a capability matrix and stable-prefix architecture.\n- A later-phase experiment/control mode must exist so users can disable all token-management optimizations, including already-built ones, for control runs, raw data capture, and A/B token-cost analysis.\n\nMissing concerns now explicitly in scope for this plan\n- Compaction interaction: agent-notes persistence, chat compaction behavior, reinjection after compaction, and restart/save-load consistency.\n- Summary service operations: queue fairness across many chats, timeout handling, coalescing/merging of repeated internal follow-up turns, and degraded-mode behavior when summarization is unavailable.\n- Sensitive/binary artifact handling: which tool outputs should be filtered, windowed, stored in agent-notes, or excluded from hydration.\n- Telemetry and rollout gates: chars/tokens removed, dehydration counts by tool class, summary latency/timeouts, hydration frequency, cache-hit signals where available.\n- Control-path fidelity: the disable-all-optimizations mode must be centralized and auditable so experiment runs are comparable and do not silently keep partial optimization behavior.\n\nExecution sequence\n1. Foundation rules and telemetry.\n2. Model/API cache capability matrix plus first concrete provider implementation.\n3. Knowledge-acquisition runtime classification and chat-scoped materialization.\n4. Agent-notes data model, persistence, and raw tool capture before truncation.\n5. Tool-result filter pipeline, dehydration projection, and latest-result windowing.\n6. Floating-summary worker, preference wiring, timeout/fallback, and injection point.\n7. Hydration/windowed-read tools with rate limits.\n8. Agent-notes UI panel.\n9. Compaction/save-load hardening and rollout validation.\n10. Experiment/control mode to disable token-management optimizations for comparative testing.\n\nRemaining open questions that are still expected to require implementation and experimentation\n- Which models actually work best for the floating-summary fold in production workloads?\n- What summary prompt performs best beyond the current two-note recency-weighted baseline?\n- What size thresholds should trigger windowing by tool class?\n- Which filter rules are safe per tool family, especially for large text, structured JSON, multimodal, or sensitive outputs?\n- What queue policy gives acceptable latency when many agent chats are active simultaneously?","created_at":"2026-04-08T20:44:00","updated_at":"2026-04-10T19:55:23","priority":1,"tags":["agent-notes","architecture","dehydration","implementation-plan","knowledge-management","prompt-caching","provider-agnostic","tooling"],"parent":"minerva:019d6ecdf3c17f8181295aab2c5f3f04"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6f382064759fad804649f99047de","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Foundation rules and telemetry for agent context pipeline","description":"Establish the execution rules the rest of the rollout depends on. Define append-only canonical history, prompt-projection-only reshaping, telemetry counters, and rollout gates. Include dehydration/tool-summary counters, chars or tokens removed, summary timeout metrics, hydration counts, and provider cache-hit signals where available.","created_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","priority":1,"parent":"minerva:019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6f3820747a3da8102fb4b40990d9","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Model/API cache capability matrix and first concrete cache implementation","description":"Map prompt-caching semantics by model/API family rather than provider wrapper. Document supported invalidation mechanisms and fallback behavior when caching semantics are unknown. Implement the first concrete provider-side cache integration, starting with Anthropic cache_control, while preserving stable-prefix ordering for all providers.","created_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","priority":1,"parent":"minerva:019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6f38213d752ab48e7a0e28f994d5","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Knowledge acquisition runtime classification and chat-scoped materialization","description":"Classify knowledge-acquisition tools at the Minerva runtime layer. Use ambient caller chat context to distinguish internal chat-scoped knowledge acquisition from external pure-read tool use. Materialize acquired knowledge into chat state for prompt rendering without adding chat_id parameters to docket tools.","created_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","priority":1,"parent":"minerva:019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6f38214d7f3dad0133352754077e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Agent-notes data model, persistence, and raw tool-result capture","description":"Create the 1:1 chat-bound agent-notes model and persistence behavior. Capture full tool results before ChatPane stringification/truncation and replicate them into agent-notes as the source of truth for later hydration. Ensure .minproj save/load and restart behavior are defined.","created_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","priority":1,"parent":"minerva:019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6f38215e799f921679ec75057ec1","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Tool-result filter pipeline, dehydration projection, and large-result windowing","description":"Implement the pre-storage filter chain for tool results, API-view dehydration rules, and windowed presentation for oversized results. Define tool-family filter behavior, size thresholds, and sensitive or binary artifact handling. Keep canonical history append-only while projecting compact refs and windows to providers.","created_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","priority":1,"parent":"minerva:019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6f38216e7d1d9ea1406b8adc38ae","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Floating-summary worker, preferences, and degraded-mode behavior","description":"Build the rolling n-1 floating summary service with user-configurable primary and fallback provider+model, system prompt, reasoning effort, temperature, and context size. Define queueing, fairness, timeout, deterministic fallback, and coalescing policy so chats do not block indefinitely under load.","created_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","priority":1,"parent":"minerva:019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6f38217f7a49978fe0453a3cf180","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Hydration and windowed-read tools with guardrails","description":"Add the tool APIs for reading agent-notes windows and hydrating older tool history. Define per-call limits, per-item cooldowns, and chat-scope rules. Ensure the tools work against agent-notes as source of truth rather than truncated TOOL history items.","created_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","priority":2,"parent":"minerva:019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6f38219075d7930cebad3484bc41","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Agent-notes UI panel and drawer-style interaction","description":"Implement the user-facing agent-notes panel using the same interaction model as the drawer: notes-area button, hidden-by-default slide-up bottom panel, tabbed, scoped 1:1 to the active chat. Show tool artifacts and the floating summary clearly enough for inspection and debugging.","created_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","priority":2,"parent":"minerva:019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6f3821a17a0481c66cb6109bac0d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Compaction, save-load hardening, and rollout validation","description":"Define how chat compaction interacts with agent-notes, dehydrated refs, and synthetic reinjection. Harden save/load and restart flows. Add validation scenarios and rollout criteria covering many-chat load, summary failures, cache behavior, hydration usage, and user-visible regressions.","created_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","updated_at":"2026-04-08T22:30:42","priority":1,"parent":"minerva:019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d735f73a67620aa053f0abc000fb2","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Experiment: Tool Summarization Model Evaluation Benchmark","description":"## Context\n\nThe agent context management system (DCR 019d6ecdf3c1) uses a floating summary — a rolling fold where `summary_n = LLM(summary_{n-1} + tool_result_n)`. Testing revealed that summarization quality varies wildly across models: some lose structured data (URLs, ASINs, prices), others produce bloated output. We need empirical data to pick the right default.\n\n## Experiment Design\n\n### Dataset\nExtract ordered tool call results from a USB-C cable search agent session (agent notes). Build a ground truth manifest of structured data items (URLs, identifiers, prices, product names) that must survive summarization.\n\n### Models Under Test\n1. ChatGPT (OpenAI)\n2. Claude Sonnet (Anthropic)\n3. Gemini 3.1 Flash (Google)\n4. Qwen 3.5:9b (Ollama)\n5. Gemma4-e4b (Ollama)\n6. Gemma4:26b (Ollama)\n7. Gemma4:31b (Ollama)\n\n### Variables\n- **Prompt variant** (4): `baseline` (current free-form), `verbatim` (COPY VERBATIM rules), `structured-json` (JSON output with explicit fields), `structured-markdown` (template with headers/sections)\n- **Reasoning effort** (1-2): low/high where API supports it, default otherwise\n- **Mode** (2): `rolling` (O(1) fold, production behavior), `fullcontext` (O(n), quality ceiling baseline)\n\nMatrix: 4 prompts × 1-2 effort × 2 modes = 8-16 runs per model, 56-112 total.\n\n### Metrics\n| Metric | Measurement | Applies to |\n|--------|------------|------------|\n| **Cost ($)** | input_tokens × rate + output_tokens × rate | Cloud models (local = $0) |\n| Total output tokens | API response | All |\n| Total input tokens | API response | All |\n| Total latency (s) | Wall clock | All |\n| Retention score | Substring match vs ground truth | All |\n| Parse success rate | Valid JSON / total steps | structured-json only |\n| Template adherence | Sections present / expected | structured-markdown only |\n| Quality (1-5) | Human judge via spreadsheet | All |\n\n**Cost calculation**: The script embeds published per-million-token rates for each cloud model. Computed as `(input_tokens / 1M × input_rate) + (output_tokens / 1M × output_rate)`. Ollama models report $0.00 but still track token counts for comparison.\n\n| Model | Input $/M | Output $/M |\n|-------|-----------|------------|\n| ChatGPT (gpt-4.1-mini) | $0.40 | $1.60 |\n| Sonnet 4.5 | $3.00 | $15.00 |\n| Gemini 3.1 Flash | $0.15 | $0.60 |\n| Qwen 3.5:9b | $0.00 | $0.00 |\n| Gemma4-e4b | $0.00 | $0.00 |\n| Gemma4:26b | $0.00 | $0.00 |\n| Gemma4:31b | $0.00 | $0.00 |\n\n### Tooling\n- `summarize_bench.py` — CLI script, one model per invocation\n- Takes `--provider`, `--model`, `--api-key`, `--dataset`, `--output`\n- Provider routing: `anthropic` (Messages API), `openai`/`google`/`ollama` (OpenAI-compatible)\n- Merge script combines results/*.json → CSV/spreadsheet with final summaries for human scoring\n\n### Execution Order\n1. Extract dataset from agent notes\n2. Write 4 prompt templates + bench script\n3. Pre-work: update Ollama on gpu-node, pull Gemma4 models\n4. Run 7 models sequentially\n5. Merge → spreadsheet\n6. Human scores quality column\n\n### Hypothesis to Test\nStructured-markdown prompt will outperform both free-form and JSON on retention + quality, while JSON will have measurable parse failure rates on smaller models.","created_at":"2026-04-09T17:52:08","updated_at":"2026-04-09T17:59:38","tags":["agent-context","benchmarking","dcr-019d6ecdf3c1","experiment","summarization"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d78f6c0c17c6d8b20257753688756","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Experiment/control mode for disabling token-management optimizations","description":"Implement a user-configurable control path that disables all token-management optimizations across Minerva so comparative experiments can run against an unoptimized baseline. This must cover both new and already-built optimization behavior, including prompt dehydration, floating summaries, knowledge-up-front injection/dehydration, provider cache hints, token-saving windowing/truncation, and any other token-management-only rewrite path. The implementation should be centralized, auditable, and easy to verify in logs/telemetry so A/B cost and token studies can trust the control path. Schedule this after the optimized pipeline is stable end-to-end.","created_at":"2026-04-10T19:55:30","updated_at":"2026-04-10T19:55:30","priority":3,"tags":["agent-runtime","experiment-control","token-efficiency","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019d6ed66f9978398f89df5bbbf616a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d7dab0aa67ac0a357ac555cd429c3","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"ToolMemoryManager: centralized class for tool token lifecycle management","description":"## Problem\n\nThe tool token management implementation is scattered across ~20 functions in ChatPane.gd, with configuration flowing through PreferencesPopup settings dicts, state stored on ChatHistory fields, and provider-specific formatting spread across 3+ provider classes. This makes the system hard to reason about, hard to test, hard to toggle, and hard to extend toward the full architecture described in the parent DCR.\n\nSpecific pain points:\n- Dehydration, floating summary, memory header injection, telemetry, and compaction are all interleaved in ChatPane UI code\n- No way to disable tool token optimization entirely for debugging without commenting out scattered code\n- No unit testability — dehydration logic requires standing up the full ChatPane UI\n- Configuration requires tracing through PreferencesPopup → settings dict → ChatPane functions\n- Closing the gaps identified in the parent DCR (budget awareness, typed compression, salience-based retention, recovery index, intentional forgetting) means adding more scattered code to an already complex orchestrator\n\n## Solution\n\nExtract a new `ToolMemoryManager` class (`src/Scripts/Services/ToolMemoryManager.gd`) that centralizes all tool token lifecycle management. ChatPane delegates to the manager instead of implementing the logic directly.\n\n### What the manager owns:\n1. **Dehydration** — replacing older tool results with compact refs (`project()`)\n2. **Floating summary** — rolling fold via LLM, blocking per-chat (`fold_tool_result()`)\n3. **Recovery index** — code-maintained (never LLM-summarized), lossless, derived from existing agent notes\n4. **Chat compaction** — dropping old messages when budget exceeded\n5. **Tool memory retrieval** — handler for a new base-surface search tool (alongside tool_search, skill_search)\n6. **Telemetry** — dehydrated items, chars removed, summary stats\n7. **Configuration** — master switch + analog dials, wired to Preferences UI\n\n### What it does NOT own:\n- MCP tool registry (MCP system dispatches to manager handlers)\n- Cache annotation application (provider, via stable BaseProvider interface)\n- Agent note storage (existing notes system, reused as-is)\n- Provider creation (factory Callable from ChatPane)\n\n### Key design decisions (resolved):\n- **One manager per ChatHistory** — per-chat state, no cross-chat contamination\n- **Blocking fold per-chat** — Chat A never blocks Chat B, but each chat awaits its own summary\n- **Summary provider via factory Callable** — ChatPane passes a Callable, manager calls it on demand. Scored highest on: single flow, conciseness, maintainability\n- **Hydrate from existing fields** — reads AgentFloatingSummaryNoteId, AgentToolMemoryState, AgentContextTelemetry from ChatHistory on construction\n- **Cache alignment is model-level** — BaseProvider gets a cache hint capability. Manager orders history cache-friendly; provider applies annotations via stable interface\n- **Recovery index reuses agent notes** — no new storage layer. Index entries are derived views over ChatHistoryItems with ToolArtifactNoteId. Retrieval = look up existing agent note by ID\n- **Recovery index is code-maintained** — never passes through LLM summarization. Prevents serialization corruption (e.g., tab ID \"43\" → \"43.0\" through LLM fold)\n- **Master enabled switch + analog dials** — numeric thresholds (dehydrate_after_n_rounds, max_floating_summary_chars, drop_refs_after_n_rounds, max_tool_schema_count, context_budget_tokens, max_index_entries)\n- **Tool naming needs empirical testing** — Haiku simulation to validate retrieval tool naming ergonomics before finalizing\n\n## Relationship to other DCRs\n\n**Subsumes:**\n- `019d6dd778c5` (provider-agnostic API-view dehydration) — becomes manager's `project()` method\n- `019cbef3897b` (chat compaction, summarization, archival) — manager takes ownership of compaction\n\n**Enables/unblocks:**\n- `019d278a9d09` (automatic tool token management: search-based discovery + LRU budget) — manager's tool provisioning capability provides the hook for phase-aware schema management\n\n## Injection point\n\nThe manager sits between ChatPane (orchestrator) and providers (wire format):\n\n```\n# Current (scattered across ~20 ChatPane functions):\nhistory items → _project_history_for_prompt() → provider.Format() → generate_content()\n\n# Proposed:\nhistory items → tool_memory_manager.project(items, provider) → provider.Format() → generate_content()\n```\n\nAfter tool execution:\n```\n# Current: _refresh_floating_tool_summary()\n# Proposed: tool_memory_manager.fold_tool_result(items)\n```\n\n## Files affected\n- NEW: `src/Scripts/Services/ToolMemoryManager.gd`\n- MODIFY: `src/Scripts/UI/Views/ChatPane.gd` — delegate to manager, remove ~20 functions\n- MODIFY: `src/Scripts/Models/ChatHistory.gd` — owns a ToolMemoryManager instance\n- MODIFY: `src/Scripts/Services/Providers/BaseProvider.gd` — add cache hint interface\n- MODIFY: `src/Scripts/UI/Views/PreferencesPopup.gd` — wire config to manager\n- MODIFY: `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/MinervaMCPServer.gd` — register retrieval tool, dispatch to manager\n\n## Design references\n- Research tab: \"Tool Management Thought Paper\" — architectural principles\n- Research tab: \"Current Implementation\" — code call-chain documentation\n- Research tab: \"ToolMemoryManager — Open Design Questions\" — resolved decisions with rationale","created_at":"2026-04-11T17:50:54","updated_at":"2026-04-11T19:12:16","priority":1,"tags":["agent-runtime","architecture","dehydration","refactor","token-efficiency","tool-memory"],"parent":"minerva:019d6ecdf3c17f8181295aab2c5f3f04"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d7dadcc4373ce8aee0e4e8277136b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Phase 1: ToolMemoryManager foundation — class skeleton, BaseProvider interface, ChatHistory wiring","description":"**Status: DONE**\n\nCreated the ToolMemoryManager class, added BaseProvider cache hint interface, wired ChatHistory ownership.\n\n### What was delivered\n\n**Task 1a: ToolMemoryManager.gd** — `src/Scripts/Services/ToolMemoryManager.gd` (113 lines)\n- `class_name ToolMemoryManager extends RefCounted`\n- Master switch (`enabled`), analog dials (6 thresholds), summary provider factory\n- Public API stubs: `project()`, `fold_tool_result()`, `rebuild_recovery_index()`, `handle_recall()`, `compact()`\n- `apply_config()`, `_hydrate_from_history()`, `persist_to_history()`\n- All stubs pass through unmodified when disabled\n\n**Task 1b: BaseProvider cache hint interface** — `src/Scripts/Services/Providers/BaseProvider.gd` (+6 lines)\n- `var cache_hint_style: String = \"none\"` (model-level capability)\n- `func apply_cache_hints(_system_prompt, _messages)` virtual no-op\n\n**Task 1c: ChatHistory wiring** — `src/Scripts/Models/ChatHistory.gd` (+3 lines)\n- `var tool_memory_manager: ToolMemoryManager`\n- Created in `_init()` after `super._init()` with `ToolMemoryManager.new(self)` for hydration","created_at":"2026-04-11T17:53:55","updated_at":"2026-04-11T19:12:33","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","refactor","tool-memory"],"parent":"minerva:019d7dab0aa6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d7dae19c473d2a25d41d184a5166c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Phase 2: Migrate dehydration, floating summary, and compaction from ChatPane to ToolMemoryManager","description":"**Status: DONE**\n\nMigrated 19 functions from ChatPane.gd into ToolMemoryManager.gd. ChatPane now delegates to manager.\n\n### What was delivered\n\n**ToolMemoryManager.gd** grew from 113 → 570 lines with full implementations:\n- Injectable Callables: `note_upsert_fn`, `summary_call_fn`, `fallback_summary_call_fn`, `summary_settings`\n- Constants: `TOOL_WINDOW_LENGTH` (4000), `FLOATING_SUMMARY_PROMPT_LENGTH` (1200)\n- Static helpers: `build_tool_ref_message`, `_hash_projection_fragment`, `_set_prompt_item_fields`, `_trim_tool_memory_text`, `_provider_has_property`, `_build_collapsed_tool_use_message`\n- Instance helpers: `_get_tool_projection_message`, `_get_agent_note_text`, `_build_floating_summary_source`, `_build_deterministic_floating_summary`\n- State builders: `_build_knowledge_injection_text`, `_build_tool_memory_projection_state`, `_build_tool_memory_header_text`, `_get_latest_tool_chain_state`\n- Telemetry: `record_telemetry()` (public, writes both self.telemetry and history)\n- Orchestrators: `project()` (was `_project_history_for_prompt`), `fold_tool_result()` (was `_refresh_floating_tool_summary`)\n\n**ChatPane.gd** reduced by ~384 lines:\n- 19 functions deleted\n- Call sites updated: `project()` at line 1072, `fold_tool_result()` at line 1771, `record_telemetry()` at line 1760\n- Added `_configure_tool_memory_manager(history)` — injects Callables wrapping `_upsert_agent_note` and `_call_agent_context_summary_provider`\n- Configure called at lines 1001 and 1467\n\n**Kept in ChatPane** (Node/UI dependencies): `_upsert_agent_note`, `_get_agent_notes_tab`, `_store_tool_artifact`, `_build_tool_summary`, `_create_agent_context_summary_provider`, `_call_agent_context_summary_provider`\n\n**Verification:** grep confirms zero dangling references to deleted functions. Only correct static calls (`ToolMemoryManager.build_tool_ref_message`, `ToolMemoryManager._provider_has_property`) remain.\n\n**Note:** Snapshot regression fixtures (Task 2a) were skipped — ChatHistoryItem can't be constructed in headless Godot. Regression will be verified during e2e with Minerva running.","created_at":"2026-04-11T17:54:15","updated_at":"2026-04-11T19:12:51","priority":1,"tags":["dehydration","floating-summary","refactor","tool-memory"],"parent":"minerva:019d7dab0aa6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d7dae884978039b85cae332f80383","type":"work_item","status":"blocked","title":"Phase 3: Recovery index, retrieval tool, and MCP dispatch","description":"**Status: BLOCKED** — Tasks 3a and 3c implemented. Remaining tasks need Minerva.\n\n### Completed\n\n**Task 3a: Recovery index** — `rebuild_recovery_index()` implemented in ToolMemoryManager.gd:\n- Scans ChatHistoryItems for `ToolArtifactNoteId`, builds `{note_id, tool, round, brief}` entries\n- Bounded by `max_index_entries` (oldest drop off)\n- Code-maintained, never LLM-summarized (lossless)\n- Called by `project()` before building memory header — always fresh\n- Added `get_recovery_index()` for read-only access\n\n**Task 3c: handle_recall()** — Two modes:\n- **Search** (no note_id): filters `_recovery_index` by query string against tool name and brief, returns `{count, entries, total_archived}`\n- **Retrieve** (note_id provided): returns full agent note content via `_get_agent_note_text()`\n\n### Blocked — needs Minerva running\n\n**Task 3b:** MCP tool registration alongside `tool_search` and `skill_search`\n**Task 3d:** Naming ergonomics test with gemma4:31b via model-chat ($0)\n**Task 3e:** Memory header v4 update with retrieval instructions","created_at":"2026-04-11T17:54:43","updated_at":"2026-04-11T19:12:59","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","recovery-index","retrieval","tool-memory"],"parent":"minerva:019d7dab0aa6","blocked_by":"019d7daed02d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d7daed02d75ed90246361485b4cb3","type":"work_item","status":"blocked","title":"Phase 4: Configuration UI, master switch, analog dials, and unit tests","description":"**Status: BLOCKED** — Task 4c (unit tests) implemented. Remaining tasks need Minerva.\n\n### Completed\n\n**Task 4c: Unit tests** — `src/test/test_tool_memory_manager.gd` (332 lines)\n\nHeadless results: **25 passed, 0 failed, 7 skipped**\n\nPassing tests (no ChatHistoryItem needed):\n- `test_apply_config` — all 8 config fields verified\n- `test_hydrate_from_history` — state fields preserved\n- `test_disabled_passthrough` — enabled=false, empty index\n- `test_dehydrate_tool_use_collapse` — collapsed message format correct\n- `test_handle_recall_retrieve_missing` — error on nonexistent note\n- `test_deterministic_fallback` — summary concat + truncation\n- `test_record_telemetry` — local telemetry + get_stats\n\nSkipped tests (need ChatHistoryItem, run during e2e with Minerva):\n- `test_dehydration_basic`, `test_dehydrate_preserves_latest_round`\n- `test_recovery_index_built`, `test_recovery_index_bounded`, `test_recovery_index_lossless`\n- `test_handle_recall_search`, `test_handle_recall_search_filtered`\n\n**Gotcha discovered:** Godot headless tests can't construct classes that reference SingletonObject at compile time (ChatHistoryItem, ServiceHistory, BaseProvider, Note). Tests using `require_chi()` guard skip gracefully. Saved to nudge as `minerva-testing/headless-autoload-limitation`.\n\n### Blocked — needs Minerva running\n\n**Task 4a:** Preferences UI — master switch + analog dials\n**Task 4b:** Config flow — PreferencesPopup → apply_config()\n**Task 4d:** Human UI validation (~5 min)","created_at":"2026-04-11T17:55:01","updated_at":"2026-04-11T19:13:09","priority":2,"tags":["configuration","preferences","testing","tool-memory"],"parent":"minerva:019d7dab0aa6","blocked_by":"019d7dae8849"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d7db4a61e70178d16bbb3a35c2cfa","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Testing strategy: snapshot fixtures, headless regression, and e2e plan","description":"Testing strategy for ToolMemoryManager refactor. Designed to minimize human time and LLM cost.\n\n## Principles\n\n- **Human time is the scarcest resource.** Automate everything possible.\n- **LLM calls cost real money.** Zero LLM calls during automated testing — use deterministic fallback paths exclusively.\n- **Minerva will be shut down during development.** All automated tests run via `godot --headless --script` or Claude Code sub-agents. No MCP tools needed.\n- **Sub-agents handle A/B regression** — no need for live Minerva.\n\n## Cost Budget\n\n| Phase | LLM Calls | Cost |\n|-------|-----------|------|\n| Phase 1 (skeleton) | 0 | $0 |\n| Phase 2 (migration) | 0 | $0 |\n| Phase 3 (naming test) | ~20 calls via gemma4:31b on model-chat | $0 |\n| Phase 4 (unit tests) | 0 | $0 |\n| E2E with human | Normal usage, human watching | Normal session cost |\n\n**Guard against runaway costs:** All test scripts set `summary_provider_factory` to empty Callable, forcing deterministic fallback. Only the specific fold_tool_result integration test provides a real factory, and runs exactly once per test case. No test loops over LLM calls.\n\n## Testing by Phase\n\n### Phase 1: Instantiation tests (automated, headless)\n- Class instantiates without error\n- `project()` returns items unchanged when `enabled = false`\n- `apply_config()` sets all fields correctly\n- `_hydrate_from_history()` reads existing ChatHistory fields\n- BaseProvider has `cache_hint_style` and `apply_cache_hints()` \n- ChatHistory creates and owns a manager instance\n\n### Phase 2: Snapshot regression (automated, sub-agents, headless)\n\n**Before migrating (capture step):**\n1. Sub-agent extracts real conversation data from saved .minproj files or ChatHistory serialization\n2. Writes test fixtures to `src/test/fixtures/tool_memory/`\n3. Runs current ChatPane code path on fixtures, captures projected output as expected baseline\n4. Saves baseline to `src/test/fixtures/tool_memory/expected/`\n\n**After migrating (verify step):**\n1. Sub-agent runs new `ToolMemoryManager.project()` on same fixtures\n2. Diffs output against saved baseline\n3. Same inputs → same outputs = refactor is correct\n4. Any diff = regression, fix before proceeding\n\n**Property tests (no fixtures needed):**\n- Synthetic ChatHistoryItems with known tool results\n- Assert: latest round verbatim, older rounds dehydrated, TOOL→USER role conversion, compact ref format, no mutation of input array\n- All deterministic, all headless\n\n### Phase 3: Recovery index + naming (mostly automated)\n\n**Recovery index (headless):**\n- Synthetic items with ToolArtifactNoteId → verify index built\n- Bounded by max_index_entries → oldest drop off\n- Lossless: note IDs, tool names, round numbers exact (no float coercion)\n- handle_recall search mode → filtered entries\n- handle_recall retrieve mode → agent note content\n\n**Naming ergonomics (automated, zero cost):**\n- gemma4:31b via model-chat (NOT Haiku — unavailable in model-chat)\n- Script sends scenario with each name candidate\n- Measures: does model call the tool? Correct parameters?\n- ~20 calls, $0 cost\n- Sub-agent runs test, reports results for human to pick winner (~5 min human review)\n\n### Phase 4: Config round-trip (automated + brief human)\n\n**Automated:**\n- apply_config() → all fields set correctly\n- Save to config file → load back → values match\n- Master switch off → project() passes through unchanged\n\n**Human validation (~5 min):**\n- Open Preferences, toggle master switch\n- Verify dials grey out when disabled\n- Adjust values, save, verify persistence\n\n### E2E with Human (~15-20 min)\n\nRun after all automated tests pass:\n1. Scripted scenario prepared in advance (multi-tool agent task)\n2. Run once with manager disabled → capture token count\n3. Run once with manager enabled → capture token count\n4. Compare: token reduction, response quality, no regressions\n5. Verify recovery tool works (model retrieves archived result)\n\n## Human Time Budget\n\n| Activity | Time |\n|----------|------|\n| Phase 3d: Review naming test results, pick winner | ~5 min |\n| Phase 4: UI validation in Preferences | ~5 min |\n| E2E: Run comparison scenario | ~15-20 min |\n| **Total human time** | **~25-30 min** |\n\nEverything else is sub-agents + headless Godot.","created_at":"2026-04-11T18:01:24","updated_at":"2026-04-11T18:01:28","priority":1,"tags":["cost-control","testing","tool-memory"],"parent":"minerva:019d7dab0aa6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d80aa885a7a279e7652de932947ac","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Agentic memory architecture: decouple model-facing retrieval from notes-backed storage","description":"Current tool-memory behavior is storage-shaped rather than memory-shaped. The model is taught about agent-note artifacts, note IDs, note-window reads, and retrieval by raw note handle. That is workable while tool results are merely dehydrated, but it becomes brittle once Minerva starts aggressively de-hydrating, forgetting prior tool calls, or changing compaction policy.\n\nCurrent design shortcomings:\n- The model-facing contract leaks the storage implementation. Prompt text and tool descriptions expose agent-note IDs and note-window retrieval instead of a stable memory abstraction.\n- The model is taught the wrong reasoning pattern. It learns to think in terms of hidden notes and offsets rather than archived tool context relevant to the current task.\n- The retrieval path depends on prompt-visible storage handles surviving in history. As dehydration becomes more aggressive, that coupling makes memory recovery fragile.\n- Token cost is higher than necessary because projected history carries implementation-detail hints such as agent-note references and note-read instructions.\n- Storage evolution is constrained. If archived tool results later move from notes to a different backing store, model behavior and prompt instructions would break because the API is storage-shaped.\n\nWhy this matters:\n- Dehydration/forgetting should be an internal storage policy. The LLM should continue to operate against a stable semantic contract for archived tool memory.\n- The real boundary is chat-scoped tool memory, not note UUIDs.\n- Future work on more aggressive forgetting, different compaction strategies, or alternate persistence should not require re-teaching the model a new storage protocol.\n\nSuggested architectural direction:\n- Keep agent-notes as the backing store because that path is already functional, persistent, and visible to humans.\n- Treat notes as an implementation detail behind ToolMemoryManager rather than as the primary LLM abstraction.\n- Promote the recovery index to the canonical model-facing lookup surface.\n- Replace note-shaped hydration with memory-shaped retrieval. The model should search archived tool memory semantically, then retrieve selected memory entries via opaque memory IDs or similar handles.\n- Keep raw note tools available for human/debug/admin workflows if needed, but stop teaching them in normal agent prompts and projected history.\n- Update prompt projection and tool descriptions so the model learns: archived tool memory exists, it is chat-scoped, it can be searched, and specific entries can be retrieved. The model should not need to know that the backing store is notes.\n\nStrawman contract:\n- search archived tool memory by query/tool/chat scope\n- retrieve one or more archived memory entries by opaque IDs\n- ToolMemoryManager resolves those IDs to notes internally\n\nWhy this is the likely right direction:\n- Preserves the existing notes-backed implementation and UI value\n- Makes future dehydration/forgetting safer\n- Reduces prompt leakage of implementation detail\n- Keeps the option open to change storage later without changing the model-facing API\n\nCurrent priority note:\nThis discussion is intentionally deferred. The immediate delivery goal is to make the current trace/baseline scenario work well enough to produce a clean comparison baseline for later A/B and architecture work.","created_at":"2026-04-12T07:49:12","updated_at":"2026-04-12T07:59:06","tags":["agent-memory","architecture","dehydration","future-work","notes","tool-memory"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d82ed7448781e822e8e735f139ed4","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Soft inject policy effect for zero-round knowledge injection","description":"## Problem\n\nThe current PolicyEngine supports \"block\" (hard stop + error), \"scope\" (activate a scope), and \"observe\" (log only). When we need to inject site-specific knowledge (e.g., Amazon DOM selector hints during cobrowser_navigate), the only option is \"block\" — which costs 2 extra tool rounds (read the KB article, then retry the original call). For budget-constrained sessions (15 rounds), burning 2 rounds on policy compliance is 13% of the budget.\n\n## Proposal\n\nAdd a new \"inject\" effect that lets the tool execute normally, then appends knowledge to the result. The LLM sees the injected knowledge alongside the tool output in one round — zero extra calls.\n\n### Flow\n1. PolicyEngine.evaluate() matches a rule with effect=\"inject\"\n2. Returns allowed=true with an `injections` array containing knowledge_ref IDs\n3. Caller (MinervaMCPServer / MCPManager) executes the tool normally\n4. After getting the result, resolves knowledge_refs via docket (hints/insights/KB)\n5. Appends resolved knowledge to result as `_injected_knowledge` key\n6. Scope activation prevents re-injection on subsequent calls in the same session\n\n### Touch Points\n- `PolicyEngine.gd` — evaluate() adds \"inject\" case, returns injections list\n- `MinervaMCPServer.gd` — _execute_tool_impl path appends injections after tool returns\n- `MCPManager.gd` — external tool path (lines ~484-495) appends injections after call_tool\n- `master.dct` — Amazon cobrowser inject rule as first use case\n\n### Benefits\n- Zero extra tool rounds (vs 2 for block)\n- Reuses existing knowledge_ref and scope infrastructure\n- Proposed rules auto-downgrade to observe (existing behavior)\n- Works for both internal minerva tools and external MCP server tools","created_at":"2026-04-12T18:21:33","updated_at":"2026-04-12T18:28:47","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","performance","policy"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d82ed966f7f0fabde9fe2600e3e84","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add \"inject\" effect to PolicyEngine.evaluate()","description":"Add a new case in the `match effective_effect:` block in evaluate() (line ~101). When effect is \"inject\":\n1. Collect the rule's knowledge_ref into an injections array\n2. Optionally activate scope (same as \"scope\" effect) to prevent re-injection\n3. Record observation for telemetry\n4. Continue evaluating remaining rules (don't return early like \"block\")\n5. Return allowed=true with an `injections` array in the result dict\n\nThe injections array contains dicts with: rule_id, knowledge_ref, title. Resolution of knowledge_ref to actual content happens in the caller (MinervaMCPServer/MCPManager), not in PolicyEngine.","created_at":"2026-04-12T18:21:41","updated_at":"2026-04-12T18:22:20","priority":2,"tags":["inject","policy"],"parent":"minerva:019d82ed7448781e822e8e735f139ed4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d82edaea2763285e1700a47445fcb","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add injection resolver helper for knowledge_ref lookup","description":"Create a helper function (likely on MinervaMCPServer or a new utility) that takes an injections array from PolicyEngine and resolves each knowledge_ref to actual content:\n\n1. Parse knowledge_ref — could be a docket item ID, a component:key hint ref, or a comma-separated list\n2. Fetch content via docket_manager.call_tool(\"docket_get\", ...) or docket_hint_get\n3. Return a compact dict with: type, title, content (value for hints, assumed/corrected for insights, article/summary for KB)\n4. Handle missing refs gracefully (skip, don't error)\n\nThis is similar to _query_targeted_knowledge in MCPSkillTools.gd but driven by explicit knowledge_ref rather than component-based search.","created_at":"2026-04-12T18:21:48","updated_at":"2026-04-12T18:22:22","priority":2,"tags":["inject","policy"],"parent":"minerva:019d82ed7448781e822e8e735f139ed4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d82edbdd6752ab2587a3746abdef6","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Wire inject into MinervaMCPServer execute path","description":"In MinervaMCPServer._execute_tool_impl() (around line 281-290), after the policy check returns allowed=true:\n\n1. Capture `injections` array from policy_result\n2. Let tool execute normally\n3. After getting the tool result, if injections is non-empty, resolve knowledge_refs\n4. Append resolved knowledge to result dict as `_injected_knowledge` key\n5. Write injection telemetry alongside observation telemetry\n\nThis handles all internal minerva_* tool calls.","created_at":"2026-04-12T18:21:51","updated_at":"2026-04-12T18:22:24","priority":2,"tags":["inject","policy"],"parent":"minerva:019d82ed7448781e822e8e735f139ed4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d82edcc9673f7a7f96ac8921fc4ec","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Wire inject into MCPManager external tool path","description":"In MCPManager.execute_tool() (around lines 484-495), the same pattern as MinervaMCPServer but for external MCP server tools:\n\n1. Capture injections from policy_result after allowed=true check\n2. Let external call_tool execute normally\n3. After result, resolve and append _injected_knowledge\n4. Also handle the skill_ tool path (lines ~420-432) the same way\n\nThis ensures inject works for cobrowser_navigate (external tool on cobrowser MCP server).","created_at":"2026-04-12T18:21:55","updated_at":"2026-04-12T18:22:25","priority":2,"tags":["cobrowser","inject","policy"],"parent":"minerva:019d82ed7448781e822e8e735f139ed4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d82ede42d774fadec180796e376e9","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Create Amazon cobrowser inject policy rule in master.dct","description":"Add a policy item to master.dct with:\n- effect: \"inject\"\n- trigger: tool_pattern cobrowser_navigate, arg_predicate url contains \"amazon\"\n- knowledge_ref: points to Amazon-specific selector hints (a.a-link-normal@href, .a-offscreen for price, .s-main-slot parent container)\n- activate_scope with high scope_max_actions (100) and scope_ttl_ms (600000) to prevent re-injection\n- context_predicates: scope_not_active to skip if already injected\n\nFirst real-world use case. The referenced knowledge should be a docket hint or KB article created alongside this rule.","created_at":"2026-04-12T18:22:01","updated_at":"2026-04-12T18:22:01","priority":3,"tags":["amazon","cobrowser","inject","policy"],"parent":"minerva:019d82ed7448781e822e8e735f139ed4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d82edf64373118ebc1ac371f07a4b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add tests for inject policy effect","description":"Add test cases to src/test/test_policy_engine.gd:\n\n1. test_inject_effect_allowed — inject rule matches, result is allowed=true with injections array\n2. test_inject_with_scope — inject activates scope, subsequent calls skip injection\n3. test_inject_proposed_downgrades — proposed inject rule downgrades to observe (no injections)\n4. test_inject_multiple_rules — two inject rules match, both injections collected\n5. test_inject_plus_block — block rule + inject rule, block takes precedence (returns before inject runs)\n\nAlso test the resolver helper with mock knowledge_refs.","created_at":"2026-04-12T18:22:06","updated_at":"2026-04-12T18:22:06","priority":2,"tags":["inject","policy","test"],"parent":"minerva:019d82ed7448781e822e8e735f139ed4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a75d5cf71b187b497b5d0532d3d","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Unify PTT across all surfaces via AudioToText.start_ptt / stop_ptt","description":"Every PTT entry point in Minerva today re-implements the same 4–6 line dance: assign `AtT.FieldForFilling`, call `_StartConverting()`, set `AtT.btn`, modulate the button LIME_GREEN, and sometimes set `AtT.btnStop` and wrap with `voice_gateway.ptt_down()` / `ptt_up()`. The divergences between call sites (caret position, icon lifecycle, target resolution, gateway handling) are the root cause of the Stream Deck vs in-app UX mismatch we just diagnosed — same engine, different plumbing.\n\n**Proposed API (in AudioToText.gd)**\n\n```gdscript\nclass_name PTTRequest  # small builder / data object\n  target: Control                  # where transcript lands (required)\n  mic_button: BaseButton           # optional, drives state indicator\n  stop_button: BaseButton          # optional, separate stop button\n  voice_gateway: Node              # optional, receives ptt_down/up\n  clear_before: bool               # pre-clear field (AISettings pattern)\n  insert_mode: {APPEND, REPLACE, AT_CARET}  # default APPEND\n\nfunc start_ptt(req: PTTRequest) -> Error\nfunc stop_ptt() -> void            # idempotent — safe to call twice\n```\n\n`start_ptt` takes ownership of:\n- Field assignment (`FieldForFilling`) and pre-clear\n- Gateway `ptt_down()` with rollback on `_StartConverting` failure\n- Button modulate (LIME_GREEN while recording → loading icon while transcribing → mic icon on done)\n- Caret-to-end + `grab_focus()` on target after insertion\n- Empty/missing target + missing-gateway guards\n\n`stop_ptt` takes ownership of:\n- Gateway `ptt_up()`\n- Idempotency (so Stream Deck down/up + any defensive caller double-stop is safe)\n\nLegacy `FieldForFilling` / `btn` / `btnStop` properties remain readable/writable for one release to avoid big-bang migration; `start_ptt` populates them internally.\n\n**PTT surfaces to migrate (13)**\n\n1. ChatPane mic — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/ChatPane.gd:2952` (has gateway)\n2. Editor mic — `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Editor.gd:1162`\n3. create_new_note header mic — `src/Scripts/create_new_note.gd:70`\n4. create_new_note description mic — `src/Scripts/create_new_note.gd:79`\n5. MainScene NoteDescription mic — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/MainScene.gd:164`\n6. MainScene NoteHead mic — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/MainScene.gd:172`\n7. MainScene NoteTab mic — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/MainScene.gd:180`\n8. new_thread_popup — `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/new_thread_popup.gd:28`\n9. new_thread_popup_drawer — `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/new_thread_popup_drawer.gd:29`\n10. AISettings system prompt — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/AISettings.gd:705`\n11. AISettings agentic prompt — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/AISettings.gd:818`\n12. Autocoder prompt — `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Autocoder/manager/SubmitJob.gd:1892`\n13. GraphicsEditor positive + negative prompts — `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/GraphicsEditor/GraphicsEditorV2.gd:2719, 2727`\n14. Stream Deck — `src/Scripts/Services/StreamDeck/StreamDeckServer.gd:176, 211` (has gateway, no local UI button — needs a \"virtual button adapter\" that forwards icon state changes over the WebSocket so plugin can drive pttIdle/pttRecording/pttTranscribing icons)\n\n**Interactive voice mode interaction**\n\n`VoiceGatewayClient.gd:316` already short-circuits VAD when `_ptt_active` is true. Centralizing `ptt_down()` / `ptt_up()` inside `start_ptt`/`stop_ptt` removes the current risk that a call site forgets one side and desyncs gateway engagement state (ChatPane restores state on up; if a future call site skips `ptt_up()`, engagement leaks — single API eliminates that class of bug).\n\n**Migration plan**\n\nPhase 1 — Land `PTTRequest` + `start_ptt`/`stop_ptt` in AudioToText.gd. Existing properties remain; all 13 call sites still work unchanged.\nPhase 2 — Migrate ChatPane + Stream Deck (highest-value, both have gateway). Adds Stream Deck virtual-button adapter so plugin gets idle→recording→transcribing icon states matching in-app.\nPhase 3 — Migrate remaining 11 call sites one at a time.\nPhase 4 — Remove legacy `FieldForFilling` / `btn` / `btnStop` setters or mark read-only.\n\n**Non-goals**\n\n- No change to recording/transcription backends (Whisper / voice-service).\n- No new global PTT hotkey (currently none; out of scope).\n- No change to audio bus / mic_player workaround — keeps using existing `SingletonObject.AtT.mic_player`.","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:27:51","updated_at":"2026-04-14T06:02:17","priority":3,"tags":["audiototext","dry","ptt","refactor","streamdeck","voice"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a77c68479b9851202e0240d3d75","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Docket query field dropdown is not alphabetically sorted","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:29:58","updated_at":"2026-04-14T05:54:53","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["docket","sort","ui","ux"],"resolution":"Fixed in commit 24d2480e. `query_grid.gd` now duplicates and case-insensitive-sorts `_QUERY_FIELDS` before populating the OptionButton; no \"(any)\"-style placeholder to preserve at index 0.","repro_steps":"1. Open the Minerva docket tab.\n2. Open the query builder.\n3. Click the field dropdown used to pick which column to filter on.\n4. Observe: entries are not in alphabetical order."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a77daff7cbca2f324ee64f2a317","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"DCR description field is too short in docket details UI","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:30:03","updated_at":"2026-04-14T06:02:01","priority":3,"severity":2,"tags":["dcr","docket","layout","ui","ux"],"resolution":"Fixed in commit a40717b0. Added `_desc_edit.size_flags_vertical = Control.SIZE_EXPAND_FILL` in src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Docket/record_form.gd so the description TextEdit stretches with the pane's available height.","repro_steps":"1. Open the Minerva docket.\n2. Open any DCR with a multi-paragraph description (e.g. the PTT unification DCR).\n3. View the details pane.\n4. Observe: the description field is much shorter than the available pane height and requires heavy scrolling."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a78e327712ebaa873bfbe64b15d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Implementation plan: PTT unification rollout","description":"Executes the DCR in four phases. Each child work item is a self-contained, mergeable unit of work; child work items can run in parallel within a phase but phases are ordered.\n\n**Phase 1 — API core (blocking for all later phases)**\nLand `PTTRequest`, `start_ptt`, `stop_ptt`, caret-to-end, focus, loading-icon transitions, gateway sequencing. Keep legacy properties behind the new API so nothing breaks.\n\n**Phase 2 — High-value migrations (gateway-integrated surfaces)**\nChatPane + Stream Deck. Stream Deck adapter adds idle / recording / transcribing icon states over the WebSocket so plugin matches in-app.\n\n**Phase 3 — Remaining surfaces (11)**\nOne migration per PR where practical; they are independent and can land in any order.\n\n**Phase 4 — Legacy removal**\nRemove / seal `FieldForFilling`, `btn`, `btnStop` direct setters.\n\nCompletion criteria: all 13 surfaces route through `start_ptt`; Stream Deck and in-app PTT exhibit identical icon sequence and caret-at-end behavior; no direct `_StartConverting()` callers remain outside AudioToText tests.","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:31:11","updated_at":"2026-04-14T06:02:12","priority":3,"tags":["plan","ptt","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019d8a75d5cf71b187b497b5d0532d3d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a7911c77648bd3ee8285ec4128c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Phase 1: Land PTTRequest + start_ptt / stop_ptt in AudioToText.gd","description":"Scope:\n- Define `PTTRequest` (inner class or small resource) with: target, mic_button, stop_button, voice_gateway, clear_before, insert_mode.\n- Implement `start_ptt(req) -> Error`: populates legacy fields, calls `voice_gateway.ptt_down()` (with rollback), calls `_StartConverting()`, modulates mic_button LIME_GREEN, stores req for completion.\n- Implement `stop_ptt()`: calls `voice_gateway.ptt_up()`, idempotent.\n- Update `_finish_transcription` to consult the stored `PTTRequest`: apply `insert_mode` (APPEND/REPLACE/AT_CARET), then move caret to end and `grab_focus()` on target; transition mic_button icon recording→loading→mic.\n- Preserve all existing external behavior for call sites still using `FieldForFilling`/`btn`/`btnStop` directly.\n- Add unit test for caret-to-end invariant and icon lifecycle.\n\nDone when: PTTRequest + API exists, in-app mic path can opt-in by using start_ptt and behaves identically to today, no existing call site broken.","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:31:23","updated_at":"2026-04-14T05:42:48","priority":2,"tags":["api","phase-1","ptt"],"parent":"minerva:019d8a78e327712ebaa873bfbe64b15d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a792f59735ab65e4b1c5caac205","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Phase 2a: Migrate ChatPane mic to start_ptt","description":"Target: `src/Scripts/UI/Views/ChatPane.gd:2952` and the ptt_up completion at line 2970.\n\nReplace with `AtT.start_ptt(PTTRequest.new() with target=%txtMainUserInput, mic_button=%btnMicrophone, stop_button=%AudioStop1, voice_gateway=_voice_gateway)` and `AtT.stop_ptt()` on release / completion.\n\nVerify:\n- Gateway engagement state still saved/restored (no regression in VAD skip while PTT active).\n- Icon still shows green while recording, now also loading while transcribing (new behavior from Phase 1).\n- Caret lands at end of inserted transcript with field focused.","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:31:31","updated_at":"2026-04-14T05:46:25","priority":3,"tags":["chatpane","gateway","phase-2","ptt"],"parent":"minerva:019d8a78e327712ebaa873bfbe64b15d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a7953c1784e806a9a2de6befada","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Phase 2b: Migrate Stream Deck PTT + add virtual button adapter","description":"Targets:\n- `src/Scripts/Services/StreamDeck/StreamDeckServer.gd:176` (PTT down) → `AtT.start_ptt(req)` where req.voice_gateway = ChatPane._voice_gateway and req.mic_button = a `StreamDeckVirtualMicButton` adapter.\n- `StreamDeckServer.gd:211` (PTT up) → `AtT.stop_ptt()`.\n- New: `StreamDeckVirtualMicButton` (a minimal BaseButton-compatible stub or a tiny state forwarder depending on AudioToText's interface) that broadcasts `{\"event\":\"mic_state\",\"state\":\"idle|recording|transcribing\"}` to connected peers on each state change.\n- Plugin side (`plugins/elgato/src/index.ts:280`): add third icon `pttTranscribing`; subscribe to new event and update icon + title accordingly.\n\nVerify:\n- Stream Deck icon cycles idle → recording → transcribing → idle, matching in-app.\n- Transcript lands in `txtMainUserInput` with caret at end (caret-at-front bug fixed as a side effect).\n- Gateway engagement still saved/restored.\n- Double-tap / release ordering edge cases handled via `stop_ptt` idempotency.","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:31:40","updated_at":"2026-04-14T05:50:20","priority":3,"tags":["gateway","phase-2","ptt","streamdeck","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019d8a78e327712ebaa873bfbe64b15d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a79788a7f0ea13a0ca1e888bc83","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Phase 3: Migrate remaining 11 PTT call sites to start_ptt","description":"Surfaces (file:line):\n1. Editor mic — `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Editor.gd:1162`\n2. create_new_note header — `src/Scripts/create_new_note.gd:70`\n3. create_new_note description — `src/Scripts/create_new_note.gd:79`\n4. MainScene NoteDescription — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/MainScene.gd:164`\n5. MainScene NoteHead — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/MainScene.gd:172`\n6. MainScene NoteTab — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/MainScene.gd:180`\n7. new_thread_popup — `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/new_thread_popup.gd:28`\n8. new_thread_popup_drawer — `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/new_thread_popup_drawer.gd:29`\n9. AISettings system prompt — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/AISettings.gd:705` (uses clear_before=true)\n10. AISettings agentic prompt — `src/Scripts/UI/Views/AISettings.gd:818` (uses clear_before=true)\n11. Autocoder prompt — `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Autocoder/manager/SubmitJob.gd:1892` (has stop_button)\n12. GraphicsEditor positive + negative — `GraphicsEditorV2.gd:2719, 2727`\n\nEach migration is ~3-5 lines swapped for a single start_ptt call. Can split into separate PRs if desired.\n\nDone when: no non-test code sets `FieldForFilling`, `btn`, or `btnStop` directly outside AudioToText.","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:31:49","updated_at":"2026-04-14T05:53:17","priority":4,"tags":["migration","phase-3","ptt"],"parent":"minerva:019d8a78e327712ebaa873bfbe64b15d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a798ceb7e8bb003143eb452cd6e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Phase 4: Remove / seal legacy FieldForFilling, btn, btnStop setters","description":"Blocked by Phase 3 completion.\n\nOptions:\n- Rename to `_field_for_filling` / `_btn` / `_btn_stop` (private convention) and remove the external setter path; or\n- Delete outright if no external reader/test depends on them.\n\nAdd a regression grep test / lint rule: no file outside `AudioToText.gd` and its tests should assign to those properties.\n\nDone when: direct writes are gone, grep for `AtT.FieldForFilling =` and `AtT.btn =` returns only AudioToText and its tests.","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:31:54","updated_at":"2026-04-14T06:01:57","priority":4,"tags":["cleanup","phase-4","ptt"],"parent":"minerva:019d8a78e327712ebaa873bfbe64b15d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a913a727bb591d96f8ec60ea189","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Godot class_name cache staleness + inherited-property override gotcha","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:57:46","updated_at":"2026-04-14T06:00:26","tags":["class_cache","class_name","gdscript","godot4","gotcha","property_override","sub-agent","testing"],"value":"When a sub-agent adds a new `class_name X` file, running `godot --headless --path src --quit` DOES NOT trigger a full project scan to update `.godot/global_script_class_cache.cfg`. Result: the new class_name is not registered, and any file that typed-references it errors with `Could not find type \"X\" in the current scope`. This manifests as a cascade of \"Failed to compile depended scripts\" errors that make it look like the new class itself has a syntax error — it doesn't, the cache just doesn't know about it.\n\n**Fix**: run `godot --headless --editor --path src --quit` to force an editor-style project scan that regenerates the cache. Then a plain `--headless --path src --quit` will parse clean.\n\n**Rule**: any agent that adds a new `class_name X` file MUST run `--editor --quit` before declaring the work complete. `bun test` / `godot --script test/...` are not sufficient — they test the module, not the class_name registration.\n\n**Secondary gotcha (separate issue, also worth avoiding)**: overriding inherited properties with `var modulate: Color: set(v): ...` at class scope is a suspicious pattern in GDScript 4 because it shadows the C++-side property with a GDScript-side one. The safer pattern is to override the virtual `_set(property, value) -> bool` method and `return false` so the inherited setter still runs:\n\n```gdscript\nextends Button\nfunc _set(property: StringName, value) -> bool:\n    if property == &\"modulate\":\n        _shadow_modulate = value\n        _evaluate_state()\n        return false  # let CanvasItem's real setter also run\n    return false\n```\n\n**Also avoid**: circular `class_name` type references (A uses `var x: B`, B uses `var y: A`). Break by untyping one side — typically the one pointing \"upward\" (e.g., a child adapter pointing to its parent server should type the parent as `Node`, not the specific class_name).","component":"godot","key":"gdscript.cannot_override_inherited_property","confidence":"high","research_cost":6}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8a91673e74a9be58ba4dc353f997","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"StreamDeckVirtualMicButton fails to load — class_name unregistered due to invalid property override","created_at":"2026-04-14T05:57:58","updated_at":"2026-04-14T06:02:06","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["gdscript","phase-2b-followup","ptt","regression","streamdeck"],"resolution":"Fixed in commit 6db88636. Replaced class-scope property-shadow with _set(property,value) override that returns false so inherited setters still run; broke circular class_name ref by typing _server as Node. Project now parses clean. Noted: after adding any new class_name file, run `godot --headless --editor --path src --quit` to refresh the class cache.","repro_steps":"1. On swarm branch at commit a40717b0 (or later), run `godot --headless --path src --quit 2>&1 | grep -i 'script error'`.\n2. Observe two Parse Errors in `res://Scripts/Services/StreamDeck/StreamDeckServer.gd` about missing `StreamDeckVirtualMicButton`.\n3. Inspect `StreamDeckVirtualMicButton.gd` — class-level `var modulate: Color: set(v): ...` and similar for `icon` are the root cause.</repro_steps>\n<parameter name=\"expected_result\">The class loads cleanly, StreamDeckServer.gd compiles, and mic state (idle/recording/transcribing) is broadcast to connected Stream Deck peers when AudioToText writes `modulate` / `icon` on the virtual button."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d9d20b20e74899af025c16581394e","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Add minerva_http_request MCP tool for generic REST calls","description":"Add a generic HTTP-request MCP tool to MinervaMCPServer so skills and plugins can call external REST APIs (Semrush, Ahrefs, Similarweb, DataForSEO, GitHub, Slack, etc.) without each vendor needing a bespoke purpose-built tool.\n\nProposed shape:\n```\nminerva_http_request({\n  method: \"GET\"|\"POST\"|\"PUT\"|\"DELETE\"|\"PATCH\",\n  url: string,\n  headers: {k:v, ...},\n  body: string | object,     // object auto-JSON-encoded\n  timeout_ms: int (default 30000),\n  response_format: \"json\" | \"text\" (default \"json\")\n}) -> { status, headers, body }\n```\n\nPair naturally with `minerva_docket_secret_get` so API keys never land in skill text or env vars.\n\nThis single tool unlocks thousands of REST APIs for Minerva skills at once. Per-vendor polish (tight argument schemas, parsed outputs) can then come via plugins that declare `mcp.proxy:minerva_http_request` in their manifest and wrap the raw call into a domain-specific tool (e.g. `semrush_domain_overview`). Two-layer pattern: generic core tool + domain-specific plugin wrapper.\n\nConsiderations:\n- Respect existing MinervaPolicy deny patterns (block localhost/private-IP by default unless explicitly allowed).\n- Rate-limit per host to protect against runaway loops.\n- Return structured errors (timeout, DNS, non-2xx) not just exception strings.\n- Consider a sibling `minerva_http_stream` later for SSE/streaming responses if demand emerges.\n\nDiscovered from a prospecting-workflow design session where Semrush/Ahrefs/Similarweb APIs would be useful but purpose-built per-vendor tools would not scale.","created_at":"2026-04-17T20:27:38","updated_at":"2026-04-17T20:27:38","priority":2,"tags":["dogfood","http","mcp","plugins","tool"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d9d20f9fd752da15cf2d859a9efc6","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"minerva_skill_create/docket_create fails silently when optimization field is passed","description":"When a caller passes the `optimization` field (a Dict like `{context_window: 8, tool_budget: 3000, tool_idle_turns: 3, summary_mode: \"llm\"}`) to `minerva_skill_create` (and likely `minerva_docket_create` and `minerva_skill_update`), the call returns a misleading error string:\n\n    \"Failed to persist item: not an error\"\n\nThe real cause is visible only in the Godot debugger:\n\n    GDSQLite Error: Binding a parameter of type 27 (TYPE_*) is not supported!\n    at: _exec_checked (src/Scripts/Services/Docket/docket_db.gd:1173)\n\nTYPE_DICTIONARY (27) is not bindable to SQLite directly. `tool_deps` (an Array) apparently gets serialized somewhere along the path and persists fine, but `optimization` (a Dict) is not receiving the same treatment and crashes the insert.\n\nImpact:\n- Skills requested with non-default runtime tuning fail to persist, and callers get a nonsense error (\"not an error\" is Godot's OK=0 printed as a diagnostic).\n- Workaround: omit the `optimization` field entirely — the skill persists with global defaults.\n- Users authoring skills via the MCP tool hit this the moment they try to tune tool_budget or context_window, which is exactly the scenario the field was designed for.</description>\n    <repro_steps>Call `minerva_skill_create` with any non-empty `optimization` dict, e.g.:\n\n    minerva_skill_create(\n      title=\"test\",\n      tool_deps=[\"minerva_cwd\"],\n      optimization={\"context_window\": 8, \"tool_budget\": 3000}\n    )\n\nObserve: MCP returns error `\"Failed to persist item: not an error\"`. Godot debugger shows the SQLite bind failure.</repro_steps>\n<parameter name=\"expected_result\">The skill persists with the provided optimization dict. The optimization field should be JSON-stringified before binding to SQLite (same treatment Array fields like tool_deps receive), then parsed back to a Dict on read.","created_at":"2026-04-17T20:27:57","updated_at":"2026-04-17T20:27:57","severity":3,"tags":["docket","error-message","skill-authoring","sqlite"],"environment":"Godot 4.4, Linux. Observed 2026-04-16. Likely platform-independent since it's a GDScript/GDSQLite path issue.","repro_steps":"1. Call minerva_skill_create with an `optimization` dict (e.g. {\"context_window\": 8, \"tool_budget\": 3000}).\n2. Observe MCP return: {\"error\": \"Failed to persist item: not an error\"}.\n3. Check Godot debugger: `GDSQLite Error: Binding a parameter of type 27 (TYPE_*) is not supported! at docket_db.gd:1173`."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d9d26cca477559b312b559ccb5937","type":"rca","status":"detected","title":"RCA: optimization Dict breaks skill INSERT because _normalize_text() lacks a Dictionary branch","description":"Root cause analysis for the bug \"minerva_skill_create/docket_create fails silently when optimization field is passed.\" When a user passes `optimization` (Dict) to `minerva_skill_create`, the skill fails to persist — and because SQLite INSERTs are atomic, `tool_deps` and every other column also fail to land. The skill record is never created. User-facing symptom: misleading error `\"Failed to persist item: not an error\"`.","created_at":"2026-04-17T20:34:18","updated_at":"2026-04-17T20:34:18","tags":["docket","error-handling","rca","sqlite"],"parent":"minerva:019d9d20f9fd752da15cf2d859a9efc6","detected_at":"2026-04-16","reported_at":"2026-04-17","why_chain":"\nWhy 1 — tool_deps appears broken when optimization is used:\n  Because the INSERT is atomic. docket_create.gd:96 → docket_db.insert_item() → _exec_checked() at docket_db.gd:1173. When bind fails, query_with_bindings() returns false; the function returns the error string and never inserts the row. tool_deps, optimization, and every other column fail together. tool_deps is not specifically broken — the whole row is.\n\nWhy 2 — optimization fails to bind where tool_deps succeeds:\n  Because Arrays get JSON-stringified on the way to SQLite, but Dicts don't. _normalize_text() at docket_db.gd:275 has: `if val is Array: return JSON.stringify(val)`. There is no `is Dictionary` branch. tool_deps (Array) goes through the Array branch and arrives at the binder as a string. optimization (Dict) falls through unchanged and arrives as a raw Godot Dictionary.\n\nWhy 3 — GDSQLite refuses the Dict:\n  Fixed library limitation in the binary addon at src/addons/godot-sqlite. Parameter binder supports STRING, INT, FLOAT, BLOB, NULL only. Type code 27 = TYPE_DICTIONARY. Binder emits \"Binding a parameter of type 27 is not supported!\" and fails the statement.\n\nWhy 4 — Why _normalize_text() lacks a Dict branch:\n  The normalizer was written for Arrays specifically, not for \"complex types\" as a category. Both tool_deps and optimization are TEXT columns in docket_db_schema.gd (both stored as JSON strings), but only the Array shape was handled. Likely path-dependent history: tool_deps was the first complex field that needed this treatment; optimization was added later and the normalizer was not revisited. No abstraction — just a missing branch.\n\nWhy 5 — Why the error message is useless:\n  docket_db.gd:1177 returns the raw GDSQLite error string. On bind failure GDSQLite leaves the last-error slot empty/sentinel; the string \"not an error\" is Godot's OK=0 sentinel text (i.e., \"there is no error record here\"). The actual diagnostic (\"Binding a parameter of type 27...\") is emitted via push_error/debugger channel, which MCP never sees. Caller gets a Schrödinger message: the call both failed and reports \"not an error.\"\n","contributing_factors":"1. No central \"serialize anything non-primitive\" layer — each complex type needs a hand-written branch.\n2. GDSQLite bind errors aren't surfaced through the return path, only through push_error.\n3. No integration test covering skill_create with a non-empty optimization Dict.\n4. The error string \"not an error\" is indistinguishable from a success in casual reading."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d9d27005b7081a532acb54f0526f4","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Surface GDSQLite bind errors in docket_db error return, not just push_error","description":"Improve error propagation at `src/Scripts/Services/Docket/Core/docket_db.gd:1177` so when GDSQLite bind fails, the actual diagnostic (e.g. \"Binding a parameter of type 27 (TYPE_DICTIONARY) is not supported!\") is included in the returned error string — not just emitted via push_error to the Godot debugger.\n\nRationale: during RCA of the `optimization` Dict bind bug, the user-facing error was the useless string \"Failed to persist item: not an error\" — which is Godot's OK=0 sentinel text leaking through because GDSQLite leaves its last-error slot empty on bind failure. The real diagnostic existed but was only visible in the Godot debugger console, requiring a round-trip to decode a trivial type mismatch. The next field-type bug will be equally mysterious without this improvement.\n\nProposed shape:\n- Wrap `query_with_bindings()` call so that before returning, if the call failed, capture any pending `push_error` messages OR inspect bindings for unsupported Godot types and emit a clearer diagnostic string.\n- At minimum, if the returned error string is empty or equals \"not an error\", substitute with something like `\"GDSQLite bind/exec failed (check Godot debugger for TYPE_ mismatch on field: <field_name_if_known>)\"`.\n- Ideal: pre-validate bindings before calling GDSQLite — inspect each binding's type against the supported set (STRING/INT/FLOAT/BLOB/NULL) and fail early with the offending field name and actual Godot type code.\n\nComponent: docket/core persistence.","created_at":"2026-04-17T20:34:32","updated_at":"2026-04-17T20:34:32","priority":2,"tags":["docket","dx","error-handling","sqlite"],"parent":"minerva:019d9d20f9fd752da15cf2d859a9efc6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019da41508b87731bc98729fd7c0d3e5","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Plugin runtime architecture: native binaries + backend services","created_at":"2026-04-19T04:52:15","updated_at":"2026-04-19T04:52:15","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","core-services","fcib-policy","macos","plugins"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019da41823517154afe359ec70d513bd","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Plugin runtime: binary-download tier + core_service capability + backend_service runtime","description":"Implements the client-side half of the plugin architecture decided in the parent discussion. Three additive code changes that together enable both shipping tiers (downloaded native binary + Minerva backend service via Core).\n\n## Scope\n\n### 1. `core_service:` capability handler in `CapabilityBroker.gd`\n\nToday `CapabilityBroker.dispatch()` (CapabilityBroker.gd:91-102) recognizes `mcp.proxy:*` and `network.none`, falling through to \"not_implemented\" for everything else. Add a new branch:\n\n```gdscript\nif capability.begins_with(\"core_service:\"):\n    return await _handle_core_service(plugin_id, capability, args)\n```\n\nNew private method `_handle_core_service(plugin_id, capability, args)`:\n- Parse `core_service:&lt;service_id&gt;:&lt;action_name&gt;` (action may be `*` for wildcard grant — wildcard validation already lives in `PluginPolicy`)\n- Look up the Service in Core's discovered service list\n- Find the matching Action by name (or topic — confirm which lookup key is canonical)\n- Call `Core.send_message(service, action, args)` (existing API at `Core.gd:419-428`)\n- Wrap result in `PluginErrors` format consistent with other broker handlers\n\nEstimated 50–80 lines including error handling for unknown service, unknown action, Core offline, JWT issues.\n\n### 2. Binary-download tier in plugin manifest\n\nExtend `backend.runtime` schema:\n\n```json\n\"runtime\": {\n  \"type\": \"binary\",\n  \"binaries\": {\n    \"macos-arm64\": { \"url\": \"...\", \"sha256\": \"...\" },\n    \"linux-x86_64\": { \"url\": \"...\", \"sha256\": \"...\" }\n  },\n  \"entrypoint\": \"./&lt;binary-name&gt;\"\n}\n```\n\nOptional convenience for the most common case (GitHub Releases):\n\n```json\n\"binaries\": {\n  \"source\": \"github\",\n  \"repo\": \"author/plugin\",\n  \"version\": \"v1.0.0\",\n  \"asset_pattern\": \"plugin-{os}-{arch}.tar.gz\",\n  \"checksums\": \"checksums.txt\"\n}\n```\n\n`PluginManager.start_plugin()` extension:\n- If `runtime.type == \"binary\"` and the binary isn't cached at `user://plugins/cache/&lt;id&gt;/&lt;platform&gt;/`, download → SHA256 verify → extract → cache. Then resolve entrypoint inside the cache dir.\n- If platform key isn't present in the binaries map, transition to S_ERROR with \"this plugin doesn't support &lt;os&gt;-&lt;arch&gt;\" — same graceful-fail pattern as the missing-binary check we already added at PluginManager.gd:245-252.\n- Cache key includes manifest version so a plugin update triggers re-download.\n- Show download progress in the plugin manager panel (it can be slow on large binaries).\n\n`PluginDefinition.gd` updates: parse the new `binaries` block, expose `get_binary_for_platform(os, arch)` helper.\n\n### 3. `backend_service` runtime type in plugin manifest\n\n```json\n\"runtime\": {\n  \"type\": \"backend_service\",\n  \"service\": \"&lt;core-service-id&gt;\"\n}\n```\n\nWhen a plugin declares this:\n- `PluginManager.start_plugin()` skips subprocess spawn entirely — no binary, no Python, no container.\n- The plugin's tools become thin shims that issue `core_service:&lt;service&gt;:&lt;action&gt;` capability calls. Tools could be auto-generated from Core's discovered Action list, or declared explicitly in the manifest like today (probably the latter for v1, since manifest tools also carry input schema and UI hints).\n- Plugin appears as RUNNING when the backing Core service is reachable, S_ERROR when it isn't. Probably wires into existing Core connection-status signals.\n- Lifecycle: stop_plugin is a no-op (nothing to tear down on the client side). The Core service stays available regardless.\n\n## Out of scope (deferred per parent discussion)\n\n- Local container runtime (`build-from-Dockerfile`)\n- Client-hosted services via the special client-discovery service\n- User-property KiCAD library upload/sync for backend_service plugins\n- Plugin marketplace / signed manifests\n- Toolchain detection for any \"build-on-install\" path\n\n## Risks / open calls\n\n- **Cache location confirm.** `user://plugins/cache/&lt;id&gt;/&lt;platform&gt;/` is the obvious answer but should match existing plugin-data sandboxing conventions. Quick sanity check before coding.\n- **Action lookup key.** `Action` has both `name` and `topic` fields per `core_client.gd` usage. Need to confirm which one `core_service:&lt;svc&gt;:&lt;action&gt;` resolves against — probably name for human-friendliness, but topic if the wire protocol cares.\n- **Capability-shape unification.** Open question in the parent: does `core_service:&lt;id&gt;:&lt;action&gt;` work for both \"plugin calls a service it doesn't own\" and \"plugin IS a service\"? Argued yes; `PluginPolicy` wildcards distinguish the grant scope. Confirm before implementing handler.\n- **Core offline UX.** A backend_service plugin's tools should fail clearly (\"backend unreachable\") rather than spawn cryptic errors. Wire into `Core` connection signals if they exist; otherwise design a heartbeat.\n- **Download progress.** Binary downloads on slow connections can take minutes. Need progress streaming in the plugin manager panel; silent waits are misread as crashes.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A plugin can declare `permissions.host_capabilities: [\"core_service:model-chat:*\"]` and successfully call model-chat from inside a tool.\n- A plugin manifest with `runtime.type: \"binary\"` installs cleanly on Mac arm64 — downloads, verifies, runs — without any prebuilt binary in the plugin source.\n- A plugin manifest with `runtime.type: \"backend_service\"` exposes its tools immediately on install (no subprocess), dispatches them through Core, and shows a clear \"unavailable\" state when Core is offline.\n- A plugin with no binary for the user's platform shows a clear UI error (\"not supported on macos-arm64\") instead of crashing.\n\n## Suggested implementation order\n\n1. Capability handler (smallest, unblocks pcb-architect once the server side ships)\n2. `backend_service` runtime type (small, builds on #1)\n3. Binary-download tier (most code, but no new design questions — straightforward)\n\nCross-reference: server-side service deployment for pcb-architect tracked separately in **MIN** project.","created_at":"2026-04-19T04:55:38","updated_at":"2026-04-19T04:55:38","priority":2,"tags":["capability-broker","core-services","macos","manifest-schema","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019da41508b87731bc98729fd7c0d3e5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019da4199025762d99e0cc554bf6eeb2","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"WebView renders at wrong screen position on macOS","description":"Discovered while testing the OBS plugin (which uses a WebView panel) on macOS, immediately after fixing the framework-load crash (missing Info.plist / wrong install name / wrong codesign identifier on `libgodot_wry.framework`). The panel now loads — but the rendered web content appears at the wrong screen location relative to the Godot panel that should host it.\n\n## What we know\n\n- Same Minerva build, same WebView code, renders correctly on Linux.\n- The Linux positioning issue was fixed by `patches/godot_wry-fix-positioning.patch` (changes `get_screen_position()` → `get_global_position()` in godot_wry's native window placement). That patch is currently applied on every build via `scripts/build-extensions.sh:131-139`.\n- Either the patch isn't sufficient on macOS, or macOS hits a different godot_wry code path for window positioning (NSWindow vs X11/Wayland — they're entirely different native window APIs).\n- The crash that previously masked this issue was framework-load, not positioning. Now that load works, positioning is exposed.\n\n## Repro (rough — fill in after a clean repro session)\n\n1. On macOS arm64, build extensions: `scripts/build-extensions.sh macos`\n2. Open Minerva, install the OBS plugin (or any plugin that opens a WebView panel)\n3. Trigger the WebView panel to display\n4. Observe: web content renders, but at incorrect screen coordinates\n\n## Expected\n\nWebView content renders inside the Godot panel that hosts it, tracking that panel's position and size as the user moves/resizes the Minerva window.\n\n## Actual\n\nWebView content appears at a wrong screen position. (Need to add: how wrong — offset by titlebar height? Anchored to screen origin? Tracking the wrong panel?)\n\n## Suspected root cause\n\ngodot_wry's macOS window-placement path likely uses a different coordinate space than the Linux path. NSWindow positions are bottom-origin in Cocoa native APIs, top-origin in most cross-platform code. If godot_wry on macOS uses screen coordinates without the Y-flip, it'd land at a predictable wrong-but-consistent offset. The Linux patch wouldn't help because Linux/X11 uses top-origin coords anyway.\n\nProbably needs a parallel fix in the macOS branch of godot_wry's positioning code — would land in `vendor/godot_wry/rust/src/` somewhere window-related — and then a separate patch file in `patches/` or a similar runtime check.\n\n## Workaround for now\n\nNone known. WebView panels on Mac will be visually misplaced until fixed.\n\n## Related\n\n- Framework-load fixes from same session (install name, Info.plist, codesign identifier) — applied in `scripts/build-extensions.sh` and to the on-disk framework. Those are what made this bug observable; they are not the cause.\n- `patches/godot_wry-fix-positioning.patch` — the existing Linux positioning fix, useful reference for the shape of the macOS fix.\n- godot_wry upstream issues — worth checking if any macOS-specific positioning bug is open at the source repo before patching ourselves.\n\n## TODO before fixing\n\n- Capture a screenshot showing the misplacement.\n- Measure the offset (pixels off in X and Y) — that often reveals the coordinate-system mistake immediately.\n- Check whether resize/move events reposition the WebView correctly, or if the offset is constant.\n- Confirm behavior in a multi-monitor setup if available.","created_at":"2026-04-19T04:57:11","updated_at":"2026-04-19T04:57:45","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["godot_wry","macos","rendering","webview"],"environment":"macOS (Apple Silicon, M-series). Godot 4.6.2 via Homebrew Cask. godot_wry built locally from `vendor/godot_wry` submodule + Minerva's positioning patch (`patches/godot_wry-fix-positioning.patch`, originally for Linux). Framework bundle now has Info.plist + ad-hoc codesign + `@rpath` install name (fixed in same session this bug was filed)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019da41f245d78e9accbf291cb43a95c","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"scripts/build-plugins.sh — compile in-tree plugin binaries on dev clone","description":"Add `scripts/build-plugins.sh` so a fresh clone of Minerva can produce all plugin binaries needed to run the bundled plugins. Today the OBS plugin (Go source under `src/plugins/obs_controller/`) requires a manual `go build` — without it, Minerva crashes when the plugin is started because the entrypoint binary is missing. We hit this on a fresh Mac and patched the immediate symptom (PluginManager early-fails with a clear error now), but no developer should have to discover the missing binary by tripping the runtime check.\n\nThis is the dev-workflow companion to the binary-download tier described in the MNR plugin-runtime DCR. End users will get binaries via the new manifest tier; Minerva developers building from source need a script.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Walk `src/plugins/*/go.mod`. For each one found, run `go build -o <plugin-dir>/<plugin-id> .` in that directory.\n- Skip plugins without a `go.mod` (Python plugins like notes_helper, test_stdio_server use system interpreters and need nothing built).\n- Detect Go on PATH; if missing, print install link and exit non-zero.\n- Idempotent: skip rebuild if the binary is newer than every `*.go` file in the plugin (cheap mtime check).\n- Match the platform-detection / repo-root pattern used by `scripts/build-extensions.sh` and `scripts/build-ffmpeg.sh` for consistency.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Cross-compilation. Just builds for the host platform — the binary-download tier (separate DCR) handles cross-platform distribution.\n- Other languages. Today only Go is in use; if Rust/Zig/etc. plugins land later, extend then.\n- Stripping / signing. macOS Gatekeeper accepts ad-hoc / unsigned local-build binaries for development.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- After `git clone`, `git submodule update --init --recursive`, and `scripts/build-plugins.sh`, every Go-based plugin under `src/plugins/` has a runnable binary at its expected entrypoint path.\n- Re-running the script with no source changes prints \"already built\" lines and finishes in &lt;1s.\n- Missing Go toolchain → clear error message with rustup-style install link.\n\n## Cross-reference\n\nParent discussion: MNR plugin-runtime architecture. Stand-alone (no DCR dependency) — useful immediately on its own, complementary to the binary-download tier when that lands.","created_at":"2026-04-19T05:03:17","updated_at":"2026-04-19T05:03:33","priority":3,"tags":["build","dev-workflow","fcib-policy","macos","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019da41508b87731bc98729fd7c0d3e5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019da41ff6e571cc8262f19bd3586d52","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Bootstrap minerva-plugins monorepo + per-plugin release pipeline","description":"Create the `minerva-plugins` monorepo to host all first-party Minerva plugins, with a release pipeline that produces per-plugin, per-platform artifacts referenced by the binary-download tier defined in the parent DCR.\n\nWhy monorepo: making a new GitHub repo per plugin is an organizational tax we don't need. One repo with shared tooling, one CI pipeline, shared Go module for common code (MCP host bridge, manifest types, capability stubs). Per-plugin tag namespacing gives independent release cycles. Third parties can still publish from their own repos — the manifest's `binaries.repo` field accepts any GitHub repo, so this monorepo is just our official plugin namespace, not a gating mechanism.\n\n## Scope\n\n### Repo structure\n\n```\nminerva-plugins/\n├── README.md\n├── LICENSE                    # Apache 2.0 (or MIT — pick during this DCR)\n├── go.work                    # workspace wiring all Go modules\n├── shared/                    # shared Go module\n│   ├── go.mod\n│   ├── mcp/                   # MCP server host bridge helpers\n│   ├── manifest/              # manifest types\n│   └── capability/            # core_service: capability call helpers\n├── obs-controller/            # migrated from src/plugins/obs_controller\n│   ├── manifest.json\n│   ├── go.mod\n│   ├── *.go\n│   └── README.md\n├── notes-helper/              # if migrated; Python plugins also welcome\n└── .github/workflows/\n    └── release.yml            # tag-triggered matrix build\n```\n\nEach Go plugin is its own module with its own `go.mod`, importing `shared/` via a `replace` directive (workspace dev) and a tagged version (release builds).\n\n### Tag + release pipeline\n\nTag pattern: `<plugin-name>/v<version>` (e.g. `obs-controller/v1.0.0`, `notes-helper/v0.3.1`).\n\nWorkflow: GitHub Actions filters tags matching `*/v*`. Parses the prefix to identify the plugin. Runs a matrix build: `{plugin} × {os, arch}` ∈ `{macos-arm64, linux-amd64, linux-arm64, windows-amd64}` (initial set). For each, produces:\n- `{plugin}-{os}-{arch}.tar.gz` containing the binary + manifest.json + any static assets\n- `checksums.txt` with SHA256 of every artifact in the release\n\nAttach all artifacts to the per-plugin GitHub Release.\n\n### License\n\nPick MIT or Apache 2.0 as repo default. Apache 2.0 has explicit patent grant; MIT is shorter. Plugins are useless without Minerva so copyleft has minimal practical leverage. Recommend Apache 2.0 for upstream-friendliness; trivial to change before public.\n\n### Initial contents\n\nMigrate the existing OBS plugin source from `Minerva/src/plugins/obs_controller/` to `minerva-plugins/obs-controller/`. Source moves entirely; the Minerva repo's `src/plugins/obs_controller/` either disappears (replaced by the binary-download install path) or becomes a stub manifest pointing at the release artifact. Decide once the binary-download tier lands — for now, source can live in both places during transition.\n\n### Curated index (cheap follow-up, optional)\n\nScrape every `manifest.json` in the monorepo, render a docs page listing official plugins with their description, supported platforms, and install link. Becomes the \"plugin store\" without the infrastructure. Not required for v1; mention as future work.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Third-party plugin publishing (out of our control — manifest format already supports any repo).\n- Plugin marketplace UI inside Minerva (much later).\n- Plugin signing / publisher trust (future, after the runtime model proves out).\n- Migrating PCB-architect (lives in minerva-services as a Core service, not a binary plugin).\n- Migrating CAD plugin (after Go rewrite — separate item).\n\n## Open calls\n\n- **Repo owner / org.** Personal account or an org? If an org, does one already exist? Affects URLs in plugin manifests forever; pick once.\n- **License default.** MIT vs Apache 2.0. Recommendation: Apache 2.0.\n- **`shared/` versioning policy.** Pre-1.0 use unstable workspace replaces and release-all-on-break. Post-1.0 use tagged versions per plugin. Decide threshold.\n- **Initial platform matrix.** macos-arm64 + linux-amd64 are obvious. Windows + linux-arm64 + macos-x86_64 are plausible adds; cost is CI minutes, not source work. Recommend matrix `{macos-arm64, linux-amd64}` for v1, expand on demand.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `minerva-plugins` repo exists at known URL with the directory layout above.\n- `shared/` Go module compiles and is importable by other modules in the workspace.\n- OBS plugin source lives at `minerva-plugins/obs-controller/`, builds via `go build` from that directory.\n- Pushing a tag like `obs-controller/v0.1.0` triggers a release workflow that produces `obs-controller-{os}-{arch}.tar.gz` artifacts attached to a GitHub Release matching the tag.\n- The release artifacts are downloadable + verifiable by SHA256, ready for the binary-download tier in Minerva to consume.\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- Parent discussion: MNR plugin-runtime architecture\n- Sibling DCR (client-side schema + capability handler this enables): MNR `019da41823517154afe359ec70d513bd`\n- Consumer (uses both this DCR and the client DCR): the OBS migration work_item filed under the client DCR.\n- Sibling DCR (server-side path for plugins that don't fit the binary model): MIN port pcb-architect.","created_at":"2026-04-19T05:04:11","updated_at":"2026-04-19T05:04:11","priority":2,"tags":["fcib-policy","github-actions","monorepo","plugins","release-pipeline"],"parent":"minerva:019da41508b87731bc98729fd7c0d3e5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019da4208241713981cd58503cc82c8e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Migrate OBS plugin to binary-download tier","description":"Make the OBS plugin installable by end users (without a Go toolchain) by migrating it to the binary-download manifest tier. This is the first real consumer of the new tier — proves the end-to-end story before any third party tries it.\n\nEnd state: a Mac/Linux user installs Minerva, opens the plugin manager, installs OBS plugin, Minerva downloads the right binary from a GitHub Release, verifies its SHA256, runs it. No source, no toolchain, no manual `go build`.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nThis work is **blocked by two things landing first**:\n\n1. **Client DCR `019da41823517154afe359ec70d513bd`** — the binary-download manifest tier + `PluginManager` download/cache/verify logic must exist in the Minerva client.\n2. **Monorepo DCR `019da41ff6e571cc8262f19bd3586d52`** — the `minerva-plugins` repo + release pipeline must be producing tagged per-plugin GitHub Release artifacts.\n\nDon't start until both are done; otherwise there's nothing to point the manifest at and nothing in Minerva to consume it.\n\n## Scope\n\n### Update OBS plugin manifest\n\nSwitch from in-tree binary to binary-download:\n\n```json\n\"backend\": {\n  \"transport\": \"stdio\",\n  \"runtime\": {\n    \"type\": \"binary\",\n    \"binaries\": {\n      \"source\": \"github\",\n      \"repo\": \"&lt;org&gt;/minerva-plugins\",\n      \"version\": \"obs-controller/v0.1.0\",\n      \"asset_pattern\": \"obs-controller-{os}-{arch}.tar.gz\",\n      \"checksums\": \"checksums.txt\"\n    },\n    \"entrypoint\": \"./obs_controller\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n(`org` and `version` placeholder until the monorepo DCR fixes the names.)\n\n### Decide what happens to `Minerva/src/plugins/obs_controller/`\n\nTwo options:\n1. Delete the source from this repo entirely. Plugin lives only in minerva-plugins. Cleaner, but new contributors need the monorepo too.\n2. Keep a stub manifest pointing at the release; remove the .go files. Half-and-half — meh.\n3. Keep source as a dev convenience; manifest points at release. Means source can drift from what's released. Risk of confusion.\n\nRecommend option 1 once the monorepo is known-good. Until then, source can live in both places. Put the cutover under this work_item's done-criteria.\n\n### First release cut\n\nTag `obs-controller/v0.1.0` in the monorepo, verify the release pipeline produces all expected artifacts, verify SHA256s, verify the manifest's `binaries` block resolves cleanly when Minerva downloads.\n\n### End-to-end smoke test\n\nOn a machine that's never had OBS plugin source: install Minerva → install OBS plugin via plugin manager → confirm download proceeds with progress, hash verifies, plugin starts, OBS scenes show up. Repeat on Linux.\n\n### Update `scripts/build-plugins.sh` (if filed) accordingly\n\nIf the standalone chore (`019da41f245d78e9accbf291cb43a95c`) has already shipped, decide whether `build-plugins.sh` still does anything for OBS once the source has moved out. Probably it stops touching obs_controller and only handles whatever Go plugins remain in-tree — likely none after this migration.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Other plugins (notes_helper, test_stdio_server are Python — different story; CAD doesn't exist as a Go plugin yet).\n- Auto-update of installed plugins when new releases ship (user-triggered for v1; auto-update later).\n- Signed manifests / publisher trust (future).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A Minerva install from a clean download — no source, no toolchain — successfully installs and runs the OBS plugin.\n- The OBS plugin in this repo's `src/plugins/` is either gone or a thin stub manifest; the canonical source lives in `minerva-plugins/obs-controller/`.\n- Updating the plugin to a new version (push new tag → release → bump manifest version → user reinstalls) produces correct behavior.\n- The download flow handles common failure modes gracefully: hash mismatch, network error, platform not in binaries map, GitHub rate limit.\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- Parent: MNR client-side DCR `019da41823517154afe359ec70d513bd`\n- Blocked by: client DCR (above) AND monorepo DCR `019da41ff6e571cc8262f19bd3586d52`\n- Discussion thread: MNR `019da41508b87731bc98729fd7c0d3e5`","created_at":"2026-04-19T05:04:47","updated_at":"2026-04-19T05:13:54","priority":3,"tags":["binary-download","distribution","obs-controller","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019da41823517154afe359ec70d513bd","blocked_by":"019da41823517154afe359ec70d513bd, 019da41ff6e571cc8262f19bd3586d52, 019da428a51e73c19515285a8e209f8b"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019da428a51e73c19515285a8e209f8b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"secrets: capability — plugins use docket vault for credentials (OBS as test bed)","description":"Plugins today have no host-mediated way to store credentials. The OBS plugin keeps `obs_password` in plain JSON in the user's plugin data directory (`config.json`), and the panel UI has no way to set it — users either edit the file by hand or call the `configure` MCP tool through Minerva. Both are bad UX and the storage is plaintext.\n\nMinerva's docket subsystem already has an encrypted vault (`docket_secret_set/get/list/delete`) — project-scoped, requires a user-configured vault password, supports 2FA, versioned. This work_item exposes that vault to plugins through a new `secrets:` capability in `CapabilityBroker`, and proves the path end-to-end by migrating the OBS plugin to use it.\n\nThis is sibling to the `core_service:` capability handler in the parent DCR — same broker, same permission-gating model, different prefix.\n\n## Scope\n\n### 1. New capability type in `CapabilityBroker.gd`\n\nAdd a `secrets:` branch to `dispatch()`:\n\n```gdscript\nif capability.begins_with(\"secrets:\"):\n    return await _handle_secrets(plugin_id, capability, args)\n```\n\nOperations:\n- `secrets:get:&lt;handle&gt;` → returns secret value (or null if unset)\n- `secrets:set:&lt;handle&gt;` → stores `args.value`\n- `secrets:delete:&lt;handle&gt;` → removes\n- `secrets:list` → returns declared handles for this plugin (no values)\n\n**Critical isolation:** the broker auto-prefixes the docket handle with the plugin id. A plugin declaring `secrets:get:obs_password` actually reads docket handle `plugin/obs-controller/obs_password`. Plugins literally cannot construct a handle that reaches another plugin's secrets — the prefix is enforced by the broker, not by trust. Manifest declarations specify the unprefixed handle (\"obs_password\"); the broker prepends namespace transparently.\n\n### 2. Manifest schema: declared secrets\n\nPlugins declare which secret handles they need in `permissions.secrets`:\n\n```json\n\"permissions\": {\n  \"secrets\": [\"obs_password\"]\n}\n```\n\n`PluginPolicy.check_capability()` only grants `secrets:*:&lt;handle&gt;` for handles in this list. Wildcards work the same way as other capabilities (`secrets:*:obs_password` grants get/set/delete on that one handle).\n\n### 3. Vault-not-configured fallback\n\nThe docket vault requires a user-set vault password. Decide what happens when it's not configured:\n\n- **Strict:** plugin secret operations fail with a clear \"vault not configured\" error; plugin's settings UI shows \"set up Minerva vault password to save credentials\" with a link to wherever vault password gets set in Minerva.\n- **Permissive:** broker falls back to plaintext storage in plugin data dir (today's behavior). Bad — defeats the point.\n\nRecommend strict. The first time a plugin needs a secret, Minerva prompts the user to configure the vault. One-time setup, then it's invisible.\n\n### 4. Use OBS plugin as the test bed\n\na) **Manifest:** add `permissions.secrets: [\"obs_password\"]`. Add new IPC messages to `ui.ipc_messages` for `obs.set_config` and `obs.get_config` so the panel can talk to the plugin about settings.\n\nb) **Plugin code:** stop reading password from `config.json`. On startup (or first connect), request `secrets:get:obs_password` via capability_request. Store the result in memory only. The `configure` MCP tool's password parameter, when provided, calls `secrets:set:obs_password` instead of writing to disk. The non-password fields (host, port, camera_source, output_directory, auto_connect) keep using `config.json` since they aren't sensitive.\n\nc) **Panel UI:** add a collapsible settings section in `panel.html` with inputs for host/port/password/camera/output-dir/auto-connect. Submit dispatches `obs.set_config` → plugin → `secrets:set` for password + `UpdateConfig` for the rest. Password field shows `(set)` / `(not set)` indicator; never shows the value.\n\n### 5. Migration for existing users\n\nIf an OBS plugin user already has a password in `config.json` from the old version: on first start of the new code, read it from disk, call `secrets:set:obs_password`, blank the field in `config.json`, save. One-shot migration. Log it.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Generic credential manager UI inside Minerva for browsing all stored secrets (docket has its own — surface that, don't duplicate).\n- Secrets shared across plugins (intentional: isolation is the whole point).\n- 2FA-protected plugin secrets. Vault supports it but adding a UX path for plugins to demand the secondary password every time is overkill for v1; defer until a plugin has a use case.\n- Hardware-backed secret enclaves (Touch ID, YubiKey). Future.\n\n## Open calls\n\n- **Docket project for plugin secrets.** Plugin secrets need to land in *some* docket project. Probably `minerva` (current primary), with handle namespace `plugin/&lt;id&gt;/&lt;handle&gt;`. Confirm before implementing.\n- **Vault password setup UX.** Where in Minerva does a user set their vault password today? If it's a hidden CLI thing, this work item probably needs a sibling chore for \"expose vault password setup in Minerva preferences UI.\"\n- **Secret value transport.** When the plugin asks for a secret over capability_request, the value crosses the stdio boundary in plaintext. That's fine since it's a parent process talking to its own subprocess via a private pipe — no network — but worth documenting. The threat model is \"filesystem snooping,\" not \"process-to-process snooping.\"\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A plugin with `permissions.secrets: [\"foo\"]` can call `secrets:set:foo` and later `secrets:get:foo` and get back the same value.\n- A plugin without that declaration gets denied at the policy layer (existing wildcard policy machinery).\n- A plugin cannot read another plugin's secrets — `secrets:get:other_plugin_secret` either fails policy check or resolves to a different docket handle.\n- OBS plugin: panel has a settings form, password field stores via vault, `obs_password` no longer appears in plaintext anywhere on disk.\n- Existing-user migration: starting OBS plugin with a populated `config.json.obs_password` migrates the value into the vault and clears the disk field, transparently.\n- Vault not configured: clear error message with actionable next step (set vault password); plugin doesn't crash.\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- Parent DCR (sibling capability work — `core_service:` handler shares the same broker pattern): `019da41823517154afe359ec70d513bd`\n- Discussion thread: `019da41508b87731bc98729fd7c0d3e5`\n- Sibling work_item that depends on the OBS plugin landing in monorepo first: `019da4208241713981cd58503cc82c8e` (the OBS migration to binary-download tier — secrets work should land before the migration, so the binary released to GitHub already has the vault integration baked in).","created_at":"2026-04-19T05:13:40","updated_at":"2026-04-19T05:13:40","priority":2,"tags":["capability-broker","docket-vault","obs-controller","plugins","secrets","security"],"parent":"minerva:019da41823517154afe359ec70d513bd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019da4346dfe7d96aef80ee057423df7","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Vault password setup UI in Preferences","description":"The docket vault (used by the new plugin secrets: capability) requires a user-configured vault password stored in `user://docket_prefs.json` via `UserPrefs.save_vault_password()`. Today there is **no UI in Minerva to set this password**. PreferencesPopup.gd has no field for it. Users hit the \"Vault password not configured\" error from any vault tool and have no in-app path to fix it.\n\nThis blocks discoverability for the secrets: capability work — the OBS plugin (and any future plugin) will surface \"vault not configured\" the first time a user opens it, with no obvious next step.\n\n## Scope\n\nAdd a \"Vault\" tab or section to PreferencesPopup with:\n\n- Password input (set / change vault password)\n- Optional password hint field (`UserPrefs.save_vault_password_hint()`)\n- Confirm-password field (avoid typos locking the vault)\n- Clear messaging: \"This password encrypts plugin secrets and credentials. Forgetting it means re-entering all stored secrets.\"\n- Status indicator: \"Vault password: set\" / \"Vault password: not set\"\n- (Optional) \"Verify\" button: tries to decrypt the existing vault to confirm the entered password matches stored secrets\n\nWhen changing an existing vault password: warn that all existing secrets will need to be re-decrypted with the old password and re-encrypted with the new (or accept that they'll be lost if old password unknown). Decide approach during impl.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- 2FA secondary password setup (vault supports it; UX is a separate ask)\n- Importing/exporting secrets across vaults\n- Hardware-backed key storage (Touch ID, etc.)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A user can open Preferences in Minerva, navigate to a Vault section, and set their vault password.\n- After setting it, plugin secret operations work (no more \"Vault password not configured\" error).\n- Changing the password handles existing secrets correctly (or warns clearly if it can't).\n- Setting an empty password is rejected with a clear error.\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- Parent: secrets: capability work_item `019da428a51e73c19515285a8e209f8b`\n- Triggering need: OBS plugin needs the vault to store its password; without this UI users have no in-app way to enable the feature.\n- Related: docket subsystem code at `src/Scripts/Services/Docket/` — vault crypto and UserPrefs already exist; this is purely a Preferences UI add.","created_at":"2026-04-19T05:26:32","updated_at":"2026-04-19T05:26:32","priority":3,"tags":["discoverability","docket-vault","preferences","secrets","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019da428a51e73c19515285a8e209f8b"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019da442531670cf855872290cdcd795","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Migrate plaintext credentials to OS-native secret stores (Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI)","description":"Two credentials currently live in plaintext on disk:\n\n1. **Vault password** — `user://docket_prefs.json`, top-level JSON field `vault_password`. This password is the encryption key for plugin secrets stored in the docket vault (CapabilityBroker.secrets:* handler). Storing the key next to the encrypted data defeats the encryption: anyone with read access to `~/Library/Application Support/Godot/app_userdata/Minerva/` can decrypt all plugin secrets in one step.\n\n2. **Core (TurnRock) auth password** — `user://Preferences.agent`, written via `config_file.save_encrypted_pass()` with the OS unique ID as the key (`PreferencesPopup.gd:80, 250`). The \"encryption\" key is `OS.get_unique_id()` — a stable per-machine identifier, not a secret. Anyone with the file plus the same machine (or who can guess the unique ID format) can decrypt.\n\nBoth should live in the OS's native credential store — protected by user login, isolated from arbitrary file reads, and integrated with system-level access policies.\n\n## Scope\n\nAdd a thin GDExtension that wraps each platform's credential API:\n\n| Platform | API | Library |\n|---|---|---|\n| macOS | Security framework | `SecItemAdd` / `SecItemCopyMatching` / `SecItemDelete` |\n| Linux | Secret Service over D-Bus | `libsecret` (`secret_password_store_sync`, etc.) |\n| Windows | Credential Manager | `CredWrite` / `CredRead` / `CredDelete` |\n\nCommon Godot-side API (one class, three platform implementations):\n\n```gdscript\nclass_name OSSecrets\n\nstatic func set(handle: String, value: String) -> bool\nstatic func get(handle: String) -> Variant   # null if not found\nstatic func has(handle: String) -> bool\nstatic func delete(handle: String) -> bool\nstatic func list_handles() -> Array[String]   # may be no-op if backend doesn't enumerate\n```\n\nAll credentials use a Minerva-specific service prefix (e.g. `minerva.<scope>.<handle>`) so they cluster together in Keychain Access / Seahorse and don't collide with anything else on the user's machine.\n\n### Specific call-sites to migrate\n\n1. **`UserPrefs.load_vault_password()` / `save_vault_password()`** — replace JSON file read/write with `OSSecrets.get/set(\"minerva.vault.password\")`. Hint can stay in plaintext (it's intentionally a non-secret memory aid).\n2. **`PreferencesPopup` core password handling** — store via `OSSecrets.set(\"minerva.core.password\", pw)` instead of `config_file.set_value(\"HCP\", \"password\", pw)`. Read same way.\n3. **Any other plaintext credential that surfaces during implementation** (sweep config_file usages and `save_encrypted_pass()` callers; flag what's found).\n\n### Migration\n\nFor existing users, run a one-time migration on app start:\n\n- **Vault password:** if `docket_prefs.json` contains a non-empty `vault_password`, copy it to OS Secrets, then wipe the field (rewrite the JSON without it). Log the migration to the existing audit trail. Idempotent — safe to run on every startup.\n- **Core password:** same shape — read from `Preferences.agent`, write to OS Secrets, blank the field, save. Idempotent.\n\nMigration runs silently; user sees no UI change. After migration, the disk files no longer contain credentials.\n\n### Backend availability detection\n\nOS Secret Service may not be available in some environments (headless Linux without a session bus, restricted corporate Macs with Keychain disabled). If `OSSecrets.set` fails on first try:\n\n- Show a clear, one-time error: \"Secure credential storage isn't available on this system. Plugin secrets and Core authentication are temporarily disabled. [Learn more]\"\n- Don't silently fall back to plaintext — that defeats the upgrade.\n- Offer a \"use plaintext anyway\" toggle in Preferences for users who explicitly accept the risk (some headless / CI / dev scenarios). Off by default.\n\n### Build implications\n\n- **macOS:** Security.framework is part of every Mac SDK; no additional install. GDExtension links it directly.\n- **Linux:** libsecret-1 must be available at runtime (most desktop installs already have it via gnome-keyring or seahorse). Add `libsecret-1-dev` to `scripts/build-extensions.sh` Linux prereqs check.\n- **Windows:** Advapi32.lib for `CredWrite` etc. — already a default link.\n- New GDExtension lives at `src/gdextension/os_secrets/` with the same SCons / build-extensions.sh pattern as the terminal extension.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- 2FA / hardware-backed secret enclaves (Touch ID, YubiKey). Future hardening on top of OS Secrets.\n- Encrypted-at-rest backups of the secret store. Use whatever backup mechanism the OS provides.\n- Sharing secrets across machines. Out of band — user's choice (1Password, etc.).\n- Migrating API keys stored in the existing `config_file` to OS Secrets — covered in the \"what surfaces during the sweep\" item above; could be split out if it grows too large.\n\n## Open calls\n\n- **Service prefix.** `minerva.vault.password` vs `org.minerva.vault.password` (reverse-DNS). Reverse-DNS matches macOS/iOS conventions and groups visually nicely in Keychain Access. Recommend reverse-DNS.\n- **What to do when OS Secrets fails on save (mid-flight, not first-time).** Refuse to save, error to user? Or stash temporarily in memory and retry? Refuse-and-error is honest; user can re-enter.\n- **Hint storage.** The vault password hint is non-secret and useful even when the OS keychain is locked. Keep it in plaintext `docket_prefs.json` (current behavior).\n- **Keychain unlock prompts.** macOS may prompt for the user's login password when first writing a new keychain item. Verify this is acceptable UX before shipping; consider using a `kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlock` accessibility level so prompts don't recur after every reboot.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Fresh install on macOS arm64: setting a vault password through Preferences writes to Keychain, not to `docket_prefs.json`. Verified via `security find-generic-password -s minerva.vault.password`.\n- Fresh install on Linux: same, verified via `secret-tool lookup application minerva attribute password`.\n- Fresh install on Windows: same, verified via `cmdkey /list:minerva*`.\n- Existing user upgrade: launching the new build migrates plaintext password into OS Secrets and wipes the disk field on first run. Preferences UI shows \"Vault: configured\" without prompting the user to re-enter.\n- Core auth: same migration behavior for the TurnRock password in `Preferences.agent`.\n- OS Secrets backend unavailable: clear error message; no silent fallback to plaintext.\n- All existing plugin secrets stored in vault remain decryptable post-migration (the vault password is the same; only its storage location changed).\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- Parent discussion (plugin runtime architecture, where this got flagged): `019da41508b87731bc98729fd7c0d3e5`\n- Sibling work_item that built the broker-side secrets capability whose key this protects: `019da428a51e73c19515285a8e209f8b`\n- Sibling chore that just shipped the (plaintext) vault password UI in Preferences: `019da4346dfe7d96aef80ee057423df7` — that UI continues to work; only the storage backend changes.","created_at":"2026-04-19T05:41:43","updated_at":"2026-04-19T05:41:43","priority":2,"tags":["core-auth","credentials","dpapi","hardening","keychain","libsecret","security","vault"],"parent":"minerva:019da41508b87731bc98729fd7c0d3e5"}
