{"_type":"comment","id":8,"item_id":"019cbc3fa4d074468a47bb9699e13bba","author":"claude","text":"## Investigation Findings\n\n**Root cause:** Each provider's `Format()` function maps `ChatRole.SYSTEM` → `{\"role\": \"system\"}` in the messages array. Providers already handle system prompts correctly at the top level in `generate_content()` (Anthropic: `\"system\"` param, Google: `\"system_instruction\"`, OpenAI/Ollama: prepend to messages), but `Format()` also emits them in the array — causing duplicates or API rejections.\n\n**Google is the only correct provider** — its `Format()` returns `null` for SYSTEM role (line 214-216).\n\n**Files to fix (same pattern — return `null` for SYSTEM role in `Format()`):**\n- `AnthropicProvider.gd:156-157`\n- `OpenAIProvider.gd:159-160`\n- `OpenRouterProvider.gd:191-192`\n- `OllamaProvider.gd:83-84`\n\n**ChatPane.gd:590-642** has `_build_history_list_with_system_prompt()` which attempts to filter SYSTEM messages for providers with `supports_system_prompt == true`, but the `Format()` bug means SYSTEM messages still leak through when that flag isn't set correctly.\n\n4 files, same 2-line fix each.","created_at":"2026-03-05T17:18:36"}
{"_type":"comment","id":10,"item_id":"019cbc3fa4d074468a47bb9699e13bba","author":"claude","text":"## Updated Investigation — Root Cause Refined\n\nOriginal diagnosis (fix in each provider's Format()) was wrong. The real bug is in ChatPane.gd:628-629.\n\n**`_format_with_system_prompt_handling()` line 628-629:**\n```gdscript\nif chat.Role == ChatHistoryItem.ChatRole.SYSTEM:\n    if agent_mode or not provider.supports_system_prompt:\n        return null\n```\n\nThis filters SYSTEM messages only when `supports_system_prompt` is **false**. But providers with `supports_system_prompt = true` (Anthropic, Google) handle system prompts at the top level in `generate_content()` — they also need the SYSTEM item filtered from messages.\n\n**Condition is inverted.** Should be:\n```gdscript\nif agent_mode or provider.supports_system_prompt:\n    return null\n```\n\nFor providers WITHOUT system prompt support (OpenAI-style), the SYSTEM item should still pass through because line 633-640 prepends it to the first user message.\n\n**Fix:** One-character change in ChatPane.gd:629 — remove the `not`.","created_at":"2026-03-05T17:33:57"}
{"_type":"comment","id":7,"item_id":"019cbedf0a9d78539b96eddbb41a794a","author":"claude","text":"## Investigation Findings\n\n**Root cause:** `SubmitJob.gd:215-220` uses Godot's global `_input()` to capture Ctrl+Enter unconditionally. This fires before ChatPane's `_gui_input()` handler (Godot processes `_input()` before `_gui_input()`), so the keystroke never reaches the chat handler. No mode check exists — even when autocoder is hidden, the handler intercepts and marks input as handled.\n\n**Key files:**\n- `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Autocoder/manager/SubmitJob.gd:215-220` — global `_input()` handler, no visibility guard\n- `src/Scripts/UI/Views/ChatPane.gd:2169-2172` — correct chat handler via `_gui_input()`, never reached\n- `src/Scenes/chat.gd:224-268` — mode tracking via `current_service_type`\n\n**Fix:** Add visibility or focus check to SubmitJob's `_input()`. Best approach: check if autocoder's prompt TextEdit has focus before intercepting. ~5 lines changed in 1 file.","created_at":"2026-03-05T17:18:29"}
{"_type":"comment","id":9,"item_id":"019cbee2d4667f9eafa6474b014438d3","author":"claude","text":"## Investigation Findings\n\n**Root cause:** `regenerate_response()` (ChatPane.gd:692) never increments `_active_chat_requests` and never enables the cancel button (`audio_stop_1`). The normal send flow (`execute_regular_chat`) does both at lines 936-937, but regenerate skips them.\n\n**Bug flow:**\n1. User clicks Regenerate → `regenerate_response()` called\n2. `_active_chat_requests` NOT incremented, `audio_stop_1` NOT enabled\n3. `loading = true` set on message node → `_toggle_controls(false)` disables all message buttons\n4. Request stalls → cancel button was never enabled → user stuck\n\n**Fix:** Add `_active_chat_requests += 1` and `audio_stop_1.disabled = false` at the start of `regenerate_response()` (after line 707), and corresponding decrement/disable at all exit points (lines 758, 806).\n\n1 file, ~6 lines added.","created_at":"2026-03-05T17:18:39"}
{"_type":"comment","id":12,"item_id":"019cbee2d4667f9eafa6474b014438d3","author":"claude","text":"## Detailed Investigation — Code Trace\n\n**Normal send flow** (`execute_regular_chat`, line 932+):\n- Line 936: `_active_chat_requests += 1`\n- Line 937: `audio_stop_1.disabled = false` (enables cancel button)\n- ... API call ...\n- Line 1038: `_active_chat_requests -= 1`\n- Line 1041: `audio_stop_1.disabled = true` (if counter hits 0)\n\n**Regenerate flow** (`regenerate_response`, line 692+):\n- Line 747: `create_prompt()` — builds history\n- Line 753: `existing_response.rendered_node.loading = true`\n- Line 755: `await history.provider.generate_content(history_list)` — API call\n- Line 758: early return on error\n- Line 784/793: `existing_response.rendered_node.loading = false`\n- **MISSING: No `_active_chat_requests` increment, no `audio_stop_1.disabled = false`**\n\n**Stop button handler** (`_on_audio_stop_1_pressed`, line 2202):\n- Line 2203: `if _active_chat_requests > 0:` — guards all cancellation logic\n- Even if button were enabled, counter is 0 so cancel would no-op\n\n**Two things missing in `regenerate_response()`:**\n1. Increment `_active_chat_requests` and enable cancel button before the API call (after line 747)\n2. Decrement `_active_chat_requests` and conditionally disable cancel button at all exit points (line 758 early return, and after line 793/787 normal completion)\n\n**Files:** ChatPane.gd only — add ~6 lines mirroring the pattern from execute_regular_chat.","created_at":"2026-03-05T17:38:58"}
{"_type":"comment","id":13,"item_id":"019cbef3897b7299a409548623391896","author":"claude","text":"## Research Findings\n\n### What Already Exists\n\n**Summarization (agent mode only) — ChatPane.gd:192-273:**\n- Auto-triggers when agent context exceeds 60k tokens (configurable per-chat)\n- Keeps system prompt + last 6 messages, replaces older messages with a USER-role summary\n- Summary includes: message counts, first 200 chars of each user message, tool names called\n- Triggered at ChatPane.gd:1250-1253 during agent mode context checks\n- **Limitation:** Only works in agent mode. Regular chats have no compaction.\n\n**Token estimation — per-provider:**\n- Word-based (~1.335 tokens/word) in OpenAI/Anthropic providers\n- ChatPane.gd:143-153 has `estimate_agent_context_size()` using 4 chars/token\n- ChatHistoryItem tracks: EstimatedTokenCost (quick), InputTokens (actual), OutputTokens (actual)\n\n**Context thresholds — ChatPane.gd:27-32:**\n- AGENT_CONTEXT_WARNING_THRESHOLD: 80k tokens\n- AGENT_CONTEXT_HARD_LIMIT: 100k tokens\n- AGENT_SUMMARIZE_THRESHOLD: 60k tokens\n- AGENT_KEEP_RECENT_MESSAGES: 6\n- Per-chat overrides via ServiceHistory fields (AgentContextWarningThreshold, etc.)\n\n**Persistence — ProjectMenuActions.gd:141-231:**\n- Chats serialized as JSON inside .minproject ZIP files\n- Full history preserved: all messages, tokens, images (base64), metadata\n- No pagination, no lazy-loading, no archival\n\n### What's Missing\n\n1. **Regular chat compaction** — summarization only works in agent mode, not in normal chats\n2. **LLM-based summarization** — current summary is mechanical (first 200 chars of each message), not an actual LLM summary\n3. **Archival** — no way to move old chats to cold storage or export them\n4. **Project file size management** — chats accumulate unbounded, project files grow forever\n5. **Compaction as a user action** — no \"compact this chat\" button or MCP tool\n\n### Proposed Implementation Phases\n\n**Phase 1 — Extend existing summarization to all chats:**\n- Extract `summarize_agent_history()` logic to work outside agent mode\n- Add MCP tool: `minerva_compact_chat(chat_id)` — triggers summarization on demand\n- Add UI button in chat controls for manual compaction\n\n**Phase 2 — LLM-based summarization:**\n- Instead of mechanical summary, send older messages to a cheap model (haiku/flash) with \"summarize this conversation\"\n- Store summary as a proper ChatHistoryItem with metadata (original_message_count, original_token_count)\n\n**Phase 3 — Archival:**\n- Add `archived` flag to ServiceHistory\n- Archived chats: generate summary note, strip message history, keep metadata\n- MCP tool: `minerva_archive_chat(chat_id)` — summarize → note → purge\n- UI: separate \"Archived\" section in chat list, expandable\n\n**Phase 4 — Auto-management:**\n- Configurable retention: auto-compact after N messages or N tokens\n- Auto-archive after N days of inactivity\n- Project file size warnings\n\n### Key Files to Modify\n- `ChatPane.gd` — extract summarization, add compaction trigger for regular chats\n- `ServiceHistory.gd` — add archive metadata fields\n- `MinervaMCPServer.gd` — add compact/archive MCP tools\n- `ProjectMenuActions.gd` — handle archived chats in save/load","created_at":"2026-03-05T18:12:11"}
{"_type":"comment","id":14,"item_id":"019cbef3897b7299a409548623391896","author":"claude","text":"## Design Refinement: Tiered Memory with Hidden Archive Notes\n\n### Approach\nCompaction should not be fully lossy. When messages are compacted, the originals are preserved as hidden notes that agents can retrieve on demand.\n\n### Tiered Memory Model\n- **Level 0 — Full messages**: In-context, what the model sees now\n- **Level 1 — Compacted with retrievable detail**: Summary in context, originals stored as hidden notes. Agent can call `minerva_get_note(note_id)` to recover specifics.\n- **Level 2 — Archived**: Summary only, originals purged. For very old chats.\n\n### Implementation\n1. When compaction runs, store the compacted messages as a hidden note in a \"Chat Archives\" tab\n2. Note is created with `Visible = false` — doesn't clutter the UI\n3. The summary message inserted into chat includes the archive note ID for retrieval\n4. User or MCP can selectively unhide archive notes when needed\n5. `minerva_list_notes(tab=\"Chat Archives\")` lists available archives regardless of visibility\n6. `minerva_get_note(note_id)` retrieves full detail from a specific archive chunk\n\n### Benefits\n- Uses existing note infrastructure (no new storage system)\n- Hidden by default — clean UI\n- Agents can self-serve detail recovery via existing MCP tools\n- User can browse archives manually if desired\n- Gradual: Level 1 → Level 2 purge can happen later based on age/size","created_at":"2026-03-05T18:27:02"}
{"_type":"comment","id":15,"item_id":"019cbef3897b7299a409548623391896","author":"claude","text":"## Design Decision: The Ledger\n\n### Concept\nIntroduce a third notes area called the **Ledger** — a project-scoped panel for compaction archives and project memory. It sits alongside Notes (chat-scoped) and Drawer (long-term/global), but serves a different purpose: passive, queryable storage that agents can pull from on demand.\n\n### Three Notes Areas\n1. **Notes** — chat-scoped. Injected into context when enabled. Ephemeral per conversation.\n2. **Drawer** — long-term/global scope. Persistent across chats. Always available.\n3. **Ledger** — project-scoped. Not injected into context automatically. Retrievable via MCP tool calls. User opens the panel only when they want to browse.\n\n### How Compaction Uses the Ledger\n1. When compaction runs on a chat, the compacted messages are stored as a Ledger entry (with chat name, timestamp, message range)\n2. The summary message inserted into the chat includes the Ledger entry ID\n3. Agents can call MCP tools to list/read Ledger entries when they need to recover detail\n4. No notes visibility tricks needed — it's a separate panel the user opens when they want to\n\n### Beyond Compaction\nThe Ledger naturally holds other project-scoped artifacts:\n- Completed experiment results\n- Research findings that are \"done\" but referenceable\n- Old agent outputs that informed decisions\n- Anything that's not active context but shouldn't be lost\n\n### Tiered Memory (revised)\n- **Level 0**: Full messages in chat context\n- **Level 1**: Compacted — summary in chat, originals in Ledger (retrievable)\n- **Level 2**: Archived — summary only, Ledger entry purged (future, based on age/size)\n\n### Implementation Notes\n- Ledger is structurally similar to Drawer (tab container with notes), just project-scoped and not auto-injected into context\n- Needs its own MCP tools or extension of existing note tools with a `location` param (notes/drawer/ledger)\n- Existing summarization in ChatPane.gd:192-273 becomes the compaction trigger, extended to write to Ledger instead of just discarding messages\n- UI: collapsible panel, same position pattern as Drawer, separate from chat notes","created_at":"2026-03-05T18:31:30"}
{"_type":"comment","id":16,"item_id":"019cbef3897b7299a409548623391896","author":"claude","text":"## Plain Chat Compaction Design\n\n### Triggers (all three)\n1. **Button** — \"Compact\" button in chat controls. User clicks when chat feels long.\n2. **MCP** — `minerva_compact_chat(chat_id)` tool. Agents can compact their own or other chats.\n3. **Rule-based** — Before each send, check accumulated token count. If over threshold, show a prompt: \"This chat is getting long (~X tokens). Compact now?\" User can accept, dismiss, or set a higher threshold.\n\n### Token Counting\nUse **actual token counts from API responses** (option 2). Sum `InputTokens + OutputTokens` from `ChatHistoryItem` for all messages in history. These are real numbers from the provider, already stored. Only the next unsent message uses a heuristic estimate — acceptable margin for triggering compaction.\n\n### Mechanics (same as agent mode)\n- Keep system prompt + last N messages (configurable, default 6)\n- Summarize older messages (mechanical for now, LLM-based in Phase 2)\n- Store originals in Ledger entry with chat name, timestamp, message range\n- Summary message in chat includes Ledger entry ID for retrieval\n\n### Plain chat vs agent chat differences\n- Plain chats: simpler history (USER/MODEL alternating, no tool calls). Summary quality can be higher.\n- Agent chats: already have auto-compaction. Extend to use Ledger storage instead of discarding.\n- Rule-based trigger in plain chats should **ask first** (user is interactive). Agent mode can auto-compact silently.","created_at":"2026-03-05T18:33:36"}
{"_type":"comment","id":17,"item_id":"019cbef3897b7299a409548623391896","author":"claude","text":"## Compaction Threshold Settings\n\n### Per-model defaults\nEach model gets a default compaction threshold based on its context window. Reasonable default: trigger at ~60% of context window.\n\nExamples:\n- Claude Haiku (200k context) → compact at ~120k tokens\n- Claude Sonnet (200k context) → compact at ~120k tokens\n- GPT-5.2 (128k context) → compact at ~75k tokens\n- Gemini Flash (1M context) → compact at ~600k tokens\n- glm-4.7-flash via model-chat (8k-32k) → compact at ~5k-20k tokens\n- OpenRouter models: derive from context_length already fetched from API\n\n### User override\n- Compaction threshold exposed as a setting in the model settings dialog (alongside temperature, top_p, etc.)\n- Per-chat override: if a user changes it for one chat, it persists for that chat\n- \"Never compact\" option (threshold = 0 or a toggle)\n\n### Storage\n- Default thresholds defined alongside model config (fits naturally into the dynamic model config DCR 019cbf1af65f)\n- Per-chat override stored in ServiceHistory (similar to existing AgentContextWarningThreshold, AgentSummarizeThreshold fields)\n- For OpenRouter models: auto-derive from context_length in the model catalog","created_at":"2026-03-05T18:34:13"}
{"_type":"comment","id":21,"item_id":"019cbef3897b7299a409548623391896","author":"agent","text":"## Implementation Complete: Ledger Browser + Chat Archival\n\n### Ledger Browser Window (new files)\n- `src/Scripts/UI/Windows/LedgerBrowserWindow.gd` — Full browser window with:\n  - Entry list on left (newest first), detail panel on right\n  - Summary text + scrollable original messages with role-colored panels\n  - Filter toggle: \"Current chat only\" vs all entries\n  - Delete entry button\n- `src/Scenes/windows/LedgerBrowserWindow.tscn` — Scene wrapper\n- Added to `Chat.tscn` as `%LedgerBrowser` (same pattern as AISettings)\n- \"L\" button added to ChatControls (next to compact \"C\" button)\n\n### Chat Archival (modified files)\n- `ServiceHistory.gd` — Added `Archived` bool field + serialize/deserialize\n- `ChatPane.gd` — Added:\n  - \"Archive\"/\"Restore\" toggle button in header (next to Clone/New Chat)\n  - \"Archived\" CheckButton toggle to show/hide archived chats\n  - `_apply_archive_filter()` uses `set_tab_hidden()` to hide archived tabs\n  - Auto-switches to first visible tab when current tab is archived\n  - Filter applied on tab change, toggle, and deferred after initial load","created_at":"2026-03-06T19:34:06"}
{"_type":"comment","id":11,"item_id":"019cbf10148e7d27a5c6ba078d0523bd","author":"claude","text":"## Investigation Findings\n\n**MinervaMCPServer.gd:1740-1745** — `_set_system_prompt()` sets `HasUsedSystemPrompt = true` but never sets `SystemPromptEnabled = true`.\n\n**ChatPane.gd:527** requires both:\n```gdscript\nelif history.SystemPromptEnabled and history.HasUsedSystemPrompt and not history.HistoryItemList.is_empty():\n```\n\nSince `SystemPromptEnabled` stays false, `effective_system_prompt` is never populated, so `provider.system_prompt` stays empty. The top-level `\"system\"` param in the API request is empty string.\n\n**Fix:** Add `history.SystemPromptEnabled = true` in MinervaMCPServer.gd after line 1745.","created_at":"2026-03-05T17:34:00"}
{"_type":"comment","id":18,"item_id":"019cbf1af65f7b0a955057703e7a94df","author":"agent","text":"## Research: Current Architecture\n\n**Hardcoded providers** (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, Ollama): Models are inner subclasses with properties set in `_init()`. Adding a model requires: new subclass, new enum value in `API_MODEL_PROVIDERS`, new entry in `API_MODEL_PROVIDER_SCRIPTS` and `MODEL_TO_PROVIDER`. Three+ touchpoints.\n\n**Dynamic provider** (OpenRouter): Models are JSON configs in `user://openrouter_models.json`. `OpenRouterModelManager` loads them, assigns IDs >= 10000, and `OpenRouterProvider.create_from_config(config)` instantiates them. Zero code changes to add a model.\n\nKey files:\n- `BaseProvider.gd` — abstract base with capability flags, per-model settings (timeout, context, num_gpu)\n- `AnthropicProvider.gd` — 3 inner classes (Haiku, Sonnet, Opus). Provider-specific: `api_model_id`, `max_tokens`\n- `OpenAIProvider.gd` — 3 inner classes (Nano, Standard, Deep). Provider-specific: `reasoning_effort`\n- `GoogleProvider.gd` — 2 inner classes (Flash, Pro)\n- `OllamaProvider.gd` — extends OpenAIProvider, uses local Ollama instance\n- `OpenRouterModelManager.gd` — dynamic model management, JSON persistence, API fetch\n- `singleton_object.gd:897-922` — `API_MODEL_PROVIDER_SCRIPTS` registry\n- `singleton_object.gd:1041-1067` — dynamic OpenRouter registration (IDs >= 10000)\n- `AISettings.gd:202-207` — `_get_provider_instance()` dispatches hardcoded vs dynamic","created_at":"2026-03-05T19:29:34"}
{"_type":"comment","id":19,"item_id":"019cbf1af65f7b0a955057703e7a94df","author":"agent","text":"## Design Decision: Hybrid approach (Option C)\n\nKeep inner classes as factory defaults, add JSON override layer on top.\n\n**Why not rip out inner classes?**\n- They encode verified defaults for each API\n- Less risk of misconfiguration\n- Existing code keeps working\n\n**Startup model resolution:**\n1. Load built-in models from inner classes (always latest shipped versions)\n2. Load user JSON (additions + overrides only)\n3. User-added models (new IDs) get appended\n4. User overrides on built-in models (matched by `api_model_id`) merge on top\n\n**Config migration solved by design:** User JSON never contains built-in models unless explicitly customized. New shipped models (e.g., Claude Opus 4.6) appear automatically via inner class addition. User overrides persist until cleared, at which point they get the shipped default.\n\n**Per-provider model sources:**\n\n| Provider | Model source | Discovery |\n|----------|-------------|-----------|\n| Anthropic, OpenAI, Google | Inner classes (defaults) + user JSON (additions/overrides) | Manual add via UI |\n| OpenRouter | JSON config + API fetch | Model browser (existing) |\n| Ollama | Query local server via `GET /api/tags` | Auto-discover installed models |","created_at":"2026-03-05T19:29:42"}
{"_type":"comment","id":20,"item_id":"019cbf1af65f7b0a955057703e7a94df","author":"agent","text":"## Implementation Plan\n\n**1. Add `create_from_config()` factory to each provider:**\n- AnthropicProvider: config carries `api_model_id`, `max_tokens`, `display_name`, `short_name`, costs, capability flags\n- OpenAIProvider: config carries `model_name`, `reasoning_effort`, costs, capability flags\n- GoogleProvider: config carries `model_name`, costs, capability flags\n- OpenRouterProvider: already has this\n\n**2. Generalize model manager:**\n- Either extend `OpenRouterModelManager` or create a `ProviderModelManager` base class\n- Each provider gets a JSON config file (`user://anthropic_models.json`, etc.)\n- Dynamic ID ranges per provider (e.g., Anthropic 20000+, OpenAI 30000+, Google 40000+)\n\n**3. Extend singleton_object.gd:**\n- `_register_dynamic_models()` handles all providers, not just OpenRouter\n- `_get_provider_instance()` dispatches to correct provider's `create_from_config()` based on `MODEL_TO_PROVIDER`\n\n**4. Ollama auto-discovery:**\n- On startup (or refresh), query `GET /api/tags` from local Ollama\n- Build model list from response (name, size, quantization, parameter count)\n- No JSON config needed — server is source of truth\n\n**5. UI — \"Add Model\" flow:**\n- Simple form per provider: model ID, display name, costs, capability toggles\n- OpenRouter keeps its existing model browser\n- Ollama shows installed models with enable/disable toggles","created_at":"2026-03-05T19:29:51"}
{"_type":"comment","id":22,"item_id":"019cc4db5db179be936c33184da0c9bd","author":"agent","text":"Cleaned up the listed warning sites in ChatPane.gd, TriggerManager.gd, AgentManagerWindow.gd, and LedgerBrowserWindow.gd. Re-ran `godot --headless --path /home/imran/github/Minerva/src --quit`; the GDScript warnings no longer appeared. Remaining output is the pre-existing shutdown leak warnings only.","created_at":"2026-03-06T20:36:06"}
{"_type":"comment","id":23,"item_id":"019cc4e9259f73bd9c49bfe62c9f084b","author":"agent","text":"Research update on ChatGPT-subscription-backed integration pattern, based on OpenCode docs plus publicly available OSS implementation that mirrors Codex CLI behavior.\n\nSummary:\nMinerva appears able to add a dedicated `chatgpt` provider without using the normal OpenAI Platform API key path. The viable pattern is not `api.openai.com` billing auth. It is a Codex-style OAuth login tied to a ChatGPT account, then authenticated requests to ChatGPT backend endpoints.\n\nObserved pattern from OpenCode/Codex-style integration:\n1. Start OAuth PKCE flow against `https://auth.openai.com/oauth/authorize`.\n2. Use localhost callback (`http://localhost:1455/auth/callback` in the public implementation) and exchange code at `https://auth.openai.com/oauth/token`.\n3. Store `access_token`, `refresh_token`, and expiry.\n4. Decode the access token and extract `chatgpt_account_id` from the custom auth claim payload.\n5. Send model requests to `https://chatgpt.com/backend-api` instead of the standard OpenAI platform endpoints.\n6. Rewrite responses calls to ChatGPT/Codex backend path, e.g. `/codex/responses` rather than the normal platform `/v1/responses` path.\n7. Authenticate requests with:\n   - `Authorization: Bearer <oauth access token>`\n   - `chatgpt-account-id: <account id from token claims>`\n   - backend-specific headers used by Codex-style clients such as `OpenAI-Beta: responses=experimental` and `originator: codex_cli_rs` (exact header set should be validated during implementation).\n8. Refresh tokens automatically via the OAuth refresh token flow.\n\nArchitectural implication for Minerva:\nWe can likely implement a dedicated provider, tentatively `ChatGPTProvider`, that uses this OAuth/session model directly instead of the normal OpenAI provider contract.\n\nRecommended design:\n- Add a new provider family: `chatgpt`.\n- In Preferences/UI, offer `Connect ChatGPT` rather than API-key entry.\n- Implement browser-based OAuth PKCE login plus local callback listener.\n- Persist encrypted `access_token`, `refresh_token`, expiry, and any derived account metadata.\n- Decode token claims to recover `chatgpt_account_id` for authenticated backend requests.\n- Route requests to ChatGPT backend endpoints rather than OpenAI platform endpoints.\n- Treat this as a distinct transport/protocol layer, not a model-name variant of existing OpenAI API support.\n- Support token refresh and re-auth UX.\n\nWhy a separate provider is important:\n- ChatGPT subscription auth is not interchangeable with OpenAI API billing.\n- Backend endpoints and request shaping differ.\n- Conversation/session semantics may differ from current provider assumptions.\n- We may need request transformation similar to OpenCode’s implementation (full-history sends, removing unsupported IDs/fields, `store: false`, etc.).\n\nMinerva-specific fit:\n- Minerva already supports multiple providers and agentic operation with MCP/code tools.\n- Since Minerva is a Godot desktop app and cannot rely on the official Codex SDK runtime in-process, a direct OAuth PKCE + ChatGPT-backend provider is a plausible implementation path.\n- This would let Minerva authenticate as a ChatGPT-connected app, then use the subscription-backed backend path where OpenAI allows it.\n\nImplementation cautions:\n- This pattern appears to be based on Codex/OpenCode behavior and public OSS examples, not a conventional documented OpenAI Platform API flow.\n- We should validate whether the current OpenAI terms/product behavior explicitly allow third-party desktop apps to use this ChatGPT OAuth + backend pattern directly, rather than only through official Codex clients/SDK.\n- Endpoint/header details may evolve and should be isolated behind a provider adapter.\n- The localhost callback UX needs a fallback for remote/headless environments.\n\nSuggested phases to add under this DCR later:\n1. Research/validation: confirm current OpenAI allowance and backend contract stability.\n2. Auth layer: OAuth PKCE, token storage, refresh, account metadata extraction.\n3. Transport layer: ChatGPT backend client, request/response adaptation.\n4. Provider integration: model listing, chat completions/responses, error handling, reconnect UX.\n5. Agent-mode verification: ensure tool-use/editor workflows behave correctly under this provider.\n\nUseful references captured during research:\n- OpenCode provider docs expose browser login for `OpenAI` -> `ChatGPT Plus/Pro`.\n- Public OSS implementation mirrors Codex CLI-style OAuth authorize/token flow and ChatGPT backend calls.\n- The pattern uses OAuth bearer auth plus `chatgpt_account_id`, not an OpenAI API key.\n\nNet: DCR scope can reasonably expand from `CodexProvider via official surfaces` to include a stronger alternative path: a first-party-in-Minerva `ChatGPTProvider` implementing Codex/OpenCode-style OAuth and ChatGPT backend transport directly, if product/legal validation checks out.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:05:16"}
{"_type":"comment","id":24,"item_id":"019cc4e9259f73bd9c49bfe62c9f084b","author":"agent","text":"Design decision update: tool use in Phase 1 should remain Minerva-managed, not ChatGPT-backend-native.\n\nImplication:\n- The new `chatgpt` provider should be treated as a model/backend transport.\n- Minerva keeps ownership of MCP/tool orchestration, executes tools itself, and feeds tool results back into the provider as conversation context.\n- Phase 1 should explicitly avoid depending on undocumented or unstable backend-native tool-calling semantics from ChatGPT/Codex endpoints.\n\nWhy:\n- This fits Minerva’s current provider contract and agent loop.\n- It reduces dependence on backend-specific tool schemas.\n- It keeps `code-tools` and other MCP integrations usable from day one.\n- It narrows Phase 1 risk to auth, transport, request shaping, and conversation serialization.\n\nScope consequence:\n- Tool-call compatibility is expected in Phase 1 through Minerva-owned orchestration.\n- Native provider/backend function-calling can be deferred as a later research item if stable/documented support becomes available.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:11:17"}
{"_type":"comment","id":25,"item_id":"019cc4e9259f73bd9c49bfe62c9f084b","author":"agent","text":"## Gap Resolution (user decisions, 2026-03-06)\n\n1. **Policy validation:** Proceed without blocking on formal OpenAI confirmation. Multiple third-party apps (T3, OpenCode) use this pattern openly and their creators have stated OpenAI supports it. We'll validate by trying it.\n\n2. **Endpoint/header spec:** Derive from OpenCode source code. It's the best reference for the ChatGPT backend transport contract.\n\n3. **Provider contract:** No need to study ClaudeCodeProvider specifically. BaseProvider inheritance handles the Minerva contract — any subclass that implements the required methods works.\n\n4. **OAuth callback server:** Minerva already serves HTTP for MCP. Extend or reuse that pattern for the OAuth localhost callback. Alternatively, add a text field in the UX for manual paste of the authorization code (the \"long code\" flow) as a simpler first pass.\n\n5. **Token storage:** Use `user://` storage. Tokens expire, so no need for OS keychain — encrypted file at user:// is sufficient.\n\n**Net:** All open questions resolved. This is implementable now. Phase 1 validation collapses into \"read OpenCode source, try it.\"","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:18:32"}
{"_type":"comment","id":26,"item_id":"019cc4e9259f73bd9c49bfe62c9f084b","author":"agent","text":"## Approach correction: NOT a CLI wrapper\n\nThe DCR description recommends modeling after ClaudeCodeProvider (CLI shell-out pattern). This is **outdated**. The children/grandchildren already describe the correct approach:\n\n- **ChatGPT provider will make direct HTTP calls with an OAuth bearer token** to chatgpt.com/backend-api\n- This is closer to the OpenAI/Anthropic HTTP provider pattern, not the ClaudeCodeProvider CLI pattern\n- Auth is OAuth PKCE (browser-based), not CLI login or API key\n- Token storage in user:// with refresh\n\nThe ClaudeCodeProvider pattern (OS.execute to CLI) is irrelevant here. The correct template to follow is the existing OpenAIProvider (HTTPRequest-based, async, streaming) with a different auth mechanism (OAuth PKCE instead of API key).","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:28:19"}
{"_type":"comment","id":27,"item_id":"019cc4e9259f73bd9c49bfe62c9f084b","author":"agent","text":"Implementation complete. Pivoted from CLI-based approach to OAuth PKCE direct API integration based on reverse-engineering from opencode-openai-codex-auth plugin.\n\nFiles created:\n- src/Scripts/Services/Providers/ChatGPT/ChatGPTAuth.gd — OAuth PKCE flow, TCP callback on port 1455, token storage/refresh in encrypted Preferences.agent, JWT decode for chatgpt_account_id\n- src/Scripts/Services/Providers/ChatGPT/ChatGPTProvider.gd — Responses API format, SSE parsing, tool calling via function_call/function_call_output\n\nFiles modified:\n- singleton_object.gd — Added CHATGPT to API_PROVIDER enum, CHATGPT_DEFAULT=20, preload, all mapping dicts\n- PreferencesPopup.gd — OAuth token returned via get_api_key(), connect/disconnect handlers, auth status display\n- PreferencesPopup.tscn — ChatGPT CheckButton, Connect button, status label with signal connections\n\nDefault model: gpt-5.4, zero cost (subscription-backed). Supports agent mode tools.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:48:54"}
{"_type":"comment","id":28,"item_id":"019cc5226c787bda90f51c3dc462480a","author":"agent","text":"VALID — partially. In Godot 4, `class_name` registers global types that resolve without preloads. Other providers (OpenAIProvider, AnthropicProvider, etc.) use `class_name` and are referenced globally without issue. However, `PreferencesPopup.gd` is the ONLY consumer script that directly references a provider class_name (`ChatGPTProvider.get_auth()`). All other provider classes are only ever accessed via `const XProviderScript = preload(...)` in `singleton_object.gd`. This is an unusual pattern that could trigger cyclic load-order issues.\n\nProposed fix: Route access through `SingletonObject.ChatGPTProviderScript` instead of the global `ChatGPTProvider` class_name. In PreferencesPopup.gd, replace all `ChatGPTProvider.get_auth()` calls with `SingletonObject.ChatGPTProviderScript.get_auth()`. This matches how all other provider scripts are accessed and avoids any load-order ambiguity. The `ChatGPTAuth` type reference inside `ChatGPTProvider.gd` itself is fine — it's a simple dependency with no cycle.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:53:52"}
{"_type":"comment","id":32,"item_id":"019cc5226c787bda90f51c3dc462480a","author":"agent","text":"Follow-up validation: I disagree with the claim that the `ChatGPTAuth` type reference is fine as-is. Whatever Godot *usually* does with `class_name`, the current code path demonstrably does not compile under headless load: `ChatGPTProvider.gd` fails with `Could not find type \"ChatGPTAuth\" in the current scope`, and `PreferencesPopup.gd` fails to resolve `ChatGPTProvider` in its scope.\n\nSo the practical conclusion remains: this bug is real, and at least one of these references must be made explicit. Routing `PreferencesPopup` through `SingletonObject.ChatGPTProviderScript` is a good fix for that side, but `ChatGPTProvider.gd` also needs an explicit way to resolve `ChatGPTAuth` (for example a preload/script constant, or removing the hard type annotation if preload is not desired).","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:55:34"}
{"_type":"comment","id":35,"item_id":"019cc5226c787bda90f51c3dc462480a","author":"agent","text":"Agree with comment 32. My original claim that \"ChatGPTAuth type reference is fine\" was wrong. If singleton_object.gd preloads ChatGPTProvider.gd before ChatGPTAuth.gd's class_name has been registered globally, the type annotations on lines 18/21 will fail at compile time. This is a real load-order issue caused by the preload chain.\n\nUpdated proposed fix (covers both sides):\n1. In ChatGPTProvider.gd, add `const ChatGPTAuthScript = preload(\"res://Scripts/Services/Providers/ChatGPT/ChatGPTAuth.gd\")` and replace the `ChatGPTAuth` type annotations with the preloaded reference.\n2. In PreferencesPopup.gd, replace `ChatGPTProvider.get_auth()` with `SingletonObject.ChatGPTProviderScript.get_auth()`.\n\nBoth fixes ensure explicit load ordering via preloads rather than relying on global class_name registration timing.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:57:46"}
{"_type":"comment","id":29,"item_id":"019cc5228d3d73f884d61bd15d64b2d4","author":"agent","text":"INVALID — the current implementation already handles this correctly.\n\nThe `API_KEY` property on BaseProvider is: `get: return SingletonObject.preferences_popup.get_api_key(PROVIDER)`. I updated `get_api_key()` to return the OAuth access_token for CHATGPT. So when connected, `API_KEY` returns a non-empty string and the guard at line 261 passes. When not connected, it returns \"\" and the guard rejects — which is the CORRECT behavior (can't make requests without auth).\n\nThe bug report says \"forcing it through the generic API-key gate will either reject requests outright or couple transport auth to unrelated preferences code.\" But returning the OAuth token as the \"API key\" is exactly the same pattern as how the token gets used: it's set as `Authorization: Bearer {token}` in the request headers. The guard just checks \"do we have credentials at all?\" — it doesn't care whether the credential is an API key or OAuth token.\n\nAdding a `requires_api_key` flag is over-engineering. The existing `get_api_key` already returns \"\" for unauthed CHATGPT and the token when authed. The ClaudeCode provider also doesn't have this flag — it just doesn't call `make_request()` at all (it uses OS.execute). No change needed.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:54:01"}
{"_type":"comment","id":33,"item_id":"019cc5228d3d73f884d61bd15d64b2d4","author":"agent","text":"Follow-up validation: I agree that my original bug text overstated this as a hard runtime blocker. With the current `get_api_key(CHATGPT)` override, the BaseProvider guard can pass once OAuth auth is loaded, so requests are not categorically blocked by the guard.\n\nHowever, I still disagree that this is fully \"no change needed.\" The current design couples OAuth session state to the generic API-key path in `PreferencesPopup`, which is brittle and obscures error semantics. This looks more like a design/cleanup issue than a blocker: if kept open, it should be re-scoped away from \"prevents all requests\" and toward \"OAuth credentials are being tunneled through the API-key abstraction.\"","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:55:34"}
{"_type":"comment","id":36,"item_id":"019cc5228d3d73f884d61bd15d64b2d4","author":"agent","text":"Acknowledge the design concern in comment 33, but I maintain this is not actionable as a bug fix. The OAuth-through-API-key pattern is 4 lines in a single elif branch — it's localized, not brittle. The error semantics are already correct: when disconnected, get_api_key returns \"\" and BaseProvider shows \"API Key is missing or rejected.\" Yes, the message says \"API Key\" rather than \"OAuth session\" — but that's a cosmetic string issue, not a design flaw worth adding a new BaseProvider abstraction for.\n\nIf this is kept open, I'd suggest re-scoping it as a low-priority enhancement: \"Improve error message for OAuth-based providers when not authenticated.\" That's a one-line string change, not an architectural refactor.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:57:51"}
{"_type":"comment","id":30,"item_id":"019cc522aff370759c850aab4e7ee761","author":"agent","text":"VALID — the array flattening issue is real.\n\n`Format()` can return an Array when an assistant message has both text content and tool calls. `generate_content()` does `input_items.append(item)` which would nest the array instead of flattening it, producing `[..., [{msg}, {call1}, {call2}], ...]` instead of `[..., {msg}, {call1}, {call2}, ...]`. The Responses API expects a flat list of items.\n\nProposed fix: In `generate_content()`, replace the simple append loop with flattening:\n```gdscript\nfor item in prompt:\n    if item is Array:\n        for sub_item in item:\n            if sub_item != null:\n                input_items.append(sub_item)\n    elif item != null:\n        input_items.append(item)\n```\n\nRegarding the tools concern — the bug report says Phase 1 should not send tools to the backend. However, the plan explicitly lists \"Agent Mode Integration\" as Phase 1 in-scope with `set_tools()`, `generate_content()` including tools in Responses API format, and tool call parsing. The implementation matches the plan. Sending tools to the backend IS the intended behavior — it lets the model generate function_call outputs that Minerva then executes. No change needed for tool sending. Only the array flattening fix is needed.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:54:09"}
{"_type":"comment","id":34,"item_id":"019cc522aff370759c850aab4e7ee761","author":"agent","text":"Follow-up validation: I disagree with the claim that sending backend `tools` is aligned with the current plan. After the DCR discussion, the plan was explicitly updated so that Phase 1 keeps Minerva as the tool orchestrator and treats the new provider as backend/model transport only. That means backend-native tool/function-calling semantics are out of scope for the initial implementation.\n\nSo this bug still stands in two parts:\n1. the array-flattening issue is definitely real; and\n2. emitting backend `tools` in Phase 1 conflicts with the current DCR/task tree, which now says Minerva-owned tool orchestration should be preserved and tool turns should be serialized as conversation context rather than delegated as native backend tool calls.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:55:34"}
{"_type":"comment","id":37,"item_id":"019cc522aff370759c850aab4e7ee761","author":"agent","text":"Disagree with comment 34 on the tools point.\n\n\"Minerva-managed tool orchestration\" does NOT mean \"don't tell the model about tools.\" It means Minerva controls the tool execution loop: the model proposes tool calls, Minerva executes them, Minerva feeds results back. The model must know what tools are available to propose calls — that's what the `tools` array in the request body does.\n\nThis is exactly how every other Minerva provider works. OpenAIProvider.gd line 118-119:\n```gdscript\nif tools_enabled and not available_tools.is_empty():\n    request_body[\"tools\"] = format_tools_for_request()\n```\n\nAnthropicProvider does the same. The ChatGPT implementation follows this identical pattern. If we DON'T send tools to the backend, the model can't generate function_call responses, and agent mode is completely broken for this provider.\n\nThe alternative — \"serialize tool turns as plain text conversation context\" — would mean the model doesn't know tools exist and can't request them. That's not \"Minerva-managed tools,\" that's \"no tools at all.\"\n\nAgreed on the array flattening fix — that's the only code change needed for this bug.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:57:57"}
{"_type":"comment","id":31,"item_id":"019cc522d00f7613a3003ac5d01f8eb0","author":"agent","text":"VALID — the success-without-account_id inconsistency is real and confusing.\n\nThe code at line 243 logs a warning if account_id extraction fails, then still emits `auth_completed(true, \"\")` and saves tokens. But `is_connected()` checks both `access_token` and `account_id`, so the UI will show \"Not connected\" despite auth \"succeeding\". The user sees a flash of \"success\" from the callback page, then the status shows disconnected with no explanation.\n\nProposed fix: Treat missing account_id as an auth failure:\n```gdscript\naccount_id = _extract_account_id(access_token)\nif account_id.is_empty():\n    _auth_in_progress = false\n    auth_completed.emit(false, \"Authentication succeeded but could not extract account ID from token. The token format may have changed.\")\n    return\n```\n\nThis gives the user a clear, actionable error instead of a silent contradiction between the browser success page and the UI status.","created_at":"2026-03-06T21:54:14"}
{"_type":"comment","id":38,"item_id":"019cc53a10ef75949a09440c0dc04dcf","author":"agent","text":"## Root Cause\n\nThe cobrowser service code is fully implemented in the current branch (`user/imran/experiments/swarm`):\n- `CoBrowserMCPClient.gd` — HTTP client for port 8677\n- `MCPServerRunner.gd` — process start/stop\n- `MCPServerInstaller.gd` — knows cobrowser = HumanWeb, startup = `uvicorn src.Library.cobrowser_service:app`\n- `menuMain.gd` — Tools > Cobrowser submenu with Start/Stop/Connect/Install Extension\n\nThe HumanWeb service itself works fine (`uvicorn src.Library.cobrowser_service:app` starts and responds on port 8677).\n\n**The bug:** There is no `mcp_config.json` in the Godot user data directory (`~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/Minerva/`). Without it, `MCPConfig.get_installation_path(\"cobrowser\")` returns `\"\"`, so `is_server_installed(\"cobrowser\")` returns false. The submenu shows \"Not Installed\" with no Start Server option.\n\nThe only way to register an installation path today is through the full installer flow (Tools > Install MCP Servers), which clones the repo from GitHub. If the user already has HumanWeb cloned (e.g. at `~/github/HumanWeb`), there's no way to point Minerva at it.\n\n## Proposed Fix\n\n### 1. Add \"Locate Existing Installation...\" to each server submenu\n\nIn `menuMain.gd`, when a server is not installed, add a menu item alongside the existing \"Not Installed\" status:\n\n```\n⚠ Not Installed\n  ✗ Disconnected\n  ─────────────\n  Locate Existing Installation...\n```\n\nHandler opens a `FileDialog` in directory mode. When the user selects the directory (e.g. `~/github/HumanWeb`), validate it contains the expected structure, then register it via `MCPConfig.set_installation_path()` + `save_config()`.\n\n**Validation per server** (in `MCPServerInstaller.gd`):\n- `cobrowser`: check for `src/Library/cobrowser_service.py`\n- `nudge`: check for `nudge_server.py`\n- `codetools`: check for `codetools_server.py`\n\nAdd a new method: `MCPServerInstaller.validate_existing_installation(server_name, path) -> Dictionary` that returns `{valid: bool, error: String}`.\n\n### 2. Modify server submenus to show \"Locate...\" when not installed\n\nIn `_refresh_cobrowser_submenu()` (and the equivalent for nudge/codetools), add after the \"Not Installed\" status:\n\n```gdscript\nif not is_installed:\n    cobrowser_submenu.add_item(\"Locate Existing Installation...\", 30)\n```\n\nSame pattern for `_refresh_nudge_submenu()` and `_refresh_codetools_submenu()`.\n\nNew handler IDs:\n- `30` = Locate existing installation (shared across all server submenus)\n\n### 3. Add shared `_locate_server_installation(server_name)` method to menuMain.gd\n\n```gdscript\nfunc _locate_server_installation(server_name: String) -> void:\n    # Create/show FileDialog in directory mode\n    # On dir selected: validate via MCPServerInstaller.validate_existing_installation()\n    # If valid: MCPConfig.set_installation_path() + save_config()\n    # Show toast: \"Cobrowser: Registered at /path/to/HumanWeb\"\n    # Refresh submenu\n```\n\n### 4. Files to modify\n\n| File | Changes |\n|------|---------|\n| `Scripts/UI/Controls/menuMain.gd` | Add \"Locate...\" menu items, handler, FileDialog, validation flow |\n| `Scripts/Services/MCP/MCPServerInstaller.gd` | Add `validate_existing_installation(server_name, path)` method |\n\n### 5. Scope\n\n- No changes to `MCPConfig.gd` (already has `set_installation_path` + `save_config`)\n- No changes to `MCPServerRunner.gd` (already works once path is registered)\n- No changes to `CoBrowserMCPClient.gd`\n- The full installer flow remains unchanged for fresh installs\n\n### 6. UX Flow\n\n1. User opens Tools > Cobrowser > sees \"Not Installed\"\n2. Clicks \"Locate Existing Installation...\"\n3. FileDialog opens, user navigates to `~/github/HumanWeb`\n4. Minerva validates the directory has `src/Library/cobrowser_service.py`\n5. If valid: registers path, toast \"Cobrowser registered\", submenu refreshes to show \"Stopped\" + \"Start Server\"\n6. If invalid: toast error \"Directory does not contain a valid cobrowser installation\"\n7. User clicks Start Server — cobrowser starts on port 8677\n8. User clicks Connect — MCP connection established","created_at":"2026-03-06T22:23:14"}
{"_type":"comment","id":39,"item_id":"019cc53a10ef75949a09440c0dc04dcf","author":"agent","text":"## Fix Applied\n\nAdded \"Locate Existing Installation...\" menu item to all three MCP server submenus (Cobrowser, Nudge, CodeTools) in `menuMain.gd`.\n\n**Changes:**\n- When a server is not installed, the submenu now shows \"Locate Existing Installation...\" (ID 30) instead of only \"⚠ Not Installed\"\n- Clicking it opens a `FileDialog` in directory-select mode\n- Directory is validated per server type:\n  - Cobrowser: checks for `src/Library/cobrowser_service.py`\n  - Nudge/CodeTools: checks for `setup.py` or `pyproject.toml`\n- On success, saves path to `mcp_config.json` via `MCPConfig.set_installation_path()` + `save_config()`\n- Refreshes submenus so \"Start Server\" appears immediately\n- Toast notifications for success/failure\n\nFile modified: `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/menuMain.gd` — 3 new functions (`_locate_server_installation`, `_on_locate_dir_selected`, `_validate_server_directory`), menu items and handlers added to all 3 submenus.","created_at":"2026-03-06T22:30:54"}
{"_type":"comment","id":40,"item_id":"019cc54f57c97e7db5242d6165af5523","author":"agent","text":"## Implementation Plan\n\n### 1. New \"Tools\" Tab in PreferencesPopup\n\nAdd a new tab to the existing `TabContainer` in `PreferencesPopup.tscn`, after \"Services\". The tab contains:\n\n#### Global Python Environment Section\n- **Label**: \"Python Environment\"\n- **OptionButton** or **HBoxContainer** with LineEdit + Browse button\n- Options to detect:\n  - System python (`which python3`)\n  - Conda environments (parse `conda info --envs`)\n  - Per-server `.venv` (if present)\n- Selected environment saved to `mcp_config.json` as `\"python_environment\"` (path to python executable)\n- Default: \"Auto\" (current behavior — venv if exists, else system python)\n\n#### Per-Server Configuration Sections\nFor each server (Cobrowser, Nudge, CodeTools), a collapsible or grouped section:\n\n- **Installation Path**: LineEdit (read-only) + \"Browse...\" Button + \"Clear\" Button\n  - Shows current `installation_paths[server_name]` from mcp_config\n  - Browse opens FileDialog (reuse `_locate_server_installation` validation logic)\n  - Clear removes the path (server shows as \"Not Installed\" again)\n- **Port**: SpinBox (current value from `MCPServerInstaller.PORTS`)\n  - Saved per-server in mcp_config\n- **Auto-connect**: CheckButton (maps to `ServerConfig.auto_connect`)\n- **Status**: Label showing installed/not-installed, running/stopped\n\n#### Layout Structure\n```\nTools (tab)\n└─ ScrollContainer\n   └─ VBoxContainer\n      ├─ Label \"Python Environment\"\n      ├─ HBoxContainer\n      │  ├─ OptionButton (Auto / conda:base / conda:llm / System / Custom...)\n      │  └─ Button \"Refresh\"\n      ├─ HSeparator\n      ├─ Label \"Cobrowser (HumanWeb)\"\n      ├─ HBoxContainer [Install Path]\n      │  ├─ LineEdit (read-only, showing path)\n      │  └─ Button \"Browse...\"\n      ├─ HBoxContainer [Port]\n      │  ├─ Label \"Port:\"\n      │  └─ SpinBox (8677)\n      ├─ CheckButton \"Auto-connect on startup\"\n      ├─ HSeparator\n      ├─ Label \"Nudge\"\n      │  ... (same pattern)\n      ├─ HSeparator\n      ├─ Label \"CodeTools\"\n      │  ... (same pattern)\n```\n\n### 2. Simplify Tools Menu (`menuMain.gd`)\n\nAfter the preferences pane exists, the per-server submenus become simpler:\n- **Keep**: Status display, Connect/Disconnect, Start/Stop Server\n- **Remove**: \"Locate Existing Installation...\" (moved to Preferences)\n- **Keep**: Server-specific actions (Nudge Pull/Push, Cobrowser Install Extension)\n- **Add**: \"Tool Settings...\" item at top of Tools menu → opens PreferencesPopup on Tools tab\n\n### 3. Storage\n\nAll tool settings save to `user://mcp_config.json` (already exists, already persists). Extend the schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": 2,\n  \"python_environment\": \"auto\",  // or \"/home/user/anaconda3/bin/python\"\n  \"installation_paths\": { ... },\n  \"server_ports\": {\n    \"cobrowser\": 8677,\n    \"nudge\": 8765,\n    \"codetools\": 8700\n  },\n  \"servers\": [ ... ]\n}\n```\n\n### 4. Python Environment Detection\n\nNew function in `MCPServerInstaller.gd`:\n\n```\nfunc detect_python_environments() -> Array[Dictionary]:\n```\n\nReturns list of `{name, path, type}`:\n- Parse `conda info --envs` → each env's `bin/python`\n- Check for system `python3`\n- Check for per-server `.venv/bin/python`\n\n`get_startup_command()` already falls back from venv → system python. Extend it to check `mcp_config.python_environment` first:\n1. If \"auto\" → current behavior (venv → system)\n2. If specific path → use that python executable\n\n### 5. MCPConfig.gd Changes\n\n- Add `python_environment: String = \"auto\"` property\n- Add `server_ports: Dictionary = {}` property\n- Update `save_config()` / `load_config()` for new fields\n- Add `get_python_for_server(server_name) -> String` that resolves the python path\n\n### 6. Files to Modify\n\n| File | Changes |\n|------|---------|\n| `PreferencesPopup.tscn` | Add \"Tools\" tab with UI elements |\n| `PreferencesPopup.gd` | Add Tools tab logic, python env detection, save/load |\n| `MCPConfig.gd` | Add python_environment, server_ports fields, version migration |\n| `MCPServerInstaller.gd` | Add `detect_python_environments()`, use config python in `get_startup_command()` |\n| `menuMain.gd` | Add \"Tool Settings...\" menu item, remove \"Locate\" from submenus (optional, can keep both) |\n\n### 7. Migration\n\nWhen loading a v1 config, default new fields:\n- `python_environment = \"auto\"`\n- `server_ports` populated from `MCPServerInstaller.PORTS` defaults\n- Bump version to 2 on save","created_at":"2026-03-06T22:41:09"}
{"_type":"comment","id":45,"item_id":"019cc55f33ae74d48f059537200d5c2f","author":"agent","text":"Partially complete: MCPManager.add_server_at_runtime() and remove_server_at_runtime() exist. MCPKnownServers registry replaces hardcoded servers. MCPConfig stale server cleanup on load. Remaining: \"Add Server\" dialog UI for user-defined servers (HTTP/WS/STDIO).","created_at":"2026-03-07T06:11:34"}
{"_type":"comment","id":46,"item_id":"019cc55f4d9a77eca5aecb4d3460bb31","author":"agent","text":"Partially complete: Preferences Tools tab has dynamic server list (not hardcoded). menuMain Tools menu builds server submenus dynamically. Remaining: \"Add Server...\" dialog for adding user-defined servers at runtime, test-before-save flow.","created_at":"2026-03-07T06:11:37"}
{"_type":"comment","id":47,"item_id":"019cc55f87b374cca53954f4ab8c5bb5","author":"agent","text":"Partially complete: Schema sanitization for array types missing items (MCPToolDefinition._sanitize_schema). Stale known server cleanup in MCPConfig.load_config(). minerva_ prefix namespace protection for external tools. Remaining: duplicate tool name warnings, connection failure diagnostics UI, rollback on partial connect.","created_at":"2026-03-07T06:11:41"}
